window.SITE = {"exams":{"A":{"name":"Databricks Certified Data Engineer Associate","short":"Associate","version":"Exam guide, August 2026","guide_url":"https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Data-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","page_url":"https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/data-engineer-associate","questions":45,"minutes":90,"sprint_minutes":70,"description":"Tests whether you can build and run basic data engineering work on Databricks: ingest data, transform it with SQL and PySpark, orchestrate it with Lakeflow Jobs, deploy it with bundles, and govern it with Unity Catalog.","details":[["Scored questions","45 multiple choice, plus up to 10 unscored items that are not marked"],["Time","90 minutes (the time for unscored items is included)"],["Fee","USD 200 plus tax"],["Delivery","Online proctored or at a test centre"],["Languages","English, Japanese, Portuguese (Brazil), Korean"],["Test aids","None"],["Prerequisites","None; related training is recommended"],["Recommended experience","About 6 months of hands-on Databricks work"],["Validity","2 years"],["Recertification","Every 2 years, with the renewal exam or the current full exam"]],"sections":[{"id":1,"name":"Databricks Intelligence Platform","weight":6,"per_mock":3,"objectives":[{"id":"A1.1","text":"Explain the core platform components (lakehouse architecture, control and compute planes, Delta Lake, Unity Catalog).","topics":["platform","delta","governance"]},{"id":"A1.2","text":"Compare compute options (serverless, classic all-purpose and job clusters, SQL warehouses, pipeline compute), their limits and cost model, and pick one for a workload.","topics":["platform"]}]},{"id":2,"name":"Data Ingestion and Loading","weight":21,"per_mock":9,"objectives":[{"id":"A2.1","text":"Apply batch, streaming and incremental ingestion patterns, including local file uploads and Lakeflow Connect standard and managed connectors.","topics":["ingest-files","ingest-sources","streaming"]},{"id":"A2.2","text":"Load files from cloud object storage into Unity Catalog tables incrementally with COPY INTO.","topics":["ingest-files"]},{"id":"A2.3","text":"Use Auto Loader with schema enforcement and schema evolution, in scheduled (batch-style) runs, with directory listing or file notification.","topics":["ingest-files"]},{"id":"A2.4","text":"Configure Lakeflow Connect to ingest reliably from enterprise applications and databases into Unity Catalog tables.","topics":["ingest-sources"]},{"id":"A2.5","text":"Land data from JDBC, ODBC or REST sources with notebook code, orchestrated by Lakeflow Jobs.","topics":["ingest-sources"]},{"id":"A2.6","text":"Choose between Auto Loader, Lakeflow Connect (standard and managed), partner connectors and other methods based on volume, frequency, data type and governance needs.","topics":["ingest-files","ingest-sources","sharing"]},{"id":"A2.7","text":"Ingest semi-structured and unstructured data (JSON, nested structures, documents) into Delta tables in Unity Catalog.","topics":["semi-structured"]}]},{"id":3,"name":"Data Transformation and Modeling","weight":22,"per_mock":10,"objectives":[{"id":"A3.1","text":"Clean bronze data into silver tables with PySpark or SQL (handle nulls, standardise types, write the result).","topics":["transform","modeling","delta","cdc"]},{"id":"A3.2","text":"Combine data with inner, left, multi-key, cross and broadcast joins, and with UNION and UNION ALL.","topics":["transform"]},{"id":"A3.3","text":"Add, drop, split, rename and filter columns and rows, and explode arrays.","topics":["transform","semi-structured"]},{"id":"A3.4","text":"Deduplicate and aggregate data (counts, approximate distinct counts, averages, summary statistics).","topics":["transform"]},{"id":"A3.5","text":"Know the main Spark tuning settings (shuffle partitions, default parallelism, executor and driver memory, broadcast join threshold) and measure again after each change.","topics":["performance"]},{"id":"A3.6","text":"Choose and build gold-layer objects in Unity Catalog (views, materialized views, streaming tables, tables) for BI and analytics.","topics":["modeling","pipelines"]},{"id":"A3.7","text":"Apply data quality checks and validation rules to keep silver and gold data reliable.","topics":["pipelines","modeling"]}]},{"id":4,"name":"Working with Lakeflow Jobs","weight":16,"per_mock":7,"objectives":[{"id":"A4.1","text":"Implement control flow in jobs (retries, If/else conditions, For each loops).","topics":["jobs"]},{"id":"A4.2","text":"Configure notebook, SQL, dashboard and pipeline tasks and the dependencies between them.","topics":["jobs"]},{"id":"A4.3","text":"Schedule jobs with time-based (cron), file arrival and table update triggers.","topics":["jobs"]},{"id":"A4.4","text":"Choose between time-based and data-driven triggers based on when data arrives and on upstream dependencies.","topics":["jobs"]}]},{"id":5,"name":"Implementing CI/CD","weight":10,"per_mock":5,"objectives":[{"id":"A5.1","text":"Work with Git folders in the workspace (branch, switch, commit, push, open pull requests).","topics":["cicd"]},{"id":"A5.2","text":"Use bundle variables and target overrides to promote the same code through dev, test and prod.","topics":["cicd"]},{"id":"A5.3","text":"Deploy bundles that package jobs, declarative pipelines and other workspace assets.","topics":["cicd"]},{"id":"A5.4","text":"Use the Databricks CLI to validate, deploy and run bundles in automated CI/CD.","topics":["cicd"]}]},{"id":6,"name":"Troubleshooting, Monitoring and Optimization","weight":10,"per_mock":4,"objectives":[{"id":"A6.1","text":"Spot performance trends by comparing current run times with the job's run history.","topics":["monitoring"]},{"id":"A6.2","text":"Monitor job and pipeline health (run statuses, the task graph, upstream blockers, durations and failure rates).","topics":["monitoring","jobs"]},{"id":"A6.3","text":"Find data skew, expensive shuffles and disk spill from stage metrics in the Spark UI.","topics":["performance"]},{"id":"A6.4","text":"Explain liquid clustering and predictive optimization.","topics":["layout"]},{"id":"A6.5","text":"Diagnose cluster start failures, library conflicts and out-of-memory errors.","topics":["platform","performance"]}]},{"id":7,"name":"Governance and Security","weight":15,"per_mock":7,"objectives":[{"id":"A7.1","text":"Distinguish managed and external tables in Unity Catalog, and create, alter, drop and convert them.","topics":["governance"]},{"id":"A7.2","text":"Grant and revoke privileges with the UI and SQL at the right level of the object hierarchy, for users, groups and service principals.","topics":["governance"]},{"id":"A7.3","text":"Apply column masks and row filters so each group sees only the data it should.","topics":["privacy"]},{"id":"A7.4","text":"Use ABAC policies in Unity Catalog to manage row filters and column masks centrally.","topics":["privacy"]}]}]},"P":{"name":"Databricks Certified Data Engineer Professional","short":"Professional","version":"Exam guide, 9 October 2026","guide_url":"https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Data-Engineer-Professional-Exam-Guide-9-Oct-2026.pdf","page_url":"https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/data-engineer-professional","questions":60,"minutes":120,"sprint_minutes":95,"description":"Tests whether you can design, build, secure, optimise and operate production data pipelines on Databricks: streaming and CDC, testing and deployment, monitoring and cost control, privacy and governance.","details":[["Scored questions","60 multiple choice, plus possible unscored items that are not marked"],["Time","120 minutes (the time for unscored items is included)"],["Fee","USD 200 plus tax"],["Delivery","Online proctored or at a test centre"],["Languages","English"],["Test aids","None"],["Prerequisites","None; related training is recommended"],["Recommended experience","About 1 year of hands-on data engineering on Databricks"],["Validity","2 years"],["Recertification","Every 2 years, by passing the current exam"]],"sections":[{"id":1,"name":"Developing Code for Data Processing using Python and SQL","weight":23,"per_mock":14,"objectives":[{"id":"P1.1","text":"Structure Python projects for Declarative Automation Bundles so code is modular and ready for CI/CD.","topics":["cicd","testing"]},{"id":"P1.2","text":"Troubleshoot dependency conflicts and installation failures for PyPI packages, wheels and source archives on serverless, pipelines and bundles.","topics":["platform"]},{"id":"P1.3","text":"Write SQL, Python and pandas UDFs, including Unity Catalog functions, and choose between them.","topics":["udf-ai"]},{"id":"P1.4","text":"Build production streaming pipelines with Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines and Auto Loader.","topics":["pipelines","ingest-files"]},{"id":"P1.5","text":"Create and automate jobs with the UI, the REST API and the CLI.","topics":["jobs"]},{"id":"P1.6","text":"Choose between a streaming table and a materialized view based on latency, cost and refresh needs.","topics":["pipelines"]},{"id":"P1.7","text":"Implement CDC with the AUTO CDC APIs, for SCD type 1 and type 2.","topics":["cdc"]},{"id":"P1.8","text":"Choose between Structured Streaming code and declarative pipelines for a workload and its constraints.","topics":["streaming","pipelines"]},{"id":"P1.9","text":"Build jobs with control flow tasks (If/else condition, For each).","topics":["jobs"]},{"id":"P1.10","text":"Choose compute and configure environments and dependencies (serverless environments and performance mode, high-memory tasks, retry settings).","topics":["platform","jobs"]},{"id":"P1.11","text":"Write unit and integration tests with schema and DataFrame equality assertions, DataFrame.transform and a test framework.","topics":["testing"]},{"id":"P1.12","text":"Apply stateful streaming (watermarks, output modes, foreachBatch, checkpoints and exactly-once recovery).","topics":["streaming"]}]},{"id":2,"name":"Data Ingestion and Acquisition","weight":12,"per_mock":7,"objectives":[{"id":"P2.1","text":"Ingest Delta, Parquet, JSON, Iceberg, CSV and binary data from message buses and cloud storage.","topics":["ingest-files","ingest-sources","semi-structured","delta"]},{"id":"P2.2","text":"Build incremental CDC pipelines with Delta or Iceberg target tables.","topics":["cdc"]},{"id":"P2.3","text":"Ingest CDC from SQL Server, MySQL and PostgreSQL with Lakeflow Connect.","topics":["ingest-sources","cdc"]},{"id":"P2.4","text":"Share data with OpenSharing (Databricks-to-Databricks and open sharing) and collaborate in Clean Rooms.","topics":["sharing"]},{"id":"P2.5","text":"Configure Lakehouse Federation with connections, credentials and Unity Catalog permissions.","topics":["sharing"]}]},{"id":3,"name":"Data Manipulation","weight":12,"per_mock":7,"objectives":[{"id":"P3.1","text":"Use window functions, joins and aggregations on large data with Spark SQL and PySpark.","topics":["transform"]},{"id":"P3.2","text":"Model and query semi-structured data with VARIANT and functions such as parse_json and variant_get.","topics":["semi-structured"]},{"id":"P3.3","text":"Use AI functions such as ai_query to enrich and classify data in pipelines.","topics":["udf-ai"]},{"id":"P3.4","text":"Use expectations to drop, quarantine or fail on bad records in declarative pipelines.","topics":["pipelines"]}]},{"id":4,"name":"Monitoring and Alerting","weight":10,"per_mock":6,"objectives":[{"id":"P4.1","text":"Use system tables (billing, compute, access, lakeflow) for cost analysis, auditing and workload monitoring.","topics":["monitoring"]},{"id":"P4.2","text":"Monitor jobs and pipelines with the REST API, the CLI and the SDK.","topics":["monitoring"]},{"id":"P4.3","text":"Use the pipeline event log to monitor pipeline health and data quality.","topics":["monitoring","pipelines"]},{"id":"P4.4","text":"Use Databricks SQL alerts (the guide calls them Lakehouse alerts) on metrics, data quality, cost, warehouse health, audit events and job branching.","topics":["monitoring"]},{"id":"P4.5","text":"Use the Jobs UI and the Jobs API to follow job status and performance.","topics":["monitoring","jobs"]}]},{"id":5,"name":"Cost and Performance Optimization","weight":15,"per_mock":9,"objectives":[{"id":"P5.1","text":"Explain how managed tables, predictive optimization and liquid clustering reduce maintenance work.","topics":["layout","governance"]},{"id":"P5.2","text":"Choose a Delta optimization (deletion vectors, liquid clustering, CLUSTER BY AUTO) for an access pattern.","topics":["layout"]},{"id":"P5.3","text":"Explain how the disk cache speeds up repeated reads of the same data.","topics":["performance"]},{"id":"P5.4","text":"Use Change Data Feed to process row-level inserts, updates and deletes incrementally.","topics":["cdc"]},{"id":"P5.5","text":"Use the query profile to find poor data skipping, join strategy problems and shuffles.","topics":["performance"]},{"id":"P5.6","text":"Compare liquid clustering with partitioning and Z-ordering for a table size and query pattern.","topics":["layout"]}]},{"id":6,"name":"Ensuring Data Security and Compliance","weight":8,"per_mock":5,"objectives":[{"id":"P6.1","text":"Apply least privilege to Unity Catalog securables and workspace objects.","topics":["governance"]},{"id":"P6.2","text":"Apply ABAC policies with governed tags to enforce row filters and column masks at scale.","topics":["privacy"]},{"id":"P6.3","text":"Anonymise and pseudonymise confidential data (hashing, tokenization, suppression, generalization).","topics":["privacy"]},{"id":"P6.4","text":"Build compliant batch and streaming pipelines that detect and mask PII.","topics":["privacy"]},{"id":"P6.5","text":"Implement retention and deletion, including GDPR erasure, with Delta Lake and Unity Catalog.","topics":["privacy","delta"]}]},{"id":7,"name":"Data Governance","weight":5,"per_mock":3,"objectives":[{"id":"P7.1","text":"Use Unity Catalog tags and comments to make data discoverable.","topics":["governance"]},{"id":"P7.2","text":"Explain privilege inheritance across catalogs, schemas and objects.","topics":["governance"]}]},{"id":8,"name":"Debugging and Deploying","weight":10,"per_mock":6,"objectives":[{"id":"P8.1","text":"Find diagnostic information in the Spark UI, cluster logs, system tables and query profiles.","topics":["performance","monitoring"]},{"id":"P8.2","text":"Repair failed job runs and rerun with parameter overrides.","topics":["jobs"]},{"id":"P8.3","text":"Deploy Databricks resources with Declarative Automation Bundles.","topics":["cicd"]},{"id":"P8.4","text":"Set up Git-based CI/CD with Databricks Git folders.","topics":["cicd"]}]},{"id":9,"name":"Data Modeling","weight":5,"per_mock":3,"objectives":[{"id":"P9.1","text":"Design Delta or Iceberg table layouts (partitioning that matches the data grain, clustering that matches access patterns, file sizes kept healthy by compaction).","topics":["layout"]},{"id":"P9.2","text":"Design dimensional models with materialized views for pre-computed aggregates and Unity Catalog metric views for governed metrics.","topics":["modeling"]}]}]}},"topics":[{"id":"platform","title":"Platform and compute","group":"Foundations","order":1,"summary":"Lakehouse architecture, control and compute planes, compute types and how to choose one, access modes, libraries and environments, and why clusters fail to start.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-lakehouse\">The lakehouse</h2>\n<p>A lakehouse keeps all data in cloud object storage in open formats (Delta Lake or Apache Iceberg, both on Parquet files) and adds what a data warehouse gives you: ACID transactions, schema enforcement, fine-grained security and fast SQL. One copy of the data serves BI, data engineering, streaming and machine learning, so there is no separate lake and warehouse to keep in sync.</p>\n<p>Databricks calls its product the <strong>Data Intelligence Platform</strong>: the lakehouse plus AI features that use your metadata (Databricks Assistant, Genie, AI functions, predictive optimization).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Building block</th>\n<th>Role</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud object storage (S3, ADLS, GCS)</td>\n<td>Where the data files live, in your cloud account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delta Lake</td>\n<td>Transaction log on top of Parquet: ACID, time travel, schema enforcement</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unity Catalog</td>\n<td>One governance layer for data and AI assets: permissions, lineage, audit, discovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Photon</td>\n<td>Vectorised C++ query engine that speeds up SQL and DataFrame work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lakeflow</td>\n<td>Ingestion (Lakeflow Connect), pipelines (Spark Declarative Pipelines) and orchestration (Jobs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Databricks SQL</td>\n<td>SQL warehouses, queries, dashboards and alerts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"control-plane-and-compute-plane\">Control plane and compute plane</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Control plane</th>\n<th>Compute plane</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>What runs there</td>\n<td>Web application, notebooks and job definitions, job scheduler, cluster manager, REST APIs, Unity Catalog service</td>\n<td>The machines that process data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Where</td>\n<td>Always in the Databricks account</td>\n<td><strong>Classic compute</strong>: virtual machines in <em>your</em> cloud account and network. <strong>Serverless compute</strong>: in a Databricks-managed account, isolated per workspace, in the same region</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Your data stays in your cloud storage in both cases. Serverless only changes who owns the machines.</p>\n<p><strong>Account and workspaces.</strong> The account console manages workspaces, identities (users, groups, service principals) and Unity Catalog metastores. A metastore is the top-level Unity Catalog container; you create <strong>one per region</strong> and attach every workspace in that region to it, so all those workspaces see the same catalogs and the same grants.</p>\n<p><strong>Identities.</strong> Users are people. Groups should be <strong>account groups</strong> (created at account level), not workspace-local groups. <strong>Service principals</strong> are identities for automation: run production jobs and CI/CD as a service principal, not as a person.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compute-types\">Compute types</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Compute</th>\n<th>What it is</th>\n<th>Use it for</th>\n<th>Watch out</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Serverless compute for notebooks</td>\n<td>Managed compute that starts in seconds</td>\n<td>Interactive Python and SQL</td>\n<td>No R, no RDD API, no Spark UI (use the query profile), only a few Spark settings</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serverless jobs</td>\n<td>Managed compute for job tasks</td>\n<td>Default for scheduled jobs</td>\n<td>Streaming only with <code>availableNow</code> or <code>once</code> triggers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serverless SQL warehouse</td>\n<td>Managed SQL engine with fast start and automatic scaling</td>\n<td>BI, dashboards, ad hoc SQL, SQL tasks in jobs</td>\n<td>Runs SQL only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pro or classic SQL warehouse</td>\n<td>SQL warehouse whose machines run in your account</td>\n<td>SQL when serverless is not allowed or when the warehouse needs your private network</td>\n<td>Slower start (minutes); classic lacks some features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All-purpose cluster (classic)</td>\n<td>Cluster you create and share for interactive work</td>\n<td>Development and exploration that needs classic features</td>\n<td>Highest DBU rate; never for scheduled production jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job compute (classic)</td>\n<td>Cluster created for one job run and terminated after it</td>\n<td>Production jobs that need R, RDD, init scripts, GPUs or specific instance types</td>\n<td>Several minutes to start</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pipeline compute</td>\n<td>Compute for Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines</td>\n<td>Pipelines; serverless is recommended</td>\n<td>Classic pipeline clusters are configured in the pipeline settings</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Cost model.</strong> Work is billed in DBUs (Databricks Units) per second. On classic compute you pay DBUs to Databricks <em>and</em> the virtual machines to your cloud provider. On serverless the DBU price already includes the machines. All-purpose compute has a higher DBU rate than job compute, which is why production jobs never run on all-purpose clusters.</p>\n<p>Ways to cut classic costs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Job compute</strong> instead of all-purpose for anything scheduled.</li>\n<li><strong>Autoscaling</strong> (min and max workers) when the volume varies from run to run.</li>\n<li><strong>Auto termination</strong> on all-purpose clusters so idle clusters shut down.</li>\n<li><strong>Spot instances</strong> for workers on jobs that tolerate interruption.</li>\n<li><strong>Pools</strong> keep idle instances ready to shorten start times. Idle pool instances cost no DBUs, but the cloud provider still bills the machines.</li>\n<li><strong>Photon</strong> speeds up most SQL and DataFrame work but has a higher DBU rate on classic compute; it pays off on heavy workloads, not on small simple ones. It is always on in serverless and SQL warehouses. Python UDFs and RDD code do not run in Photon.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Serverless performance modes.</strong> Serverless jobs and pipelines run either <em>performance optimized</em> (fast start, faster runs) or <em>standard</em> (cheaper, a start-up delay of a few minutes). Choose standard for jobs where a few extra minutes do not matter.</p>\n<h2 id=\"access-modes\">Access modes</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode (old name)</th>\n<th>Who uses it</th>\n<th>Supports</th>\n<th>Does not support</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Standard</strong> (Shared)</td>\n<td>Many users, isolated from each other</td>\n<td>Python, SQL, Scala, Unity Catalog with full security</td>\n<td>R, RDD API, some low-level APIs; init scripts and JARs must be on the metastore allowlist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dedicated</strong> (Single user)</td>\n<td>One user or one group</td>\n<td>All languages including R and the RDD API, ML runtimes, GPUs</td>\n<td>Sharing with other users</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No isolation shared</td>\n<td>Legacy</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Unity Catalog</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Rule of thumb: R or RDD code means <strong>dedicated</strong> classic compute.</p>\n<p><strong>Cluster policies</strong> restrict what users can create (instance types, maximum workers, auto termination, tags, runtime versions) and can preinstall libraries. Use them to standardise configuration and control cost across a team.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-compute\">Choosing compute</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Choose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nightly production ETL in Python or SQL, lowest effort</td>\n<td>Serverless jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same, but the code needs R, RDD, init scripts, GPUs or custom instance types</td>\n<td>Classic job compute (dedicated access mode for R or RDD)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Several tasks in one job with the same classic configuration</td>\n<td>One shared job cluster referenced by <code>job_cluster_key</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Many analysts running SQL and dashboards with bursty load</td>\n<td>Serverless SQL warehouse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL that must reach a database inside your private network, with no extra networking set up</td>\n<td>Pro or classic SQL warehouse (runs in your network)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A team exploring data together in notebooks for weeks</td>\n<td>All-purpose cluster or serverless notebooks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL task inside a job</td>\n<td>Serverless or pro SQL warehouse (not classic)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weekly small job that can simply be rerun if it fails, cheapest option</td>\n<td>Job compute with spot workers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"libraries-and-environments\">Libraries and environments</h2>\n<p>Libraries can be installed at several levels. When the same package exists at more than one level, the most specific wins: <strong>notebook-scoped</strong> (<code>%pip install</code>) beats <strong>compute-scoped</strong> (cluster or task libraries), which beats the version <strong>built into the runtime</strong>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">%pip install requests==2.32.3 -r /Volumes/main/config/libs/requirements.txt\ndbutils.library.restartPython()   # restarts Python; variables defined before are lost\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Put <code>%pip</code> at the top of the notebook, then restart Python.</li>\n<li><strong>Pin versions</strong> (<code>==</code>). An unpinned library breaks the day a new release changes its API or its dependencies.</li>\n<li>Prefer <strong>wheels</strong> (<code>.whl</code>) to source archives (<code>.tar.gz</code>): a source archive must be built during installation and fails when build tools or headers are missing.</li>\n<li>Keep library files in workspace files or Unity Catalog volumes, not in the DBFS root.</li>\n<li>On <strong>serverless</strong>, there are no cluster libraries and no init scripts. Dependencies go in the notebook's <strong>Environment</strong> side panel, or in an <code>environments</code> entry referenced by <code>environment_key</code> for Python script, wheel and dbt tasks. Pipelines list their dependencies in the pipeline settings.</li>\n<li>If a serverless notebook or notebook task runs out of memory in its Python process (for example after <code>toPandas()</code>), set <strong>Memory: High</strong> in the Environment panel: 32 GB instead of 16 GB, at a higher DBU rate. It does not enlarge the Spark session, and only notebook tasks have it.</li>\n<li><strong>Serverless auto-optimization</strong> is on by default and may retry a failed serverless task on its own. For a task that must run at most once (a non-idempotent append, a call that sends emails), untick <em>Enable serverless auto-optimization</em> in the task's retry policy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-a-cluster-fails-to-start\">Why a cluster fails to start</h2>\n<p>Start with the cluster <strong>event log</strong>: the termination reason code tells you which layer failed.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Termination reason</th>\n<th>Likely cause</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>INIT_SCRIPT_FAILURE</code></td>\n<td>An init script exited with an error</td>\n<td>Read the init script logs in the cluster log delivery path (<code>&lt;log path&gt;/&lt;cluster id&gt;/init_scripts/</code>), fix the script</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloud provider errors (quota, capacity, no spot capacity, subnet out of IPs)</td>\n<td>The cloud could not supply the machines</td>\n<td>Another instance type or zone, on-demand instead of spot, a quota increase</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>LIBRARY_INSTALLATION_ERROR</code> on a library</td>\n<td>A package or one of its dependencies failed to install</td>\n<td>Pin versions, check the package index, use a wheel</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Policy validation error</td>\n<td>The configuration breaks the cluster policy</td>\n<td>Use a configuration the policy allows</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"product-names-old-and-new\">Product names: old and new</h2>\n<p>Training material and question banks written before 2026 use the old names. The exam uses the new ones; the concepts did not change.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Current name</th>\n<th>Old name</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines</td>\n<td>Delta Live Tables (DLT)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lakeflow Jobs</td>\n<td>Databricks Workflows, Jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Declarative Automation Bundles</td>\n<td>Databricks Asset Bundles (DABs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Git folders</td>\n<td>Repos</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OpenSharing</td>\n<td>Delta Sharing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AUTO CDC</code></td>\n<td><code>APPLY CHANGES INTO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard and Dedicated access mode</td>\n<td>Shared and Single user access mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pyspark.pipelines</code> (<code>dp</code>)</td>\n<td><code>dlt</code> Python module</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data quality monitoring (data profiling, anomaly detection)</td>\n<td>Lakehouse Monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">The control plane is never in your account</p>\n<p>Classic compute runs in your cloud account; serverless runs in the Databricks account. The control plane is in the Databricks account in both cases.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">All-purpose compute for a scheduled job</p>\n<p>Any answer that runs a production schedule on an all-purpose cluster is wrong on cost.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Serverless limits</p>\n<p>No R, no RDD API, no init scripts, no Spark UI, few Spark settings, and streaming only with <code>availableNow</code> or <code>once</code>. A question that mentions one of these needs classic compute.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">More workers do not help the driver</p>\n<p>Adding workers adds executor capacity. It does not give the driver more memory.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a Databricks Free Edition account. It is serverless only, which is enough for almost every exercise on this site.</li>\n<li>Create a notebook, attach it to serverless compute and run <code>SELECT current_catalog(), current_schema(), current_user()</code>.</li>\n<li>Open the Environment side panel and add <code>faker==30.8.2</code> as a dependency; import it in the next cell.</li>\n<li>Open <strong>SQL Warehouses</strong>, run a query on the starter warehouse, then open its <strong>query profile</strong>.</li>\n<li>In Catalog Explorer, browse the <code>samples</code> and <code>system</code> catalogs.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A1","A6","P1"]},{"id":"delta","title":"Delta Lake fundamentals","group":"Foundations","order":2,"summary":"The transaction log, ACID guarantees, writing with INSERT, overwrite and MERGE, schema enforcement and evolution, history, time travel, RESTORE, VACUUM and retention.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-delta-lake-works\">How Delta Lake works</h2>\n<p>A Delta table is a folder of <strong>Parquet data files</strong> plus a <strong>transaction log</strong> in <code>_delta_log/</code>. Every write adds a numbered commit file (<code>00000000000000000012.json</code>) that lists the data files added and removed; every few commits a Parquet checkpoint summarises the log. A reader replays the log to find the files of the latest version, so it never sees half-written data.</p>\n<p>What the log gives you:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>ACID transactions</strong>: a commit is all or nothing; readers see only committed versions (snapshot isolation).</li>\n<li><strong>Optimistic concurrency</strong>: writers do not lock the table. At commit time Delta checks whether a concurrent commit touched the same data; if so, one writer fails with a concurrent modification error and must retry. Deletion vectors and row-level concurrency make such conflicts rarer.</li>\n<li><strong>Schema enforcement</strong>, <strong>history</strong>, <strong>time travel</strong> and table-level properties.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In Unity Catalog, <code>CREATE TABLE</code> without a format creates a <strong>managed Delta table</strong>. Unity Catalog can also manage <strong>Apache Iceberg</strong> tables, and <strong>UniForm</strong> lets Iceberg clients read a Delta table; the commands on this page apply to Delta.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-data\">Writing data</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Goal</th>\n<th>SQL</th>\n<th>PySpark</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Add rows</td>\n<td><code>INSERT INTO t SELECT ...</code></td>\n<td><code>df.write.mode(\"append\").saveAsTable(\"t\")</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace all rows, same schema</td>\n<td><code>INSERT OVERWRITE t SELECT ...</code></td>\n<td><code>.mode(\"overwrite\")</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace only some rows</td>\n<td><code>INSERT INTO t REPLACE WHERE region = 'EU' SELECT ...</code></td>\n<td><code>.option(\"replaceWhere\", \"region = 'EU'\")</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace data <strong>and</strong> schema, keep history and grants</td>\n<td><code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE t AS SELECT ...</code></td>\n<td><code>.mode(\"overwrite\").option(\"overwriteSchema\", \"true\")</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Create a new table from a query</td>\n<td><code>CREATE TABLE t AS SELECT ...</code> (fails if <code>t</code> exists)</td>\n<td><code>.saveAsTable(\"t\")</code> (default mode fails if <code>t</code> exists)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upsert or apply changes</td>\n<td><code>MERGE INTO</code></td>\n<td><code>DeltaTable.forName(...).merge(...)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change or remove rows</td>\n<td><code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code></td>\n<td><code>DeltaTable.update/delete</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE</code> is not the same as <code>DROP</code> plus <code>CREATE</code>: it keeps the table's history, grants, row filters and column masks. Dropping and recreating loses all of them.</p>\n<h3 id=\"merge\">MERGE</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">MERGE INTO silver.customers AS t\nUSING updates AS s\n  ON t.customer_id = s.customer_id\nWHEN MATCHED AND s.op = 'DELETE' THEN DELETE\nWHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE AND t.active THEN UPDATE SET active = false;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>WHEN MATCHED</code> can <code>UPDATE</code> or <code>DELETE</code>; several clauses are evaluated in order and conditions make them selective.</li>\n<li><code>WHEN NOT MATCHED</code> (by target) can only <code>INSERT</code>: the row exists in the source and not in the target.</li>\n<li><code>WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE</code> acts on target rows that are missing from the source (<code>DELETE</code> or <code>UPDATE</code>), for example to remove rows that disappeared from a full extract.</li>\n<li>The source must have <strong>at most one row per key</strong>. Otherwise the merge fails with <code>DELTA_MULTIPLE_SOURCE_ROW_MATCHING_TARGET_ROW_IN_MERGE</code>; keep the latest row per key first (see the transformations topic).</li>\n<li>An <strong>insert-only merge</strong> (<code>WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT</code> only) is the standard way to add records without creating duplicates, whatever the time gap between duplicates.</li>\n<li><code>MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO ...</code> lets the merge add new source columns to the target.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"schema-enforcement-and-evolution\">Schema enforcement and evolution</h2>\n<p><strong>Enforcement.</strong> A write whose data does not match the table schema is rejected: a column with an incompatible type, or a column the table does not have. Columns missing from the data are written as NULL. This is deliberate: a source change must not corrupt a table silently.</p>\n<p><strong>Evolution</strong>, when the change is wanted:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>How</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Accept new columns on append or overwrite</td>\n<td><code>.option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\")</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Accept new columns in a merge</td>\n<td><code>MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace the whole schema (types change, columns removed)</td>\n<td>Overwrite with <code>overwriteSchema</code>, or <code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add a column explicitly</td>\n<td><code>ALTER TABLE t ADD COLUMNS (channel STRING)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Widen a type (for example INT to BIGINT)</td>\n<td>Type widening table feature, or a rewrite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rename or drop a column</td>\n<td>Column mapping: <code>ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.columnMapping.mode' = 'name')</code>, then <code>RENAME COLUMN</code> / <code>DROP COLUMN</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The session setting <code>spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled</code> is the legacy way to turn on evolution for every write in a session; prefer the per-write options.</p>\n<p>Renaming or dropping a column with column mapping changes only metadata. A dropped column's data stays in the files until <code>REORG TABLE t APPLY (PURGE)</code> rewrites them and <code>VACUUM</code> removes the old files. Both operations can break streams that read the table.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints</strong> apply to every writer, not just one pipeline:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">ALTER TABLE silver.orders ALTER COLUMN order_id SET NOT NULL;\nALTER TABLE silver.orders ADD CONSTRAINT positive_amount CHECK (amount &gt; 0);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Primary and foreign keys in Unity Catalog are <strong>informational</strong> (not enforced). Identity columns (<code>id BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY</code>) generate unique surrogate keys.</p>\n<h2 id=\"history-time-travel-and-restore\">History, time travel and RESTORE</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">DESCRIBE HISTORY sales.orders;                       -- version, timestamp, operation, user, metrics\nSELECT * FROM sales.orders VERSION AS OF 42;\nSELECT * FROM sales.orders TIMESTAMP AS OF '2026-11-20T08:00:00';\nRESTORE TABLE sales.orders TO VERSION AS OF 42;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Time travel reads an old snapshot. <code>table_changes()</code> is different: it returns row-level changes and needs Change Data Feed.</li>\n<li><code>RESTORE</code> writes a <strong>new commit</strong> that brings back the old state; the bad version stays in the history. It needs <code>MODIFY</code>.</li>\n<li>Two retention settings decide how far back you can go:</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>Default</th>\n<th>Controls</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>delta.logRetentionDuration</code></td>\n<td>30 days</td>\n<td>How long commits stay in the log (history)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration</code></td>\n<td>7 days</td>\n<td>How old a removed data file must be before <code>VACUUM</code> may delete it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Time travel needs both the log entry and the data files. After a <code>VACUUM</code>, versions older than the file retention can no longer be read or restored.</p>\n<h2 id=\"vacuum-and-other-utilities\">VACUUM and other utilities</h2>\n<p><code>VACUUM t</code> deletes data files that the current version no longer references and that are older than the retention threshold. It does not compact anything and never makes queries faster by itself; it saves storage and is how deleted data is physically removed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>VACUUM t RETAIN 720 HOURS</code> applies to that run only. To change retention for every future run, set <code>delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration</code> on the table.</li>\n<li>Retaining less than 7 days requires turning off a safety check and can break running readers and writers. Never use <code>RETAIN 0 HOURS</code> in production.</li>\n<li><code>VACUUM t DRY RUN</code> lists what would be deleted.</li>\n<li>Predictive optimization runs <code>VACUUM</code> automatically on managed tables.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Shows or does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DESCRIBE DETAIL t</code></td>\n<td>Location, number and size of files, clustering columns, properties</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED t</code></td>\n<td>Columns plus table type (managed or external), owner, provider</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SHOW TBLPROPERTIES t</code></td>\n<td>Table properties</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CREATE TABLE dev.t SHALLOW CLONE prod.t</code></td>\n<td>New table that references the source files; cheap copy for tests</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CREATE TABLE bkp.t DEEP CLONE prod.t</code></td>\n<td>Full, incremental copy of data and metadata for backup or migration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CONVERT TO DELTA parquet.`/path/to/folder`</code></td>\n<td>Turns a Parquet folder into a Delta table in place</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Useful table properties: <code>delta.appendOnly = true</code> (blocks <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> and row-changing merges; good for audit tables), <code>delta.enableChangeDataFeed</code>, <code>delta.enableDeletionVectors</code>, <code>delta.enableRowTracking</code>, <code>delta.columnMapping.mode</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-a-delta-table-as-a-stream\">Reading a Delta table as a stream</h2>\n<p>A Delta table can be a streaming source. The stream expects <strong>appends</strong>: a commit that updates or deletes existing rows stops the stream with an error. Options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>skipChangeCommits = true</code>: ignore commits that change or delete rows and process appends only (you lose those changes on purpose).</li>\n<li><code>ignoreDeletes</code> and <code>ignoreChanges</code> are older options; <code>ignoreChanges</code> re-emits rewritten rows, which creates duplicates downstream.</li>\n<li>To propagate updates and deletes, read the <strong>Change Data Feed</strong> instead (see the CDC topic).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Overwrite keeps the schema</p>\n<p><code>INSERT OVERWRITE</code> and <code>mode(\"overwrite\")</code> replace the rows but keep the schema, so a new or changed column makes them fail. To change the schema too, use <code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE</code> or <code>overwriteSchema</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">VACUUM is not OPTIMIZE</p>\n<p><code>OPTIMIZE</code> compacts small files and improves reads. <code>VACUUM</code> deletes unreferenced old files and limits time travel.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">mergeSchema vs overwriteSchema</p>\n<p><code>mergeSchema</code> adds columns (append or overwrite). <code>overwriteSchema</code> replaces the schema and works only with overwrite.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">One row per key before a MERGE</p>\n<p>If the source can contain several versions of a key, deduplicate it first, keeping the latest by timestamp.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS workspace.delta_lab;\nUSE workspace.delta_lab;\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE customers (id INT, name STRING, city STRING);\nINSERT INTO customers VALUES (1, 'Ana', 'Lisbon'), (2, 'Rui', 'Porto');\nCREATE OR REPLACE TEMP VIEW updates AS\n  SELECT * FROM VALUES (2, 'Rui', 'Braga'), (3, 'Eva', 'Faro') AS u(id, name, city);\nMERGE INTO customers t USING updates s ON t.id = s.id\n  WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET * WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT *;\nDELETE FROM customers WHERE id = 1;\nDESCRIBE HISTORY customers;\nSELECT * FROM customers VERSION AS OF 1;\nRESTORE TABLE customers TO VERSION AS OF 1;\nDESCRIBE HISTORY customers;            -- RESTORE is a new version\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df = spark.createDataFrame([(4, &quot;Teo&quot;, &quot;Lisbon&quot;, &quot;web&quot;)],\n                           &quot;id INT, name STRING, city STRING, channel STRING&quot;)\ndf.write.mode(&quot;append&quot;).saveAsTable(&quot;customers&quot;)                               # fails: schema enforcement\ndf.write.mode(&quot;append&quot;).option(&quot;mergeSchema&quot;, &quot;true&quot;).saveAsTable(&quot;customers&quot;) # adds the column\n</code></pre>\n<p>Compare <code>DESCRIBE DETAIL customers</code> and <code>DESCRIBE HISTORY customers</code> before and after.</p>","sections":["A1","A3","P2","P6"]},{"id":"layout","title":"Table layout and maintenance","group":"Foundations","order":3,"summary":"Data skipping, small files, partitioning versus Z-order versus liquid clustering, CLUSTER BY AUTO, OPTIMIZE, deletion vectors and predictive optimization.","html":"<h2 id=\"why-layout-matters\">Why layout matters</h2>\n<p>Delta stores <strong>statistics</strong> for each data file: minimum, maximum and null count of the first 32 columns. A query with a filter skips every file whose range cannot contain a match. That <strong>data skipping</strong> only works when similar values sit in the same files. If <code>event_date</code> values are spread across all files, every file's min/max range covers the filter and nothing is skipped, however good the statistics are.</p>\n<p>File size matters too. Thousands of tiny files mean high overhead to list and open them; a few huge files limit parallelism and skipping. Databricks tunes file sizes automatically; your job is to avoid creating the problem (for example by partitioning on a high-cardinality column) and to compact regularly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"partitioning-z-order-and-liquid-clustering\">Partitioning, Z-order and liquid clustering</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Hive-style partitioning</th>\n<th>Z-order</th>\n<th>Liquid clustering</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Syntax</td>\n<td><code>PARTITIONED BY (col)</code></td>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (a, b)</code></td>\n<td><code>CLUSTER BY (a, b)</code> or <code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How it works</td>\n<td>One folder per value</td>\n<td>Co-locates values inside files when <code>OPTIMIZE</code> runs</td>\n<td>Clusters data incrementally when <code>OPTIMIZE</code> runs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good for</td>\n<td>Very large tables, low-cardinality column, each partition at least about 1 GB</td>\n<td>Legacy tables</td>\n<td>New tables of any size, high-cardinality keys, changing filters, merge keys, concurrent writers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Change the columns</td>\n<td>Recreate the table</td>\n<td>Rerun Z-order on everything</td>\n<td><code>ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (...)</code>; no immediate rewrite</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skewed data</td>\n<td>Uneven partitions</td>\n<td>Full rewrite each time</td>\n<td>Handled well</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Combine with others</td>\n<td>Not with liquid clustering</td>\n<td>Not with liquid clustering</td>\n<td>Replaces both; up to 4 keys</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Recommendations:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <strong>liquid clustering</strong> for new tables. Choose the columns most used in filters and join or merge conditions.</li>\n<li>Use <strong><code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code></strong> when you do not know the filter columns or they change: Databricks picks and adjusts keys from the query history. It needs a Unity Catalog <strong>managed</strong> table with <strong>predictive optimization</strong>.</li>\n<li>Never partition on a high-cardinality column such as <code>customer_id</code> or <code>order_id</code>: you get millions of tiny partitions and files.</li>\n<li>Do not partition tables smaller than about 1 TB.</li>\n<li>An unpartitioned table can switch to liquid clustering with <code>ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY</code>. A partitioned table must be recreated (for example <code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ... CLUSTER BY (...) AS SELECT ...</code>).</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE TABLE sales.events (event_id BIGINT, customer_id BIGINT, event_ts TIMESTAMP, country STRING)\nCLUSTER BY (customer_id, event_ts);\n\nALTER TABLE sales.events CLUSTER BY (country, event_ts);  -- new keys; old data is not rewritten yet\nOPTIMIZE sales.events FULL;                              -- recluster all existing data with the new keys\nALTER TABLE sales.events CLUSTER BY AUTO;                -- let Databricks choose\nALTER TABLE sales.events CLUSTER BY NONE;                -- stop clustering\n</code></pre>\n<p>Clustering happens when <code>OPTIMIZE</code> runs, not on every small write, so recently inserted rows stay unclustered until the next <code>OPTIMIZE</code> (manual or by predictive optimization).</p>\n<p><strong>Iceberg tables</strong> follow the same principles: partition only on low-cardinality columns that match the data grain (for example a daily partition transform for data loaded and queried by day), cluster on the columns queries filter on, and compact small files regularly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"optimize-and-automatic-file-management\">OPTIMIZE and automatic file management</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command or setting</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE t</code></td>\n<td>Compacts small files into larger ones; on clustered tables also clusters the data that is not yet clustered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE t FULL</code></td>\n<td>Reclusters all data, for example after changing clustering keys (can take hours on big tables)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE t WHERE date &gt;= '2026-11-01'</code></td>\n<td>Limits the work to some partitions (partitioned tables only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (a)</code></td>\n<td>Legacy Z-ordering; not allowed on clustered tables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Optimized writes (<code>delta.autoOptimize.optimizeWrite</code>)</td>\n<td>Writes fewer, larger files by shuffling data before the write</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto compaction (<code>delta.autoOptimize.autoCompact</code>)</td>\n<td>Compacts small files right after a write</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ANALYZE TABLE t COMPUTE STATISTICS FOR ALL COLUMNS</code></td>\n<td>Column statistics for the query optimizer (join planning), not for file skipping</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>OPTIMIZE</code> makes reads faster. <code>VACUUM</code> deletes old unreferenced files and frees storage; it does not compact anything.</p>\n<h2 id=\"deletion-vectors\">Deletion vectors</h2>\n<p>Without deletion vectors, deleting or updating one row rewrites the whole Parquet file that contains it. With <strong>deletion vectors</strong>, <code>DELETE</code>, <code>UPDATE</code> and <code>MERGE</code> write a small file that marks the affected rows as removed, and readers skip those rows (merge-on-read). Writes become much cheaper; reads pay a small cost until the files are rewritten by <code>OPTIMIZE</code>, by <code>REORG TABLE t APPLY (PURGE)</code> or automatically.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">ALTER TABLE accounts SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.enableDeletionVectors' = true);\n</code></pre>\n<p>Deletion vectors are on by default for new tables on recent runtimes. They matter for privacy: a deleted row physically stays in the old file until it is purged and the old files are vacuumed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"predictive-optimization\">Predictive optimization</h2>\n<p>Predictive optimization runs <strong><code>OPTIMIZE</code>, <code>VACUUM</code> and <code>ANALYZE</code></strong> automatically on <strong>Unity Catalog managed tables</strong>, on serverless compute, when it estimates the work pays off. You stop scheduling maintenance jobs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It never runs Z-order.</li>\n<li>It does <strong>not</strong> run on external tables. Convert them with <code>ALTER TABLE t SET MANAGED</code>, or schedule <code>OPTIMIZE</code> and <code>VACUUM</code> yourself.</li>\n<li>It is enabled or disabled per account, catalog or schema, and objects inherit the setting:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">ALTER CATALOG sales ENABLE PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;\nALTER SCHEMA sales.staging DISABLE PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;\nALTER SCHEMA sales.gold INHERIT PREDICTIVE OPTIMIZATION;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Its <code>VACUUM</code> respects <code>delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration</code>.</li>\n<li>What it did is recorded in <code>system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history</code>.</li>\n<li>It is required for <code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Why managed tables cost less to run</strong>: predictive optimization, automatic clustering, automatic file sizing, metadata caching and <code>UNDROP</code> all come with Unity Catalog managed tables. Combined with liquid clustering, there is almost no table maintenance left to schedule.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-layout\">Choosing a layout</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Choose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>New table, known filter columns</td>\n<td><code>CLUSTER BY (those columns)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filters unknown or changing every quarter</td>\n<td><code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily <code>MERGE</code> on a key with many scattered updates</td>\n<td>Cluster by the merge key and enable deletion vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequent single-row <code>UPDATE</code>s rewrite whole files</td>\n<td>Deletion vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thousands of small appends make reads slow</td>\n<td>Optimized writes and auto compaction, plus <code>OPTIMIZE</code> or predictive optimization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changed clustering keys, old data still badly laid out</td>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE t FULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predictive optimization enabled but one table never maintained</td>\n<td>It is external: convert it or schedule maintenance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Changing keys does not rewrite data</p>\n<p><code>ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY</code> only affects how future <code>OPTIMIZE</code> runs cluster data. Run <code>OPTIMIZE t FULL</code> to recluster what already exists.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Predictive optimization and external tables</p>\n<p>Predictive optimization works only on Unity Catalog managed tables, and never runs Z-order.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Liquid clustering does not mix</p>\n<p>A clustered table cannot also be partitioned or Z-ordered.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">High cardinality means clustering</p>\n<p>A filter on a column with millions of distinct values is a clustering key, never a partition column.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">USE workspace.delta_lab;\nCREATE OR REPLACE TABLE events CLUSTER BY (country) AS\nSELECT id AS event_id, id % 1000 AS customer_id,\n       element_at(array('PT','ES','FR','DE'), CAST(id % 4 + 1 AS INT)) AS country,\n       timestampadd(MINUTE, CAST(id AS INT), TIMESTAMP '2026-01-01') AS event_ts\nFROM range(1000000);\nDESCRIBE DETAIL events;                 -- numFiles, clusteringColumns\nOPTIMIZE events;\nALTER TABLE events CLUSTER BY (customer_id);\nOPTIMIZE events FULL;\nDESCRIBE HISTORY events;                -- see the OPTIMIZE operations and their metrics\nSELECT * FROM system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history LIMIT 20;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Run a query that filters on <code>customer_id</code> before and after <code>OPTIMIZE FULL</code> and compare <em>files read</em> in the query profile.</p>","sections":["A6","P5","P9"]},{"id":"ingest-files","title":"Ingesting files: COPY INTO and Auto Loader","group":"Ingestion","order":4,"summary":"Uploads and volumes, read_files, COPY INTO, Auto Loader with checkpoints, schema inference and evolution, file detection modes, triggers, and how to choose between them.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-options-at-a-glance\">The options at a glance</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Incremental</th>\n<th>Scale</th>\n<th>Interface</th>\n<th>Use it when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Upload page (<em>Create or modify table</em>)</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Up to 10 files, under 2 GB in total; CSV, TSV, JSON, Avro, Parquet or text; no archives</td>\n<td>UI</td>\n<td>One small file from your laptop</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upload to a volume, then create the table</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Larger files (the UI takes up to 5 GB per file; CLI and API more)</td>\n<td>UI, CLI, API</td>\n<td>A one-off file that is too big for the upload page, or that you want to keep for reloads</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>read_files</code> / <code>spark.read</code></td>\n<td>No: reads everything every time</td>\n<td>Any</td>\n<td>SQL, Python</td>\n<td>Exploring files, one-time loads (<code>CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>COPY INTO</code></td>\n<td>Yes, idempotent</td>\n<td>Thousands of files per run</td>\n<td>SQL</td>\n<td>Scheduled SQL loads of files into an existing table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto Loader (<code>cloudFiles</code>)</td>\n<td>Yes, exactly once through a checkpoint</td>\n<td>Millions of files</td>\n<td>Python, or <code>STREAM read_files</code> in SQL</td>\n<td>Continuous or frequent arrivals, schema that changes, large volumes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>All of them read from a <strong>Unity Catalog volume</strong> (<code>/Volumes/catalog/schema/volume/...</code>, needs <code>READ VOLUME</code>) or from an <strong>external location</strong> (cloud URI, needs <code>READ FILES</code>). Volumes are the recommended landing zone: access control and lineage come with them.</p>\n<p>Every file source exposes a hidden <strong><code>_metadata</code></strong> column (<code>file_path</code>, <code>file_name</code>, <code>file_size</code>, <code>file_modification_time</code>). Select it explicitly to record where each row came from; <code>input_file_name()</code> is the old way and is not supported everywhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"read_files\">read_files</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT *, _metadata.file_path AS source_file\nFROM read_files('/Volumes/main/raw/landing/orders/',\n                format =&gt; 'csv', header =&gt; true,\n                schemaHints =&gt; 'amount DECIMAL(10,2)');\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>read_files</code> infers column types by default. It is batch: run it twice and you read every file twice. Inside a declarative pipeline, <code>STREAM read_files(...)</code> makes it incremental (Auto Loader underneath).</p>\n<h2 id=\"copy-into\">COPY INTO</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS main.bronze.orders;   -- schemaless placeholder, filled by the first load\n\nCOPY INTO main.bronze.orders\nFROM (SELECT *, _metadata.file_path AS source_file\n      FROM '/Volumes/main/raw/landing/orders/')\nFILEFORMAT = CSV\nPATTERN = 'orders_*.csv'\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true', 'inferSchema' = 'true', 'mergeSchema' = 'true')\nCOPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true');\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Idempotent.</strong> The table remembers which files it loaded and skips them on the next run, <strong>even if they were modified</strong>. Rerunning the command or retrying the job does not duplicate data.</li>\n<li><strong>Reloading a corrected file with the same name</strong>: <code>force</code> reloads but does not replace anything, so delete the old rows first, then load just that file:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">DELETE FROM main.bronze.orders WHERE source_file LIKE '%orders_2026-11-20.csv';\nCOPY INTO main.bronze.orders FROM '/Volumes/main/raw/landing/orders/'\nFILEFORMAT = CSV FILES = ('orders_2026-11-20.csv')\nFORMAT_OPTIONS ('header' = 'true') COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true');\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>FILES</code> (an explicit list, up to 1,000 names) and <code>PATTERN</code> (a glob) cannot be used together.</li>\n<li><code>FORMAT_OPTIONS ('inferSchema', 'mergeSchema')</code> shape the schema <strong>read from the files</strong>. <code>COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')</code> lets the <strong>target table</strong> gain new columns. A schemaless placeholder table only accepts <code>COPY INTO</code> with <code>mergeSchema</code>.</li>\n<li><code>FROM (SELECT ...)</code> casts, renames or adds columns during the load.</li>\n<li><code>VALIDATE ALL</code> (or <code>VALIDATE 10 ROWS</code>) checks the files and reports problems without writing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"auto-loader\">Auto Loader</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">(spark.readStream.format(&quot;cloudFiles&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;cloudFiles.format&quot;, &quot;json&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;cloudFiles.schemaLocation&quot;, &quot;/Volumes/main/raw/_meta/orders/schema&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode&quot;, &quot;addNewColumns&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;cloudFiles.schemaHints&quot;, &quot;amount DECIMAL(10,2), order_ts TIMESTAMP&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;pathGlobFilter&quot;, &quot;*.json&quot;)\n    .load(&quot;/Volumes/main/raw/landing/orders/&quot;)          # the base directory, not a glob\n    .select(&quot;*&quot;, &quot;_metadata.file_path&quot;)\n  .writeStream\n    .option(&quot;checkpointLocation&quot;, &quot;/Volumes/main/raw/_meta/orders/checkpoint&quot;)\n    .trigger(availableNow=True)                         # process what is new, then stop\n    .toTable(&quot;main.bronze.orders&quot;))\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Checkpoint.</strong> The checkpoint records which files were processed, which gives exactly-once ingestion across restarts. Use one checkpoint per stream, keep it outside the source folder and the table, and never share it. Deleting it, or pointing a stream at a different source with the old one, makes files be read again.</p>\n<p><strong>Schema location.</strong> <code>cloudFiles.schemaLocation</code> stores the inferred schema and every version of it. It is required when Auto Loader infers the schema.</p>\n<p><strong>Inference.</strong> Auto Loader samples the first 50 GB or 1,000 files. For <strong>JSON, CSV and XML every column is inferred as STRING</strong> unless <code>cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes = true</code>; this avoids type conflicts between files. Parquet and Avro keep their types. <code>schemaHints</code> force the type of chosen columns.</p>\n<h3 id=\"schema-evolution-modes\">Schema evolution modes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode</code></th>\n<th>New column appears</th>\n<th>When it is the default</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>addNewColumns</code></td>\n<td>The stream <strong>fails</strong> with <code>UnknownFieldException</code> and updates the stored schema; after a restart the column is included</td>\n<td>No schema provided</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rescue</code></td>\n<td>Schema never changes; unexpected data goes to <code>_rescued_data</code>; the stream keeps running</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>failOnNewColumns</code></td>\n<td>The stream fails and does <strong>not</strong> evolve until someone updates the schema or removes the file</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>none</code></td>\n<td>Ignored; nothing is rescued unless <code>rescuedDataColumn</code> is set</td>\n<td>A schema is provided</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Run <code>addNewColumns</code> streams in a job with retries so the restart happens automatically. Existing column types never change by evolution; a type mismatch lands in <code>_rescued_data</code> instead.</p>\n<p><strong><code>_rescued_data</code></strong> holds, as JSON, whatever did not fit: unknown columns (in <code>rescue</code> mode), values of the wrong type, and column names that differ only in case, plus the source file path. A <strong>malformed</strong> record (broken JSON) is different: it fails the read in <code>FAILFAST</code> mode and is dropped in <code>DROPMALFORMED</code> mode; with <code>badRecordsPath</code> the bad records are written to that folder for later inspection.</p>\n<h3 id=\"finding-new-files\">Finding new files</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Choose it when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Directory listing (default)</td>\n<td>Lists the input directory on each run</td>\n<td>Moderate numbers of files</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>File notification</td>\n<td>Cloud storage events tell Auto Loader which files arrived</td>\n<td>Millions of files, or listing has become slow</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The recommended form of file notification is <strong>managed file events</strong>: enable file events on the external location and set <code>cloudFiles.useManagedFileEvents = true</code>. Switching from listing to file events keeps the same checkpoint and exactly-once processing. The older <code>cloudFiles.useNotifications = true</code> creates queues and subscriptions in your cloud account and needs extra cloud permissions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"other-options-worth-knowing\">Other options worth knowing</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger</code></td>\n<td>Upper bound on files per micro-batch (default 1,000)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cloudFiles.maxBytesPerTrigger</code></td>\n<td>Soft upper bound on bytes per micro-batch, for files of very different sizes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cloudFiles.includeExistingFiles</code></td>\n<td>Whether the first run also processes files already in the folder (default true)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cloudFiles.allowOverwrites</code></td>\n<td>Reprocess a file whose content was rewritten in place (default false: each path once); deduplicate downstream</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pathGlobFilter</code></td>\n<td>Only consider files whose names match a pattern</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"triggers\">Triggers</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trigger</th>\n<th>Behaviour</th>\n<th>Use for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>availableNow=True</code></td>\n<td>Processes everything new in rate-limited batches, then stops</td>\n<td>Scheduled incremental runs (the \"batch mode\" of Auto Loader); serverless jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>processingTime=\"1 minute\"</code></td>\n<td>Runs a micro-batch every minute, forever</td>\n<td>Low-latency continuous ingestion on classic compute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No trigger</td>\n<td>Micro-batch after micro-batch with no pause</td>\n<td>Rarely what you want: constant listing cost; fails on serverless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>once=True</code></td>\n<td>One batch, then stop</td>\n<td>Deprecated; use <code>availableNow</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-a-method\">Choosing a method</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Method</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Millions of JSON files arriving all day, new fields appear over time</td>\n<td>Auto Loader with schema evolution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A few thousand CSV files a day, SQL team, idempotent scheduled load</td>\n<td><code>COPY INTO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One 3 GB CSV on a laptop</td>\n<td>Upload it to a volume, then <code>read_files</code>, <code>COPY INTO</code> or Catalog Explorer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Check what a folder contains once before building anything</td>\n<td><code>spark.read</code> or <code>read_files</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Incremental file ingestion inside a declarative pipeline</td>\n<td>Streaming table with <code>STREAM read_files</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unexpected columns must stop ingestion until someone approves</td>\n<td>Auto Loader <code>failOnNewColumns</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ingestion must never stop and nothing may be lost</td>\n<td>Auto Loader <code>rescue</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files from a database or SaaS application, not a folder</td>\n<td>See <em>Connectors, databases and message buses</em></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">COPY INTO skips modified files</p>\n<p>A corrected file with the same name is not loaded again unless you use <code>force</code>, and <code>force</code> does not remove the old rows.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">rescue versus addNewColumns</p>\n<p><code>rescue</code> never changes the schema. <code>addNewColumns</code> fails once, then includes the column after a restart.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Strings by default</p>\n<p>Auto Loader infers JSON and CSV columns as strings; <code>read_files</code> infers types. Same files, different column types.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Checkpoint equals memory</p>\n<p>Every \"files are processed again\" scenario comes back to a checkpoint that was deleted, shared or reused for another source.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a volume: <code>CREATE VOLUME IF NOT EXISTS workspace.default.landing;</code> and upload two small CSV files into an <code>orders/</code> folder.</li>\n<li>Query them with <code>read_files</code>, then load them with <code>COPY INTO</code>. Run the <code>COPY INTO</code> a second time and check the row count and the number of inserted rows reported.</li>\n<li>Write the Auto Loader stream above with <code>availableNow</code>, run it, add a third file with an extra column, run it again and watch the <code>UnknownFieldException</code>; run once more and see the new column.</li>\n<li>Change the stream to <code>rescue</code>, add a file with yet another column, and query <code>_rescued_data</code>.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A2","P1","P2"]},{"id":"ingest-sources","title":"Connectors, databases and message buses","group":"Ingestion","order":5,"summary":"Lakeflow Connect managed and standard connectors, database CDC with an ingestion gateway, Kafka and other message buses, JDBC, ODBC and REST from notebooks, and how to pick an ingestion method.","html":"<h2 id=\"lakeflow-connect\">Lakeflow Connect</h2>\n<p>Lakeflow Connect is the ingestion part of Lakeflow. It offers two families of connectors:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Managed connectors</th>\n<th>Standard connectors</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Sources</td>\n<td>SaaS applications (Salesforce, Workday, ServiceNow, Google Analytics, SharePoint and others) and databases (SQL Server, MySQL, PostgreSQL and others)</td>\n<td>Cloud object storage, SFTP, message buses (Kafka, Kinesis, Event Hubs, Pub/Sub, Pulsar)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What you build</td>\n<td>Nothing but configuration: Databricks runs a managed, incremental ingestion pipeline</td>\n<td>Your own pipeline or stream (Auto Loader, <code>read_kafka</code>, Structured Streaming)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Output</td>\n<td>Streaming tables in Unity Catalog</td>\n<td>Whatever your code writes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Control</td>\n<td>Low effort, less customisation</td>\n<td>More control over parsing and transformation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3 id=\"managed-connectors-in-practice\">Managed connectors in practice</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>You create the ingestion from the UI (<em>Data ingestion</em>), the API, or a bundle. Behind it is a <strong>serverless declarative pipeline</strong> that writes <strong>streaming tables</strong>; scheduling it creates a Lakeflow job.</li>\n<li>Credentials live in a Unity Catalog <strong>connection</strong> (<code>CREATE CONNECTION</code>). Someone with <code>USE CONNECTION</code> can build ingestion pipelines without ever seeing the secret.</li>\n<li>Ingestion is <strong>incremental</strong>: after the first full snapshot only changes are read.</li>\n<li>History tracking is off by default (<strong>SCD type 1</strong>: rows are updated in place). Connectors that support it can keep <strong>SCD type 2</strong> history.</li>\n<li>A <strong>full refresh</strong> reloads everything and discards the SCD type 2 history.</li>\n<li><strong>Deleting the ingestion pipeline deletes the tables it manages.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"database-connectors-and-the-ingestion-gateway\">Database connectors and the ingestion gateway</h3>\n<p>Database connectors (SQL Server, MySQL, PostgreSQL) use two parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>An <strong>ingestion gateway</strong> that runs continuously on classic compute close to the database, reads the initial snapshot and then the change log (SQL Server change tracking or CDC, MySQL binlog, PostgreSQL logical replication), and stages the data in a Unity Catalog volume.</li>\n<li>An <strong>ingestion pipeline</strong> (serverless) that applies the staged snapshots and changes to the streaming tables on its schedule.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The source must have change capture enabled, and the gateway must keep running: if it stops for too long, the database may purge change log entries that were never read, forcing a full refresh.</p>\n<h2 id=\"message-buses\">Message buses</h2>\n<p>Kafka, Kinesis, Event Hubs (through its Kafka endpoint) and Pub/Sub are read with Structured Streaming or with streaming tables.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">raw = (spark.readStream.format(&quot;kafka&quot;)\n         .option(&quot;kafka.bootstrap.servers&quot;, &quot;broker1:9092&quot;)\n         .option(&quot;subscribe&quot;, &quot;orders&quot;)\n         .option(&quot;startingOffsets&quot;, &quot;earliest&quot;)\n         .load())                                  # key, value (binary), topic, partition, offset, timestamp\norders = raw.select(F.col(&quot;value&quot;).cast(&quot;string&quot;).alias(&quot;json&quot;), &quot;timestamp&quot;)\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE bronze_orders AS\nSELECT CAST(value AS STRING) AS json, timestamp\nFROM STREAM read_kafka(bootstrapServers =&gt; 'broker1:9092', subscribe =&gt; 'orders');\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>value</code> arrives as binary: cast it, then parse it (<code>from_json</code> or <code>parse_json</code> into VARIANT).</li>\n<li>Kafka keeps messages for a limited time. A <strong>full refresh</strong> of a streaming table truncates it and rereads only what Kafka still holds, so older data is lost. Guard bronze tables from full refreshes in that case (pipeline table property <code>pipelines.reset.allowed = false</code>).</li>\n<li>Applications that need to <strong>push</strong> records straight into Delta tables, without running a broker, can use <strong>Zerobus Ingest</strong>, the push-based API of Lakeflow Connect.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"jdbc-odbc-and-rest-from-notebooks\">JDBC, ODBC and REST from notebooks</h2>\n<p>When no managed connector exists, write the ingestion yourself in a notebook or Python task and schedule it with Lakeflow Jobs.</p>\n<h3 id=\"jdbc\">JDBC</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">jdbc_url = &quot;jdbc:postgresql://ops-db:5432/shop&quot;\norders = (spark.read.format(&quot;jdbc&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;url&quot;, jdbc_url)\n    .option(&quot;dbtable&quot;, &quot;(SELECT * FROM orders WHERE updated_at &gt; '2026-11-20') AS src&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;user&quot;, dbutils.secrets.get(&quot;ops&quot;, &quot;pg_user&quot;))\n    .option(&quot;password&quot;, dbutils.secrets.get(&quot;ops&quot;, &quot;pg_password&quot;))\n    .option(&quot;partitionColumn&quot;, &quot;order_id&quot;)\n    .option(&quot;lowerBound&quot;, 1).option(&quot;upperBound&quot;, 5_000_000)\n    .option(&quot;numPartitions&quot;, 8)\n    .option(&quot;fetchsize&quot;, 10_000)\n    .load())\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Credentials come from a <strong>secret scope</strong> or a Unity Catalog connection, never from code.</li>\n<li><code>partitionColumn</code>, <code>lowerBound</code>, <code>upperBound</code> and <code>numPartitions</code> split the read into parallel queries. The bounds only set the <strong>stride</strong> of the partitions; they do <strong>not filter</strong> rows. Filter with a <code>WHERE</code> inside the <code>dbtable</code> subquery.</li>\n<li>The <code>query</code> option cannot be combined with <code>partitionColumn</code>; use a <code>dbtable</code> subquery instead.</li>\n<li>For incremental loads, keep a <strong>high-water mark</strong> (for example the last <code>updated_at</code> loaded) in a control table, read only newer rows, and <code>MERGE</code> them into the target.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"odbc\">ODBC</h3>\n<p>ODBC drivers must be installed on the machines, usually with an init script. Serverless does not run init scripts, so ODBC ingestion needs classic compute.</p>\n<h3 id=\"rest-apis\">REST APIs</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep tokens in a secret scope.</li>\n<li>Handle <strong>pagination</strong> and <strong>rate limits</strong> (retry with exponential backoff on HTTP 429).</li>\n<li>Write raw responses as files to a <strong>volume</strong> (not to local <code>/tmp</code> on the driver, which disappears), then load them with Auto Loader or <code>COPY INTO</code>. Raw files give you replay and lineage.</li>\n<li>Schedule with Lakeflow Jobs and use job retries for transient errors.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"partner-tools\">Partner tools</h2>\n<p><strong>Partner Connect</strong> sets up tools such as Fivetran with a few clicks. The partner writes through its own service principal, which <strong>owns</strong> the tables it creates, so your users need explicit grants before they can read them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-an-ingestion-method\">Choosing an ingestion method</h2>\n<p>Databricks' order of preference: a <strong>managed connector</strong> if one exists, then a <strong>declarative pipeline</strong> with standard connectors, then <strong>Structured Streaming</strong> code, then fully custom code.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Scenario</th>\n<th>Method</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Salesforce, Workday, ServiceNow; no API code to maintain</td>\n<td>Managed connector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous replication of SQL Server, MySQL or PostgreSQL with CDC</td>\n<td>Database managed connector (gateway + pipeline)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A database with no connector, nightly incremental extract</td>\n<td>JDBC notebook in a scheduled job, with a high-water mark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Occasional queries on an operational database without copying</td>\n<td>Lakehouse Federation (see <em>Sharing, Clean Rooms and Federation</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Events in Kafka or Kinesis with low latency</td>\n<td>Structured Streaming or a streaming table with <code>read_kafka</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files in cloud storage</td>\n<td>Auto Loader or <code>COPY INTO</code> (see <em>Ingesting files</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Data held by another Databricks customer</td>\n<td>OpenSharing instead of a copy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy, repeated queries that are slow over federation</td>\n<td>Ingest the data with Lakeflow Connect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">JDBC bounds do not filter</p>\n<p><code>lowerBound</code> and <code>upperBound</code> decide how the table is split across partitions. Every row is still read.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Without copying</p>\n<p>\"Query without copying the data\" points to Lakehouse Federation (databases) or OpenSharing (another organisation), never to an ingestion tool.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Deleting a managed ingestion pipeline</p>\n<p>The streaming tables it created are deleted with it.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Do not stop the gateway</p>\n<p>A database connector's gateway runs continuously; stopping it risks losing unread change log entries.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<p>Free Edition has no external database, but you can practise the patterns:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Store a fake token in a secret scope with the CLI (<code>databricks secrets create-scope lab</code>, <code>databricks secrets put-secret lab token</code>) and read it with <code>dbutils.secrets.get</code>.</li>\n<li>Call a public REST API with <code>requests</code> (for example a paginated open data API), write each page as a JSON file to a volume, then load the folder with Auto Loader.</li>\n<li>Read <code>samples.tpch.orders</code> with <code>spark.read.table</code>, write it to a new table, and implement a high-water-mark incremental load with a control table and <code>MERGE</code>.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A2","P2"]},{"id":"semi-structured","title":"Semi-structured and unstructured data","group":"Ingestion","order":6,"summary":"JSON as strings, structs or VARIANT, path syntax, parse_json and variant_get, exploding arrays, higher-order functions, and ingesting nested and binary files.","html":"<h2 id=\"three-ways-to-store-json\">Three ways to store JSON</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>JSON in a STRING column</th>\n<th>STRUCT, ARRAY, MAP columns</th>\n<th>VARIANT column</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Created with</td>\n<td>Raw text</td>\n<td><code>from_json(col, schema)</code> or schema inference</td>\n<td><code>parse_json(col)</code>, or ingestion with a VARIANT schema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flexibility</td>\n<td>Any shape</td>\n<td>Fixed schema; changes need schema evolution</td>\n<td>Any shape</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Query speed</td>\n<td>Parsed again on every query</td>\n<td>Fast</td>\n<td>Fast (binary encoding with statistics)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Path syntax</td>\n<td><code>raw:customer.city</code></td>\n<td><code>customer.city</code></td>\n<td><code>v:customer.city</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Best for</td>\n<td>Landing raw text you never query</td>\n<td>Stable, well-known structures</td>\n<td>Semi-structured data whose structure changes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Databricks recommends <strong>VARIANT</strong> for semi-structured data that changes over time: it keeps the flexibility of a string with performance close to a struct.</p>\n<h2 id=\"querying-json-strings-and-variant\">Querying JSON strings and VARIANT</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- raw is a STRING column holding JSON\nSELECT raw:customer.address.city      AS city,       -- returns a STRING\n       raw:items[0].sku                AS first_sku,\n       raw:items[*].sku                AS all_skus,   -- wildcard: JSON strings only\n       raw:price::DECIMAL(10,2)        AS price\nFROM bronze.events_json;\n\n-- v is a VARIANT column\nSELECT v:customer.address.city::STRING                    AS city,\n       variant_get(v, '$.items[0].sku', 'string')         AS first_sku,\n       try_variant_get(v, '$.price', 'decimal(10,2)')     AS price,\n       schema_of_variant(v)                               AS shape\nFROM bronze.events_variant;\n\n-- one row per array element of a VARIANT\nSELECT e.event_id, i.pos, i.value:sku::STRING AS sku\nFROM bronze.events_variant AS e,\n     LATERAL variant_explode(e.v:items) AS i;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Differences that cause wrong answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <code>[*]</code> wildcard works on <strong>JSON strings</strong> only. On VARIANT, use <code>variant_explode</code>.</li>\n<li>Field names in <strong>VARIANT</strong> paths are <strong>case sensitive</strong>. In JSON-string paths they are not (unless you use brackets, such as <code>raw:['Owner']</code>).</li>\n<li>A path on a VARIANT returns a VARIANT. Cast it (<code>::STRING</code>, <code>::INT</code>) or use <code>variant_get</code> with a type before comparing or displaying it.</li>\n<li>VARIANT values cannot be used directly in <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, <code>DISTINCT</code> or as clustering keys: cast them first.</li>\n<li><code>try_parse_json</code> and <code>try_variant_get</code> return NULL instead of failing on bad input.</li>\n<li>Dot notation (<code>customer.address.city</code>) is for <strong>STRUCT</strong> columns. <code>raw.customer.city</code> on a string column fails.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Converting between representations:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From</th>\n<th>To</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON string</td>\n<td>STRUCT</td>\n<td><code>from_json(raw, 'customer STRUCT&lt;id: INT, city: STRING&gt;')</code> (needs a schema; <code>schema_of_json</code> can derive one from a sample)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON string</td>\n<td>VARIANT</td>\n<td><code>parse_json(raw)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>STRUCT or VARIANT</td>\n<td>JSON string</td>\n<td><code>to_json(col)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"structs-arrays-and-maps\">Structs, arrays and maps</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Function</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>All fields of a struct as columns</td>\n<td><code>select(\"customer.*\")</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One row per array element</td>\n<td><code>explode(arr)</code>; drops rows whose array is NULL or empty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep rows with NULL or empty arrays</td>\n<td><code>explode_outer(arr)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Element and its position</td>\n<td><code>posexplode(arr)</code> (position starts at 0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Array of structs to rows and columns</td>\n<td><code>inline(arr)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Element by index</td>\n<td><code>arr[0]</code> (0-based), <code>element_at(arr, 1)</code> (1-based), <code>try_element_at</code> (NULL when out of range)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Transform or filter inside an array</td>\n<td><code>transform(arr, x -&gt; x * 2)</code>, <code>filter(arr, x -&gt; x &gt; 0)</code>, <code>exists(arr, x -&gt; x IS NULL)</code>, <code>aggregate(arr, 0, (acc, x) -&gt; acc + x)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Build arrays from rows</td>\n<td><code>collect_list(col)</code> (keeps duplicates), <code>collect_set(col)</code> (distinct)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Map lookup</td>\n<td><code>m['key']</code>, <code>map_keys(m)</code>, <code>map_values(m)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Array size, membership</td>\n<td><code>size(arr)</code>, <code>array_contains(arr, 'x')</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from pyspark.sql import functions as F\norders = (spark.table(&quot;bronze.orders_struct&quot;)\n          .select(&quot;order_id&quot;, F.explode_outer(&quot;items&quot;).alias(&quot;item&quot;))\n          .select(&quot;order_id&quot;, &quot;item.sku&quot;, &quot;item.qty&quot;))\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"ingesting-semi-structured-data\">Ingesting semi-structured data</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Auto Loader and <code>read_files</code> read JSON (one record per line by default; <code>multiLine</code> for documents spanning lines), XML (<code>rowTag</code>), Avro and Parquet.</li>\n<li>Auto Loader infers JSON fields as strings unless <code>inferColumnTypes</code> is set; nested objects become structs.</li>\n<li>To keep each record whole, set <code>singleVariantColumn</code> so every record lands in <strong>one VARIANT column</strong>. There is then no schema to evolve and no rescued data; you extract fields later with paths.</li>\n<li>With a schema or schema hints, a value of the wrong type goes to <code>_rescued_data</code>; it does not fail the stream. Only <strong>malformed</strong> records (broken JSON) fail in <code>FAILFAST</code> mode or are dropped in <code>DROPMALFORMED</code> mode.</li>\n<li>Databricks audit logs are delivered as JSON; <code>system.access.audit</code> exposes them as a table.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"unstructured-data\">Unstructured data</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep documents, images and audio in a <strong>volume</strong>: governed by Unity Catalog, readable from SQL and Python.</li>\n<li>The <code>binaryFile</code> reader returns one row per file with <code>path</code>, <code>modificationTime</code>, <code>length</code> and <code>content</code> (bytes). Use <code>pathGlobFilter</code> to pick file types; Auto Loader supports it for incremental loads.</li>\n<li><code>ai_parse_document</code> extracts text and layout from PDFs and images in SQL (see <em>UDFs and AI functions</em>).</li>\n<li>Managed connectors such as SharePoint ingest documents from business systems.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pdfs = (spark.readStream.format(&quot;cloudFiles&quot;)\n          .option(&quot;cloudFiles.format&quot;, &quot;binaryFile&quot;)\n          .option(&quot;pathGlobFilter&quot;, &quot;*.pdf&quot;)\n          .load(&quot;/Volumes/main/raw/contracts/&quot;))\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Colon versus dot</p>\n<p><code>raw:a.b</code> reads a JSON string or VARIANT. <code>a.b</code> reads a STRUCT. Mixing them up is a common wrong option.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">explode drops rows</p>\n<p><code>explode</code> removes rows whose array is NULL or empty. When those rows must stay, use <code>explode_outer</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">VARIANT is case sensitive</p>\n<p><code>v:Customer</code> and <code>v:customer</code> are different paths on VARIANT, but the same on a JSON string.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE TEMP VIEW raw_events AS SELECT * FROM VALUES\n ('{&quot;id&quot;:1,&quot;customer&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Ana&quot;,&quot;address&quot;:{&quot;city&quot;:&quot;Lisbon&quot;}},&quot;items&quot;:[{&quot;sku&quot;:&quot;A1&quot;,&quot;qty&quot;:2},{&quot;sku&quot;:&quot;B7&quot;,&quot;qty&quot;:1}]}'),\n ('{&quot;id&quot;:2,&quot;customer&quot;:{&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Rui&quot;},&quot;items&quot;:[]}') AS t(raw);\n\nSELECT raw:customer.address.city, raw:items[*].sku FROM raw_events;\nSELECT parse_json(raw) AS v FROM raw_events;\nSELECT v:id::INT, i.value:sku::STRING\nFROM (SELECT parse_json(raw) AS v FROM raw_events), LATERAL variant_explode(v:items) AS i;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Then build the same result with <code>from_json</code> and <code>explode_outer</code> in PySpark and check what happens to order 2.</p>","sections":["A2","A3","P2","P3"]},{"id":"sharing","title":"Sharing, Clean Rooms and Federation","group":"Ingestion","order":7,"summary":"Using data without copying it - OpenSharing between organisations (Databricks-to-Databricks and open), Clean Rooms for private collaboration, and Lakehouse Federation for external databases.","html":"<h2 id=\"three-ways-to-use-data-without-copying-it\">Three ways to use data without copying it</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Feature</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Give another organisation (or another metastore) live, read-only access to your tables</td>\n<td><strong>OpenSharing</strong> (formerly Delta Sharing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Combine your data with a partner's data while neither side sees the other's raw records</td>\n<td><strong>Clean Rooms</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Query an external database (PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Snowflake…) from Databricks, governed by Unity Catalog</td>\n<td><strong>Lakehouse Federation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"opensharing\">OpenSharing</h2>\n<p>OpenSharing is an open protocol for sharing live data. The provider keeps the data; recipients read it directly, so there is no copy to keep in sync.</p>\n<p><strong>Objects.</strong> A <strong>share</strong> is a named collection of objects (tables, views, volumes, models, notebooks). A <strong>recipient</strong> represents who receives it. The provider grants the share to the recipient.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE SHARE retail_share COMMENT 'Daily sales for partners';\nALTER SHARE retail_share ADD TABLE main.gold.daily_sales WITH HISTORY;   -- recipients can time travel and stream\nALTER SHARE retail_share ADD VIEW main.gold.eu_sales;                     -- share a subset through a view\n\nCREATE RECIPIENT consultancy USING ID 'azure:westeurope:7f3e...';         -- Databricks-to-Databricks\nCREATE RECIPIENT bi_partner;                                              -- open sharing\nGRANT SELECT ON SHARE retail_share TO RECIPIENT consultancy;\n</code></pre>\n<h3 id=\"two-sharing-modes\">Two sharing modes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Databricks-to-Databricks (D2D)</th>\n<th>Open sharing (Databricks-to-Open)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Recipient has</td>\n<td>A Unity Catalog-enabled workspace (any cloud, any region, any account)</td>\n<td>Any client: Spark, pandas, Power BI, Tableau, other platforms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recipient identified by</td>\n<td>The <strong>sharing identifier</strong> of their metastore: <code>cloud:region:metastore-uuid</code></td>\n<td>A credential: a bearer token from an activation link, or OIDC federation (no long-lived secret)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>What can be shared</td>\n<td>Tables, views, volumes, models, notebooks; history and streaming</td>\n<td>Tables (and views), read through the protocol</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>On the recipient side</td>\n<td>They create a catalog from the share (<code>CREATE CATALOG sales USING SHARE provider.retail_share</code>) and grant access to their own users</td>\n<td>They use the credential in their client</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The recipient's platform decides the mode: Databricks with Unity Catalog means D2D; anything else means open sharing.</p>\n<p><strong>Things to remember:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Shared data is read-only for recipients.</li>\n<li>The provider pays cloud <strong>egress</strong> when data leaves its cloud or region. To cut that cost, host the shared data in storage without egress fees or replicate it to the recipient's region; the sharing mode does not change the bytes transferred.</li>\n<li>You share a subset of rows or columns through a <strong>view</strong> (or partitions of a table).</li>\n<li>Databricks Marketplace is built on OpenSharing.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"clean-rooms\">Clean Rooms</h2>\n<p>A clean room is a secure, governed environment where several organisations bring data and run <strong>approved code</strong> on it, without any party seeing the others' raw data. It is built on OpenSharing and serverless compute.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each collaborator adds tables (or views) to the clean room; the others do not get direct access to them.</li>\n<li>Analysis runs as <strong>notebooks that all collaborators approve</strong>; results are written to <strong>output tables</strong> for the party that runs the notebook.</li>\n<li>Typical uses: measuring audience overlap between an advertiser and a publisher, joining customer lists by hashed identifiers, joint fraud analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Choose a clean room when the requirement says <em>neither party may see the other's records</em>. Choose plain sharing when one party simply gives the other read access.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lakehouse-federation\">Lakehouse Federation</h2>\n<p>Lakehouse Federation lets you query external databases in place. Unity Catalog governs access and records lineage and audit; the data stays in the source.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE CONNECTION pg_ops TYPE postgresql\nOPTIONS (host 'ops-db.internal', port '5432',\n         user secret('ops', 'pg_user'), password secret('ops', 'pg_password'));\n\nCREATE FOREIGN CATALOG ops USING CONNECTION pg_ops OPTIONS (database 'operations');\n\nGRANT USE CATALOG, USE SCHEMA, SELECT ON CATALOG ops TO `analysts`;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>connection</strong> stores the host and credentials once. Users who query the foreign catalog never see the credentials. Creating connections and foreign catalogs needs <code>CREATE CONNECTION</code> and <code>CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG</code> (and <code>USE CONNECTION</code> on the connection).</li>\n<li>A <strong>foreign catalog</strong> mirrors a database inside Unity Catalog; you grant on it like on any catalog.</li>\n<li>Filters and aggregates are <strong>pushed down</strong> to the source where possible, but performance and load depend on the source database.</li>\n<li>Good for ad hoc queries, proofs of concept, small reference data and \"always current, never copied\" requirements. For heavy, repeated workloads, ingest the data with Lakeflow Connect instead.</li>\n<li><strong>Catalog federation</strong> is the variant for other metastores (Hive metastore, AWS Glue, Snowflake Horizon): Databricks reads the tables' files directly from object storage.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Foreign catalog versus external location.</strong> A foreign catalog exposes an external <strong>database</strong> for federated queries. An external location grants access to a <strong>cloud storage path</strong> for files and external tables.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Choose</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Partner on Databricks with Unity Catalog needs live access to your tables</td>\n<td>D2D sharing with their metastore sharing identifier</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partner on another platform, no Databricks</td>\n<td>Open sharing (token or OIDC)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two companies must match customers without exposing their lists</td>\n<td>Clean room</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Join lakehouse tables with an operational PostgreSQL, always current, no second copy allowed</td>\n<td>Lakehouse Federation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Join with that database every five minutes on large volumes</td>\n<td>Ingest with Lakeflow Connect (CDC)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Ask for the metastore, not the cluster</p>\n<p>A D2D recipient is identified by its metastore sharing identifier. Cluster names, IP addresses or user credentials are never part of sharing.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Federation is not ingestion</p>\n<p>Federation reads the source on every query; nothing is copied, so results are always current and the source carries the load.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Egress is about location</p>\n<p>The cost of sharing across clouds or regions comes from where the data lives, not from the sharing mode.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>In Catalog Explorer, open <strong>Delta Sharing</strong> (<em>Shared with me</em>) and browse the providers available in your workspace, if any.</li>\n<li>If your workspace allows it, create a share with one table and a view, and create an open sharing recipient to see the activation link flow.</li>\n<li>Read the list of Lakehouse Federation connection types in the <em>Create connection</em> dialog to know which sources are supported.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A2","P2"]},{"id":"transform","title":"Spark SQL and PySpark transformations","group":"Transformation","order":8,"summary":"Selecting and cleaning columns, types, ANSI mode and NULLs, joins and unions, deduplication, aggregations and window functions, in both SQL and PySpark.","html":"<h2 id=\"dataframe-basics\">DataFrame basics</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>DataFrames are <strong>immutable</strong> and <strong>lazy</strong>: transformations (<code>select</code>, <code>filter</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>groupBy</code>) build a plan; actions (<code>count</code>, <code>show</code>, <code>collect</code>, <code>write</code>) run it.</li>\n<li>Read a table with <code>spark.read.table(\"cat.sch.t\")</code> (or <code>spark.table</code>), write with <code>df.write.saveAsTable(...)</code>. A temporary view (<code>df.createOrReplaceTempView(\"v\")</code>) lets you mix SQL and Python in a session.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task</th>\n<th>PySpark</th>\n<th>SQL</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Choose columns</td>\n<td><code>select(\"a\", F.col(\"b\").alias(\"c\"))</code>, <code>selectExpr(\"a * 2 AS a2\")</code></td>\n<td><code>SELECT a, b AS c</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All but some columns</td>\n<td><code>drop(\"x\", \"y\")</code></td>\n<td><code>SELECT * EXCEPT (x, y)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add or replace columns</td>\n<td><code>withColumn(\"n\", expr)</code>; several at once: <code>withColumns({\"n\": e1, \"m\": e2})</code></td>\n<td>expressions in <code>SELECT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rename</td>\n<td><code>withColumnRenamed(\"old\", \"new\")</code></td>\n<td><code>old AS new</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Filter rows</td>\n<td><code>filter(cond)</code> or its alias <code>where(cond)</code></td>\n<td><code>WHERE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conditional value</td>\n<td><code>F.when(c, v).otherwise(w)</code></td>\n<td><code>CASE WHEN ... END</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sort, limit</td>\n<td><code>orderBy(F.desc(\"ts\"))</code>, <code>limit(10)</code></td>\n<td><code>ORDER BY ts DESC LIMIT 10</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>withColumnRenamed</code> and <code>drop</code> <strong>silently do nothing</strong> when the column does not exist. Calling <code>withColumn</code> in a long loop builds a huge plan; use <code>withColumns</code> or one <code>select</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"types-ansi-mode-and-nulls\">Types, ANSI mode and NULLs</h2>\n<p><strong>ANSI mode</strong> is on by default in recent runtimes, serverless and SQL warehouses. An invalid cast, an arithmetic overflow or an array index out of range then <strong>fails</strong> the query instead of returning NULL:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fails under ANSI</th>\n<th>Returns NULL instead</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CAST('N/A' AS INT)</code></td>\n<td><code>try_cast('N/A' AS INT)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>to_date('31/02/2026', 'dd/MM/yyyy')</code></td>\n<td><code>try_to_date(...)</code>, <code>try_to_timestamp(...)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>split(ref, '-')[2]</code> on a short value</td>\n<td><code>try_element_at(split(ref, '-'), 3)</code> (1-based)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>a / 0</code></td>\n<td><code>try_divide(a, 0)</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Do not turn ANSI off for the session to make one cast pass: it changes behaviour everywhere (overflows become silent). Use the <code>try_</code> functions and count the rows that became NULL as a quality metric.</p>\n<p><strong>Date patterns</strong>: <code>yyyy</code> year, <code>MM</code> month, <code>dd</code> day, <code>HH</code> hour, <code>mm</code> <strong>minute</strong>, <code>ss</code> second. <code>to_date(s)</code> without a pattern behaves like a cast and expects <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>NULL rules</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>x = NULL</code> is never true (it is NULL). Use <code>IS NULL</code>, <code>isNull()</code>, or the null-safe operator <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> (<code>eqNullSafe</code> in PySpark).</li>\n<li>NULL join keys never match in a normal join.</li>\n<li><code>count(col)</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code>, <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> ignore NULLs; <code>count(*)</code> counts every row. If the business rule says NULL means 0, use <code>avg(coalesce(x, 0))</code>.</li>\n<li><code>coalesce(a, b, 'n/a')</code> returns the first non-NULL value.</li>\n<li><code>fillna(0)</code> fills only numeric columns and <code>fillna(\"n/a\")</code> only string columns; mix them with a dictionary: <code>fillna({\"qty\": 0, \"region\": \"n/a\"})</code>. <code>dropna(subset=[\"id\"])</code> drops rows with NULL ids.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"joins\">Joins</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Join</th>\n<th>Keeps</th>\n<th>Columns returned</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>inner</code></td>\n<td>Rows with a match on both sides</td>\n<td>Both</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>left</code> (<code>left_outer</code>)</td>\n<td>All left rows; NULLs where no match</td>\n<td>Both</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>right</code>, <code>full</code></td>\n<td>All right rows; all rows of both</td>\n<td>Both</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>left_semi</code></td>\n<td>Left rows that <strong>have</strong> a match</td>\n<td>Left only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>left_anti</code></td>\n<td>Left rows that have <strong>no</strong> match</td>\n<td>Left only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cross</code></td>\n<td>Every combination</td>\n<td>Both</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># list of names: one copy of each key in the result\norders.join(prices, [&quot;product_id&quot;, &quot;region&quot;], &quot;left&quot;)\n# expression: both copies stay, so select or drop through the DataFrame reference\norders.join(prices, (orders.product_id == prices.product_id) &amp; (orders.region == prices.region)) \\\n      .drop(prices.product_id, prices.region)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Joining on an expression keeps both key columns; selecting <code>\"product_id\"</code> afterwards fails with <code>AMBIGUOUS_REFERENCE</code>. Join on a list of names (SQL: <code>USING (product_id, region)</code>).</li>\n<li>Keep the fact table on the left of a <code>left</code> join when no fact row may be lost.</li>\n<li>Duplicate keys on the right side multiply rows (fan-out). Deduplicate dimensions first.</li>\n<li>In SQL, a <code>JOIN</code> without <code>ON</code> is a cross join.</li>\n<li><strong>Broadcast joins</strong> send a small table to every executor and avoid shuffling the large one: <code>F.broadcast(small_df)</code> or <code>/*+ BROADCAST(dim) */</code>. Spark does it automatically below <code>spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold</code> (10 MB by default; -1 turns it off). A hint overrides the threshold, so never broadcast the large side.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"unions\">Unions</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Operation</th>\n<th>Matches columns by</th>\n<th>Duplicates</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>PySpark <code>union</code> / <code>unionAll</code></td>\n<td><strong>Position</strong></td>\n<td>Kept</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>PySpark <code>unionByName(other, allowMissingColumns=True)</code></td>\n<td><strong>Name</strong>; missing columns become NULL</td>\n<td>Kept</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL <code>UNION</code></td>\n<td>Position</td>\n<td><strong>Removed</strong> (same as <code>UNION DISTINCT</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL <code>UNION ALL</code></td>\n<td>Position</td>\n<td>Kept</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><code>INTERSECT</code> and <code>EXCEPT</code> return common rows and rows of the first query missing from the second.</p>\n<h2 id=\"deduplication\">Deduplication</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Remove rows identical in every column</td>\n<td><code>distinct()</code> / <code>SELECT DISTINCT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One row per key, any of them</td>\n<td><code>dropDuplicates([\"key\"])</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The <strong>latest</strong> row per key, deterministically</td>\n<td><code>row_number()</code> over a window ordered by timestamp descending, plus a tiebreaker; keep row 1</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT *\nFROM bronze.customers\nQUALIFY row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id\n                           ORDER BY updated_at DESC, ingest_id DESC) = 1;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Without a unique tiebreaker, two rows with the same <code>updated_at</code> make the choice non-deterministic.</p>\n<h2 id=\"aggregations\">Aggregations</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">daily = (readings.groupBy(&quot;station_id&quot;)\n         .agg(F.max(&quot;temp_c&quot;).alias(&quot;temp_max&quot;),\n              F.avg(&quot;temp_c&quot;).alias(&quot;temp_avg&quot;),\n              F.countDistinct(&quot;sensor_id&quot;).alias(&quot;sensors&quot;),\n              F.approx_count_distinct(&quot;device_id&quot;).alias(&quot;devices_approx&quot;)))\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>agg</code> without <code>groupBy</code> aggregates the whole DataFrame into <strong>one row</strong>.</li>\n<li>The dictionary form (<code>agg({\"temp_c\": \"max\"})</code>) allows one function per column; several aggregations of the same column need expressions with aliases.</li>\n<li><code>count_distinct</code> is exact. <code>approx_count_distinct</code> (HyperLogLog, about 5% relative error by default) is much cheaper on very large data.</li>\n<li><code>df.describe()</code> gives count, mean, stddev, min and max; <code>df.summary()</code> adds the 25%, 50% and 75% percentiles.</li>\n<li>SQL extras: <code>GROUP BY ALL</code>, <code>HAVING</code>, <code>count_if(cond)</code>, <code>ROLLUP</code>, <code>CUBE</code>, <code>GROUPING SETS</code>, <code>percentile_approx</code>.</li>\n<li>Pivot: <code>groupBy(\"store\").pivot(\"month\", [\"Jan\", \"Feb\"]).sum(\"sales\")</code> (listing the values avoids an extra pass).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"window-functions\">Window functions</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from pyspark.sql.window import Window\nw = Window.partitionBy(&quot;region&quot;).orderBy(F.desc(&quot;total&quot;))\nrunning = Window.partitionBy(&quot;customer&quot;).orderBy(&quot;ts&quot;) \\\n                .rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)\n\ndf.select(&quot;*&quot;,\n          F.row_number().over(w).alias(&quot;rn&quot;),        # 1, 2, 3, 4 (unique)\n          F.rank().over(w).alias(&quot;rk&quot;),              # 1, 1, 3, 4 (gaps after ties)\n          F.dense_rank().over(w).alias(&quot;drk&quot;),       # 1, 1, 2, 3 (no gaps)\n          F.lag(&quot;total&quot;).over(w).alias(&quot;prev&quot;),      # previous row in the window\n          F.sum(&quot;amount&quot;).over(running).alias(&quot;cumulative&quot;))\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Without <code>partitionBy</code>, the window spans the whole dataset (and all data goes to one task).</li>\n<li>Without <code>orderBy</code>, an aggregate over a window returns the partition total.</li>\n<li>With <code>orderBy</code> and no explicit frame, the default frame is <code>RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW</code>: rows with the same order value are summed together. Use <code>rowsBetween</code> for a strict row-by-row running total.</li>\n<li><code>lag</code> looks back, <code>lead</code> looks forward.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">union is not UNION</p>\n<p>PySpark <code>union</code> keeps duplicates and matches by position. SQL <code>UNION</code> removes duplicates.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">dropDuplicates keeps any row</p>\n<p>For \"the most recent record per key\", the answer is a window with <code>row_number()</code> ordered by time.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Indexes</p>\n<p><code>[i]</code> and <code>getItem</code> start at 0; <code>element_at</code> and <code>try_element_at</code> start at 1; <code>posexplode</code> positions start at 0.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Read code from the details</p>\n<p>In code questions, check first the write mode, NULL handling, 0 versus 1 indexing, <code>union</code> versus <code>unionByName</code>, and a missing <code>groupBy</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<p>Use <code>samples.tpch</code> (available in every workspace):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Customers with no orders: <code>customer</code> left anti join <code>orders</code> on <code>c_custkey = o_custkey</code>.</li>\n<li>Latest order per customer with <code>QUALIFY row_number()</code>.</li>\n<li>Revenue per nation with <code>groupBy(...).agg(...)</code>, then rank nations inside each region with <code>dense_rank</code>.</li>\n<li>Running total of order value per customer by date with <code>rowsBetween</code>.</li>\n<li><code>try_cast</code> a column of mixed strings and count how many rows became NULL.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A3","P3"]},{"id":"udf-ai","title":"UDFs and AI functions","group":"Transformation","order":9,"summary":"When to write a UDF, SQL versus Python versus pandas UDFs, Unity Catalog functions, and AI functions such as ai_query, ai_classify and ai_parse_document in batch pipelines.","html":"<h2 id=\"built-in-functions-first\">Built-in functions first</h2>\n<p>A UDF is a black box: the optimizer cannot look inside it, Photon cannot run Python code, and a UDF in a filter prevents data skipping. Check the built-in function list before writing one. When you do need a UDF, the cost depends on its kind:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Runs where</th>\n<th>Speed</th>\n<th>Use when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Built-in function</td>\n<td>Engine (Photon where available)</td>\n<td>Fastest</td>\n<td>Always, if one exists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>SQL UDF</strong></td>\n<td>Inlined into the query plan</td>\n<td>Close to built-ins</td>\n<td>The logic can be written in SQL and you want to reuse it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>pandas UDF</strong> (vectorized)</td>\n<td>Python worker, batches through Apache Arrow</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>Logic needs Python libraries (NumPy, pandas, a parser, a model)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Python UDF</strong> (row at a time)</td>\n<td>Python worker, one call per row</td>\n<td>Slowest</td>\n<td>Small data or no alternative; mark it Arrow-optimized (<code>useArrow=True</code>) to cut serialisation cost</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>pandas UDFs are faster than plain Python UDFs because Arrow moves whole batches between the JVM and Python and the function works on pandas Series, instead of serialising and calling Python once per row. Serialisation still happens; it is just much cheaper.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sql-udfs-and-unity-catalog-functions\">SQL UDFs and Unity Catalog functions</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION main.util.normalize_phone(p STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nCOMMENT 'Keeps digits and the leading +'\nRETURN regexp_replace(p, '[^0-9+]', '');\n\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION main.util.mask_iban(iban STRING)\nRETURNS STRING\nLANGUAGE PYTHON\nAS $$\n  if iban is None:\n      return None\n  return iban[:4] + &quot;*&quot; * (len(iban) - 8) + iban[-4:]\n$$;\n\nSELECT main.util.normalize_phone(phone), main.util.mask_iban(iban) FROM silver.customers;\nGRANT EXECUTE ON FUNCTION main.util.mask_iban TO `analysts`;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A function created with a three-level name is a <strong>Unity Catalog function</strong>: stored once, governed with <code>EXECUTE</code>, visible in lineage, and usable from notebooks, jobs and SQL warehouses in every attached workspace.</li>\n<li><code>LANGUAGE PYTHON</code> functions run in an isolated Python environment; SQL functions are inlined and faster.</li>\n<li>A function can also <strong>return a table</strong> (<code>RETURNS TABLE (...)</code>) and be used in <code>FROM</code>.</li>\n<li><code>CREATE TEMPORARY FUNCTION</code> lives only for the session.</li>\n<li>SQL UDFs are what row filters and column masks are made of (see <em>Data privacy</em>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"python-and-pandas-udfs-in-pyspark\">Python and pandas UDFs in PySpark</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import pandas_udf, udf\n\n@udf(&quot;string&quot;, useArrow=True)                 # row at a time, Arrow-optimised\ndef initials(name):\n    return None if name is None else &quot;&quot;.join(p[0] for p in name.split())\n\n@pandas_udf(&quot;double&quot;)                          # Series in, Series out\ndef to_fahrenheit(c: pd.Series) -&gt; pd.Series:\n    return c * 9 / 5 + 32\n\nreadings.select(&quot;sensor&quot;, to_fahrenheit(&quot;temp_c&quot;).alias(&quot;temp_f&quot;))\nspark.udf.register(&quot;to_fahrenheit&quot;, to_fahrenheit)   # callable from SQL in this session\n</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>pandas UDF shape</th>\n<th>Signature</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Series to Series</td>\n<td><code>pd.Series -&gt; pd.Series</code></td>\n<td>Vectorised column logic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Iterator of Series</td>\n<td><code>Iterator[pd.Series] -&gt; Iterator[pd.Series]</code></td>\n<td>Expensive setup once per batch stream, such as loading a model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Series to scalar</td>\n<td><code>pd.Series -&gt; float</code></td>\n<td>Custom aggregation in <code>groupBy().agg()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>applyInPandas</code></td>\n<td>function on each group's pandas DataFrame</td>\n<td>Per-group logic (one model per store)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>mapInPandas</code></td>\n<td>iterator of DataFrames</td>\n<td>Row batches in, row batches out</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>UDFs must handle <code>None</code> themselves. Return types are declared; a value of another type becomes NULL or fails.</p>\n<p><strong>Choosing under constraints</strong>: logic expressible in SQL, used by analysts on SQL warehouses → SQL UDF in Unity Catalog. Needs a Python library, large data → pandas UDF. Must be governed and shared across teams → Unity Catalog function. Loads a model or a big lookup → iterator pandas UDF.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ai-functions\">AI functions</h2>\n<p>AI functions call models served by Databricks from SQL or Python, so you can enrich data in batch without building an API client.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Function</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ai_query(endpoint, request)</code></td>\n<td>Sends each row to any model serving endpoint (foundation model or your own model) and returns the response</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ai_classify(text, labels)</code></td>\n<td>Picks one of the given labels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ai_extract(text, labels)</code></td>\n<td>Extracts entities such as person, location, product</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ai_analyze_sentiment(text)</code></td>\n<td>positive, negative, neutral or mixed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ai_summarize</code>, <code>ai_translate</code>, <code>ai_fix_grammar</code>, <code>ai_gen</code>, <code>ai_similarity</code></td>\n<td>Summary, translation, grammar fixes, free generation, semantic similarity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ai_mask(text, labels)</code></td>\n<td>Replaces entities of the given types (for example names, emails) with a mask</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ai_parse_document(content)</code></td>\n<td>Extracts text, tables and layout from PDFs and images</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE silver_tickets AS\nSELECT ticket_id, body,\n       ai_classify(body, ARRAY('billing', 'delivery', 'product', 'other')) AS category,\n       ai_analyze_sentiment(body)                                          AS sentiment,\n       ai_query('my_llm_endpoint',\n                CONCAT('Summarise this ticket in one sentence: ', body),\n                failOnError =&gt; false)                                      AS summary\nFROM STREAM bronze_tickets;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>With <code>failOnError =&gt; false</code>, <code>ai_query</code> returns a struct with the result and an error message instead of failing the whole query on one bad row.</li>\n<li><code>ai_query</code> accepts a <code>responseFormat</code> to force structured (JSON schema) output, useful for extraction into columns.</li>\n<li>AI functions cost tokens or throughput. Run them <strong>incrementally</strong> (a streaming table processes each new row once) instead of in a materialized view that might recompute every row, and test on a <code>LIMIT</code> sample first.</li>\n<li>They run on SQL warehouses (pro or serverless), serverless compute and recent runtimes, in regions where the models are available.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">UDF performance order</p>\n<p>Built-in, then SQL UDF, then pandas UDF, then plain Python UDF. An answer that replaces a built-in function with a Python UDF \"for speed\" is wrong.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">pandas UDFs still serialise</p>\n<p>They are faster because of Arrow batches, not because data stays in the JVM.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Incremental AI enrichment</p>\n<p>Put AI functions on new rows only (streaming table, <code>MERGE</code> of new keys), not on the full table at every refresh.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create <code>workspace.default.normalize_phone</code> as a SQL UDF and use it on a few literal values.</li>\n<li>Write the same logic as a Python UDF and as a pandas UDF; apply each to <code>spark.range(5_000_000)</code> with a generated string column and compare run times.</li>\n<li>In a SQL editor, run <code>SELECT ai_analyze_sentiment('The delivery was late again')</code> and <code>ai_classify('Where is my parcel?', ARRAY('billing', 'delivery'))</code> if AI functions are available in your workspace.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["P1","P3"]},{"id":"pipelines","title":"Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines","group":"Transformation","order":10,"summary":"Streaming tables, materialized views and views, SQL and Python syntax, expectations and quarantine, pipeline modes, full refresh, and when to choose pipelines over hand-written streaming.","html":"<h2 id=\"what-a-declarative-pipeline-is\">What a declarative pipeline is</h2>\n<p>You declare <strong>datasets</strong> (what each table should contain) in SQL or Python files. The engine works out the dependency graph, runs the flows in the right order, keeps checkpoints, retries failures, scales compute, records lineage and data quality metrics, and publishes the results to Unity Catalog. Formerly called Delta Live Tables (DLT).</p>\n<p>Pipeline settings you should know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Source code</strong>: a folder of <code>.sql</code> and <code>.py</code> files (each dataset defined once).</li>\n<li><strong>Default catalog and schema</strong>: where datasets are published.</li>\n<li><strong>Compute</strong>: serverless (recommended) or classic clusters with autoscaling.</li>\n<li><strong>Mode</strong>: <em>triggered</em> (process what is available, then stop; schedule it in a job) or <em>continuous</em> (keep running for low latency).</li>\n<li><strong>Run as</strong>: the identity the pipeline uses; use a service principal in production.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"dataset-types\">Dataset types</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Streaming table</th>\n<th>Materialized view</th>\n<th>Temporary view</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Processing</td>\n<td>Incremental: each input row is processed once (append)</td>\n<td>Keeps a query result up to date; refreshes incrementally when possible, otherwise recomputes</td>\n<td>Not stored; computed when used inside the pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Typical source</td>\n<td>Streaming: <code>STREAM read_files(...)</code>, Kafka, another table read as a stream</td>\n<td>Any batch query: joins, aggregations, windows</td>\n<td>Any</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good for</td>\n<td>Ingestion (bronze), append-only cleaning, <code>AUTO CDC</code> targets</td>\n<td>Aggregates, joins with dimensions, \"latest status\" per key, gold tables</td>\n<td>Intermediate steps nobody reads outside the pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When the query changes</td>\n<td>Only new rows use the new logic, until a full refresh</td>\n<td>Next refresh uses the new logic for everything</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Choosing a streaming table or a materialized view</strong> (both are valid gold objects too):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Source is append-only and you need low latency and low cost per row: <strong>streaming table</strong>.</li>\n<li>The result depends on all the data (aggregations), sources get updates or deletes, or dimension changes must be reflected in old rows: <strong>materialized view</strong>.</li>\n<li>A streaming table joined with a dimension (stream-static join) uses the dimension as it was when each row was processed; later dimension changes do not update old rows. A materialized view would.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Raw delivery events arriving all day plus \"current status per delivery\" is the classic pair: a streaming table for the events, a materialized view for the latest status.</p>\n<h2 id=\"syntax\">Syntax</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- bronze: incremental file ingestion\nCREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE bronze_orders\nCOMMENT 'Raw orders from the landing volume'\nAS SELECT *, _metadata.file_path AS source_file\nFROM STREAM read_files('/Volumes/main/raw/landing/orders/', format =&gt; 'json');\n\n-- silver: incremental cleaning with expectations\nCREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE silver_orders (\n  CONSTRAINT valid_id     EXPECT (order_id IS NOT NULL) ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE,\n  CONSTRAINT valid_amount EXPECT (amount &gt; 0)           ON VIOLATION DROP ROW,\n  CONSTRAINT has_email    EXPECT (email IS NOT NULL)\n) AS SELECT order_id, customer_id, try_cast(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2)) AS amount, email, order_ts\nFROM STREAM bronze_orders;\n\n-- gold: aggregate\nCREATE OR REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold_daily_sales AS\nSELECT date(order_ts) AS order_date, sum(amount) AS revenue, count(*) AS orders\nFROM silver_orders GROUP BY ALL;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from pyspark import pipelines as dp        # older code: import dlt\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\n@dp.table(comment=&quot;Clean orders&quot;)          # streaming table, because the query is a stream\n@dp.expect(&quot;has_email&quot;, &quot;email IS NOT NULL&quot;)\n@dp.expect_or_drop(&quot;valid_amount&quot;, &quot;amount &gt; 0&quot;)\n@dp.expect_or_fail(&quot;valid_id&quot;, &quot;order_id IS NOT NULL&quot;)\ndef silver_orders():\n    return spark.readStream.table(&quot;bronze_orders&quot;)\n\n@dp.materialized_view\ndef gold_daily_sales():\n    return (spark.read.table(&quot;silver_orders&quot;)\n            .groupBy(F.to_date(&quot;order_ts&quot;).alias(&quot;order_date&quot;))\n            .agg(F.sum(&quot;amount&quot;).alias(&quot;revenue&quot;)))\n\n@dp.temporary_view\ndef orders_enriched():                     # intermediate step, not persisted\n    return spark.read.table(&quot;silver_orders&quot;).join(spark.read.table(&quot;dim_customers&quot;), &quot;customer_id&quot;)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Read another dataset of the pipeline with <code>spark.readStream.table(...)</code> (incremental) or <code>spark.read.table(...)</code> (complete). Legacy code uses <code>dlt.read_stream</code>, <code>dlt.read</code> and the <code>LIVE.</code> prefix.</li>\n<li>In the legacy <code>dlt</code> module, <code>@dlt.table</code> persists a dataset and <code>@dlt.view</code> does not.</li>\n<li>Several sources can feed one streaming table with <strong>append flows</strong> (<code>dp.create_streaming_table(\"all_orders\")</code> plus <code>@dp.append_flow(target=\"all_orders\")</code> per source), without a union that restarts when a source is added.</li>\n<li><strong>Sinks</strong> (<code>dp.create_sink</code>) write a flow to Kafka or to an external Delta table.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"expectations\">Expectations</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clause (SQL)</th>\n<th>Python decorator</th>\n<th>Invalid row</th>\n<th>Pipeline</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>no <code>ON VIOLATION</code></td>\n<td><code>@dp.expect</code></td>\n<td><strong>Kept</strong>, violation counted</td>\n<td>Continues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ON VIOLATION DROP ROW</code></td>\n<td><code>@dp.expect_or_drop</code></td>\n<td><strong>Dropped</strong>, counted</td>\n<td>Continues</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE</code></td>\n<td><code>@dp.expect_or_fail</code></td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>Update fails</strong>; the transaction is rolled back</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Python can group rules: <code>@dp.expect_all({...})</code>, <code>@dp.expect_all_or_drop</code>, <code>@dp.expect_all_or_fail</code>.</li>\n<li>Metrics appear in the pipeline's data quality view and in the <strong>event log</strong> (<code>flow_progress</code> events, <code>details.flow_progress.data_quality.expectations</code>), with passed and failed counts per rule.</li>\n<li><code>DROP ROW</code> keeps only a count, not the rejected rows. When rejected rows and the reason must be kept, build a <strong>quarantine table</strong> with the opposite condition. Handle NULLs: <code>NOT (amount &gt; 0)</code> is NULL, not true, for a NULL amount, so write <code>NOT coalesce(amount &gt; 0, false)</code> or compute an <code>is_valid</code> flag once and split on it.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE orders_quarantine AS\nSELECT *, 'amount must be positive' AS reason\nFROM STREAM bronze_orders WHERE NOT coalesce(amount &gt; 0, false);\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Delta constraints</strong> (<code>CHECK</code>, <code>NOT NULL</code>) are different: they apply to every writer of the table and make the write fail. Expectations exist only inside the pipeline and can warn, drop or fail.</li>\n<li>Use <code>FAIL UPDATE</code> when bad data signals a broken upstream and continuing would be worse than stopping; <code>DROP ROW</code> for expected noise; warn to monitor a rule before enforcing it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"running-and-operating-a-pipeline\">Running and operating a pipeline</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>An <strong>update</strong> runs the graph. <em>Refresh</em> processes new data; <strong>full refresh</strong> truncates streaming tables, resets their checkpoints and reprocesses everything still available in the sources. Data no longer in the source (expired Kafka messages, deleted files) is lost; protect such tables with <code>pipelines.reset.allowed = false</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Development mode</strong> reuses compute and does not retry, for fast iteration. <strong>Production mode</strong> restarts and retries on failure.</li>\n<li>The <strong>event log</strong> is queryable: <code>SELECT * FROM event_log(TABLE(main.sales.silver_orders))</code> (or by pipeline ID). Useful event types: <code>flow_progress</code> (rows, quality metrics, backlog), <code>update_progress</code> (state of each update), <code>planning_information</code> (whether a materialized view refreshed incrementally or fully), <code>user_action</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Deleting a pipeline deletes the tables it defines.</strong></li>\n<li>Schedule triggered pipelines with a <strong>pipeline task</strong> in Lakeflow Jobs, which can also request a full refresh.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"materialized-views-and-streaming-tables-outside-a-pipeline\">Materialized views and streaming tables outside a pipeline</h3>\n<p>In Databricks SQL you can create them directly; Databricks runs a serverless pipeline for them behind the scenes.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.sales_by_region\nSCHEDULE EVERY 1 HOUR\nAS SELECT region, sum(amount) AS revenue FROM silver.orders GROUP BY region;\n\nREFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW gold.sales_by_region;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>They need a pro or serverless SQL warehouse (or serverless compute). A refresh can also be scheduled or triggered when the source tables update.</li>\n<li>A refresh <strong>runs as the owner</strong>: if the owner loses access to a source, refreshes fail. Make a service principal the owner.</li>\n<li>A view is recomputed at every query; a materialized view is precomputed, so it suits expensive aggregates read often by BI.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pipelines-or-structured-streaming-code\">Pipelines or Structured Streaming code?</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Choose declarative pipelines when</th>\n<th>Choose Structured Streaming code when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard ETL: ingest, clean, join, aggregate, CDC</td>\n<td>You need custom <code>foreachBatch</code> logic or writes to several systems per batch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You want checkpoints, retries, orchestration, lineage and quality metrics managed for you</td>\n<td>You need arbitrary stateful processing or fine control of triggers and state</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A small team must keep operations low</td>\n<td>You already have tested streaming code to run inside a job</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">No ON VIOLATION means keep</p>\n<p>An expectation without an action keeps the row and records the violation.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">DROP ROW is not a quarantine</p>\n<p>Dropped rows are gone; only their count remains. Keeping them needs a separate table.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Full refresh of a streaming table</p>\n<p>It truncates the table. Anything the source no longer holds is lost for good.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Table versus view</p>\n<p>Persist only what someone reads outside the pipeline; intermediate steps can be temporary views.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<p>In Free Edition, create a pipeline (<em>Jobs &amp; Pipelines → ETL pipeline</em>) with the SQL above, pointing <code>read_files</code> at a volume with a few JSON files. Run it, add a file with a negative amount and one with a NULL <code>order_id</code>, and check the data quality counts, the failed update, and the event log:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT timestamp, details:flow_progress.data_quality.expectations\nFROM event_log(TABLE(workspace.default.silver_orders))\nWHERE event_type = 'flow_progress';\n</code></pre>","sections":["A3","P1","P3","P4"]},{"id":"streaming","title":"Structured Streaming","group":"Transformation","order":11,"summary":"Micro-batches, checkpoints and exactly-once, triggers, output modes, watermarks and stateful operations, deduplication, foreachBatch with MERGE, and monitoring streams.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-model\">The model</h2>\n<p>Structured Streaming treats a stream as a table that keeps growing. Each <strong>micro-batch</strong> reads the new input, updates the result and writes it to the sink.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from pyspark.sql import functions as F\nevents = spark.readStream.table(&quot;main.bronze.events&quot;)          # Delta table as a source\n(events.filter(&quot;event_type = 'purchase'&quot;)\n   .writeStream\n   .option(&quot;checkpointLocation&quot;, &quot;/Volumes/main/meta/checkpoints/purchases&quot;)\n   .trigger(availableNow=True)\n   .toTable(&quot;main.silver.purchases&quot;))\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Sources</strong>: Delta tables, files through Auto Loader, Kafka, Kinesis, Event Hubs, Pub/Sub.</li>\n<li><strong>Sinks</strong>: Delta tables (<code>toTable</code>), Kafka, or any code through <code>foreachBatch</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Exactly-once</strong> comes from three things together: a replayable source, the <strong>checkpoint</strong> (offsets and state, written before each batch commits) and an idempotent sink such as Delta. A restarted query resumes where it stopped.</li>\n<li>One checkpoint per query, never shared. Deleting it makes the query start over. Some changes to a running query (adding or changing stateful operations, changing sources) are incompatible with its checkpoint and need a new one.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Triggers (<code>availableNow</code>, <code>processingTime</code>, none, deprecated <code>once</code>) are described in <em>Ingesting files</em>; they work the same for every source.</p>\n<h2 id=\"output-modes\">Output modes</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Mode</th>\n<th>Writes each batch</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n<th>Delta sink</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>append</code> (default)</td>\n<td>Only new rows that will never change</td>\n<td>Row-level transformations; windowed aggregations with a watermark (a window is written once it closes)</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>complete</code></td>\n<td>The whole result table</td>\n<td>Small aggregations that are rewritten every batch</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>update</code></td>\n<td>Only rows changed in this batch</td>\n<td>Running aggregates</td>\n<td>No direct support: use <code>foreachBatch</code> with <code>MERGE</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"stateful-operations-and-watermarks\">Stateful operations and watermarks</h2>\n<p>Aggregations, deduplication and stream-stream joins keep <strong>state</strong> between batches (in the state store, RocksDB recommended, saved in the checkpoint). Without a limit, state grows forever.</p>\n<p>A <strong>watermark</strong> says how late data may arrive:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">counts = (events\n  .withWatermark(&quot;event_ts&quot;, &quot;10 minutes&quot;)\n  .groupBy(F.window(&quot;event_ts&quot;, &quot;5 minutes&quot;), &quot;store_id&quot;)\n  .count())\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Spark keeps state for windows that can still receive data: anything older than <em>latest event time seen − 10 minutes</em> is finalised, written (in append mode) and dropped from state.</li>\n<li>Events arriving later than the watermark are ignored for those windows.</li>\n<li>Window types: tumbling <code>window(ts, \"5 minutes\")</code>, sliding <code>window(ts, \"10 minutes\", \"5 minutes\")</code>, session <code>session_window(ts, \"30 minutes\")</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Deduplication</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Duplicates arrive close together (minutes or hours)</td>\n<td><code>withWatermark(...)</code> plus <code>dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark([\"event_id\"])</code>; state is kept only for the watermark period</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duplicates can arrive days apart</td>\n<td>Insert-only <code>MERGE</code> against the target table inside <code>foreachBatch</code>: it checks the whole table, with no state limit</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Joins</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stream-static join: each batch joins with the current version of a Delta table. It keeps no state; rows already processed are not updated when the static table changes.</li>\n<li>Stream-stream join: both sides need watermarks and a time condition (for example <code>click_ts BETWEEN view_ts AND view_ts + INTERVAL 1 HOUR</code>) so state can be cleaned up.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"foreachbatch\">foreachBatch</h2>\n<p><code>foreachBatch</code> hands each micro-batch to your function as a normal DataFrame, so you can use batch-only operations: <code>MERGE</code>, writes to several tables, JDBC writes.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from delta.tables import DeltaTable\n\ndef upsert_customers(batch_df, batch_id):\n    latest = batch_df.dropDuplicates([&quot;customer_id&quot;])        # one row per key for MERGE\n    (DeltaTable.forName(batch_df.sparkSession, &quot;main.silver.customers&quot;).alias(&quot;t&quot;)\n        .merge(latest.alias(&quot;s&quot;), &quot;t.customer_id = s.customer_id&quot;)\n        .whenMatchedUpdateAll()\n        .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()\n        .execute())\n\n(spark.readStream.table(&quot;main.bronze.customer_updates&quot;)\n   .writeStream.foreachBatch(upsert_customers)\n   .option(&quot;checkpointLocation&quot;, &quot;/Volumes/main/meta/checkpoints/customers&quot;)\n   .trigger(availableNow=True)\n   .start())\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>If a batch fails halfway, it is <strong>retried</strong>: <code>foreachBatch</code> is at-least-once unless your writes are idempotent. <code>MERGE</code> on a key is naturally idempotent. For appends, pass the Delta options <code>txnAppId</code> (a fixed ID for the query) and <code>txnVersion</code> (the <code>batch_id</code>) so a retried batch is skipped.</li>\n<li>When the function writes the batch to several tables, <code>persist()</code> it first so the source is not read twice, and <code>unpersist()</code> at the end.</li>\n<li>Separate streams have separate latency: if valid and invalid records need very different latencies, two independent streams on the same bronze table keep the slow path from delaying the fast one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"monitoring-a-stream\">Monitoring a stream</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>query.lastProgress</code> and <code>query.recentProgress</code> show input rows per second, processed rows per second, batch duration and state size. A processing rate lower than the input rate means a growing backlog.</li>\n<li>The Spark UI has a <strong>Structured Streaming</strong> tab with the same metrics over time.</li>\n<li><code>StreamingQueryListener</code> pushes progress events to your own monitoring.</li>\n<li>In declarative pipelines, <code>flow_progress</code> events in the event log carry the same information, including backlog.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Update mode and Delta</p>\n<p>A Delta sink does not accept <code>update</code> output mode. Upserts from a stream are done with <code>foreachBatch</code> and <code>MERGE</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Watermarks forget</p>\n<p>Deduplication or aggregation with a 1-hour watermark cannot catch a duplicate that arrives 10 days later.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">foreachBatch can run twice</p>\n<p>A failed batch is retried; non-idempotent appends inside <code>foreachBatch</code> create duplicates unless you use <code>txnAppId</code> and <code>txnVersion</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a table with <code>spark.range</code> and a timestamp column, then read it as a stream with <code>availableNow</code> into a second table. Append rows to the source and run again; only new rows are processed.</li>\n<li>Run a windowed count with a watermark in <code>complete</code> mode to a memory sink (<code>.format(\"memory\").queryName(\"counts\")</code>) on classic compute, or with <code>availableNow</code> into a Delta table in <code>append</code> mode on serverless.</li>\n<li>Write the <code>foreachBatch</code> upsert above and run it twice on the same input; check that the target has no duplicates.</li>\n<li>Print <code>query.lastProgress</code> after a run.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A2","P1"]},{"id":"cdc","title":"Change data capture and SCD","group":"Transformation","order":12,"summary":"Applying change feeds with MERGE, AUTO CDC for SCD type 1 and type 2 in declarative pipelines, out-of-order events, snapshots, and Delta Change Data Feed for incremental downstream processing.","html":"<h2 id=\"concepts\">Concepts</h2>\n<p>A <strong>change data capture (CDC)</strong> feed describes changes rather than current state. Each event has a key, an operation (insert, update, delete), a sequence value that orders events (a timestamp or log sequence number) and the column values. Real feeds bring problems you must handle: several events per key in one batch, events out of order, duplicates, and deletes.</p>\n<p><strong>Slowly changing dimensions (SCD)</strong> decide what to keep when an attribute changes:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Behaviour</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Never changes</td>\n<td>Date of birth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Overwrite; no history</td>\n<td>Correcting a typo in a name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>New row per version, with validity dates; full history</td>\n<td>Customer address, so each past sale shows the address valid at the time</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"applying-changes-with-merge\">Applying changes with MERGE</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">MERGE INTO silver.customers AS t\nUSING (\n  SELECT * FROM bronze.customer_changes\n  WHERE batch_date = current_date()\n  QUALIFY row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY change_ts DESC) = 1\n) AS s\nON t.customer_id = s.customer_id\nWHEN MATCHED AND s.op = 'DELETE' THEN DELETE\nWHEN MATCHED AND s.change_ts &gt; t.change_ts THEN UPDATE SET *\nWHEN NOT MATCHED AND s.op &lt;&gt; 'DELETE' THEN INSERT *;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the <strong>latest event per key</strong> before merging (otherwise the merge fails on multiple matches).</li>\n<li>Compare sequence values so an old event never overwrites a newer row.</li>\n<li>This is SCD type 1. SCD type 2 with <code>MERGE</code> needs two actions per changed key (close the current row, insert a new one), usually by merging a staged union of the changes; it is error-prone, which is why pipelines offer <code>AUTO CDC</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"auto-cdc-in-declarative-pipelines\">AUTO CDC in declarative pipelines</h2>\n<p><code>AUTO CDC</code> (formerly <code>APPLY CHANGES INTO</code>, <code>dlt.apply_changes</code>) applies a change feed to a streaming table and handles ordering, deletes and SCD type 2 for you.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE customers_history;\n\nCREATE FLOW customers_cdc AS AUTO CDC INTO customers_history\nFROM STREAM bronze_customer_changes\nKEYS (customer_id)\nAPPLY AS DELETE WHEN op = 'DELETE'\nSEQUENCE BY change_ts\nCOLUMNS * EXCEPT (op, change_ts)\nSTORED AS SCD TYPE 2\nTRACK HISTORY ON * EXCEPT (last_login_ts);\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from pyspark import pipelines as dp\nfrom pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\ndp.create_streaming_table(&quot;customers_current&quot;)\ndp.create_auto_cdc_flow(\n    target=&quot;customers_current&quot;,\n    source=&quot;bronze_customer_changes&quot;,\n    keys=[&quot;customer_id&quot;],\n    sequence_by=F.col(&quot;change_ts&quot;),\n    apply_as_deletes=F.expr(&quot;op = 'DELETE'&quot;),\n    except_column_list=[&quot;op&quot;, &quot;change_ts&quot;],\n    stored_as_scd_type=1,\n)\n</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Clause</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>KEYS</code></td>\n<td>Columns that identify a row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SEQUENCE BY</code></td>\n<td>Logical order of events; late events are applied in this order, not arrival order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>APPLY AS DELETE WHEN</code></td>\n<td>Which events are deletes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>APPLY AS TRUNCATE WHEN</code></td>\n<td>Events that empty the table (SCD type 1 only)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>COLUMNS</code> / <code>except_column_list</code></td>\n<td>Columns to write</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>STORED AS SCD TYPE 1</code> (default) or <code>2</code></td>\n<td>Overwrite, or keep history</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>TRACK HISTORY ON</code></td>\n<td>(Type 2) which column changes create a new version; changes to other columns update the current row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SCD type 1</strong> is the default.</li>\n<li><strong>SCD type 2</strong> adds <code>__START_AT</code> and <code>__END_AT</code> (from the sequence values); the current version has <code>__END_AT</code> NULL.</li>\n<li><strong>Out-of-order events</strong>: because of <code>SEQUENCE BY</code>, a late update older than a delete does not bring the row back; deletes are kept as tombstones for a while so late events can be resolved.</li>\n<li><strong>Snapshots instead of a feed</strong>: when the source only provides full extracts, <code>AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT</code> (Python <code>create_auto_cdc_from_snapshot_flow</code>) compares successive snapshots and derives the changes.</li>\n<li>A <strong>full refresh</strong> rebuilds the target from what the source still holds: SCD type 2 history built from events that are gone is lost.</li>\n<li>The target receives updates and deletes, so it is not append-only: downstream streams must read its change feed, not a plain stream.</li>\n<li>Lakeflow Connect database connectors use the same mechanism to keep their tables in sync with SQL Server, MySQL or PostgreSQL. If downstream consumers use Iceberg engines, make the target readable as Iceberg; the CDC logic does not change.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"delta-change-data-feed\">Delta Change Data Feed</h2>\n<p>Change Data Feed (CDF) records row-level changes of a Delta table so downstream jobs can process only what changed.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">ALTER TABLE silver.customers SET TBLPROPERTIES (delta.enableChangeDataFeed = true);\n\nSELECT customer_id, _change_type, _commit_version, _commit_timestamp\nFROM table_changes('silver.customers', 12)              -- from version 12 (or a timestamp)\nWHERE _change_type IN ('update_postimage', 'delete');\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">changes = (spark.readStream\n             .option(&quot;readChangeFeed&quot;, &quot;true&quot;)\n             .option(&quot;startingVersion&quot;, 12)\n             .table(&quot;main.silver.customers&quot;))\n</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><code>_change_type</code></th>\n<th>Row</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>insert</code></td>\n<td>New row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>update_preimage</code></td>\n<td>Values before an update</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>update_postimage</code></td>\n<td>Values after an update</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>delete</code></td>\n<td>Deleted row</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>CDF starts recording when you enable it; it is not retroactive.</li>\n<li>Updates, deletes and merges write small change files (<code>_change_data</code>); inserts are read from the data files. The extra storage is cleaned up by <code>VACUUM</code> according to the file retention.</li>\n<li>Typical uses: propagate silver changes to gold incrementally, feed a second pipeline's <code>AUTO CDC</code>, replicate changes to another system, audit row changes.</li>\n<li>CDF is not time travel: time travel returns a whole old snapshot; CDF returns the changes between versions.</li>\n<li>For privacy, remember that change files can still hold deleted values until they are vacuumed.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"choosing\">Choosing</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Change feed with inserts, updates and deletes, keep only current values</td>\n<td><code>AUTO CDC ... STORED AS SCD TYPE 1</code> (or <code>MERGE</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep full history of attribute changes with validity periods</td>\n<td><code>AUTO CDC ... STORED AS SCD TYPE 2</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source only gives full daily snapshots</td>\n<td><code>AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Downstream job must react only to updates and deletes of a table</td>\n<td>Read its Change Data Feed and filter on <code>_change_type</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Events arrive out of order</td>\n<td><code>SEQUENCE BY</code> on the event's logical time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replicate a database's changes into Databricks</td>\n<td>Lakeflow Connect database connector</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Default SCD type</p>\n<p><code>AUTO CDC</code> stores SCD type 1 unless you say <code>STORED AS SCD TYPE 2</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Full refresh and history</p>\n<p>A full refresh of an SCD type 2 target loses the history that the source no longer holds.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">skipChangeCommits is not CDC</p>\n<p>It ignores updates and deletes. To react to them, read the Change Data Feed.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create <code>customer_changes</code> with columns <code>customer_id, name, city, op, change_ts</code> and a few events, including two updates for the same customer and a delete.</li>\n<li>Apply them to a target with the <code>MERGE</code> above; check the result.</li>\n<li>In a pipeline, apply the same feed with <code>AUTO CDC</code> as SCD type 2 and look at <code>__START_AT</code> and <code>__END_AT</code>.</li>\n<li>Enable CDF on the <code>MERGE</code> target, run another merge, and query <code>table_changes</code>.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A3","P1","P2","P5"]},{"id":"modeling","title":"Medallion architecture and data modeling","group":"Transformation","order":13,"summary":"Bronze, silver and gold layers, cleaning bronze into silver, facts, dimensions and star schemas, gold objects for BI, and Unity Catalog metric views.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-medallion-architecture\">The medallion architecture</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Layer</th>\n<th>Contains</th>\n<th>Typical operations</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Landing</td>\n<td>Files as delivered, in a volume</td>\n<td>Nothing; this is the drop zone</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bronze</strong></td>\n<td>Raw data as received, append-only, plus ingestion metadata (load time, source file)</td>\n<td>Auto Loader, <code>COPY INTO</code>, connectors; minimal changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Silver</strong></td>\n<td>Clean, typed, deduplicated, validated and conformed data at entity level</td>\n<td>Casting, standardising, deduplication, joins, <code>MERGE</code>, CDC, expectations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Gold</strong></td>\n<td>Business-ready data: aggregates, dimensional models, features</td>\n<td>Aggregations, star schemas, materialized views, metric views</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Why keep bronze raw: it is the <strong>replay source</strong>. Sources such as Kafka or Event Hubs keep data for days; regulators may require the original records for years. Landing everything in bronze Delta tables on cheap object storage lets you rebuild silver and gold whenever the logic changes.</p>\n<p>Layers usually map to separate schemas or catalogs, with permissions per layer (engineers write bronze and silver; analysts read gold).</p>\n<h2 id=\"from-bronze-to-silver\">From bronze to silver</h2>\n<p>A silver table is where data becomes trustworthy. A typical checklist:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Types</strong>: <code>try_cast</code> and <code>try_to_timestamp</code>; count the rows that failed.</li>\n<li><strong>Standardise</strong>: trim, case, codes, units, time zones.</li>\n<li><strong>NULLs</strong>: defaults with <code>coalesce</code> where the business allows, rejection where it does not.</li>\n<li><strong>Deduplicate</strong>: keep the latest record per key.</li>\n<li><strong>Validate</strong>: expectations, <code>CHECK</code> constraints, or a quarantine table for rejects.</li>\n<li><strong>Flatten and conform</strong>: explode nested arrays, map source codes to common ones, join reference data.</li>\n<li><strong>Write</strong>: <code>MERGE</code> for entity tables (customers, products), append for events (orders, clicks).</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"dimensional-modelling\">Dimensional modelling</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Fact table</th>\n<th>Dimension table</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Contains</td>\n<td>Events and measures (amounts, quantities) plus foreign keys to dimensions</td>\n<td>Descriptive attributes (who, what, where, when)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Size</td>\n<td>Many rows, keeps growing</td>\n<td>Fewer rows, changes slowly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Answers</td>\n<td>\"How much?\", \"how many?\"</td>\n<td>\"Who?\", \"what?\", \"when?\", \"where?\"</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>History</td>\n<td>Append</td>\n<td>SCD type 1 or type 2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>star schema</strong> has one fact table in the middle joined directly to its dimensions; it is the standard gold model for BI. A snowflake schema normalises dimensions further at the cost of more joins.</li>\n<li>Decide the <strong>grain</strong> of each fact table first (for example, one row per order line).</li>\n<li>Use <strong>surrogate keys</strong> (<code>BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY</code>) for dimensions, especially SCD type 2, where the natural key repeats across versions.</li>\n<li>A <strong>date dimension</strong> stores precomputed attributes (fiscal quarter, weekday, holiday flag) so queries just join and group.</li>\n<li>Declare primary and foreign keys: they are informational in Unity Catalog but help BI tools and the optimizer.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE TABLE gold.dim_customer (\n  customer_sk BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY,\n  customer_id STRING NOT NULL,\n  name STRING, city STRING,\n  valid_from TIMESTAMP, valid_to TIMESTAMP, is_current BOOLEAN,\n  CONSTRAINT pk_dim_customer PRIMARY KEY (customer_sk)\n);\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"gold-objects\">Gold objects</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>Stores data</th>\n<th>Freshness</th>\n<th>Choose it when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>View</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Always current; recomputed on every query</td>\n<td>Light logic, or a security layer over tables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Materialized view</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Refreshed on schedule, on source update or incrementally</td>\n<td>Expensive aggregates or joins read often by BI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Streaming table</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Updated incrementally as data arrives</td>\n<td>Append-only or CDC-driven gold tables with low latency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Table</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Whatever your job writes</td>\n<td>Full control of the writes (<code>MERGE</code>, overwrite)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A Unity Catalog view resolves its source tables with the <strong>owner's</strong> privileges: users only need <code>SELECT</code> on the view (plus <code>USE CATALOG</code> and <code>USE SCHEMA</code>), not on the tables behind it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"metric-views\">Metric views</h2>\n<p>A <strong>metric view</strong> defines business metrics once, in Unity Catalog, as measures and dimensions over a source table or view. Every tool (SQL, dashboards, Genie) then computes \"revenue\" the same way, at whatever grain the query asks for.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW main.gold.sales_metrics\nWITH METRICS\nLANGUAGE YAML\nAS $$\nversion: 1.1\nsource: main.gold.fact_sales\ndimensions:\n  - name: region\n    expr: region\n  - name: order_month\n    expr: DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', order_date)\nmeasures:\n  - name: revenue\n    expr: SUM(amount)\n  - name: orders\n    expr: COUNT(DISTINCT order_id)\n  - name: revenue_per_order\n    expr: SUM(amount) / COUNT(DISTINCT order_id)\n$$;\n\nSELECT region, MEASURE(revenue), MEASURE(revenue_per_order)\nFROM main.gold.sales_metrics\nGROUP BY region;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Measures are aggregated at query time, so ratios such as revenue per order stay correct at any grain (a pre-aggregated table at month level could not give them by week).</li>\n<li>Metric views are governed like other views (<code>SELECT</code>, comments, lineage).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Materialized view or metric view?</strong> A materialized view <strong>stores</strong> precomputed results at a fixed grain for speed. A metric view <strong>stores definitions</strong> and computes them on demand at any grain, for consistency. They combine well: a metric view whose source is a materialized view.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Bronze is not optional</p>\n<p>\"Keep the events exactly as emitted for years, cheaply\" points to bronze Delta tables, not to longer retention in a message bus.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Facts hold measures</p>\n<p>Amounts and quantities with foreign keys are facts; descriptive attributes are dimensions.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">SCD type 2 for point-in-time truth</p>\n<p>\"Each past sale must show the address valid at that time\" requires SCD type 2.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<p>With <code>samples.tpch</code>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Build <code>silver.orders</code> from <code>samples.tpch.orders</code> with typed, renamed columns and an <code>ingest_ts</code> column.</li>\n<li>Build <code>gold.dim_customer</code> and <code>gold.fact_orders</code> (grain: one row per order) and a date dimension with <code>sequence</code> and <code>explode</code>.</li>\n<li>Create a materialized view of revenue per nation and month.</li>\n<li>Create the metric view above on your fact table and query it with <code>MEASURE()</code> by region and by month.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A3","P9"]},{"id":"jobs","title":"Lakeflow Jobs","group":"Orchestration and DevOps","order":14,"summary":"Tasks and the task graph, compute per task, parameters and task values, retries and timeouts, Run if, If/else and For each, triggers, concurrency, repair runs, and automating jobs with the API, CLI and SDK.","html":"<h2 id=\"jobs-and-tasks\">Jobs and tasks</h2>\n<p>A job is a graph of <strong>tasks</strong> plus a <strong>trigger</strong>, compute, parameters, notifications and permissions. Tasks with no dependency between them run in parallel; <code>depends_on</code> builds the graph (up to 1,000 tasks).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task type</th>\n<th>Runs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Notebook</td>\n<td>A notebook (Python, SQL, Scala, R) from the workspace or a Git repository</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Python script, Python wheel, JAR</td>\n<td>Code files and packages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL</td>\n<td>A query, a SQL file or an alert, on a SQL warehouse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dashboard</td>\n<td>Refreshes a dashboard (and sends subscriptions) after the data is ready</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pipeline</td>\n<td>A declarative pipeline update (optionally a full refresh)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>dbt</td>\n<td>A dbt project</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run Job</td>\n<td>Another job, to reuse and modularise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If/else condition</td>\n<td>Chooses a branch from a comparison</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>For each</td>\n<td>Runs a nested task once per item of a list</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Compute per task</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Serverless</td>\n<td>Recommended default. Python script, wheel and dbt tasks declare an <code>environment_key</code> with their dependencies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job cluster</td>\n<td>Classic cluster created for the run. Several tasks share it through the same <code>job_cluster_key</code>; libraries are then declared on each task</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Existing all-purpose cluster</td>\n<td>Possible, but expensive and not isolated; avoid in production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL warehouse</td>\n<td>SQL and dashboard tasks (serverless or pro, not classic). A notebook task can use a warehouse only if the notebook is entirely SQL</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"parameters-values-and-secrets\">Parameters, values and secrets</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Job parameters</strong> are defined at job level, pushed to every task, and <strong>override</strong> task parameters with the same key. A run can be started with different values.</li>\n<li><strong>Task parameters</strong> are per task: notebook parameters (read with <code>dbutils.widgets.get(\"name\")</code>) or command-line arguments for scripts.</li>\n<li><strong>Dynamic value references</strong> insert run context: <code>{{job.id}}</code>, <code>{{job.run_id}}</code>, <code>{{job.start_time.iso_date}}</code>, <code>{{job.parameters.env}}</code>, <code>{{task.name}}</code>, <code>{{tasks.ingest.values.rows}}</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Task values</strong> pass small results between tasks of the same run:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># in task &quot;ingest&quot;\ndbutils.jobs.taskValues.set(key=&quot;rows&quot;, value=new_rows)\n# in a later task\nrows = dbutils.jobs.taskValues.get(taskKey=&quot;ingest&quot;, key=&quot;rows&quot;, default=0, debugValue=0)\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>debugValue</code> is what <code>get</code> returns when you run the notebook interactively, outside a job; without it the call fails.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Secrets</strong>: store with <code>databricks secrets put-secret SCOPE KEY</code>, read with <code>dbutils.secrets.get(\"SCOPE\", \"KEY\")</code>. Values are redacted in outputs.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"control-flow\">Control flow</h2>\n<p><strong>Retries and timeouts</strong> (per task):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Retries (<code>max_retries</code>, <code>min_retry_interval_millis</code>)</td>\n<td>Rerun the task after a failure; for transient errors. Default: no retries for triggered jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>retry_on_timeout</code></td>\n<td>Also retry when the attempt timed out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timeout (<code>timeout_seconds</code>)</td>\n<td>Kills an attempt that runs too long; status <em>Timed Out</em>. Applies to each attempt</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duration warning (health rule)</td>\n<td>Only notifies; the run continues</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Run if</strong> decides whether a task runs, based on the outcome of its dependencies:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Condition</th>\n<th>Runs when</th>\n<th>Typical use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ALL_SUCCESS</code> (default)</td>\n<td>All dependencies succeeded</td>\n<td>Normal flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ALL_DONE</code></td>\n<td>All dependencies finished, whatever the result</td>\n<td>Cleanup that must always run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED</code></td>\n<td>At least one dependency failed</td>\n<td>Alerting, compensation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>NONE_FAILED</code></td>\n<td>None failed (some may be skipped or excluded)</td>\n<td>Continue after optional branches</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>AT_LEAST_ONE_SUCCESS</code></td>\n<td>At least one succeeded</td>\n<td>Fan-in of alternative paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ALL_FAILED</code></td>\n<td>All failed</td>\n<td>Fallback</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A task whose Run if condition is not met ends as <strong>Excluded</strong>, which is normal in a healthy run.</p>\n<p><strong>Run if reacts to success or failure, not to data.</strong> To skip downstream work when there is nothing new, set a task value with the row count and add an <strong>If/else condition</strong> task:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Left</th>\n<th>Operator</th>\n<th>Right</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>{{tasks.ingest.values.rows}}</code></td>\n<td><code>&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In If/else, <code>==</code> and <code>!=</code> compare values <strong>as strings</strong> (<code>0</code> and <code>0.0</code> are different), while <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&gt;=</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code> compare numbers.</p>\n<p><strong>For each</strong> runs a nested task once per element of a JSON array (a literal, a job parameter or a task value). The nested task reads the current item as <code>{{input}}</code>; each iteration is tracked and retried on its own, with a concurrency of up to 100. Use it instead of a loop inside one notebook, which hides failures and cannot retry single items.</p>\n<p>To make a task fail on purpose (a validation that does not pass), <strong>raise an exception</strong>. <code>dbutils.notebook.exit(\"...\")</code> ends the notebook <strong>successfully</strong> and returns the text.</p>\n<h2 id=\"triggers\">Triggers</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Trigger</th>\n<th>Starts the job</th>\n<th>Details</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Scheduled</td>\n<td>On a cron schedule</td>\n<td>Quartz syntax with <strong>seconds first</strong> and a time zone: <code>0 30 6 * * ?</code> = 06:30 every day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>File arrival</td>\n<td>When <strong>new</strong> files arrive in a volume or external location</td>\n<td>Checked about every minute; files rewritten with the same name do not count; options <em>minimum time between triggers</em> and <em>wait after last change</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Table update</td>\n<td>When monitored tables (up to 10, any or all) are updated</td>\n<td>The job's <em>run as</em> identity needs <code>SELECT</code> on them, otherwise the job never starts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Continuous</td>\n<td>Keeps exactly one run active; starts a new one when the last ends</td>\n<td>No task dependencies or retry policies; failures restart with exponential backoff</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manual / API</td>\n<td>Run now, CLI, REST API, SDK, other jobs</td>\n<td>—</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Time-based or data-driven?</strong> A schedule fits a fixed business deadline with predictable data. File arrival fits files that land at unpredictable times. Table update fits a job that depends on a table another team or pipeline refreshes. Continuous fits data that never stops and needs low latency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"concurrency-identity-and-permissions\">Concurrency, identity and permissions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>max_concurrent_runs</code> (default 1). When a new run would exceed it, it is <strong>queued</strong> (if queueing is on, for up to 48 hours) or <strong>Skipped</strong>.</li>\n<li>A job with a <strong>pipeline task</strong> can only have one active run, because a pipeline runs one update at a time.</li>\n<li><strong>Run as</strong> a service principal in production, so runs do not depend on a person's account.</li>\n<li>Job permissions: <em>Can View</em>, <em>Can Manage Run</em> (start, cancel, repair), <em>Is Owner</em>, <em>Can Manage</em> (edit). Grant the least level each person needs.</li>\n<li>Notifications (email, Slack, Teams, webhooks) on start, success, failure, duration warning and streaming backlog.</li>\n<li>A notebook task's total output is limited (20 MB, 8 MB per cell); a run that exceeds it fails even though its writes succeeded. Do not <code>display</code> large DataFrames in production notebooks.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"repair-runs\">Repair runs</h2>\n<p>When a multi-task run fails, <strong>Repair run</strong> reruns only the failed tasks and the tasks that depend on them, reusing the successful ones. You can change task settings and <strong>override parameters</strong> for the repair.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A repaired task starts again from the beginning, so a task that appends can create duplicates: write with <code>MERGE</code> or overwrite instead.</li>\n<li>For a single-task job, repairing and rerunning are the same thing.</li>\n<li>CLI: <code>databricks jobs repair-run RUN_ID --rerun-all-failed-tasks</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"automating-jobs\">Automating jobs</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tool</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>UI</td>\n<td>Jobs &amp; Pipelines → Create job</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>REST API (Jobs 2.2)</td>\n<td><code>POST /api/2.2/jobs/run-now</code> with <code>job_id</code> and <code>job_parameters</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CLI</td>\n<td><code>databricks jobs create --json @job.json</code>, <code>databricks jobs run-now 123</code>, <code>databricks jobs get-run 456</code>, <code>databricks jobs list-runs --job-id 123 --completed-only --start-time-from &lt;epoch ms&gt;</code>, <code>databricks jobs cancel-run 456</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Python SDK</td>\n<td><code>WorkspaceClient().jobs.run_now(job_id=123).result()</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bundles</td>\n<td>Jobs defined as YAML and deployed per environment (see <em>CI/CD</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Run if is about outcomes</p>\n<p>\"Skip the transform when no files arrived\" is an If/else on a task value, not a Run if condition, and not a deliberate failure.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Quartz has seconds</p>\n<p><code>30 6 * * *</code> is Unix cron. Databricks uses Quartz: <code>0 30 6 * * ?</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Timeout versus warning</p>\n<p>A timeout stops the attempt; a duration warning only notifies.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Upstream failed</p>\n<p>Walk up the graph to the first task that failed; the task marked <em>Upstream failed</em> did not run.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a job with three notebook tasks: <code>ingest</code> sets a task value <code>rows</code>, an If/else task checks <code>{{tasks.ingest.values.rows}} &gt; 0</code>, and <code>transform</code> runs on the true branch. Add a <code>cleanup</code> task with Run if <code>ALL_DONE</code>.</li>\n<li>Add a job parameter <code>env</code> and read it in a notebook with <code>dbutils.widgets.get(\"env\")</code>.</li>\n<li>Make <code>transform</code> raise an exception, run the job, fix the code, and use <strong>Repair run</strong>.</li>\n<li>Add a For each task over <code>[\"PT\", \"ES\", \"FR\"]</code> that passes <code>{{input}}</code> to a notebook parameter.</li>\n<li>Add a file arrival trigger on a volume folder and drop a file into it.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A4","A6","P1","P4","P8"]},{"id":"cicd","title":"CI/CD: Git folders, bundles and the CLI","group":"Orchestration and DevOps","order":15,"summary":"Git folders, Declarative Automation Bundles (databricks.yml, targets, variables, modes, deploy and bind), structuring Python projects, the Databricks CLI and authentication, and a complete CI/CD flow.","html":"<h2 id=\"git-folders\">Git folders</h2>\n<p>Git folders (formerly Repos) clone a remote repository into the workspace.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Link your Git account in <strong>Settings → Linked accounts</strong>, then <strong>Create → Git folder</strong> with the HTTPS URL.</li>\n<li>In the Git dialog you can create and switch <strong>branches</strong>, <strong>pull</strong>, <strong>commit and push</strong>, <strong>merge</strong>, <strong>rebase</strong>, <strong>reset</strong> and resolve conflicts. <strong>Pull requests are opened in the Git provider</strong> (GitHub, Azure DevOps, GitLab, Bitbucket), not in Databricks.</li>\n<li>Branch, pull and push need <strong>CAN MANAGE</strong> on the Git folder; CAN EDIT only lets you edit files.</li>\n<li>A Git folder has one checked-out branch for everyone who opens it. Give <strong>each person their own Git folder</strong> so that switching branches does not change the files under a colleague.</li>\n<li>Switching branches removes assets that do not exist on the target branch; pulling clears notebook state. Rebase and reset rewrite history (force push); merge does not.</li>\n<li><strong>Production</strong> code is never edited by hand. Either let automation update a production Git folder (for example <code>databricks repos update</code> from CI, as a service principal), let job tasks run straight from a remote Git reference (each run takes a snapshot of that commit), or, best, deploy with bundles.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"declarative-automation-bundles\">Declarative Automation Bundles</h2>\n<p>A bundle (formerly Databricks Asset Bundle) describes, in YAML next to your code, the resources to deploy (jobs, pipelines, dashboards, schemas, volumes, apps…) and how they differ per environment. The same code is promoted from dev to test to prod by changing only the <strong>target</strong>.</p>\n<pre><code>orders_project/\n├── databricks.yml            # the only root configuration file\n├── resources/\n│   ├── orders_job.yml\n│   └── orders_pipeline.yml\n├── src/orders/               # Python package (business logic)\n├── notebooks/                # thin notebooks that call the package\n├── tests/                    # pytest unit tests\n└── pyproject.toml\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\"># databricks.yml\nbundle:\n  name: orders\n\ninclude:\n  - resources/*.yml\n\nvariables:\n  catalog:\n    description: Target catalog\n    default: dev_catalog\n  warehouse_id:\n    lookup:\n      warehouse: &quot;Shared SQL Warehouse&quot;     # resolves the ID from the name\n\nartifacts:\n  orders_wheel:\n    type: whl\n    build: uv build --wheel\n\ntargets:\n  dev:\n    mode: development\n    default: true\n    workspace:\n      host: https://adb-1111.11.azuredatabricks.net\n  prod:\n    mode: production\n    git:\n      branch: main\n    workspace:\n      host: https://adb-2222.22.azuredatabricks.net\n      root_path: /Workspace/Shared/.bundle/${bundle.name}/${bundle.target}\n    run_as:\n      service_principal_name: 7c1e...-app-id\n    variables:\n      catalog: prod_catalog\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\"># resources/orders_job.yml\nresources:\n  jobs:\n    orders_job:\n      name: orders-${bundle.target}\n      parameters:\n        - name: catalog\n          default: ${var.catalog}\n      tasks:\n        - task_key: ingest\n          notebook_task:\n            notebook_path: ../notebooks/ingest.ipynb     # relative to THIS file\n        - task_key: transform\n          depends_on: [{task_key: ingest}]\n          python_wheel_task:\n            package_name: orders\n            entry_point: transform\n          environment_key: default\n      environments:\n        - environment_key: default\n          spec:\n            environment_version: &quot;4&quot;\n            dependencies:\n              - ../dist/*.whl\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Substitutions</strong>: <code>${bundle.name}</code>, <code>${bundle.target}</code>, <code>${workspace.current_user.userName}</code>, <code>${var.catalog}</code>, and <code>${resources.jobs.orders_job.id}</code> (by the resource <strong>key</strong> in YAML, not by its display name).</p>\n<p><strong>Variables</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Declared under <code>variables</code> with an optional <code>default</code>; overridden per target; types can be simple, <code>complex</code> (maps and lists) or <code>lookup</code> (resolve an ID from a name).</li>\n<li>Precedence, highest first: <code>--var=\"catalog=x\"</code> on the CLI, environment variable <code>BUNDLE_VAR_catalog</code>, the <code>variable-overrides.json</code> file, the target's <code>variables</code>, the <code>default</code>.</li>\n<li>Variables are <strong>resolved at deploy time</strong>. <code>bundle run --var</code> does not change a deployed job; values that change per run belong in <strong>job parameters</strong> (<code>databricks bundle run orders_job --params catalog=other</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>Secrets never go in YAML</strong> (it is in Git). In CI, pass them with <code>BUNDLE_VAR_...</code> from the CI secret store; at runtime, read them from secret scopes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Targets and modes</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A target's settings are <strong>merged</strong> with the top-level ones; put in the target only what changes.</li>\n<li>Without <code>-t</code>, the CLI uses the target marked <code>default: true</code>. Automation must always pass <code>-t</code>.</li>\n<li><code>mode: development</code>: resource names prefixed with <code>[dev your.name]</code>, schedules and triggers <strong>paused</strong>, concurrent runs allowed, pipelines in development mode. Safe for personal work.</li>\n<li><code>mode: production</code>: checks that the current Git branch matches <code>git.branch</code>, expects a <code>run_as</code> identity and a shared <code>root_path</code>. <code>--force</code> bypasses the branch check and should not be used.</li>\n<li>Paths in YAML are relative to the file in which they are written.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"the-bundle-lifecycle\">The bundle lifecycle</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Command</th>\n<th>Does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle init</code></td>\n<td>Creates a project from a template (Python, SQL, pipelines, dbt)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle validate -t prod</code></td>\n<td>Checks the configuration; <code>--output json</code> shows it fully resolved</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle plan -t prod</code></td>\n<td>Shows what would be created, changed or deleted, changing nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle deploy -t prod</code></td>\n<td>Builds artifacts, uploads files and creates or updates the resources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle run -t prod orders_job</code></td>\n<td>Runs a deployed job or pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle summary -t prod</code></td>\n<td>Lists what is deployed and where</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle destroy -t test --auto-approve</code></td>\n<td>Deletes the deployed resources (CI needs <code>--auto-approve</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle generate job --existing-job-id 123</code></td>\n<td>Writes YAML for a job created in the UI</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>databricks bundle deployment bind orders_job 123</code></td>\n<td>Links a resource key to an existing job, so deploy updates it instead of creating a duplicate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Adopting a UI job: <code>generate</code> (optionally with <code>--bind</code>) <strong>and</strong> bind. Deploying generated YAML without binding creates a <strong>second job</strong>.</li>\n<li>Removing a resource from the YAML <strong>deletes it</strong> on the next deploy, unless <code>lifecycle.prevent_destroy: true</code>.</li>\n<li>Jobs deployed by a bundle are marked as managed by it; edit them through the bundle. For an emergency, <em>Disconnect from source</em> in the UI, knowing the next deploy will overwrite your change.</li>\n<li><code>--force-lock</code> only clears a deployment lock left by an interrupted deploy.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"structuring-a-python-project\">Structuring a Python project</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Put business logic in a <strong>package</strong> under <code>src/</code> with a <code>pyproject.toml</code>; build it into a <strong>wheel</strong>; keep notebooks thin (parameters, calls to the package, display).</li>\n<li>Prefer <strong>imports</strong> of modules over chains of <code>%run</code>: imported functions can be unit-tested and versioned; workspace files in a Git folder can be imported directly.</li>\n<li>Make transformations functions that take and return DataFrames, so they can be tested and chained with <code>DataFrame.transform</code> (see <em>Testing</em>).</li>\n<li>Pin dependencies. Jobs install the wheel as a task library (classic) or as an environment dependency (serverless). Clusters without internet access install wheels from a volume or a private index.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-databricks-cli\">The Databricks CLI</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the current CLI (version 0.218 or later for bundles). The old <code>databricks-cli</code> installed with pip has different commands. In GitHub Actions, install it with <code>databricks/setup-cli</code>.</li>\n<li>People log in with <code>databricks auth login --host &lt;workspace-url&gt;</code> (OAuth user-to-machine); profiles are stored in <code>~/.databrickscfg</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>CI/CD authenticates as a service principal</strong>, with OAuth machine-to-machine (<code>DATABRICKS_HOST</code>, <code>DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID</code>, <code>DATABRICKS_CLIENT_SECRET</code>) or, better, workload identity federation (OIDC from the CI system, no secret stored). Never with a person's token.</li>\n<li>Useful groups: <code>bundle</code>, <code>jobs</code>, <code>pipelines</code>, <code>repos</code>, <code>workspace</code>, <code>fs</code>, <code>secrets</code>, <code>catalogs</code>, <code>api</code> (raw REST). <code>-o json</code> for machine-readable output.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"a-complete-cicd-flow\">A complete CI/CD flow</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Develop on a feature branch in your own Git folder or in an IDE (Databricks Connect, VS Code extension); <code>bundle deploy -t dev</code> deploys a personal, prefixed copy.</li>\n<li>Open a pull request in the Git provider.</li>\n<li>CI on the pull request: <code>bundle validate -t test</code>, unit tests with <code>pytest</code>, <code>bundle deploy -t test</code>, <code>bundle run -t test integration_tests</code>.</li>\n<li>After the merge to <code>main</code>, CD runs <code>bundle deploy -t prod</code> as the production service principal.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code class=\"language-yaml\"># .github/workflows/deploy.yml (extract)\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\npermissions:\n  id-token: write          # for OIDC federation\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    env:\n      DATABRICKS_HOST: ${{ vars.PROD_HOST }}\n      DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID: ${{ vars.SP_CLIENT_ID }}\n      DATABRICKS_AUTH_TYPE: github-oidc\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: databricks/setup-cli@main\n      - run: databricks bundle validate -t prod\n      - run: databricks bundle deploy -t prod\n</code></pre>\n<h2 id=\"developer-tools\">Developer tools</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Notebooks: <strong>one language per cell</strong> (switch with <code>%python</code>, <code>%sql</code>, <code>%scala</code>, <code>%r</code>, <code>%md</code>); several languages per notebook. To use a Python value in SQL, use <code>spark.sql(...)</code> with parameters, a temporary view, or widgets.</li>\n<li>The notebook <strong>debugger</strong> with the <strong>Variable Explorer</strong> inspects values and types at breakpoints, without adding prints.</li>\n<li><strong>Databricks Connect</strong> runs code written in a local IDE (VS Code, PyCharm, Jupyter…) on remote Databricks compute, next to the data.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Variables resolve at deploy</p>\n<p>Changing a bundle variable on <code>bundle run</code> does nothing to the deployed job. Use job parameters for per-run values.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Generate without bind duplicates</p>\n<p>Adopting an existing job needs <code>bind</code>; otherwise the deploy creates a copy.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Pull requests live in the Git provider</p>\n<p>Databricks Git folders commit and push; the pull request is opened in GitHub, Azure DevOps or similar.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Always pass the target</p>\n<p><code>bundle deploy</code> without <code>-t</code> goes to the default target, usually dev.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Install the CLI locally and run <code>databricks auth login --host &lt;your workspace&gt;</code>.</li>\n<li><code>databricks bundle init default-python</code>, open <code>databricks.yml</code>, add a <code>catalog</code> variable and use it in the job's parameters.</li>\n<li><code>databricks bundle validate --output json</code>, <code>databricks bundle deploy</code>, <code>databricks bundle run &lt;job key&gt;</code>; find the <code>[dev ...]</code> prefixed job in the UI and notice the paused schedule.</li>\n<li>Create a job in the UI, then adopt it with <code>bundle generate job --existing-job-id &lt;id&gt; --bind</code> and deploy.</li>\n<li><code>databricks bundle destroy</code>.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A5","P1","P8"]},{"id":"testing","title":"Testing and code quality","group":"Orchestration and DevOps","order":16,"summary":"Writing testable transformations, chaining them with DataFrame.transform, unit tests with pytest and pyspark.testing assertions, integration tests with bundles, and how tests differ from data quality checks.","html":"<h2 id=\"two-kinds-of-checks\">Two kinds of checks</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Tests</th>\n<th>Data quality checks</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td>Does my <strong>code</strong> do the right thing?</td>\n<td>Is today's <strong>data</strong> acceptable?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>When</td>\n<td>Before deployment (CI)</td>\n<td>Every run, in production</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tools</td>\n<td><code>pytest</code>, <code>pyspark.testing</code>, integration jobs</td>\n<td>Expectations, <code>CHECK</code> constraints, quarantine tables, monitoring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Input</td>\n<td>Small, hand-made datasets with known answers</td>\n<td>Real data</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>You need both: a correct transformation still fails on corrupt input, and good data does not fix a bug.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-testable-code\">Writing testable code</h2>\n<p>Keep <strong>I/O at the edges</strong> and the logic in <strong>pure functions</strong> that take DataFrames (and parameters) and return a DataFrame. Reading tables and writing results happens in a thin entry point (a notebook or a <code>main</code> function).</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># src/orders/transform.py\nfrom pyspark.sql import DataFrame, functions as F\n\ndef clean_orders(df: DataFrame) -&gt; DataFrame:\n    return (df.filter(F.col(&quot;order_id&quot;).isNotNull())\n              .withColumn(&quot;amount&quot;, F.coalesce(&quot;amount&quot;, F.lit(0.0)))\n              .dropDuplicates([&quot;order_id&quot;]))\n\ndef add_vat(df: DataFrame, rate: float = 0.23) -&gt; DataFrame:\n    return df.withColumn(&quot;amount_with_vat&quot;, F.round(F.col(&quot;amount&quot;) * (1 + rate), 2))\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># notebooks/run_orders (thin entry point)\nfrom orders.transform import clean_orders, add_vat\nresult = (spark.read.table(&quot;main.bronze.orders&quot;)\n            .transform(clean_orders)\n            .transform(add_vat, rate=0.23))       # extra arguments are passed to the function\nresult.write.mode(&quot;overwrite&quot;).saveAsTable(&quot;main.silver.orders&quot;)\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>DataFrame.transform(f, *args, **kwargs)</code> chains functions in a readable pipeline, and each function can be tested on its own. Package the functions (see <em>CI/CD</em>) instead of sharing them through <code>%run</code>, which cannot be imported by a test runner.</p>\n<h2 id=\"unit-tests-with-pytest\">Unit tests with pytest</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># tests/conftest.py\nimport pytest\nfrom pyspark.sql import SparkSession\n\n@pytest.fixture(scope=&quot;session&quot;)\ndef spark():\n    # local Spark for pure PySpark logic; use DatabricksSession (Databricks Connect)\n    # when the code needs Databricks features such as Unity Catalog tables\n    return SparkSession.builder.master(&quot;local[2]&quot;).getOrCreate()\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\"># tests/test_transform.py\nfrom pyspark.testing import assertDataFrameEqual, assertSchemaEqual\nfrom orders.transform import clean_orders, add_vat\n\ndef test_clean_orders_removes_null_ids_and_duplicates(spark):\n    raw = spark.createDataFrame(\n        [(1, 10.0), (1, 10.0), (None, 5.0), (2, None)], &quot;order_id INT, amount DOUBLE&quot;)\n    expected = spark.createDataFrame([(1, 10.0), (2, 0.0)], &quot;order_id INT, amount DOUBLE&quot;)\n    assertDataFrameEqual(clean_orders(raw), expected)\n\ndef test_add_vat_schema(spark):\n    df = spark.createDataFrame([(1, 100.0)], &quot;order_id INT, amount DOUBLE&quot;)\n    out = add_vat(df)\n    assertSchemaEqual(out.schema, spark.createDataFrame(\n        [], &quot;order_id INT, amount DOUBLE, amount_with_vat DOUBLE&quot;).schema)\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong><code>assertDataFrameEqual(actual, expected)</code></strong> compares schemas and rows and prints a readable diff. Row order is ignored by default (<code>checkRowOrder=False</code>); <code>rtol</code> and <code>atol</code> allow tolerance on floating-point values. It also accepts lists of <code>Row</code>s.</li>\n<li><strong><code>assertSchemaEqual(actual, expected)</code></strong> compares column names, types and nullability.</li>\n<li>Build tiny input DataFrames inside the test with <code>spark.createDataFrame</code> and an explicit schema. Cover the edge cases: NULLs, empty input, duplicates, out-of-range values, unexpected strings.</li>\n<li>Run tests locally, in CI on every pull request, or on Databricks with Databricks Connect. A notebook can also call <code>pytest.main([...])</code> to run the suite on a cluster.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"integration-tests\">Integration tests</h2>\n<p>Unit tests check functions; integration tests check the <strong>deployed</strong> job or pipeline against real services:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>A <code>test</code> target in the bundle points at a test catalog (<code>${var.catalog}</code>) with small, representative data (or shallow clones of production tables).</li>\n<li>CI runs <code>databricks bundle deploy -t test</code> and then <code>databricks bundle run -t test orders_job</code>.</li>\n<li>A final task asserts on the outputs (row counts, keys, totals) and <strong>raises an exception</strong> when a check fails, so the run, and the CI step, fail.</li>\n<li>Optionally <code>bundle destroy -t test --auto-approve</code> cleans up.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Expectations are not unit tests</p>\n<p>Expectations validate production data at runtime. Testing transformation logic before deployment uses <code>pytest</code> and <code>pyspark.testing</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Order of rows</p>\n<p><code>assertDataFrameEqual</code> ignores row order by default; asking for an <code>orderBy</code> before comparing is unnecessary.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Pure functions</p>\n<p>A function that reads and writes tables cannot be unit-tested in isolation. Move the reads and writes out, and pass DataFrames in.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create a folder with <code>src/orders/transform.py</code> and <code>tests/test_transform.py</code> as above, install <code>pyspark</code> and <code>pytest</code> locally, and run <code>pytest</code>.</li>\n<li>Break <code>clean_orders</code> on purpose (remove the <code>dropDuplicates</code>) and read the diff printed by <code>assertDataFrameEqual</code>.</li>\n<li>Add the tests to a bundle created with <code>databricks bundle init default-python</code> (it already includes a <code>tests/</code> folder) and run them from a GitHub Actions workflow.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["P1"]},{"id":"monitoring","title":"Monitoring, alerting and system tables","group":"Operations","order":17,"summary":"Job run history and statuses, finding upstream blockers, pipeline event logs, system tables for cost, audit and workloads, SQL alerts, monitoring with the API, CLI and SDK, and data quality monitoring.","html":"<h2 id=\"monitoring-jobs\">Monitoring jobs</h2>\n<p><strong>The Runs tab</strong> of a job has two views:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Matrix view</strong>: one column per run; the bar height is the run's duration and each cell is a task's status. Comparing bar heights against recent runs shows trends at a glance.</li>\n<li><strong>List view</strong>: runs with start time, duration, trigger and status.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The UI keeps about <strong>60 days</strong> of runs. For longer baselines use the system tables below.</p>\n<p>Inside a run, the <strong>duration breakdown</strong> separates time spent <em>queued</em>, <em>waiting for resources</em> (getting compute), <em>installing libraries</em> and <em>running</em>. If only <em>waiting for resources</em> grew, the code is not the problem: use pools, serverless (performance optimized), or group small jobs into one multi-task job.</p>\n<p><strong>Run and task statuses</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Status</th>\n<th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Succeeded</td>\n<td>Every task succeeded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failed</td>\n<td>A leaf task failed (the final state comes from the leaf tasks)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Succeeded with failures</td>\n<td>Only non-leaf tasks failed; leaf tasks still succeeded (for example with Run if <code>ALL_DONE</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Timed out</td>\n<td>An attempt exceeded its timeout</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Canceled</td>\n<td>Stopped by a user or an API call</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skipped</td>\n<td>Not started because the concurrency limit was reached and queueing was off</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upstream failed / Upstream canceled</td>\n<td>Did not run because a dependency failed or was canceled</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Excluded</td>\n<td>Did not run because its Run if condition was not met (normal)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Finding the blocker</strong>: in the task graph, walk upstream from any <em>Upstream failed</em> task to the <strong>first</strong> task that failed, and read its output and logs.</p>\n<p><strong>Health rules</strong> add a duration warning (<code>RUN_DURATION_SECONDS</code>) or streaming backlog thresholds; they notify but do not stop the run.</p>\n<h2 id=\"monitoring-pipelines\">Monitoring pipelines</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The pipeline graph shows each dataset's status, row counts and quality metrics; the data quality view shows passed and failed records per expectation.</li>\n<li>The <strong>event log</strong> records everything and is queryable:</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">SELECT timestamp, event_type, message\nFROM event_log(TABLE(main.sales.silver_orders))          -- or event_log('&lt;pipeline-id&gt;')\nWHERE level = 'ERROR' ORDER BY timestamp DESC;\n\nSELECT timestamp,\n       details:flow_progress.data_quality.expectations AS expectations,\n       details:flow_progress.metrics.backlog_bytes       AS backlog\nFROM event_log(TABLE(main.sales.silver_orders))\nWHERE event_type = 'flow_progress';\n</code></pre>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Event type</th>\n<th>Tells you</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>flow_progress</code></td>\n<td>Rows processed, backlog, data quality results per expectation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>update_progress</code></td>\n<td>State of each update (running, completed, failed)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>planning_information</code></td>\n<td>Whether a materialized view refreshed incrementally or fully, and why</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>user_action</code></td>\n<td>Who started, stopped or changed the pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>cluster_resources</code></td>\n<td>Autoscaling decisions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"system-tables\">System tables</h2>\n<p>System tables are read-only Delta tables in the <code>system</code> catalog, governed by Unity Catalog (an admin enables the schemas and grants <code>USE</code> and <code>SELECT</code>).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Table</th>\n<th>Use it for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.billing.usage</code></td>\n<td>Usage in DBUs (<code>usage_quantity</code>) by SKU, workspace, job, pipeline, warehouse and custom tags</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.billing.list_prices</code></td>\n<td>Price per SKU over time; join with usage to estimate cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.compute.clusters</code>, <code>node_timeline</code>, <code>warehouse_events</code></td>\n<td>Compute configuration, utilisation, warehouse scaling and idle time</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.query.history</code></td>\n<td>Queries on SQL warehouses and serverless: duration, user, bytes read</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.lakeflow.jobs</code>, <code>job_tasks</code>, <code>job_run_timeline</code>, <code>job_task_run_timeline</code></td>\n<td>Job definitions and run history (about a year), for trends and failure rates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.access.audit</code></td>\n<td>Who did what, when, from where (about a year of events)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.access.table_lineage</code>, <code>column_lineage</code></td>\n<td>Lineage between tables, columns, notebooks and jobs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history</code></td>\n<td>What predictive optimization did</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- daily list cost per SKU, last 30 days\nSELECT u.usage_date, u.sku_name,\n       SUM(u.usage_quantity * p.pricing.default) AS list_cost\nFROM system.billing.usage u\nJOIN system.billing.list_prices p\n  ON u.sku_name = p.sku_name\n AND u.usage_start_time &gt;= p.price_start_time\n AND (p.price_end_time IS NULL OR u.usage_start_time &lt; p.price_end_time)\nWHERE u.usage_date &gt;= current_date() - INTERVAL 30 DAYS\nGROUP BY ALL ORDER BY 1, 3 DESC;\n\n-- failure rate per job, last 90 days\nSELECT job_id,\n       count_if(result_state = 'FAILED') / count(*) AS failure_rate,\n       count(*) AS runs\nFROM system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline\nWHERE period_start_time &gt;= current_date() - INTERVAL 90 DAYS AND result_state IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY job_id ORDER BY failure_rate DESC;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Cost per job: <code>usage_metadata.job_id</code>. Cost per team: <code>custom_tags</code> (cluster tags, or serverless usage policies that add tags).</li>\n<li>Usage is measured in DBUs; <code>usage_quantity</code> is the column to sum (there is no <code>dbus</code> column).</li>\n<li>Long runs can be split across several rows of the timeline tables; aggregate by <code>run_id</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"alerts\">Alerts</h2>\n<p>A Databricks SQL alert (the Professional exam guide calls them Lakehouse alerts) runs a query on a schedule, evaluates a <strong>condition on one value</strong> of the result (column, operator, threshold) and notifies destinations (email, Slack, Teams, webhooks, PagerDuty) when it triggers.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Put composite logic in the query and return a single value: <code>SELECT CASE WHEN pct_no_email &gt; 15 AND pct_bad_phone &gt; 10 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS breach</code>, alert when <code>breach = 1</code>.</li>\n<li>Typical uses from the exam guide: governed metrics, data quality and anomalies, usage and cost (on <code>system.billing.usage</code>), SQL warehouse health, audit and security events (on <code>system.access.audit</code>), AI agent quality, and <strong>job branching</strong>: an alert can run as a task, and its outcome can decide which tasks run next.</li>\n<li>Executives who want a daily cost report: a <strong>dashboard</strong> on system tables with a scheduled refresh and a <strong>subscription</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"monitoring-with-the-api-cli-and-sdk\">Monitoring with the API, CLI and SDK</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-bash\">databricks jobs list-runs --job-id 123 --completed-only --start-time-from 1764547200000 -o json\ndatabricks jobs get-run 456 -o json\ndatabricks pipelines list-pipeline-events &lt;pipeline-id&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from databricks.sdk import WorkspaceClient\nw = WorkspaceClient()\nfor run in w.jobs.list_runs(job_id=123, completed_only=True, limit=25):\n    print(run.run_id, run.state.result_state, run.run_duration)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The Jobs REST API (<code>/api/2.2/jobs/runs/list</code>, <code>/runs/get</code>, <code>/runs/get-output</code>) offers the same information to external orchestrators and monitoring tools.</p>\n<h2 id=\"data-quality-monitoring\">Data quality monitoring</h2>\n<p>Formerly Lakehouse Monitoring:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Anomaly detection</strong>, enabled per schema, checks tables for <strong>freshness</strong> (data arrives on time) and <strong>completeness</strong> (expected volume) automatically and records results in system tables.</li>\n<li><strong>Data profiling</strong> computes profile and drift metrics for a table (snapshot, time series or model inference) into metric tables with a generated dashboard; alerts can be set on the metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"audit-and-lineage\">Audit and lineage</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Audit logs</strong>: <code>system.access.audit</code> (or JSON files delivered to storage) answer \"who read, changed or granted what\". Account-level events have <code>workspace_id = 0</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lineage</strong> is captured automatically for tables, columns, notebooks, jobs, pipelines, dashboards and queries. Before changing a column, open the table's <strong>Lineage</strong> tab in Catalog Explorer (or query the lineage system tables) to see every downstream consumer.</li>\n<li><code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> tells you what happened to one Delta table; lineage tells you who depends on it; audit tells you who accessed it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Older than 60 days</p>\n<p>The job UI shows about 60 days of runs. Longer trends come from <code>system.lakeflow</code> tables.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Run history versus code</p>\n<p>If a run got slower only in <em>waiting for resources</em>, tuning Spark will not help.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Alerts evaluate one value</p>\n<p>Combine conditions inside the query and alert on the single result.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Open any job's Runs tab and switch between matrix and list views.</li>\n<li>Query <code>system.billing.usage</code> (if your account exposes it) for your usage of the last 7 days, grouped by <code>sku_name</code>.</li>\n<li>Query the event log of the pipeline from the pipelines topic for <code>flow_progress</code> events.</li>\n<li>Create a SQL alert on a query that returns the number of rows in a quarantine table; trigger it by inserting a bad row.</li>\n<li>Use the Python SDK in a notebook (<code>from databricks.sdk import WorkspaceClient</code>) to list your last job runs.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A6","P4","P8"]},{"id":"performance","title":"Performance tuning and troubleshooting","group":"Operations","order":18,"summary":"How Spark executes work, the tuning settings the exam names, reading the Spark UI and the query profile, skew, spill, shuffles, out-of-memory errors, caching, and where to look when something fails.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-spark-executes-work\">How Spark executes work</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>driver</strong> plans the query, schedules tasks and collects results. <strong>Executors</strong> run tasks and hold cached data.</li>\n<li>Each action starts a <strong>job</strong>; a job is split into <strong>stages</strong> at every <strong>shuffle</strong> (data exchanged between executors for <code>groupBy</code>, <code>join</code>, <code>distinct</code>, <code>orderBy</code>, windows, <code>repartition</code>); each stage runs one <strong>task per partition</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Adaptive Query Execution (AQE)</strong>, on by default, re-plans at runtime: it merges small shuffle partitions, switches a join to a broadcast join when one side turns out small, and splits skewed partitions in joins.</li>\n<li><strong>Photon</strong> executes SQL and DataFrame operators in vectorised native code. Python UDFs and RDD code fall back to the standard engine.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"tuning-settings\">Tuning settings</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Setting</th>\n<th>Default</th>\n<th>Controls</th>\n<th>Remember</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code></td>\n<td>200 in Spark; Databricks recommends <code>auto</code></td>\n<td>Number of partitions after a shuffle</td>\n<td>Too few: huge partitions, spill, OOM. Too many: tiny tasks. A fixed legacy value disables auto-optimized shuffle; serverless manages it for you</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>spark.default.parallelism</code></td>\n<td>Total cores</td>\n<td>Partitions for <strong>RDD</strong> operations</td>\n<td>Does not affect DataFrame shuffles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Executor memory (worker node type)</td>\n<td>Depends on node</td>\n<td>Memory per executor</td>\n<td>Choose larger or memory-optimised workers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Driver memory (driver node type)</td>\n<td>Depends on node</td>\n<td>Memory of the driver</td>\n<td>Cannot be changed at runtime; more workers do not add driver memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold</code></td>\n<td>10 MB</td>\n<td>Size under which a table is broadcast at planning time</td>\n<td><code>-1</code> disables it; AQE can still broadcast at runtime; hints override the threshold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes</code></td>\n<td>128 MB</td>\n<td>Size of input partitions when reading files</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Serverless compute accepts only a few Spark settings, and SQL warehouses essentially none: on those, the platform tunes these values.</p>\n<p><strong>Method</strong>: change one thing at a time, rerun on the same input, and compare total time and stage metrics. Without a new measurement you do not know whether the change helped.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-the-spark-ui\">Reading the Spark UI</h2>\n<p>Start from the <strong>Jobs</strong> tab, open the slowest job, then its slowest <strong>stage</strong>. The stage page shows <strong>summary metrics</strong> for its tasks (min, 25th percentile, median, 75th percentile, max) for duration, GC time, input, shuffle read and write, and spill.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What you see</th>\n<th>Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Max task duration far above the median and 75th percentile</td>\n<td><strong>Skew</strong>: a few partitions hold much more data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Spill (memory)</em> and <em>Spill (disk)</em> columns appear</td>\n<td>Partitions did not fit in executor memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Huge shuffle read and write</td>\n<td>Expensive data exchange, often a join that could be a broadcast</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High GC time</td>\n<td>Memory pressure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CPU time close to task time</td>\n<td>CPU-bound work: more or faster cores help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CPU time much lower than task time</td>\n<td>Tasks wait (I/O, shuffle fetches, skewed neighbours)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Other tabs: <strong>SQL / DataFrame</strong> (the query plan with metrics per operator: rows, time, files read and pruned, join strategy), <strong>Executors</strong> (memory, GC, failed tasks, lost executors), <strong>Storage</strong> (cached data), <strong>Environment</strong> (settings), <strong>Structured Streaming</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Serverless and SQL warehouses have no Spark UI</strong>: use the <strong>query profile</strong>. It shows each operator's time, rows, peak memory and spill, files and bytes read versus pruned, the join strategy, and insights such as data skew or spill. From a serverless job run, open the run's query history to reach the profiles.</p>\n<h2 id=\"problems-and-fixes\">Problems and fixes</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>Symptom</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Skew in a join</td>\n<td>One task runs far longer</td>\n<td>Keep AQE skew join handling on (it splits skewed partitions in sort-merge and shuffle hash joins); salt the hot key; broadcast if one side is small; handle NULL or dummy keys separately</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skew in an aggregation</td>\n<td>One task runs far longer; AQE does not help</td>\n<td><strong>Salting</strong>: add a random suffix to the key, aggregate, remove the suffix, aggregate again</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spill</td>\n<td>Spill columns in stage metrics or query profile</td>\n<td>More (smaller) shuffle partitions or <code>auto</code>; memory-optimised workers; select only needed columns before wide operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Large shuffle of a big table joined with a small one</td>\n<td>Shuffle read/write in TBs</td>\n<td>Broadcast the small table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Driver out of memory</td>\n<td><code>collect()</code>, <code>toPandas()</code>, huge broadcast, many results</td>\n<td>Do not bring data to the driver: write to a table, aggregate, <code>limit</code>; larger driver node or high-memory serverless notebook</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Executor out of memory</td>\n<td><code>OutOfMemoryError</code> in executors, lost executors</td>\n<td>More shuffle partitions, larger worker nodes, fix skew, avoid broadcasting large tables, avoid caching when memory is short</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poor data skipping</td>\n<td>Files read close to total files</td>\n<td>Liquid clustering on filter columns (see <em>Table layout</em>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Too many small output files</td>\n<td>Thousands of tiny files</td>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE</code>, auto compaction; <code>coalesce(n)</code> before a write</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow Python logic</td>\n<td>Python UDF stages dominate</td>\n<td>Built-in functions, or pandas UDFs</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Adding workers does not fix skew (the slow task is still one task) and does not fix driver OOM. Lowering <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code> makes skewed partitions even bigger.</p>\n<p><code>coalesce(n)</code> reduces the number of partitions by merging existing ones <strong>without a full shuffle</strong>; <code>repartition(n)</code> or <code>repartition(\"col\")</code> performs a <strong>full shuffle</strong> to increase partitions or rebalance them.</p>\n<h2 id=\"caching\">Caching</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Disk cache (Delta cache)</th>\n<th>Spark cache (<code>df.cache()</code>, <code>CACHE TABLE</code>)</th>\n<th>Query result cache</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Stores</td>\n<td>Copies of remote data files on workers' local SSDs</td>\n<td>DataFrame or table in executor memory (and disk)</td>\n<td>Results of identical queries on SQL warehouses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enabled</td>\n<td>Automatically on supported node types, SQL warehouses and serverless</td>\n<td>Manually; filled by the first action</td>\n<td>Automatically</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Freshness</td>\n<td>Detects changed files automatically</td>\n<td>Can be stale until unpersisted</td>\n<td>Invalidated when the data changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Helps</td>\n<td>Repeated reads of the same files (dashboards, iterative analysis)</td>\n<td>Reusing one expensive intermediate result several times in a job</td>\n<td>Repeated identical dashboard queries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Costs</td>\n<td>Local disk space</td>\n<td>Executor memory; can cause OOM</td>\n<td>Nothing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The disk cache makes repeated reads faster because it serves data from local SSDs instead of cloud storage. It does not fix a bad layout, and caching is never a fix for out-of-memory errors.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-to-look-when-something-fails\">Where to look when something fails</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Situation</th>\n<th>Look in</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cluster does not start</td>\n<td>Cluster event log (termination reason), init script logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A task fails with an exception</td>\n<td>Run output, driver logs (stdout, stderr, log4j)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Executors lost, OOM, spot instances reclaimed</td>\n<td>Spark UI Executors tab, cluster event log, driver logs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slow query on a warehouse or serverless</td>\n<td>Query profile, <code>system.query.history</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A pipeline update fails</td>\n<td>Pipeline event log (<code>level = 'ERROR'</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A cost spike</td>\n<td><code>system.billing.usage</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who changed a permission or table</td>\n<td><code>system.access.audit</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Configure <strong>cluster log delivery</strong> to a volume so driver, executor and init script logs survive after the cluster terminates.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">AQE skew handling is for joins</p>\n<p>It does not split skewed aggregations; salting does.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">spark.default.parallelism</p>\n<p>It only affects RDDs. DataFrame shuffles follow <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Cache is not memory</p>\n<p>When the problem is out-of-memory, caching more data makes it worse.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">One change at a time</p>\n<p>Measure, change one setting, measure again on the same input.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Run <code>spark.range(0, 50_000_000).withColumn(\"k\", (F.col(\"id\") % 3).cast(\"int\"))</code>, add a skewed key (<code>when(id &lt; 40_000_000, 0)</code>), group by it and inspect the stage's summary metrics (on classic compute) or the query profile (on serverless).</li>\n<li>Join <code>samples.tpch.lineitem</code> with <code>samples.tpch.nation</code> with and without <code>F.broadcast(...)</code>, and compare the plans with <code>explain()</code>.</li>\n<li>Call <code>toPandas()</code> on a large DataFrame in a serverless notebook, observe the error, and switch the environment memory to High.</li>\n<li>Write a DataFrame with <code>repartition(200)</code> and with <code>coalesce(4)</code> and compare the number of files in <code>DESCRIBE DETAIL</code>.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A3","A6","P5","P8"]},{"id":"governance","title":"Unity Catalog and access control","group":"Governance","order":19,"summary":"The object hierarchy, managed versus external tables and volumes, storage credentials and external locations, privileges and inheritance, ownership and MANAGE, workspace permissions, tags, comments and discovery.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-object-hierarchy\">The object hierarchy</h2>\n<pre><code>metastore (one per region, attached to many workspaces)\n└── catalog\n    └── schema\n        ├── tables, views, materialized views, streaming tables, metric views\n        ├── volumes (files)\n        ├── functions\n        └── models\n</code></pre>\n<p>Objects are addressed with three levels: <code>catalog.schema.object</code>. Other securables sit beside the hierarchy: <strong>storage credentials</strong>, <strong>external locations</strong>, <strong>connections</strong> (federation and connectors), <strong>shares</strong>, <strong>recipients</strong>, <strong>providers</strong> and <strong>clean rooms</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each workspace usually gets a <strong>workspace catalog</strong> (with a <code>default</code> schema) that all its users can use; <code>hive_metastore</code> is the legacy pre-Unity Catalog store; <code>system</code> holds system tables; <code>samples</code> holds sample data.</li>\n<li>A catalog can be <strong>bound</strong> to specific workspaces, so a production catalog is only reachable from production workspaces.</li>\n<li>Grants are stored in the metastore, so they apply in every workspace attached to it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"managed-and-external-objects\">Managed and external objects</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Managed table</th>\n<th>External table</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Where the files live</td>\n<td>Managed storage chosen by Unity Catalog (set per metastore, catalog or schema with <code>MANAGED LOCATION</code>)</td>\n<td>A path you give with <code>LOCATION</code>, inside an external location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Formats</td>\n<td>Delta or Iceberg</td>\n<td>Delta, Parquet, CSV, JSON, Avro, ORC, text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>DROP TABLE</code></td>\n<td>Deletes metadata <strong>and data</strong> (recoverable with <code>UNDROP</code> for 7 days)</td>\n<td>Deletes <strong>metadata only</strong>; files stay</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predictive optimization, automatic clustering</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>No</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recommended</td>\n<td>Yes: less maintenance, better performance</td>\n<td>When other systems must own or read the files directly</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CREATE TABLE main.sales.orders (order_id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL(10,2));      -- managed\n\nCREATE TABLE main.raw.events_csv (id INT, payload STRING)\nUSING CSV OPTIONS (header 'true')\nLOCATION 'abfss://raw@account.dfs.core.windows.net/events/';                 -- external\n\nALTER TABLE main.raw.legacy_orders SET MANAGED;   -- convert external to managed in place\nSHOW TABLES DROPPED IN main.sales;\nUNDROP TABLE main.sales.orders;\nDROP SCHEMA main.tmp CASCADE;                       -- RESTRICT (the default) fails if not empty\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED</code> moves an external table into managed storage <strong>keeping its name, history and grants</strong>; <code>UNSET MANAGED</code> can roll it back for a limited time. There is no managed-to-external conversion. A <code>CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</code> copy would lose history and grants.</li>\n<li><code>UNDROP</code> brings a dropped table back with its grants and properties, if its schema and catalog still exist. Catalogs, schemas, views, volumes and functions cannot be undropped.</li>\n<li>To check whether a table is managed or external: <code>DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED</code> (field <em>Type</em>), Catalog Explorer, or <code>information_schema.tables.table_type</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Volumes</strong> hold non-tabular files under <code>/Volumes/catalog/schema/volume/</code>. Managed volumes live in managed storage; external volumes point to a path in an external location.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Storage credentials and external locations.</strong> A storage credential wraps a cloud identity (an IAM role, a managed identity, a service account). An external location combines a credential with a cloud path. External tables, external volumes and managed storage locations must be inside external locations, and paths may not overlap.</p>\n<h2 id=\"privileges\">Privileges</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Privilege</th>\n<th>Allows</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>USE CATALOG</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code></td>\n<td>Traversing the catalog and schema; required for any access to objects inside</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>BROWSE</code></td>\n<td>Seeing objects and metadata in Catalog Explorer without reading data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SELECT</code></td>\n<td>Reading tables, views and materialized views</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MODIFY</code></td>\n<td>Inserting, updating and deleting rows</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CREATE SCHEMA</code>, <code>CREATE TABLE</code>, <code>CREATE VOLUME</code>, <code>CREATE FUNCTION</code>, <code>CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW</code></td>\n<td>Creating objects in a catalog or schema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>READ VOLUME</code>, <code>WRITE VOLUME</code></td>\n<td>Reading and writing files in a volume</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>READ FILES</code>, <code>WRITE FILES</code>, <code>CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE</code>, <code>CREATE EXTERNAL VOLUME</code></td>\n<td>Working with paths in an external location</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>EXECUTE</code></td>\n<td>Calling a function (including masks and filters) or a model</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>USE CONNECTION</code>, <code>CREATE FOREIGN CATALOG</code></td>\n<td>Using a connection (federation, connectors) without seeing its credentials</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>CREATE SHARE</code>, <code>CREATE RECIPIENT</code> (on the metastore)</td>\n<td>Creating shares and recipients without being metastore admin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>APPLY TAG</code></td>\n<td>Setting tags on an object</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>MANAGE</code></td>\n<td>Managing privileges, transferring ownership, renaming and dropping an object, <strong>without</strong> data access</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code></td>\n<td>Every privilege applicable at that level, except <code>MANAGE</code> (and the external-engine privileges)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">GRANT USE CATALOG ON CATALOG main TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA, SELECT ON SCHEMA main.sales TO `analysts`;\nGRANT USE SCHEMA, SELECT, MODIFY, CREATE TABLE ON SCHEMA main.silver TO `etl-sp-app-id`;\nREVOKE SELECT ON SCHEMA main.sales FROM `analysts`;\nSHOW GRANTS ON SCHEMA main.sales;\n</code></pre>\n<p><strong>Rules that decide answers:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inheritance flows down</strong>: a privilege on a catalog or schema applies to every current <strong>and future</strong> object inside. Privileges on the metastore are not inherited.</li>\n<li>To read a table you need all three: <code>USE CATALOG</code> on its catalog, <code>USE SCHEMA</code> on its schema and <code>SELECT</code> on the table (or a parent).</li>\n<li><strong><code>REVOKE</code> acts only at the level where you run it.</strong> Revoking <code>SELECT</code> on a table does not remove <code>SELECT</code> granted on its schema, and gives no error. Revoke at the level of the original grant.</li>\n<li><strong>There is no <code>DENY</code> in Unity Catalog.</strong> Anything not granted is denied. <code>DENY</code> existed only in legacy <code>hive_metastore</code> table ACLs. To exclude one person from what their group can read, restructure the grants (for example, grant to a smaller group).</li>\n<li>Grant to <strong>account groups</strong> and <strong>service principals</strong> (by application ID), not to individuals or workspace-local groups.</li>\n<li>Only the owner of a catalog or schema (or someone with <code>MANAGE</code>) can grant <code>USE CATALOG</code> and <code>USE SCHEMA</code> on it, so table owners cannot open access beyond approved boundaries.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Ownership.</strong> The creator of an object is its owner. The owner has all privileges on it, can grant them to others and can drop it. Transfer ownership of production objects to a group or a service principal (<code>ALTER TABLE t SET OWNER TO `data-platform`</code>). A schema owner does not automatically have privileges on tables created by others in it, but can grant them to themselves.</p>\n<p><strong>Least privilege examples</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Requirement</th>\n<th>Grant</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Analysts read one schema, never write</td>\n<td><code>USE CATALOG</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code>, <code>SELECT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A team creates tables in one schema only</td>\n<td><code>USE CATALOG</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code>, <code>CREATE TABLE</code> on that schema</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A pipeline reads bronze and writes silver</td>\n<td><code>USE CATALOG</code>; <code>USE SCHEMA</code> + <code>SELECT</code> on bronze; <code>USE SCHEMA</code> + <code>SELECT</code> + <code>MODIFY</code> + <code>CREATE TABLE</code> on silver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A project group creates and manages its own schemas in a catalog but must not rename, drop or transfer the catalog</td>\n<td><code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> on the catalog (no ownership, no <code>MANAGE</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>See what exists without reading data</td>\n<td><code>BROWSE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Administer grants without reading data</td>\n<td><code>MANAGE</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"workspace-object-permissions\">Workspace object permissions</h2>\n<p>Unity Catalog governs data. Workspace objects have their own access control lists:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>Levels (lowest to highest)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Notebooks, files, folders</td>\n<td>CAN READ, CAN RUN, CAN EDIT, CAN MANAGE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jobs</td>\n<td>CAN VIEW, CAN MANAGE RUN, IS OWNER, CAN MANAGE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clusters</td>\n<td>CAN ATTACH TO, CAN RESTART, CAN MANAGE</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL warehouses</td>\n<td>CAN USE, CAN MONITOR, CAN MANAGE, IS OWNER</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Git folders</td>\n<td>CAN READ, CAN RUN, CAN EDIT, CAN MANAGE</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Give each person the lowest level their job needs, and run production jobs as a service principal.</p>\n<h2 id=\"discovery-comments-tags-and-lineage\">Discovery: comments, tags and lineage</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">COMMENT ON TABLE main.sales.orders IS 'One row per order line, loaded hourly';\nALTER TABLE main.sales.orders ALTER COLUMN amount COMMENT 'Gross amount in EUR';\nALTER TABLE main.sales.orders SET TAGS ('domain' = 'sales', 'certified' = 'true');\nALTER TABLE main.sales.orders ALTER COLUMN email SET TAGS ('pii' = 'email');\nSELECT * FROM main.information_schema.column_tags WHERE tag_name = 'pii';\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Comments</strong> describe objects and columns; Catalog Explorer can <strong>generate comments with AI</strong> for many tables at once, for review before saving.</li>\n<li><strong>Tags</strong> (key-value) classify catalogs, schemas, tables, views, volumes and columns; search and filters use them. <strong>Governed tags</strong> have an account-level policy of allowed values and who may assign them; they drive ABAC policies (see <em>Data privacy</em>).</li>\n<li><strong>Lineage</strong> is captured automatically down to the column level and shown in Catalog Explorer (see <em>Monitoring</em>).</li>\n<li><code>information_schema</code> views (<code>tables</code>, <code>columns</code>, <code>table_privileges</code>, <code>column_tags</code>…) in each catalog let you query metadata with SQL.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">DENY does not exist</p>\n<p>The Associate exam guide lists <code>GRANT</code>, <code>REVOKE</code> and <code>DENY</code>. In Unity Catalog, <code>DENY</code> is not a command; not granted means denied.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">REVOKE does not remove inherited access</p>\n<p>Revoke where the privilege was granted.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">DROP on external tables</p>\n<p>Only metadata is removed; the files remain in the external location.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Three keys to read</p>\n<p><code>USE CATALOG</code> + <code>USE SCHEMA</code> + <code>SELECT</code>. A user who sees the schema but cannot query usually lacks <code>SELECT</code>; a user with <code>SELECT</code> who cannot even see the table usually lacks a <code>USE</code> privilege.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create <code>workspace.gov_lab</code> with a managed table and a volume. Run <code>DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED</code> and find the <em>Type</em> field.</li>\n<li>Drop the table, list it with <code>SHOW TABLES DROPPED</code>, and <code>UNDROP</code> it.</li>\n<li>Add comments and tags, then query <code>information_schema.column_tags</code>.</li>\n<li>If you have a second user or a service principal, grant <code>USE CATALOG</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code> and <code>SELECT</code>, revoke at the table level only, and confirm access remains.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A1","A7","P5","P6","P7"]},{"id":"privacy","title":"Data privacy: masks, filters, ABAC and PII","group":"Governance","order":20,"summary":"Row filters and column masks, dynamic views, ABAC policies with governed tags, detecting and masking PII in pipelines, hashing, tokenization, suppression and generalization, and GDPR erasure and retention with Delta Lake.","html":"<h2 id=\"row-filters-and-column-masks\">Row filters and column masks</h2>\n<p>Both are SQL functions in Unity Catalog attached to a table. They apply to every query from every tool, without copying the data.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- row filter: returns TRUE for the rows the current user may see\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION main.sec.region_filter(region STRING)\nRETURN is_account_group_member('sales_admins')\n    OR is_account_group_member(concat('sales_', region));\n\nALTER TABLE main.sales.orders SET ROW FILTER main.sec.region_filter ON (region);\n\n-- column mask: receives the column value, returns what the user may see\nCREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION main.sec.mask_email(email STRING)\nRETURN CASE WHEN is_account_group_member('hr_admins') THEN email\n            ELSE regexp_replace(email, '^[^@]+', '***') END;\n\nALTER TABLE main.hr.employees ALTER COLUMN email SET MASK main.sec.mask_email;\n-- a mask can read other columns:  SET MASK main.sec.mask_salary USING COLUMNS (country)\n\nALTER TABLE main.hr.employees ALTER COLUMN email DROP MASK;\nALTER TABLE main.sales.orders DROP ROW FILTER;\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>row filter</strong> returns BOOLEAN; rows where it returns FALSE or NULL are hidden. One row filter per table.</li>\n<li>A <strong>column mask</strong> takes the column value as its first parameter and returns the same type. One mask per column.</li>\n<li><code>is_account_group_member()</code> checks <strong>account</strong> groups (the ones Unity Catalog uses); <code>is_member()</code> checks workspace-local groups. <code>current_user()</code> / <code>session_user()</code> return the user.</li>\n<li>Masks are applied before the user's <code>WHERE</code> and joins, so users cannot filter on the hidden real values.</li>\n<li>Applying a filter or mask needs <code>EXECUTE</code> on the function and ownership of the table (or <code>MANAGE</code> with the right privileges).</li>\n<li>Reading protected tables works on SQL warehouses, standard access mode, and dedicated access mode on Databricks Runtime 15.4 LTS or later with serverless enabled (the filtering then runs on serverless).</li>\n<li><strong>Drop the filter or mask before dropping its function</strong>; a table whose mask function was deleted becomes unreadable.</li>\n<li>Masks and filters cannot be set on views.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Dynamic views</strong> are the older approach: a view whose <code>SELECT</code> uses <code>CASE WHEN is_account_group_member(...)</code> for columns and a <code>WHERE</code> for rows. Users get <code>SELECT</code> on the view only. They still make sense when the protected result combines several tables or reshapes data; for a single table, row filters and column masks protect the table itself.</p>\n<h2 id=\"abac-policies-driven-by-governed-tags\">ABAC: policies driven by governed tags</h2>\n<p>Attribute-based access control lets a central team write <strong>one policy</strong> that applies to every matching table, now and in the future, based on <strong>governed tags</strong>.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">-- 1. tag the sensitive columns (governed tag &quot;pii&quot;, allowed values email, ssn, phone...)\nALTER TABLE crm.customers.contacts ALTER COLUMN email SET TAGS ('pii' = 'email');\n\n-- 2. one mask policy for the whole catalog\nCREATE POLICY mask_emails\nON CATALOG crm\nCOMMENT 'Emails masked for everyone except the privacy team'\nCOLUMN MASK main.sec.mask_email\nTO `account users` EXCEPT `privacy_team`\nFOR TABLES\nMATCH COLUMNS has_tag_value('pii', 'email') AS email_col\nON COLUMN email_col;\n\n-- 3. a row filter policy for tables with a tagged region column\nCREATE POLICY emea_only\nON SCHEMA sales.orders\nROW FILTER main.sec.filter_by_region\nTO `emea_team`\nFOR TABLES\nMATCH COLUMNS has_tag('region') AS rgn\nUSING COLUMNS (rgn, 'EMEA');\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Policies are created on a <strong>catalog, schema or table</strong> and target principals (<code>TO ... EXCEPT ...</code>). <code>WHEN</code> adds conditions on table tags; <code>MATCH COLUMNS</code> selects columns by their tags.</li>\n<li><strong>Column tags are set on columns.</strong> A tag on the table, schema or catalog does not make its columns match <code>MATCH COLUMNS</code>.</li>\n<li>Policies <strong>do not grant access</strong>: they only change what users see in tables they can already query.</li>\n<li>Table owners <strong>cannot override</strong> a policy set above them.</li>\n<li>Creating a policy requires <code>MANAGE</code> (or ownership) on its scope and <code>EXECUTE</code> on the function; assigning a governed tag requires permission to use the tag and <code>APPLY TAG</code> on the object.</li>\n<li>Querying needs serverless compute or Databricks Runtime 16.4 or later; if the policy cannot be evaluated, access fails (fail closed).</li>\n<li>If two policies give a user different masks for the same column (or different filters for a table), the query fails: design policies so each column matches one mask per user. <code>SHOW GRANTS</code> does not list policies; inspect them in Catalog Explorer or with the policy and <code>information_schema</code> views.</li>\n<li>Pipelines and jobs evaluate policies as their run-as identity: add the service principal to <code>EXCEPT</code> when it must process the real values.</li>\n<li>Deleting a tag value or a function that a policy uses breaks queries across the policy's whole scope.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Choosing</strong>: one table, a few rules → row filter or column mask. Hundreds of tables, rules that must follow new tables automatically, and owners who must not remove protection → ABAC.</p>\n<h2 id=\"detecting-and-masking-pii-in-pipelines\">Detecting and masking PII in pipelines</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Find it</strong>: data classification in Unity Catalog can scan tables and tag columns that look like PII (emails, phone numbers, national IDs), so ABAC policies pick them up; <code>ai_mask</code> (see <em>UDFs and AI functions</em>) masks named entities inside free text.</li>\n<li><strong>Contain it</strong>: land raw personal data in a restricted bronze schema; apply masking or pseudonymisation when building silver, with <strong>one shared Unity Catalog function</strong> used by every batch and streaming pipeline, so the logic is consistent and maintained once.</li>\n<li><strong>Mask outside too</strong>: data copied out of Unity Catalog (exports, external systems) does not carry masks with it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"anonymisation-and-pseudonymisation-techniques\">Anonymisation and pseudonymisation techniques</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Technique</th>\n<th>How</th>\n<th>Reversible</th>\n<th>Keeps joins</th>\n<th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hashing</strong></td>\n<td><code>sha2(concat(email, salt), 256)</code> with a secret salt</td>\n<td>No (but guessable without a salt)</td>\n<td>Yes (same input, same hash)</td>\n<td>Pseudonymous customer key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Tokenization</strong></td>\n<td>Replace the value with a random token; keep the mapping in a separate, tightly restricted table</td>\n<td>Yes, for authorised users</td>\n<td>Yes</td>\n<td>Payment or national IDs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Suppression</strong></td>\n<td>Remove the column or the record</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Drop the free-text notes column</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Generalization</strong></td>\n<td>Reduce precision</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td>Partly</td>\n<td>Age to age band, birth date to year, postcode to district</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Masking (display)</strong></td>\n<td>Show part of the value</td>\n<td>Not for the viewer</td>\n<td>No</td>\n<td><code>****-****-****-1234</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>Hash with a <strong>salt</strong> kept in a secret scope; an unsalted hash of an email can be reversed by hashing a list of known emails.</li>\n<li><strong>Overwrite</strong> the original column when producing a non-sensitive copy: <code>withColumn(\"email\", sha2(...))</code>, not a new <code>email_hash</code> column next to the original.</li>\n<li>Quasi-identifiers (birth date, postcode, gender) can re-identify people when combined; generalise them too.</li>\n<li>Pseudonymised data is still personal data under GDPR when it can be linked back; truly anonymised data is not.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"erasure-and-retention-with-delta-lake\">Erasure and retention with Delta Lake</h2>\n<p>A deleted row is not gone while older files still contain it. A GDPR erasure request therefore needs more than a <code>DELETE</code>:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">DELETE FROM main.silver.customers WHERE customer_id = 'C-1043';   -- every layer: bronze, silver, gold\nREORG TABLE main.silver.customers APPLY (PURGE);                   -- rewrite files that only had the row soft-deleted (deletion vectors)\nVACUUM main.silver.customers;                                      -- after the retention period, physically remove old files\n</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Time travel</strong> and <strong>Change Data Feed</strong> files still hold the deleted values until <code>VACUUM</code> removes them; set <code>delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration</code> so files are gone within your legal deadline.</li>\n<li>Deleting from a table that streams read breaks append-only streams; downstream readers use <code>skipChangeCommits</code> or the change feed (and must apply the deletes too).</li>\n<li>Keep erasure requests in a table and apply them with a scheduled job (for example a <code>MERGE ... WHEN MATCHED THEN DELETE</code> against each table).</li>\n<li>With <strong>tokenization</strong>, deleting the person's entry in the mapping table makes the remaining tokens meaningless everywhere (a form of crypto-shredding).</li>\n<li><strong>Retention policies</strong>: schedule <code>DELETE FROM t WHERE event_date &lt; current_date() - INTERVAL 730 DAYS</code>, followed by <code>VACUUM</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Policies do not grant</p>\n<p>ABAC and masks change what users see in tables they can already read; access still comes from <code>GRANT</code>.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">DELETE is not erasure</p>\n<p>Until old files are vacuumed, time travel and the change feed can still return the data.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition trap\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Account groups</p>\n<p>Use <code>is_account_group_member</code> in filters and masks; <code>is_member</code> checks workspace-local groups.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"admonition tip\">\n<p class=\"admonition-title\">Hash in place</p>\n<p>A hashed copy next to the original still exposes the original.</p>\n</div>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on\">Hands-on</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Create <code>workspace.priv_lab.employees (id INT, name STRING, email STRING, region STRING, salary INT)</code> with a few rows.</li>\n<li>Create a mask function for <code>email</code> and a row filter on <code>region</code> using <code>is_account_group_member('admins')</code>, apply them, and query the table.</li>\n<li>Create a hashed copy with <code>sha2(concat(email, '&lt;salt from a secret&gt;'), 256)</code> overwriting the column.</li>\n<li>Delete one employee, run <code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code>, read the previous version with time travel (the row is still there), then discuss what <code>REORG ... APPLY (PURGE)</code> and <code>VACUUM</code> would change.</li>\n</ol>","sections":["A7","P6"]}],"pages":{"cheatsheet":{"title":"Cheat sheet","html":"<p>The last-day review: the keyword in the question, and the answer it usually points to. Each block links to the topic with the full explanation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ingestion-files-sources-sharing\">Ingestion (<a href=\"#/topic/ingest-files\">files</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/ingest-sources\">sources</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/sharing\">sharing</a>)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question says</th>\n<th>Think</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Millions of files, schema changes, incremental</td>\n<td>Auto Loader with schema evolution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thousands of files, SQL, idempotent, scheduled</td>\n<td><code>COPY INTO</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corrected file, same name, <code>COPY INTO</code></td>\n<td>Delete old rows, <code>FILES = (...)</code> + <code>'force' = 'true'</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>New columns must stop ingestion for review</td>\n<td><code>failOnNewColumns</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Never stop, never lose unexpected data</td>\n<td><code>rescue</code> → <code>_rescued_data</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Default without a schema</td>\n<td><code>addNewColumns</code>: fail, evolve on restart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON and CSV types with Auto Loader</td>\n<td>STRING unless <code>inferColumnTypes</code> / <code>schemaHints</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Listing millions of files is slow</td>\n<td>File notification with managed file events</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Process what is new, then stop</td>\n<td><code>trigger(availableNow=True)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source file of each row</td>\n<td><code>_metadata.file_path</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SaaS or database, no code</td>\n<td>Lakeflow Connect managed connector</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Database CDC into Databricks</td>\n<td>Database connector: gateway + ingestion pipeline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use credentials without seeing them</td>\n<td>Connection + <code>USE CONNECTION</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JDBC bounds</td>\n<td>Stride only; they do not filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Query a database without copying</td>\n<td>Lakehouse Federation (connection + foreign catalog)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Another Databricks customer</td>\n<td>D2D sharing (metastore sharing identifier)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partner without Databricks</td>\n<td>Open sharing (token or OIDC)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Neither side may see the other's data</td>\n<td>Clean room</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"delta-and-layout-delta-layout\">Delta and layout (<a href=\"#/topic/delta\">delta</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/layout\">layout</a>)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question says</th>\n<th>Think</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Partial reads, concurrent writes, half-written data</td>\n<td>Delta Lake transaction log</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upsert</td>\n<td><code>MERGE</code>; one source row per key</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Remove target rows missing from the source</td>\n<td><code>WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Replace data and schema, keep history and grants</td>\n<td><code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ... AS SELECT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Add new columns on write</td>\n<td><code>mergeSchema</code> (replace schema: <code>overwriteSchema</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Undo a bad write</td>\n<td><code>RESTORE TABLE ... TO VERSION AS OF</code> (new commit)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Old version no longer readable</td>\n<td><code>VACUUM</code> removed its files</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Small files</td>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE</code>, optimized writes, auto compaction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>High-cardinality filter column</td>\n<td>Liquid clustering, never partitioning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unknown or changing filters</td>\n<td><code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code> (managed + predictive optimization)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changed clustering keys</td>\n<td><code>OPTIMIZE t FULL</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Frequent row updates rewrite files</td>\n<td>Deletion vectors</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Predictive optimization skips a table</td>\n<td>It is external</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"transformations-transform-semi-structured-udfs-and-ai\">Transformations (<a href=\"#/topic/transform\">transform</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/semi-structured\">semi-structured</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/udf-ai\">UDFs and AI</a>)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question says</th>\n<th>Think</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>PySpark <code>union</code></td>\n<td>By position, keeps duplicates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Different column order</td>\n<td><code>unionByName(allowMissingColumns=True)</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SQL <code>UNION</code></td>\n<td>Removes duplicates</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>= NULL</code></td>\n<td>Never true: <code>IS NULL</code>, <code>&lt;=&gt;</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rows with no match</td>\n<td><code>left_anti</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Latest row per key</td>\n<td><code>row_number()</code> ordered by time desc, keep 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fillna(0)</code></td>\n<td>Numeric columns only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ANSI cast failure</td>\n<td><code>try_cast</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>M</code> / <code>m</code> in date patterns</td>\n<td>Month / minute</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>[0]</code> / <code>element_at(..., 1)</code></td>\n<td>Both the first element</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ties share a rank, no gaps</td>\n<td><code>dense_rank</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>JSON string field</td>\n<td><code>raw:a.b</code>; struct: <code>a.b</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arrays with NULL must keep the row</td>\n<td><code>explode_outer</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changing JSON shape</td>\n<td>VARIANT; <code>variant_explode</code>, case-sensitive paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fastest custom logic</td>\n<td>Built-in &gt; SQL UDF &gt; pandas UDF &gt; Python UDF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Classify text into labels in SQL</td>\n<td><code>ai_classify</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"pipelines-streaming-cdc-pipelines-streaming-cdc-modeling\">Pipelines, streaming, CDC (<a href=\"#/topic/pipelines\">pipelines</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/streaming\">streaming</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/cdc\">CDC</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/modeling\">modeling</a>)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question says</th>\n<th>Think</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Append-only, each row once</td>\n<td>Streaming table</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expensive aggregate for BI</td>\n<td>Materialized view</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intermediate step</td>\n<td>Temporary view</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>No <code>ON VIOLATION</code></td>\n<td>Warn: keep row, count</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Discard and continue / stop</td>\n<td><code>DROP ROW</code> / <code>FAIL UPDATE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep rejected rows</td>\n<td>Quarantine table (NULL-safe condition)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rule for every writer</td>\n<td>Delta <code>CHECK</code> constraint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MV refresh fails after owner left</td>\n<td>Owner = service principal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upsert from a stream</td>\n<td><code>foreachBatch</code> + <code>MERGE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bound state</td>\n<td>Watermark</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duplicates days apart</td>\n<td>Insert-only <code>MERGE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>History with validity dates</td>\n<td>SCD type 2 (<code>AUTO CDC ... STORED AS SCD TYPE 2</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Out-of-order events</td>\n<td><code>SEQUENCE BY</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Only snapshots available</td>\n<td><code>AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>React to updates and deletes downstream</td>\n<td>Change Data Feed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Keep raw events for years</td>\n<td>Bronze</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Governed metrics at any grain</td>\n<td>Metric view</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"jobs-and-cicd-jobs-cicd-testing\">Jobs and CI/CD (<a href=\"#/topic/jobs\">jobs</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/cicd\">CI/CD</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/testing\">testing</a>)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question says</th>\n<th>Think</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cleanup that always runs</td>\n<td>Run if <code>ALL_DONE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alert on failure</td>\n<td>Run if <code>AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skip when no new data</td>\n<td>Task value + If/else</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same task per list item</td>\n<td>For each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Files arrive at random times</td>\n<td>File arrival trigger</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run after a table changes</td>\n<td>Table update trigger (run-as needs <code>SELECT</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kill after one hour</td>\n<td>Timeout (warning only notifies)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rerun only failed tasks</td>\n<td>Repair run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quartz 06:30</td>\n<td><code>0 30 6 * * ?</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Same code dev → prod</td>\n<td>Bundle with targets and variables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Variable on <code>bundle run</code> ignored</td>\n<td>Resolved at deploy: use job parameters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adopt a UI job</td>\n<td><code>generate</code> + <code>bind</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CI authentication</td>\n<td>Service principal, OAuth M2M or OIDC federation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pull request</td>\n<td>In the Git provider</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Testable transformations</td>\n<td>Pure functions + <code>DataFrame.transform</code> + <code>assertDataFrameEqual</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"operations-monitoring-performance-platform\">Operations (<a href=\"#/topic/monitoring\">monitoring</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/performance\">performance</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/platform\">platform</a>)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question says</th>\n<th>Think</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>One task far slower than the median</td>\n<td>Skew: AQE skew join (joins) or salting</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spill</td>\n<td>Smaller partitions, more memory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Big table joined with tiny table</td>\n<td>Broadcast the tiny one</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>collect()</code> OOM</td>\n<td>Driver: do not collect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Serverless, no Spark UI</td>\n<td>Query profile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>spark.default.parallelism</code></td>\n<td>RDDs only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slower run, <em>Waiting for resources</em></td>\n<td>Pools or serverless, not Spark tuning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>History older than 60 days</td>\n<td><code>system.lakeflow</code> tables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Who did what</td>\n<td><code>system.access.audit</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost</td>\n<td><code>system.billing.usage</code> × <code>list_prices</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expectation metrics</td>\n<td>Event log <code>flow_progress</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>R or RDD</td>\n<td>Classic, dedicated access mode</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scheduled job, lowest cost</td>\n<td>Serverless jobs or job compute, never all-purpose</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"governance-governance-privacy\">Governance (<a href=\"#/topic/governance\">governance</a>, <a href=\"#/topic/privacy\">privacy</a>)</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question says</th>\n<th>Think</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Read a table</td>\n<td><code>USE CATALOG</code> + <code>USE SCHEMA</code> + <code>SELECT</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Block one user with <code>DENY</code></td>\n<td>No <code>DENY</code> in Unity Catalog</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>REVOKE</code> on table, grant on schema</td>\n<td>Access remains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drop external table</td>\n<td>Metadata only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recover dropped managed table</td>\n<td><code>UNDROP</code> (7 days)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>External → managed</td>\n<td><code>ALTER TABLE ... SET MANAGED</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Metadata only</td>\n<td><code>BROWSE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manage grants without data</td>\n<td><code>MANAGE</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Volume files</td>\n<td><code>READ VOLUME</code> / <code>WRITE VOLUME</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One group sees a column</td>\n<td>Column mask, <code>is_account_group_member</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rows per region</td>\n<td>Row filter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One rule for all PII columns, owners cannot remove</td>\n<td>ABAC policy on governed tags</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reversible pseudonym</td>\n<td>Tokenization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>GDPR erasure</td>\n<td><code>DELETE</code> + purge + <code>VACUUM</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"numbers-to-remember\">Numbers to remember</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Associate</td>\n<td>45 scored questions, 90 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Professional</td>\n<td>60 scored questions, 120 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>VACUUM</code> default retention</td>\n<td>7 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Delta log retention</td>\n<td>30 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>UNDROP</code> window</td>\n<td>7 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto broadcast threshold</td>\n<td>10 MB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Default shuffle partitions (Spark)</td>\n<td>200 (Databricks: use auto)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Liquid clustering keys</td>\n<td>Up to 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Table update trigger</td>\n<td>Up to 10 tables</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>For each concurrency</td>\n<td>Up to 100</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Queued runs wait</td>\n<td>Up to 48 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Job runs in the UI</td>\n<td>About 60 days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Upload page</td>\n<td>10 files, under 2 GB</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Auto Loader inference sample</td>\n<td>50 GB or 1,000 files</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Notebook job output limit</td>\n<td>20 MB total, 8 MB per cell</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},"exam-day":{"title":"Exam day","html":"<p>The ticks on this page are saved in your browser.</p>\n<h2 id=\"when-you-book\">When you book</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[ ] Create or log in to your account on the exam delivery platform (Webassessor) and book the exam.</li>\n<li>[ ] For an online proctored exam, run the system check on the computer you will use, with the same network.</li>\n<li>[ ] Check that the name on your account matches your ID exactly.</li>\n<li>[ ] Download the current exam guide again two weeks before the date and compare it with the <a href=\"#/exam/A\">exam outline</a> on this site.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-day-before\">The day before</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[ ] No new material. Read the <a href=\"#/page/cheatsheet\">cheat sheet</a> once and review your missed questions.</li>\n<li>[ ] Prepare a clean desk: nothing on it except the computer, no second screen, no notes.</li>\n<li>[ ] Close every application that could start or notify during the exam; disable sleep and updates.</li>\n<li>[ ] Sleep.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"right-before-the-exam\">Right before the exam</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>[ ] Valid photo ID within reach.</li>\n<li>[ ] Laptop on power, webcam and microphone working, stable network.</li>\n<li>[ ] Water if allowed; phone out of reach.</li>\n<li>[ ] Log in 15 to 30 minutes early.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"pacing\">Pacing</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Associate</th>\n<th>Professional</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Questions on screen</td>\n<td>45 scored plus up to 10 unscored</td>\n<td>60 scored plus possible unscored</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>90 minutes</td>\n<td>120 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Budget per question</td>\n<td>About 1.5 minutes</td>\n<td>About 1.8 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Checkpoint</td>\n<td>Question 25 by minute 40</td>\n<td>Question 35 by minute 60</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Unscored questions are not marked, so treat every question as scored. The time for them is included.</p>\n<h2 id=\"during-the-exam\">During the exam</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Read the last sentence first.</strong> It tells you what is being asked: the cause, the fix, the cheapest option, the least privilege, the one that keeps history.</li>\n<li><strong>Find the deciding word.</strong> \"Incremental\", \"idempotent\", \"without copying\", \"lowest cost\", \"least privilege\", \"all current and future tables\", \"must not stop\" usually separate two plausible answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Eliminate.</strong> Two options are usually wrong at a glance: commands that do not exist, a tool from another category, or a fix for a different symptom.</li>\n<li><strong>Code questions</strong>: check write mode, NULL handling, 0- versus 1-based indexing, <code>union</code> versus <code>unionByName</code>, a missing <code>groupBy</code>, the trigger, and which decorator or clause is used.</li>\n<li><strong>Old names are not wrong.</strong> DLT, Workflows, Repos and Asset Bundles may still appear; do not discard an option only because of a name.</li>\n<li><strong>Flag and move on</strong> after about two minutes; come back at the end. Never leave a question unanswered: there is no penalty for wrong answers.</li>\n<li><strong>Multiple answers</strong>: when the question says \"choose two\", each option must be true on its own.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"after-the-exam\">After the exam</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The result appears at the end of the exam; the certificate and badge arrive by email afterwards.</li>\n<li>The certification is valid for two years. The Associate can be renewed with the renewal exam or the current full exam; the Professional by passing the current exam.</li>\n<li>Note the topics that felt weak while they are fresh: they are the starting point for the <a href=\"#/plan/P\">Professional plan</a>.</li>\n</ul>"},"resources":{"title":"Resources","html":"<p>Everything you need to study is on this site. The links below are the official sources it was written from, the places to practise hands-on, and optional extra material.</p>\n<h2 id=\"official-exam-information\">Official exam information</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Resource</th>\n<th>Use it for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Data-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf\">Associate exam guide (August 2026)</a></td>\n<td>The authoritative outline; recheck two weeks before the exam</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/data-engineer-associate\">Associate exam page</a></td>\n<td>Registration, recommended training, latest guide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Data-Engineer-Professional-Exam-Guide-9-Oct-2026.pdf\">Professional exam guide (9 October 2026)</a></td>\n<td>The authoritative outline for the Professional exam</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/data-engineer-professional\">Professional exam page</a></td>\n<td>Registration, recommended training, latest guide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.webassessor.com/databricks\">Exam registration (Webassessor)</a></td>\n<td>Booking, rescheduling, system check</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"hands-on-practice\">Hands-on practice</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Resource</th>\n<th>Use it for</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/getting-started/free-edition\">Databricks Free Edition</a></td>\n<td>A free, serverless-only workspace: every hands-on section on this site works there, except classic clusters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.databricks.com/\">Databricks documentation</a></td>\n<td>Syntax and behaviour when a detail matters</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2 id=\"training-courses\">Training courses</h2>\n<p>The exam pages recommend these Databricks Academy courses (<a href=\"https://customer-academy.databricks.com/\">Databricks Academy</a>; <a href=\"https://www.databricks.com/learn/training/data-engineering-courses\">course list</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Associate</strong>: Data Ingestion with Lakeflow Connect; Deploy Workloads with Lakeflow Jobs; Build Data Pipelines with Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines; DevOps Essentials for Data Engineering.</li>\n<li><strong>Professional</strong>: Databricks Streaming and Lakeflow Declarative Pipelines; Databricks Data Privacy; Databricks Performance Optimization; Automated Deployment with Databricks Asset Bundles.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Course titles may still use older product names.</p>\n<h2 id=\"optional-extra-practice\">Optional extra practice</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Resource</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.certsafari.com/databricks/data-engineer-associate\">CertSafari, Associate questions</a></td>\n<td>Practice by topic or in exam mode; check doubtful answers against the topics here</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0Hd5vYqin7w\">freeCodeCamp video course</a></td>\n<td>A long video walkthrough of the Associate material, if you prefer listening</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Many third-party questions online were written for older exam versions (DLT, Workflows, Repos, Asset Bundles, five sections). They still test useful concepts, but check the current names and the current outline.</p>\n<h2 id=\"about-this-site\">About this site</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Topic pages are written from the official exam guides and the Databricks documentation, in plain language, and cover both exams.</li>\n<li>Practice questions are original or adapted from practice exams; none comes from a real exam.</li>\n<li>Progress (ticked objectives, plan days, history, missed questions) is stored only in your browser.</li>\n<li>To edit content, change the Markdown and YAML files in the repository's <code>content/</code> folder and run <code>python3 build.py</code> (see the README).</li>\n</ul>"}},"plans":{"A":{"title":"Associate: 25 November to 11 December","intro":"<p>Sixteen study days before the exam on 11 December, ordered by exam weight and by how topics build on each other. Weekdays take about two hours; weekends and the 1 and 8 December public holidays take four to five. Each day: read the topic, do its hands-on exercise in Databricks Free Edition, then practise. From 5 December the plan switches to timed mocks and review. Tick a day when it is done; if you fall behind, skip hands-on steps before skipping practice.</p>","days":[{"date":"2026-11-25","focus":"Platform and compute (S1)","tasks":"Read the topic. Create a Free Edition account, run the hands-on steps, and learn the old and new product names by heart.","hours":"2 h","topics":["platform"],"practice":"section:A1"},{"date":"2026-11-26","focus":"Delta Lake fundamentals","tasks":"Transaction log, writes (<code>INSERT</code>, overwrite, <code>CREATE OR REPLACE</code>, <code>MERGE</code>), schema enforcement and evolution, history, <code>RESTORE</code>, <code>VACUUM</code>. Run the hands-on script.","hours":"2 h","topics":["delta"],"practice":"topic:delta"},{"date":"2026-11-27","focus":"Ingestion (S2) part 1, files","tasks":"Upload limits, volumes, <code>read_files</code>, <code>COPY INTO</code> (idempotency, <code>force</code>, <code>mergeSchema</code>), Auto Loader (checkpoint, schema location, evolution modes, triggers). Run both loaders twice.","hours":"2 h","topics":["ingest-files"],"practice":"topic:ingest-files"},{"date":"2026-11-28","focus":"Ingestion (S2) part 2 and transformations (S3) part 1","tasks":"Morning: Lakeflow Connect, JDBC and REST patterns, choosing a method, JSON paths, VARIANT and <code>explode</code>; skim the federation section of the sharing topic; practise S2. Afternoon: columns, types and ANSI mode, NULL rules, joins, unions, deduplication, aggregations and windows, with the five <code>samples.tpch</code> exercises.","hours":"5 h","topics":["ingest-sources","semi-structured","sharing","transform"],"practice":"section:A2"},{"date":"2026-11-29","focus":"Transformations (S3) part 2, medallion and pipelines","tasks":"Bronze, silver, gold; facts and dimensions; views, materialized views and streaming tables; expectations and quarantine. Build the pipeline from the hands-on section, then practise S3, reading every code option line by line.","hours":"5 h","topics":["modeling","pipelines"],"practice":"section:A3"},{"date":"2026-11-30","focus":"Spark tuning and table layout","tasks":"The tuning settings table and the Spark UI section of the performance topic. Then liquid clustering, <code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code>, <code>OPTIMIZE</code>, deletion vectors and predictive optimization, with the layout hands-on script.","hours":"2 h","topics":["performance","layout"],"practice":"topic:layout"},{"date":"2026-12-01","focus":"Lakeflow Jobs (S4)","tasks":"Task types, parameters and task values, Run if, If/else, For each, retries and timeouts, triggers, repair runs. Build the three-task job from the hands-on section and repair it.","hours":"5 h","topics":["jobs"],"practice":"section:A4"},{"date":"2026-12-02","focus":"Governance (S7) part 1","tasks":"Hierarchy, managed versus external, privileges and inheritance, <code>REVOKE</code> behaviour, no <code>DENY</code>. Write the least-privilege grants of the topic from memory.","hours":"2 h","topics":["governance"],"practice":"topic:governance"},{"date":"2026-12-03","focus":"Governance (S7) part 2","tasks":"Row filters, column masks, dynamic views, ABAC policies with governed tags. Apply a mask and a filter in Free Edition.","hours":"2 h","topics":["privacy"],"practice":"section:A7"},{"date":"2026-12-04","focus":"CI/CD (S5)","tasks":"Git folders, bundle structure, targets and variables, deploy modes, CLI commands and authentication. Initialise, validate, deploy and run a bundle.","hours":"2 h","topics":["cicd"],"practice":"section:A5"},{"date":"2026-12-05","focus":"Troubleshooting (S6) and Mock 1","tasks":"Morning: monitoring topic (run history, statuses) and the problems table of the performance topic. Afternoon: Mock 1 under exam conditions: 45 questions, 90 minutes, no notes.","hours":"5 h","topics":["monitoring","performance"],"practice":"mock:1"},{"date":"2026-12-06","focus":"Review Mock 1","tasks":"For every miss, write down the question, your answer and the misconception. Reread the two weakest sections' topics and practise the missed questions.","hours":"4 h","topics":[],"practice":"missed:A"},{"date":"2026-12-07","focus":"Mock 2","tasks":"Timed, then reviewed the same way.","hours":"2.5 h","topics":[],"practice":"mock:2"},{"date":"2026-12-08","focus":"Mock 3 and weak spots","tasks":"Timed mock in the morning; afternoon on the topics behind the misses, with their hands-on exercises.","hours":"4 h","topics":[],"practice":"mock:3"},{"date":"2026-12-09","focus":"Mock 4","tasks":"Aim for 80% or more with at least 15 minutes to spare. Review every miss.","hours":"2.5 h","topics":[],"practice":"mock:4"},{"date":"2026-12-10","focus":"Light review","tasks":"Cheat sheet, flashcards for the weakest topics, the missed questions, and the exam-day checklist. Stop early and sleep well.","hours":"1.5 h","topics":[],"practice":"missed:A"},{"date":"2026-12-11","focus":"Exam day","tasks":"Follow the exam-day checklist. No new material.","hours":"","topics":[]}]},"P":{"title":"Professional: four weeks","intro":"<p>Start after the Associate exam. The Professional exam goes deeper on streaming, CDC, testing, deployment, monitoring, cost, privacy and modeling, and its questions are longer scenarios. Weeks 1 to 3 cover the topics in the order they build on each other; week 4 is timed mocks. Topic pages already include the Associate material, so reread them with the Professional objectives (exam outline page) in mind.</p>","weeks":[{"title":"Week 1: platform, storage and ingestion at production depth","days":[{"focus":"Exam outline and compute environments","tasks":"Read the Professional outline. Platform topic, focusing on libraries, serverless environments, performance modes, auto-optimization retries and high memory.","hours":"2 h","topics":["platform"],"practice":"topic:platform"},{"focus":"Delta and table layout","tasks":"Concurrency, clones, <code>replaceWhere</code>, retention properties; liquid clustering versus partitioning and Z-order, deletion vectors, predictive optimization, disk cache.","hours":"2 h","topics":["delta","layout"],"practice":"topic:layout"},{"focus":"Production ingestion","tasks":"Auto Loader rate limits and file events, Kafka and <code>read_kafka</code>, Lakeflow Connect database connectors (gateway, CDC prerequisites), formats including binary files.","hours":"2 h","topics":["ingest-files","ingest-sources"],"practice":"section:P2"},{"focus":"Sharing, Clean Rooms and Federation","tasks":"D2D versus open sharing, <code>WITH HISTORY</code>, clean rooms, connections and foreign catalogs.","hours":"2 h","topics":["sharing"],"practice":"topic:sharing"},{"focus":"Data manipulation at scale","tasks":"Windows and ranking, VARIANT functions, joins and aggregations on large data.","hours":"2 h","topics":["semi-structured","transform"],"practice":"section:P3"},{"focus":"UDFs and AI functions","tasks":"SQL, Python and pandas UDFs, Unity Catalog functions, iterator UDFs, <code>ai_query</code> and task-specific AI functions.","hours":"3 h","topics":["udf-ai"],"practice":"topic:udf-ai"},{"focus":"Review week 1","tasks":"Flashcards for this week's topics and the missed questions.","hours":"1 h","topics":[],"practice":"missed:P"}]},{"title":"Week 2: pipelines, streaming, CDC and modeling","days":[{"focus":"Declarative pipelines in depth","tasks":"Streaming table versus materialized view, append flows, full refresh risks, event log, pipelines versus Structured Streaming.","hours":"2 h","topics":["pipelines"],"practice":"topic:pipelines"},{"focus":"Stateful Structured Streaming","tasks":"Output modes, watermarks, deduplication, stream-stream joins, <code>foreachBatch</code> idempotency, monitoring progress.","hours":"2 h","topics":["streaming"],"practice":"topic:streaming"},{"focus":"CDC, AUTO CDC and Change Data Feed","tasks":"SCD types, <code>SEQUENCE BY</code>, <code>TRACK HISTORY ON</code>, snapshots, CDF reads and retention.","hours":"2 h","topics":["cdc"],"practice":"topic:cdc"},{"focus":"Data modeling","tasks":"Star schemas, surrogate keys, materialized views versus metric views, <code>MEASURE()</code>.","hours":"2 h","topics":["modeling"],"practice":"topic:modeling"},{"focus":"Lakeflow Jobs in depth","tasks":"Control flow, For each concurrency, repair with parameter overrides, API, CLI and SDK automation.","hours":"2 h","topics":["jobs"],"practice":"topic:jobs"},{"focus":"Section 1 practice","tasks":"Section 1 is 23% of the exam. Practise it and reread the topics behind every miss.","hours":"3 h","topics":[],"practice":"section:P1"},{"focus":"Review week 2","tasks":"Flashcards and missed questions.","hours":"1 h","topics":[],"practice":"missed:P"}]},{"title":"Week 3: DevOps, operations and security","days":[{"focus":"CI/CD with bundles","tasks":"Project structure and wheels, targets, variables and lookups, modes, bind, lifecycle, CI authentication with federation.","hours":"2 h","topics":["cicd"],"practice":"section:P8"},{"focus":"Testing","tasks":"Pure functions, <code>DataFrame.transform</code>, <code>assertDataFrameEqual</code>, <code>assertSchemaEqual</code>, integration tests with a test target.","hours":"2 h","topics":["testing"],"practice":"topic:testing"},{"focus":"Monitoring and alerting","tasks":"System tables (billing, compute, query history, lakeflow, audit), event logs, SQL alerts, health rules, SDK and CLI monitoring.","hours":"2 h","topics":["monitoring"],"practice":"section:P4"},{"focus":"Cost and performance","tasks":"Query profile, skew, spill, broadcast, caching, Change Data Feed for incremental processing, layout choices.","hours":"2 h","topics":["performance","layout"],"practice":"section:P5"},{"focus":"Governance","tasks":"Least privilege, inheritance, <code>MANAGE</code> and <code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code>, workspace ACLs, catalog bindings, tags, comments, <code>information_schema</code>.","hours":"2 h","topics":["governance"],"practice":"section:P7"},{"focus":"Privacy and compliance","tasks":"ABAC with governed tags, PII detection and masking in pipelines, hashing, tokenization, generalization, GDPR erasure and retention.","hours":"3 h","topics":["privacy"],"practice":"section:P6"},{"focus":"Review week 3","tasks":"Flashcards and missed questions; reread the cheat sheet.","hours":"1 h","topics":[],"practice":"missed:P"}]},{"title":"Week 4: mocks","days":[{"focus":"Mock 1","tasks":"60 questions in 120 minutes, exam conditions.","hours":"2.5 h","topics":[],"practice":"mock:1"},{"focus":"Review Mock 1","tasks":"Analyse every miss; reread the weakest sections' topics.","hours":"2 h","topics":[],"practice":"missed:P"},{"focus":"Mock 2","tasks":"Timed; review the misses the same evening.","hours":"3 h","topics":[],"practice":"mock:2"},{"focus":"Mock 3","tasks":"Timed; then the topics behind the misses.","hours":"3 h","topics":[],"practice":"mock:3"},{"focus":"Random mock","tasks":"A new weighted draw from the whole bank.","hours":"2.5 h","topics":[],"practice":"random:P"},{"focus":"Sprint mock and misses","tasks":"Same number of questions with less time, then the missed questions.","hours":"2.5 h","topics":[],"practice":"sprint:P"},{"focus":"Light review","tasks":"Cheat sheet, exam-day checklist, rest.","hours":"1 h","topics":[]}]}]}},"flashcards":[{"topic":"platform","front":"Where does the control plane run?","back":"Always in the Databricks account. Classic compute runs in your cloud account; serverless compute in a Databricks-managed account."},{"topic":"platform","front":"How many Unity Catalog metastores per region?","back":"One, attached to every workspace in that region. Grants live in the metastore, so they apply in all those workspaces."},{"topic":"platform","front":"What does serverless compute not support?","back":"R, the RDD API, init scripts, the Spark UI (use the query profile), most Spark settings, and endless streaming triggers."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Most expensive compute, never for scheduled production jobs?","back":"All-purpose compute."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Workload uses R or the RDD API","back":"Classic compute in <strong>dedicated</strong> access mode (standard access mode supports neither)."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Library precedence","back":"Notebook-scoped (<code>%pip</code>) &gt; compute-scoped (cluster or task libraries) &gt; built into the runtime."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Job breaks after a library release, code unchanged","back":"Unpinned dependency. Pin versions with <code>==</code> (for example in a requirements file)."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Dependencies for serverless Python script, wheel or dbt tasks","back":"An <code>environments</code> entry in the job, referenced by the task's <code>environment_key</code>."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Serverless task must run at most once","back":"Untick <em>Enable serverless auto-optimization</em> in the task's retry policy (it may add retries)."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Cheaper serverless job, start-up delay acceptable","back":"Turn off <em>Performance optimized</em> (standard mode)."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Cluster fails with <code>INIT_SCRIPT_FAILURE</code>","back":"Read the init script logs in the cluster log delivery path (<code>&lt;log path&gt;/&lt;cluster id&gt;/init_scripts/</code>)."},{"topic":"platform","front":"Current names of DLT, Workflows, Asset Bundles, Repos, Delta Sharing","back":"Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines, Lakeflow Jobs, Declarative Automation Bundles, Git folders, OpenSharing."},{"topic":"delta","front":"What gives Delta Lake ACID transactions?","back":"The <code>_delta_log</code> transaction log: each commit is atomic and readers see only committed versions."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Replace data and schema, keep history and grants","back":"<code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ... AS SELECT</code> (or overwrite with <code>overwriteSchema</code>)."},{"topic":"delta","front":"<code>INSERT OVERWRITE</code> with a changed schema","back":"Fails: overwrite keeps the existing schema."},{"topic":"delta","front":"<code>mergeSchema</code> vs <code>overwriteSchema</code>","back":"<code>mergeSchema</code> adds new columns (append or overwrite). <code>overwriteSchema</code> replaces the whole schema (overwrite only)."},{"topic":"delta","front":"MERGE fails with <code>DELTA_MULTIPLE_SOURCE_ROW_MATCHING_TARGET_ROW_IN_MERGE</code>","back":"Several source rows per key. Keep the latest row per key before merging."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Delete target rows missing from the source in a MERGE","back":"<code>WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE</code>."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Does <code>RESTORE</code> delete later versions?","back":"No. It writes a new commit with the old state; history keeps everything."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Default retention: log and deleted files","back":"<code>delta.logRetentionDuration</code> 30 days (history). <code>delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration</code> 7 days (what <code>VACUUM</code> may delete)."},{"topic":"delta","front":"What does <code>VACUUM</code> do to time travel?","back":"Deletes unreferenced files older than the retention, so older versions can no longer be read or restored."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Rename or drop a column","back":"Enable column mapping: <code>'delta.columnMapping.mode' = 'name'</code>. Metadata only; can break streams."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Make a table insert-only","back":"<code>delta.appendOnly = true</code>."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Cheap writable copy of a big table for tests","back":"<code>SHALLOW CLONE</code> (metadata only). <code>DEEP CLONE</code> copies the data."},{"topic":"delta","front":"Overwrite only one day of a table atomically","back":"<code>replaceWhere</code> (SQL: <code>INSERT INTO t REPLACE WHERE ...</code>)."},{"topic":"layout","front":"Many small files make reads slow","back":"<code>OPTIMIZE</code> (plus optimized writes, auto compaction or predictive optimization)."},{"topic":"layout","front":"Changed liquid clustering keys; old data still badly laid out","back":"<code>OPTIMIZE t FULL</code>."},{"topic":"layout","front":"<code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code> requires","back":"A Unity Catalog managed table with predictive optimization."},{"topic":"layout","front":"What predictive optimization runs","back":"<code>OPTIMIZE</code>, <code>VACUUM</code> and <code>ANALYZE</code>, on Unity Catalog managed tables only. Never Z-order."},{"topic":"layout","front":"Liquid clustering combined with partitioning or Z-order?","back":"Not allowed. Liquid clustering replaces both; up to 4 keys."},{"topic":"layout","front":"Filter column with millions of distinct values","back":"Liquid clustering key. Never a partition column."},{"topic":"layout","front":"Frequent single-row updates rewrite whole files","back":"Deletion vectors."},{"topic":"layout","front":"Rows inserted after the last <code>OPTIMIZE</code> on a clustered table","back":"Stay unclustered until the next <code>OPTIMIZE</code> (manual or predictive optimization)."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Millions of files, schema that changes","back":"Auto Loader (<code>cloudFiles</code>) with schema evolution."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"<code>COPY INTO</code> run twice with no new files","back":"Loads nothing: already loaded files are skipped (idempotent), even if modified."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Reload a corrected file with the same name in <code>COPY INTO</code>","back":"Delete its old rows, then <code>FILES = ('name')</code> with <code>COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')</code>."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"<code>FORMAT_OPTIONS</code> vs <code>COPY_OPTIONS</code> <code>mergeSchema</code>","back":"FORMAT: schema read from the files. COPY: lets the target table gain columns."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Auto Loader modes when a new column appears","back":"<code>addNewColumns</code>: fail, then evolve on restart. <code>rescue</code>: to <code>_rescued_data</code>, never evolves. <code>failOnNewColumns</code>: fail until fixed. <code>none</code>: ignore."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Default Auto Loader schema evolution mode","back":"Without a schema: <code>addNewColumns</code>. With a schema: <code>none</code>."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"<code>cloudFiles.schemaLocation</code> vs <code>checkpointLocation</code>","back":"Schema location: inferred schema and its history. Checkpoint: which files were processed."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Auto Loader types for JSON and CSV","back":"STRING by default (use <code>inferColumnTypes</code> or <code>schemaHints</code>). <code>read_files</code> infers types."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Auto Loader on millions of files: listing is slow","back":"File notification with managed file events (<code>cloudFiles.useManagedFileEvents</code>); same checkpoint."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Upload page limits","back":"Up to 10 files, under 2 GB in total, no archives. Bigger: upload to a volume first."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Source file path of each row","back":"<code>_metadata.file_path</code>."},{"topic":"ingest-files","front":"Limit micro-batches by volume","back":"<code>cloudFiles.maxBytesPerTrigger</code> (by count: <code>maxFilesPerTrigger</code>)."},{"topic":"ingest-sources","front":"Salesforce or Workday without API code","back":"Lakeflow Connect managed connector."},{"topic":"ingest-sources","front":"Database connector components","back":"An always-on ingestion gateway (classic compute, stages snapshots and change logs) + a serverless ingestion pipeline."},{"topic":"ingest-sources","front":"Use connector credentials without seeing them","back":"A Unity Catalog connection plus <code>USE CONNECTION</code>."},{"topic":"ingest-sources","front":"JDBC <code>lowerBound</code> / <code>upperBound</code>","back":"Only set the partition stride; they do not filter rows."},{"topic":"ingest-sources","front":"ODBC driver installed by an init script","back":"Needs classic compute; serverless does not run init scripts."},{"topic":"ingest-sources","front":"Full refresh of a Kafka bronze table","back":"Loses data Kafka no longer holds. Protect with <code>pipelines.reset.allowed = false</code>."},{"topic":"ingest-sources","front":"Deleting a managed ingestion pipeline","back":"Deletes the tables it created."},{"topic":"semi-structured","front":"Extract a field from a JSON string column","back":"<code>raw:customer.address.city</code>. Dot notation is for STRUCT columns."},{"topic":"semi-structured","front":"Best storage for JSON whose shape changes","back":"VARIANT (<code>parse_json</code>)."},{"topic":"semi-structured","front":"<code>[*]</code> wildcard on VARIANT","back":"Not supported: use <code>LATERAL variant_explode(v:items)</code>. VARIANT paths are case sensitive."},{"topic":"semi-structured","front":"<code>GROUP BY</code> on a VARIANT path","back":"Cast first: <code>v:country::STRING</code>."},{"topic":"semi-structured","front":"<code>explode</code> vs <code>explode_outer</code>","back":"<code>explode</code> drops NULL or empty arrays; <code>explode_outer</code> keeps those rows with NULL."},{"topic":"semi-structured","front":"JSON string to STRUCT","back":"<code>from_json(col, schema)</code> (schema required; <code>schema_of_json</code> can derive it)."},{"topic":"semi-structured","front":"Ingest PDFs and images incrementally","back":"Auto Loader with <code>cloudFiles.format = binaryFile</code> (path, modificationTime, length, content)."},{"topic":"sharing","front":"Share with a partner on Databricks with Unity Catalog","back":"D2D sharing using their metastore sharing identifier <code>cloud:region:metastore-id</code>."},{"topic":"sharing","front":"Share with a partner without Databricks","back":"Open sharing (token or OIDC federation)."},{"topic":"sharing","front":"Query PostgreSQL without copying data","back":"Lakehouse Federation: connection + foreign catalog + grants."},{"topic":"sharing","front":"Two companies must match customers without seeing each other's data","back":"A clean room with approved notebooks."},{"topic":"sharing","front":"Cross-cloud sharing egress costs","back":"Depend on where the data is stored, not the sharing mode. Host data where egress is free or replicate."},{"topic":"sharing","front":"Recipients must time travel and stream a shared table","back":"<code>ALTER SHARE s ADD TABLE t WITH HISTORY</code>."},{"topic":"transform","front":"PySpark <code>union</code> vs SQL <code>UNION</code>","back":"<code>union</code> keeps duplicates and matches by position. SQL <code>UNION</code> removes duplicates."},{"topic":"transform","front":"Stack DataFrames with different column order or missing columns","back":"<code>unionByName(other, allowMissingColumns=True)</code>."},{"topic":"transform","front":"<code>x = NULL</code>","back":"Never true. Use <code>IS NULL</code>, <code>isNull()</code>, or null-safe <code>&lt;=&gt;</code>."},{"topic":"transform","front":"<code>left_semi</code> vs <code>left_anti</code>","back":"Left rows that have / do not have a match. Both return left columns only."},{"topic":"transform","front":"Latest record per key","back":"<code>row_number()</code> over a window partitioned by key, ordered by time desc (plus tiebreaker); keep 1. SQL: <code>QUALIFY</code>."},{"topic":"transform","front":"<code>count(col)</code> vs <code>count(*)</code>","back":"<code>count(col)</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>avg</code> ignore NULLs; <code>count(*)</code> counts every row."},{"topic":"transform","front":"<code>fillna(0)</code>","back":"Fills only numeric columns. Use a dict for several types."},{"topic":"transform","front":"Invalid cast with ANSI mode on","back":"Fails. Use <code>try_cast</code>, <code>try_to_date</code>, <code>try_element_at</code>."},{"topic":"transform","front":"Date pattern letters <code>MM</code> and <code>mm</code>","back":"<code>MM</code> = month, <code>mm</code> = minute."},{"topic":"transform","front":"Array indexing","back":"<code>[i]</code> and <code>getItem</code> are 0-based; <code>element_at</code> is 1-based."},{"topic":"transform","front":"<code>row_number</code> vs <code>rank</code> vs <code>dense_rank</code>","back":"1,2,3,4 / 1,1,3,4 / 1,1,2,3."},{"topic":"transform","front":"Running total per customer","back":"Window partitioned by customer, ordered by time, <code>rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)</code>."},{"topic":"transform","front":"Cheap distinct count on huge data","back":"<code>approx_count_distinct</code> (HyperLogLog)."},{"topic":"transform","front":"<code>describe()</code> vs <code>summary()</code>","back":"<code>summary()</code> adds the 25%, 50% and 75% percentiles."},{"topic":"udf-ai","front":"UDF speed order","back":"Built-in &gt; SQL UDF &gt; pandas UDF &gt; plain Python UDF."},{"topic":"udf-ai","front":"Why pandas UDFs are faster","back":"Arrow batches and vectorised pandas code instead of one call per row."},{"topic":"udf-ai","front":"Reusable, governed function for all workspaces","back":"Unity Catalog function (<code>CREATE FUNCTION cat.sch.f ...</code>) with <code>EXECUTE</code> grants."},{"topic":"udf-ai","front":"Load a model once in a pandas UDF","back":"Iterator of Series to Iterator of Series."},{"topic":"udf-ai","front":"Classify text into fixed labels in SQL","back":"<code>ai_classify(text, ARRAY('a', 'b'))</code>. Any served model: <code>ai_query</code>."},{"topic":"udf-ai","front":"<code>ai_query</code> should not fail the whole batch","back":"<code>failOnError =&gt; false</code> returns result and error per row."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Streaming table vs materialized view","back":"Streaming table: incremental, each row once (append). Materialized view: keeps a query result current (aggregates, joins, latest state)."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Expectation without <code>ON VIOLATION</code>","back":"Keeps the row and records the violation (warn)."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"<code>DROP ROW</code> vs <code>FAIL UPDATE</code>","back":"DROP removes invalid rows and continues. FAIL stops the update and rolls back."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Keep rejected rows and the reason","back":"A quarantine table with the opposite, NULL-safe condition."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Python expectation decorators","back":"<code>@dp.expect</code>, <code>@dp.expect_or_drop</code>, <code>@dp.expect_or_fail</code> (and <code>expect_all...</code> to group)."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Rule enforced for every writer of a table","back":"Delta <code>CHECK</code> / <code>NOT NULL</code> constraint (expectations only work inside the pipeline)."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Materialized view refresh runs as","back":"The owner. Make a service principal the owner."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Changing a streaming table's query","back":"Applies to new rows only, until a full refresh."},{"topic":"pipelines","front":"Add a source to a streaming table without a full refresh","back":"Another append flow targeting the same table."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"<code>trigger(availableNow=True)</code>","back":"Process everything available in rate-limited batches, then stop."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"No trigger on serverless","back":"Fails (<code>INFINITE_STREAMING_TRIGGER_NOT_SUPPORTED</code>). Use <code>availableNow</code>."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"Output modes","back":"append (new final rows), complete (whole result), update (changed rows; not supported by the Delta sink)."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"Streaming upsert into Delta","back":"<code>foreachBatch</code> + <code>MERGE</code>."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"Watermark","back":"Lateness threshold: lets Spark finalise windows and drop old state; later events are ignored."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"Duplicates minutes apart vs days apart","back":"<code>dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark</code> vs insert-only <code>MERGE</code> in <code>foreachBatch</code>."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"Idempotent appends in <code>foreachBatch</code>","back":"Delta write options <code>txnAppId</code> + <code>txnVersion</code> (= batch_id)."},{"topic":"streaming","front":"Delta stream source with updates and deletes","back":"Fails on change commits. <code>skipChangeCommits</code> ignores them; Change Data Feed processes them."},{"topic":"cdc","front":"AUTO CDC default SCD type","back":"Type 1. Add <code>STORED AS SCD TYPE 2</code> for history (<code>__START_AT</code>, <code>__END_AT</code>)."},{"topic":"cdc","front":"Out-of-order CDC events","back":"<code>SEQUENCE BY</code> the event's logical order."},{"topic":"cdc","front":"Only some columns should create SCD2 versions","back":"<code>TRACK HISTORY ON * EXCEPT (...)</code>."},{"topic":"cdc","front":"Source only gives full snapshots","back":"<code>AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT</code>."},{"topic":"cdc","front":"Read row-level changes of a Delta table","back":"Change Data Feed: <code>table_changes()</code> or <code>readChangeFeed</code>; <code>_change_type</code> = insert, update_preimage, update_postimage, delete."},{"topic":"cdc","front":"Is Change Data Feed retroactive?","back":"No. It records changes from the version where it was enabled."},{"topic":"cdc","front":"Full refresh of an SCD type 2 table","back":"Loses the history the source no longer holds."},{"topic":"modeling","front":"Bronze, silver, gold","back":"Raw as received / clean, deduplicated, conformed / business-ready aggregates and models."},{"topic":"modeling","front":"Keep years of raw events cheaply when the source keeps 7 days","back":"Land them in bronze Delta tables."},{"topic":"modeling","front":"Fact vs dimension","back":"Fact: measures + foreign keys, grows fast. Dimension: descriptive attributes, changes slowly."},{"topic":"modeling","front":"Each past sale shows the address valid at the time","back":"SCD type 2 dimension with surrogate keys."},{"topic":"modeling","front":"Metric view vs materialized view","back":"Metric view: governed measure definitions computed at any grain (<code>MEASURE()</code>). MV: precomputed results at a fixed grain."},{"topic":"modeling","front":"Users read a view but not its tables","back":"Grant <code>SELECT</code> on the view; Unity Catalog uses the view owner's privileges on the sources."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Cleanup task that always runs","back":"Run if <code>ALL_DONE</code>."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Alert only when something failed","back":"Run if <code>AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED</code>."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Skip the transform when no new data arrived","back":"Task value with the row count + If/else condition (<code>&gt; 0</code>). Run if reacts to outcomes, not data."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"If/else <code>==</code> and <code>!=</code>","back":"Compare strings (<code>0</code> ≠ <code>0.0</code>). <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code> compare numbers."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Pass a value between tasks","back":"<code>dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set/get</code>, or <code>{{tasks.&lt;task&gt;.values.&lt;key&gt;}}</code>."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Job parameter vs task parameter with the same key","back":"The job parameter wins."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Same notebook for each item of a list","back":"For each task (<code>{{input}}</code>, concurrency up to 100)."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Quartz cron for 06:30 daily","back":"<code>0 30 6 * * ?</code> (seconds first), with a time zone."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"File arrival trigger and overwritten files","back":"Only new files trigger a run."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Table update trigger never fires","back":"The run-as identity lacks <code>SELECT</code> on the table."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Continuous jobs","back":"Always one run active; no task dependencies or retry policies; restarts with exponential backoff."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Timeout vs duration warning","back":"Timeout kills the attempt (Timed Out). Warning only notifies."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Rerun only what failed","back":"Repair run (can override parameters). Appending tasks may duplicate."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Job with a pipeline task: concurrent runs","back":"At most 1."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Concurrency limit reached, queueing off","back":"The new run is Skipped."},{"topic":"jobs","front":"Make a notebook task fail","back":"Raise an exception. <code>dbutils.notebook.exit()</code> ends successfully."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"Bundle lifecycle","back":"<code>init</code> → <code>validate</code> → <code>plan</code> → <code>deploy -t &lt;target&gt;</code> → <code>run</code> → <code>destroy</code>."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"When are bundle variables resolved?","back":"At deploy. Per-run values: job parameters (<code>bundle run --params</code>)."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"Bundle variable precedence","back":"<code>--var</code> &gt; <code>BUNDLE_VAR_x</code> &gt; overrides file &gt; target &gt; default."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"<code>mode: development</code>","back":"<code>[dev user]</code> prefix, schedules and triggers paused, concurrent runs allowed."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"<code>mode: production</code>","back":"Checks the Git branch (<code>git.branch</code>), expects <code>run_as</code> and a shared root path."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"Adopt a job created in the UI","back":"<code>bundle generate job --existing-job-id &lt;id&gt;</code> + <code>bundle deployment bind</code> (else a duplicate is created)."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"Resource removed from bundle YAML","back":"Deleted on the next deploy (protect with <code>lifecycle.prevent_destroy</code>)."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"CI/CD authentication","back":"Service principal with OAuth M2M or, better, OIDC workload identity federation."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"Where is the pull request opened?","back":"In the Git provider, not in Databricks."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"Branch, pull and push in a Git folder need","back":"CAN MANAGE. One Git folder per person."},{"topic":"cicd","front":"Destroy a bundle in CI","back":"<code>databricks bundle destroy -t test --auto-approve</code>."},{"topic":"testing","front":"Compare two DataFrames in a unit test","back":"<code>pyspark.testing.assertDataFrameEqual</code> (ignores row order by default)."},{"topic":"testing","front":"Assert a schema","back":"<code>pyspark.testing.assertSchemaEqual</code>."},{"topic":"testing","front":"Make transformations testable","back":"Pure functions DataFrame → DataFrame, I/O at the edges, chained with <code>DataFrame.transform</code>."},{"topic":"testing","front":"Integration test of a deployed job","back":"Deploy the bundle to a test target, run the job on test data, raise on failed checks."},{"topic":"testing","front":"Tests vs expectations","back":"Tests check code before deployment; expectations check data at runtime."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Job runs older than about 60 days","back":"<code>system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline</code> and related tables."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Matrix view","back":"Bar height = run duration; cells = task statuses."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Slower run, extra time in <em>Waiting for resources</em>","back":"Compute acquisition: pools, serverless, or fewer separate jobs. Not a code problem."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"<em>Succeeded with failures</em>","back":"Only non-leaf tasks failed; the final state comes from leaf tasks."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"<em>Upstream failed</em>","back":"Walk up the graph to the first failed task."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Who accessed what","back":"<code>system.access.audit</code>."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Cost per SKU or job","back":"<code>system.billing.usage</code> (<code>usage_quantity</code>) joined with <code>system.billing.list_prices</code>."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Expectation pass and fail counts","back":"Pipeline event log, <code>flow_progress</code> events, <code>details.flow_progress.data_quality.expectations</code>."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Alert on two conditions","back":"Compute one value in the query (<code>CASE WHEN a AND b THEN 1 ELSE 0 END</code>) and alert on it."},{"topic":"monitoring","front":"Freshness and volume checks across a schema","back":"Anomaly detection (data quality monitoring)."},{"topic":"performance","front":"One task much slower than the median","back":"Skew. AQE skew join (joins only) or salting. More workers do not help."},{"topic":"performance","front":"Aggregation skew with AQE on","back":"AQE does not split aggregations: salt the key, aggregate twice."},{"topic":"performance","front":"Spill to disk","back":"Partitions too big for memory: more shuffle partitions (or auto), bigger nodes, fewer columns."},{"topic":"performance","front":"Driver out of memory","back":"<code>collect()</code>, <code>toPandas()</code>, large broadcasts. Do not bring data to the driver; bigger driver."},{"topic":"performance","front":"<code>spark.default.parallelism</code>","back":"RDDs only. DataFrame shuffles use <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code> (or auto)."},{"topic":"performance","front":"Auto broadcast threshold","back":"<code>spark.sql.autoBroadcastJoinThreshold</code> = 10 MB by default; -1 disables; hints override."},{"topic":"performance","front":"No Spark UI on serverless","back":"Use the query profile."},{"topic":"performance","front":"<code>coalesce</code> vs <code>repartition</code>","back":"<code>coalesce</code> reduces partitions without a full shuffle; <code>repartition</code> shuffles."},{"topic":"performance","front":"Disk cache","back":"Copies of remote files on workers' local SSDs; speeds repeated reads; detects changes automatically."},{"topic":"performance","front":"High CPU time vs task time","back":"CPU-bound: more or faster cores. Low CPU time: tasks are waiting."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Read a table","back":"<code>USE CATALOG</code> + <code>USE SCHEMA</code> + <code>SELECT</code>."},{"topic":"governance","front":"<code>DENY</code> in Unity Catalog","back":"Does not exist. Not granted = denied."},{"topic":"governance","front":"<code>REVOKE</code> on a table when the grant was on the schema","back":"No effect: revoke where it was granted."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Grant inheritance","back":"From catalog and schema to all current and future objects. Metastore privileges do not inherit."},{"topic":"governance","front":"<code>DROP TABLE</code> managed vs external","back":"Managed: data deleted (<code>UNDROP</code> for 7 days). External: metadata only."},{"topic":"governance","front":"External to managed in place","back":"<code>ALTER TABLE t SET MANAGED</code> (keeps name, history, grants)."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Metadata without data access","back":"<code>BROWSE</code>."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Administer grants without data access","back":"<code>MANAGE</code>. <code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> does not include <code>MANAGE</code>."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Volume file access","back":"<code>READ VOLUME</code> / <code>WRITE VOLUME</code> (external locations: <code>READ FILES</code> / <code>WRITE FILES</code>)."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Use a function or mask","back":"<code>EXECUTE</code>."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Grant to whom?","back":"Account groups and service principals, not individuals or workspace-local groups."},{"topic":"governance","front":"Catalog only reachable from the production workspace","back":"Workspace-catalog binding."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Only one group sees a column's real values","back":"Column mask with <code>is_account_group_member</code>."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Each team sees only its rows","back":"Row filter: <code>ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER f ON (col)</code>."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"One central rule for PII columns in all current and future tables","back":"ABAC policy with governed tags (<code>CREATE POLICY ... MATCH COLUMNS has_tag_value(...)</code>)."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Do column tags inherit from the table?","back":"No. <code>MATCH COLUMNS</code> only sees tags set on columns."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Do ABAC policies grant access?","back":"No. They only change what users see in tables they can already read."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Dropped the mask function first","back":"The table becomes unreadable. Drop the mask or filter before the function."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Reading masked tables on dedicated compute","back":"Databricks Runtime 15.4 LTS or later with serverless enabled (or a SQL warehouse, or standard access mode)."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Reversible for a few, opaque for others","back":"Tokenization (mapping table under strict access)."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Hashing PII safely","back":"Salted hash (salt in a secret scope), overwriting the original column."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"Quasi-identifiers (birth date, postcode)","back":"Generalize (year, age band, district) or suppress."},{"topic":"privacy","front":"GDPR erasure on Delta","back":"<code>DELETE</code> in every layer + <code>REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)</code> + <code>VACUUM</code>; mind time travel and change feed files."}],"questions":[{"id":"site-003","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An older study guide mentions \"Delta Live Tables (DLT)\". What is the current name of this product?</p>","o":["Lakeflow Connect","Lakeflow Jobs","Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines","Declarative Automation Bundles"],"ok":[2],"w":["The ingestion connectors.","The orchestrator (formerly Workflows).","DLT was renamed; the concepts (streaming tables, materialized views, expectations) are the same.","The deployment tool (formerly Asset Bundles)."],"e":"DLT → Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines; Workflows → Lakeflow Jobs; Asset Bundles → Declarative Automation Bundles; Repos → Git folders."},{"id":"site-001","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Which statement about the Databricks architecture is correct?</p>","o":["The control plane runs in the customer's cloud account when classic compute is used","The control plane is always hosted in the Databricks account; classic compute runs in the customer's cloud account","Serverless compute runs in the customer's cloud account","Both planes always run in the customer's cloud account"],"ok":[1],"w":["The control plane never moves to the customer's account; only the compute plane differs.","Classic machines are VMs in your account; serverless machines are in the Databricks account.","Serverless compute runs in a Databricks-managed account, in the same region.","The control plane (web app, scheduler, APIs) is always operated by Databricks."],"e":"The control plane is always in the Databricks account. Only the compute plane can be in your account (classic) or in Databricks' account (serverless)."},{"id":"site-002","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A team runs a nightly Python ETL in production and wants the lowest-cost compute option. Which should they AVOID?</p>","o":["Serverless jobs compute","Job compute (classic)","All-purpose compute","A job cluster shared across tasks with job_cluster_key"],"ok":[2],"w":["A good default for scheduled jobs: no idle cost and no cluster to manage.","Created for the run and terminated after it, at the lower jobs DBU rate.","All-purpose clusters are meant for interactive work and have the highest DBU rate.","Sharing one job cluster between tasks of the same run saves start-up time and cost."],"e":"Scheduled production work runs on job compute or serverless jobs, never on all-purpose clusters."},{"id":"site-047","sec":["A1","P1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A workload uses the RDD API and R. Which compute should it run on?</p>","o":["Serverless jobs","Serverless SQL warehouse","Classic job compute with dedicated access mode","Any serverless option"],"ok":[2],"w":["Serverless supports neither R nor the RDD API.","SQL warehouses run SQL only.","Dedicated access mode supports R and the RDD API; standard access mode supports neither.","No serverless option runs R or RDD code."],"e":"R or RDD code means classic compute in dedicated access mode."},{"id":"sa-29","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>An ETL process runs every night, uses a lot of resources, and its volume varies a great deal from day to day. The team wants a cost-effective option that adapts to the load. Which compute fits?</p>","o":["A job cluster with autoscaling","An all-purpose cluster that is always on","A dedicated cluster of fixed size","A serverless SQL warehouse"],"ok":[0],"w":["The cluster exists only for the run, so there is no idle cost, and autoscaling follows the varying volume.","Higher DBU rate than a job cluster, and it keeps billing while idle.","A fixed size is either too small on peak nights or wasteful on quiet ones.","A SQL warehouse serves SQL queries and BI, not a notebook or Spark ETL pipeline."],"e":"Scheduled and automated work runs on job compute; all-purpose compute is for interactive development."},{"id":"sa-30","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Four analysts will spend the next few weeks exploring a dataset in notebooks, interactively and together. Which type of cluster suits this work?</p>","o":["An all-purpose cluster","A job cluster","A serverless SQL warehouse","One minimum-size cluster per analyst"],"ok":[0],"w":["All-purpose clusters are built for interactive, shared work: several users attached to the same cluster all day.","A job cluster is created and destroyed for each job run; it cannot host an interactive session.","It runs SQL queries and dashboards, not Python or Scala notebooks.","Four times the fixed cost, and no shared environment for the collaboration the scenario describes."],"e":"Interactive and collaborative work means all-purpose compute (or serverless notebooks); scheduled and automated work means job compute."},{"id":"sa-31","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A simple script joins about 10 GB of data once a week. It is not critical: if it fails it can be rerun without consequences. The explicit requirement is the cheapest compute possible. Which configuration do you choose?</p>","o":["A job cluster with spot instances","A job cluster with Photon enabled","A job cluster with on-demand instances","An all-purpose cluster shared with other teams"],"ok":[0],"w":["The job cluster avoids idle cost and spot instances add a large discount. Losing an instance mid-run is acceptable for a small job that can be rerun.","Photon speeds up execution but has a higher DBU rate on classic compute; for a small, simple job the extra cost does not pay off.","More expensive than spot, with no benefit when interruptions are acceptable.","Higher DBU rate and unpredictable performance, since it depends on what other teams are running."],"e":"Photon is not a free upgrade on classic compute. Spot workers are the classic way to cut the cost of jobs that tolerate interruption."},{"id":"sa-32","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A BI team runs SQL queries with very irregular volume: busy peaks and long idle periods. They do not want to manage or size infrastructure and want to pay only for what runs. Which option meets every requirement?</p>","o":["A serverless SQL warehouse","A classic SQL warehouse of fixed size","A job cluster scheduled every hour","An all-purpose cluster with autoscaling"],"ok":[0],"w":["It starts in seconds, scales with the query load and stops when idle, so you pay for actual use.","You must size and manage it, and it starts slowly when it has been stopped.","It cannot serve interactive queries; analysts would wait for the next scheduled run.","It scales, but still needs configuration and management, and it starts slowly when stopped."],"e":"SQL, bursty load, no infrastructure to manage and pay per use: serverless SQL warehouse."},{"id":"sa-33","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>An analyst must query, with SQL, a database that lives inside the company's private cloud network. No extra networking has been set up for serverless compute. Which SQL warehouse type reaches the database without further network configuration?</p>","o":["A pro or classic SQL warehouse","A serverless SQL warehouse","A job cluster running a SQL task","An all-purpose cluster in standard access mode"],"ok":[0],"w":["Their machines run in the customer's cloud account and network, so they can reach resources in that network directly.","It runs in the Databricks-managed serverless plane; reaching a private network needs extra setup (private connectivity), which the scenario rules out.","SQL tasks run on SQL warehouses, and a job cluster is not interactive compute for an analyst.","It runs notebooks; it is not the compute designed to serve SQL queries and dashboards."],"e":"When a question stresses the customer's own private network, look for compute that runs in the customer's account."},{"id":"sa-34","sec":["A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A team wants to cut costs by moving workloads to serverless while keeping today's SLAs. Several candidates exist. Where should they start to get the best balance of savings and risk?</p>","o":["An efficient pipeline that runs often and already works on a recent runtime with Unity Catalog","Legacy pipelines with old dependencies and JDBC links to obsolete systems","Dashboards that are rarely used","The most business-critical workload, to test the worst case"],"ok":[0],"w":["Frequent runs maximise the savings, and existing compatibility keeps the migration risk low.","These carry the highest risk of incompatibility; starting here turns the migration into a remediation project.","Little use means little saving; the migration effort does not pay off.","Testing on the most critical system exposes the business to migration risk for no reason."],"e":"Migrate first what runs often and is already compatible."},{"id":"sp-50","sec":["A1","P1"],"topic":"platform","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>How do you standardise libraries, environment variables and Spark settings across all the clusters a team creates?</p>","o":["A cluster policy","Configure them by hand in each notebook","Init scripts stored in the DBFS root","Let each user choose"],"ok":[0],"w":["A policy defines and enforces those settings centrally for every cluster created with it.","Inconsistent and hard to audit.","Discouraged practice, and restricted on governed compute.","Leads to drift between environments."],"e":"Cluster policies standardise and restrict classic compute configuration, including libraries."},{"id":"new-platform-01","sec":["A6","P8"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A classic job cluster fails to start. The cluster event log shows the termination reason <code>INIT_SCRIPT_FAILURE</code>. What do you check next?</p>","o":["The Spark UI of the failed run","The init script logs in the cluster log delivery location","The query profile of the last query","<code>system.billing.usage</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["The cluster never started, so there is no Spark application to inspect.","Init script output (<code>.stdout.log</code> and <code>.stderr.log</code>) is written under <code>&lt;log path&gt;/&lt;cluster id&gt;/init_scripts/</code> and shows why the script failed.","Query profiles exist for SQL warehouse and serverless queries, not for a cluster that failed to start.","Billing records show usage, not why a script failed."],"e":"Cluster start problems: event log first (termination reason), then the logs of the layer it names."},{"id":"new-platform-02","sec":["A6","P1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A job that has not changed in months starts failing at the library installation step with <code>LIBRARY_INSTALLATION_ERROR</code>. The task installs <code>pandas</code> and <code>pyarrow</code> from PyPI without versions. What is the most robust fix?</p>","o":["Add more workers to the job cluster","Increase the task's retry count","Pin the versions that are known to work, for example in a requirements file","Move the installation into an init script"],"ok":[2],"w":["The failure happens while installing packages; cluster size is irrelevant.","Retrying installs the same incompatible versions again.","Unpinned libraries pick up new releases whose dependencies can conflict with the runtime; pinning makes installs repeatable.","The same unpinned versions would still be resolved, and serverless does not run init scripts."],"e":"Unpinned dependencies are the usual cause of jobs that break \"without any change\". Pin with <code>==</code>."},{"id":"new-platform-03","sec":["P1"],"topic":"platform","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A Python wheel task must run on serverless compute and needs a private wheel stored in a Unity Catalog volume plus <code>pydantic==2.9.2</code>. How do you provide these dependencies?</p>","o":["Install them in a cluster-scoped init script","Add them as cluster libraries on the job cluster","Declare an environment with both dependencies in the job and reference it from the task with <code>environment_key</code>","Run <code>%pip install</code> in a separate notebook task that runs first"],"ok":[2],"w":["Serverless compute does not run init scripts.","A serverless task has no cluster to attach libraries to.","Serverless Python script, wheel and dbt tasks get their dependencies from a job environment.","Notebook-scoped installs apply only to that notebook's own Python process."],"e":"On serverless, dependencies are declared in the environment (side panel for notebooks, <code>environment_key</code> for script, wheel and dbt tasks)."},{"id":"new-platform-04","sec":["P1","A4"],"topic":"platform","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A serverless job task calls an external API that charges each customer once. The team discovers the task sometimes runs twice after a transient failure, even though no retry policy is configured. What explains it and how do you stop it?</p>","o":["The job has a continuous trigger; switch to a scheduled trigger","Serverless auto-optimization retried the task; untick <em>Enable serverless auto-optimization</em> in the task's retry policy","Spark speculative execution reran the task; set <code>spark.speculation</code> to false","Repair runs happen automatically on failure; disable repair"],"ok":[1],"w":["Nothing in the scenario points to a continuous trigger.","Auto-optimization is on by default and can retry failed tasks; turn it off for work that must run at most once.","Speculation duplicates Spark tasks inside one run, not job task runs, and serverless does not let you set it.","Repair runs are only started by a person or an API call."],"e":"Serverless auto-optimization may add retries. Disable it for non-idempotent tasks."},{"id":"new-platform-05","sec":["P1","A1"],"topic":"platform","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A nightly serverless job has a six-hour window and the team wants to cut its cost. A start-up delay of a few minutes is acceptable. Which setting helps?</p>","o":["Enable Photon on the job","Turn off <em>Performance optimized</em> so the job's serverless tasks run in standard mode","Switch the tasks to an all-purpose cluster","Set <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code> to 2000"],"ok":[1],"w":["Photon is always on in serverless; it is not a setting you can turn on.","Standard mode costs less in exchange for a longer start-up, which fits a job with a wide window.","All-purpose compute has the highest DBU rate.","Unrelated to cost, and a fixed value turns off automatic shuffle tuning."],"e":"Serverless jobs and pipelines have two modes: performance optimized (fast start) and standard (cheaper, slower start)."},{"id":"new-platform-06","sec":["A1","P7"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A company has two workspaces in the same cloud region, both attached to Unity Catalog. A data engineer grants <code>SELECT</code> on <code>sales.gold.orders</code> to the <code>analysts</code> account group from the first workspace. What happens in the second workspace?</p>","o":["Members of <code>analysts</code> can query the table there too, because both workspaces share the region's metastore","Nothing; grants are per workspace and must be repeated","The table is copied to the second workspace","The grant fails until the second workspace is detached"],"ok":[0],"w":["There is one metastore per region; grants are stored in the metastore, not in the workspace.","Unity Catalog grants live in the metastore and apply in every workspace attached to it (unless the catalog is bound to specific workspaces).","Workspaces share the same data; nothing is copied.","Attaching many workspaces to one metastore is the normal setup."],"e":"One Unity Catalog metastore per region, shared by the workspaces of that region; grants follow the data, not the workspace."},{"id":"new-platform-07","sec":["A6","P1"],"topic":"platform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A notebook defines several variables, then a cell in the middle runs <code>%pip install great-expectations</code> followed by <code>dbutils.library.restartPython()</code>. Later cells fail with <code>NameError</code>. Why?</p>","o":["<code>%pip</code> is not supported on Unity Catalog compute","Restarting Python clears the notebook's state, so variables defined earlier no longer exist","The library replaced the built-in variables","Notebook-scoped libraries are only visible in the next notebook"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>%pip</code> works on serverless and on classic compute.","Put <code>%pip</code> installs at the top of the notebook, before any other code.","Installing a package does not delete user variables; the restart does.","They are visible in the same notebook after the restart."],"e":"<code>%pip</code> at the top, then <code>restartPython()</code>, then the rest of the code."},{"id":"new-platform-08","sec":["P1"],"topic":"platform","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A team installs an internal package from a source archive (<code>.tar.gz</code>). Installation fails on some clusters because a C compiler and headers are missing. What is the best long-term fix?</p>","o":["Install the compiler with an init script on every cluster","Build a wheel (<code>.whl</code>) in CI and install the wheel from a volume or a package index","Copy the package source into every notebook","Use a larger instance type"],"ok":[1],"w":["Works only on classic compute and slows every start; it treats the symptom.","Wheels are prebuilt, so nothing needs compiling at install time, and they install the same way on classic, serverless and pipelines.","Duplicates code and is unmaintainable.","Instance size has nothing to do with missing build tools."],"e":"Prefer wheels to source archives: they install quickly and the same way everywhere."},{"id":"site-048","sec":["A1"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>What gives Delta Lake its ACID guarantees on top of object storage?</p>","o":["The Hive metastore","The <code>_delta_log</code> transaction log","Photon","Parquet statistics only"],"ok":[1],"w":["A metastore stores table metadata; it does not make writes atomic.","Each commit is an atomic entry in the log, and readers only see committed versions.","Photon is a query engine; it makes queries faster, not transactional.","Statistics help data skipping; they give no transactional guarantees."],"e":"Delta = Parquet files + <code>_delta_log</code>. The log provides atomic commits, isolation, history and schema enforcement."},{"id":"sa-01","sec":["A1"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>An insurer stores policies as Parquet files in an ADLS container. Two nightly routines write to the same folder, and in the morning some reports occasionally read records from a write that was still in progress. Which change fixes the problem at its root?</p>","o":["Convert the folder into a Delta table","Add more workers to the cluster that writes","Schedule the two routines two hours apart","Enable Unity Catalog on the container"],"ok":[0],"w":["The transaction log makes each write atomic: readers only see data from completed commits, and failed writes leave nothing visible.","The write finishes sooner, but a reader can still catch it halfway. The problem is atomicity, not speed.","Lowers the odds but guarantees nothing; any delay brings the problem back.","Unity Catalog governs who can access what; it does not make writes transactional."],"e":"Partial reads and clashing concurrent writes are the signature of missing ACID transactions. On Databricks the answer is Delta Lake."},{"id":"sa-02","sec":["A1","A3"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Twenty minutes ago a <code>DELETE</code> with a wrong filter removed about three million rows from a production Delta table. A backup exists, but restoring it takes hours. What is the fastest way to get the previous state back?</p>","o":["<code>RESTORE TABLE ... TO VERSION AS OF</code> the version before the delete","Run <code>VACUUM</code>, then reprocess the source","Run <code>OPTIMIZE</code> to rebuild the table's files","Run <code>REFRESH TABLE</code> to invalidate the metadata cache"],"ok":[0],"w":["The log still references the previous version, so <code>RESTORE</code> brings it back quickly, as long as <code>VACUUM</code> has not removed its files.","<code>VACUUM</code> deletes exactly the old files that time travel needs; it would make things worse.","<code>OPTIMIZE</code> compacts files; it does not bring back deleted rows.","The rows really were removed from the current version; refreshing a cache does not restore them."],"e":"Time travel and <code>RESTORE</code> work within the retention window. After an aggressive <code>VACUUM</code> they no longer can."},{"id":"sa-03","sec":["A1","A2"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A pipeline appends to a Delta table whose column <code>monthly_premium</code> is declared as <code>DECIMAL(10,2)</code>. After a change in the source system, the field arrives as text and the append starts failing. What explains the failure?</p>","o":["Schema enforcement: Delta rejects writes whose schema does not match the table","The table ran out of storage space","Append mode does not support numeric columns","<code>OPTIMIZE</code> was not run after the last write"],"ok":[0],"w":["Instead of silently corrupting the column, Delta refuses the write until someone decides how to handle the change.","Lack of space gives an I/O error, not a type mismatch.","Append works with any column type.","<code>OPTIMIZE</code> is optional maintenance, never a prerequisite for writing."],"e":"Schema enforcement fails on purpose. Intended changes are accepted explicitly (cast the data, or evolve the schema)."},{"id":"sp-80","sec":["A1","P1"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>A write to a Delta table contains a column that does not exist in the table schema, and <code>mergeSchema</code> is not enabled. What happens?</p>","o":["The write fails because of schema enforcement","The column is added automatically","The new column is silently ignored","The table is recreated with the new schema"],"ok":[0],"w":["Delta rejects data that does not match the table schema.","Only with <code>mergeSchema</code> or automatic schema merging turned on.","Delta never drops columns silently.","Only an overwrite with <code>overwriteSchema</code> would replace the schema."],"e":"Extra column without <code>mergeSchema</code>: the write fails. Missing columns, on the other hand, are written as NULL."},{"id":"sp-11","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>An append-only pipeline occasionally receives new columns from the source and must not fail. Which write option evolves the table schema automatically?</p>","o":["<code>option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\")</code>","<code>option(\"overwriteSchema\", \"true\")</code>","<code>option(\"ignoreChanges\", \"true\")</code>","<code>spark.sql.ansi.enabled = false</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["New columns are added to the table schema on appends, in batch and in streaming writes.","Replaces the whole schema and only works with overwrite, not append.","A streaming read option for sources with updates, unrelated to schemas.","Controls SQL error semantics, not table schemas."],"e":"<code>mergeSchema</code> adds columns; <code>overwriteSchema</code> replaces the schema and needs overwrite."},{"id":"site-021","sec":["A3"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>You need to apply inserts and updates from a daily batch of changes to an existing Delta table, matching on <code>id</code>. Which command?</p>","o":["<code>INSERT INTO</code>","<code>INSERT OVERWRITE</code>","<code>MERGE INTO ... WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT</code>","<code>COPY INTO</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Appends everything, so changed rows become duplicates.","Replaces the whole table with the batch and loses every row not in it.","This is the upsert pattern (SCD type 1).","Loads new files; it cannot update existing rows."],"e":"Upsert = <code>MERGE</code>. The source must contain at most one row per key."},{"id":"site-105","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A daily <code>MERGE</code> into silver fails with <code>DELTA_MULTIPLE_SOURCE_ROW_MATCHING_TARGET_ROW_IN_MERGE</code>. What is the cause and fix?</p>","o":["The target has duplicates; run <code>OPTIMIZE</code>","The source batch has several rows for the same key; keep only the latest row per key before merging","The merge condition is missing; add <code>ON</code>","Schema evolution is disabled; add <code>mergeSchema</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["The error is about the source, and <code>OPTIMIZE</code> never removes duplicates.","Use <code>row_number()</code> over the key ordered by timestamp descending and keep row 1.","Without <code>ON</code> the statement would not parse.","The error is about duplicate matches, not columns."],"e":"Deduplicate the merge source by key, keeping the latest version."},{"id":"site-063","sec":["A3"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>You need to replace a table's data AND change its schema, while keeping the table history and grants. Which command?</p>","o":["<code>DROP TABLE</code> then <code>CREATE TABLE</code>","<code>INSERT OVERWRITE</code>","<code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ... AS SELECT</code>","<code>DELETE FROM</code> then <code>INSERT</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Loses history, grants, row filters and masks.","Keeps the existing schema, so it fails when the schema changes.","It replaces data and schema in a new version and keeps history and grants.","Keeps the old schema and takes two commits."],"e":"<code>CREATE OR REPLACE</code> replaces data and schema while keeping the table's identity, history and grants."},{"id":"site-107","sec":["A3"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p><code>ALTER TABLE t RENAME COLUMN cust_name TO customer_name</code> fails on a Delta table. What is required?</p>","o":["Run <code>OPTIMIZE</code> first","Enable column mapping: <code>ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.columnMapping.mode' = 'name')</code>","Convert the table to external","Use <code>withColumnRenamed</code> and overwrite the table"],"ok":[1],"w":["File layout has nothing to do with renaming.","Column mapping lets Delta rename and drop columns by changing metadata only.","The table type does not matter.","That rewrites all the data; column mapping avoids it."],"e":"Rename and drop column need column mapping. They are metadata-only changes and can interrupt streams that read the table."},{"id":"site-083","sec":["A6","A1"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>After a <code>VACUUM</code> with the default retention, a query using <code>VERSION AS OF 3</code> (a version from 30 days ago) fails. Why?</p>","o":["<code>OPTIMIZE</code> disabled time travel","<code>VACUUM</code> deleted the data files that the old version needs","<code>VERSION AS OF</code> only works on managed tables","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> must be run first"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>OPTIMIZE</code> creates a new version; old versions stay readable until vacuumed.","With the default 7-day file retention, files removed more than 7 days ago are deleted.","Time travel works on managed and external Delta tables.","History is informational; it is not a prerequisite."],"e":"<code>VACUUM</code> limits time travel to the file retention period (7 days by default)."},{"id":"sp-09","sec":["A1","P5"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>An analyst needs to see the <code>sales</code> table as it was at version 42, before a faulty load. What should they use?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT * FROM sales VERSION AS OF 42</code>","<code>SELECT * FROM table_changes('sales', 42)</code>","<code>DESCRIBE DETAIL sales</code>","<code>VACUUM sales RETAIN 42 HOURS</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Time travel reads that version's snapshot while its files still exist.","Returns row-level changes (needs Change Data Feed), not the full snapshot.","Shows current metadata, not old data.","Deletes files and could destroy exactly the history needed."],"e":"Snapshot of a past version: time travel. Changes between versions: Change Data Feed."},{"id":"sp-05","sec":["P6","P5"],"topic":"delta","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A policy requires removed files of a table to be kept for 30 days by every future <code>VACUUM</code>. Which command meets it permanently?</p>","o":["<code>ALTER TABLE t SET TBLPROPERTIES ('delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration' = 'interval 30 days')</code>","<code>VACUUM t RETAIN 720 HOURS</code>","<code>spark.conf.set(\"spark.databricks.delta.deletedFileRetentionDuration\", \"30 days\")</code>","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY t LIMIT 30</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["The property is stored in the table and applies to every later <code>VACUUM</code>.","Applies to that run only; the next <code>VACUUM</code> without <code>RETAIN</code> uses the table setting again.","A session setting: it is lost on restart and is not tied to the table.","Lists versions; it sets nothing."],"e":"Retention that must hold for everyone is a table property, not an option on one command."},{"id":"sp-06","sec":["P6","A1"],"topic":"delta","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which table property prevents rows of an audit table from being changed or deleted after they are written?</p>","o":["<code>delta.appendOnly = true</code>","Time travel","<code>OPTIMIZE</code>","<code>VACUUM</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["<code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> and merges that change rows are rejected.","Lets you read old versions; it does not prevent changes.","Compacts files; it does not restrict writes.","Cleans up old files; it protects nothing."],"e":"<code>delta.appendOnly</code> makes a table insert-only."},{"id":"sp-66","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"delta","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>In a <code>MERGE INTO</code>, how do you delete target rows that no longer exist in the source?</p>","o":["<code>WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE THEN DELETE</code>","<code>WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN DELETE</code>","<code>WHEN MATCHED THEN INSERT</code>","<code>WHEN MATCHED AND false THEN UPDATE</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["This clause acts on target rows with no matching source row.","<code>WHEN NOT MATCHED</code> covers source rows missing from the target, and it only allows <code>INSERT</code>.","Matched rows can be updated or deleted, not inserted.","A condition that is never true does nothing."],"e":"<code>NOT MATCHED</code> = in source, not in target (insert). <code>NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE</code> = in target, not in source (delete or update)."},{"id":"new-delta-01","sec":["A1","A3"],"topic":"delta","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A table is at version 15. An engineer runs <code>RESTORE TABLE orders TO VERSION AS OF 12</code>. What does <code>DESCRIBE HISTORY orders</code> show afterwards?</p>","o":["Versions 13 to 15 are gone and the latest version is 12","A new version 16 with operation <code>RESTORE</code>; versions 13 to 15 are still listed","Nothing changes in the history; only the data files change","Version 12 is renamed to version 16"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>RESTORE</code> never deletes history.","Restore writes a new commit that brings back the state of version 12.","Every change to a Delta table is a commit in the log.","Versions are never renamed."],"e":"<code>RESTORE</code> is a new commit. You could even restore \"forwards\" to version 15 later, while its files exist."},{"id":"new-delta-02","sec":["P1","P8"],"topic":"delta","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A team wants to test a risky change to a 5 TB production table in a development schema, without copying the data and without touching the production table. What should they use?</p>","o":["<code>CREATE TABLE dev.orders SHALLOW CLONE prod.orders</code>","<code>CREATE TABLE dev.orders DEEP CLONE prod.orders</code>","<code>CREATE VIEW dev.orders AS SELECT * FROM prod.orders</code>","Time travel on the production table"],"ok":[0],"w":["A shallow clone copies only metadata and references the source files; writes to the clone go to its own new files.","Works, but copies all 5 TB.","A view cannot be written to, so the change cannot be tested.","Reads old versions; it does not give a separate writable table."],"e":"Shallow clone: cheap, writable copy for tests. Deep clone: full copy for backup or migration. A shallow clone breaks if the source files are vacuumed."},{"id":"new-delta-03","sec":["P1","P8"],"topic":"delta","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Two jobs update different rows of the same Delta table at the same time. One of them fails at commit with a concurrent modification error. Which statement is correct?</p>","o":["Delta locks the table during writes, so the second job should have waited","Delta uses optimistic concurrency; when concurrent commits conflict, one writer fails and can retry, and row-level concurrency (with deletion vectors) reduces such conflicts","The table is now corrupted and must be restored","Concurrent writes are never allowed on Delta tables"],"ok":[1],"w":["Delta uses optimistic concurrency, not locks.","Conflicts are detected at commit time.","The failed commit never became visible; the table is consistent.","Concurrent appends never conflict, and many concurrent updates succeed."],"e":"Optimistic concurrency: no locks, conflicts detected at commit, the loser retries. Make jobs idempotent so a retry is safe."},{"id":"new-delta-04","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"delta","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A job recomputes one day of data and must replace only the rows where <code>event_date = '2026-11-20'</code>, leaving all other days untouched, in one atomic write. Which option does that?</p>","o":["<code>mode(\"overwrite\")</code>","<code>mode(\"overwrite\").option(\"replaceWhere\", \"event_date = '2026-11-20'\")</code>","<code>mode(\"append\")</code> followed by <code>OPTIMIZE</code>","<code>DELETE</code> followed by <code>INSERT</code> in two jobs"],"ok":[1],"w":["Replaces the whole table.","Only rows matching the predicate are replaced, atomically.","Adds a second copy of the day instead of replacing it.","Two commits; readers can see the day missing in between."],"e":"<code>replaceWhere</code> (SQL: <code>INSERT INTO ... REPLACE WHERE</code>) overwrites a slice of a table atomically."},{"id":"sp-01","sec":["P5","A6"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A 2 TB managed table receives a daily <code>MERGE</code> on <code>order_id</code> and many scattered single-row updates. Which TWO actions most reduce the cost of the merge?</p>","o":["Liquid clustering with <code>CLUSTER BY (order_id)</code>","Enable deletion vectors on the table","Run <code>VACUUM RETAIN 0 HOURS</code> before each merge","Partition the table by <code>order_id</code>","Replace the merge with <code>DROP TABLE</code> and <code>CREATE TABLE</code> each day"],"ok":[0,1],"w":["Rows with nearby keys are stored together, so the merge finds and rewrites fewer files.","Updated and deleted rows are marked in small side files instead of rewriting whole Parquet files.","Does not speed up the merge, breaks concurrent readers and destroys time travel.","A very high-cardinality key creates millions of tiny partitions and files.","Rewrites everything and loses history: the opposite of an efficient incremental load."],"e":"Merge-heavy tables: cluster on the merge key and use deletion vectors."},{"id":"sp-02","sec":["P5","P9"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A query with <code>WHERE event_ts = '2025-03-10'</code> on a Delta table of 40,000 files reads almost all of them, although min/max statistics are collected. What is the most likely cause?</p>","o":["<code>event_ts</code> values are spread across all files, so each file's min/max range covers the filtered date","The filter is applied only after the whole table is read","Timestamp columns do not support data skipping","Delta does not collect statistics on large tables"],"ok":[0],"w":["Data skipping only works when files hold narrow ranges; without clustering almost no file can be skipped.","Spark pushes predicates down into the scan; the problem is the file layout.","Date and timestamp columns are supported.","Statistics are collected on the first columns regardless of table size."],"e":"Statistics only help when the layout keeps values together. That is what clustering is for."},{"id":"sa-46","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A Delta table has accumulated tens of thousands of very small files from frequent micro-batch writes, and reads have become slow. Which operation tackles this problem directly?</p>","o":["<code>OPTIMIZE</code>, to compact the small files into larger ones","<code>VACUUM</code>, to remove old files","<code>REFRESH TABLE</code>, to update the metadata","More workers on the cluster that reads"],"ok":[0],"w":["It rewrites the data into well-sized files, removing the overhead of opening thousands of files on every read.","It deletes files that no retained version uses; it does not compact the active small files.","Refreshes a metadata cache; it does not change the physical files.","More parallelism does not remove the per-file overhead; it treats the symptom at a higher cost."],"e":"<code>OPTIMIZE</code> compacts; <code>VACUUM</code> cleans up old versions' files. They often appear together as distractors."},{"id":"sp-72","sec":["P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A table receives thousands of small writes and reads become slow. Which combination reduces the problem?</p>","o":["Enable optimized writes and auto compaction, and run <code>OPTIMIZE</code> (or let predictive optimization run it)","Partition the table by timestamp","Disable statistics collection","Run <code>VACUUM</code> with <code>RETAIN 0 HOURS</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Writes produce larger files, and the remaining small files are compacted.","Creates even more partitions and small files.","Hurts data skipping and solves nothing.","Removes obsolete files but does not compact small ones, and it is dangerous."],"e":"Prevent small files at write time (optimized writes), compact after writes (auto compaction), and maintain (OPTIMIZE or predictive optimization)."},{"id":"site-127","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Yesterday an engineer ran <code>ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (region, order_date)</code>, but queries filtering on <code>region</code> still scan most files. What should they run?</p>","o":["<code>VACUUM t</code>","<code>OPTIMIZE t FULL</code>","<code>ANALYZE TABLE t</code>","Recreate the table"],"ok":[1],"w":["Deletes old files; it does not reorganise data.","Changing keys does not rewrite existing data; <code>OPTIMIZE FULL</code> reclusters all of it (it can take hours on a large table).","Collects statistics for the optimizer; it does not move data.","Unnecessary; liquid clustering keys can change in place."],"e":"New clustering keys apply to future clustering. <code>OPTIMIZE FULL</code> reclusters existing data."},{"id":"site-128","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Predictive optimization is enabled on the catalog, but one table never gets <code>OPTIMIZE</code> or <code>VACUUM</code>. What is the likely reason?</p>","o":["The table uses liquid clustering","It is an external table; predictive optimization only runs on Unity Catalog managed tables","Predictive optimization only runs on Mondays","The table has fewer than 1,000 files"],"ok":[1],"w":["Clustered managed tables are exactly what predictive optimization maintains.","Convert it with <code>SET MANAGED</code> or schedule the maintenance yourself.","It runs when it estimates the work is worthwhile, not on a fixed day.","There is no such file-count rule."],"e":"Predictive optimization = managed tables only. External tables need your own maintenance jobs."},{"id":"sp-07","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which TWO maintenance operations does predictive optimization run automatically on Unity Catalog managed tables?</p>","o":["<code>OPTIMIZE</code>","<code>VACUUM</code>","<code>PARTITION BY</code>","Bucketing","<code>DROP COLUMN</code>"],"ok":[0,1],"w":["It compacts files and clusters data when worthwhile.","It removes unreferenced files after the retention period.","A table definition clause, not maintenance.","A Hive/Spark layout technique, not part of the service.","A schema change the service never makes."],"e":"Predictive optimization runs <code>OPTIMIZE</code>, <code>VACUUM</code> and <code>ANALYZE</code>, never Z-order."},{"id":"sp-03","sec":["P5","P9"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A 1 TB table is heavily skewed on <code>customer_id</code>, and the most filtered columns change every quarter. The team wants to change the layout strategy without rewriting the whole history each time. What should they use?</p>","o":["Liquid clustering, changing keys with <code>ALTER TABLE ... CLUSTER BY</code>","Hive-style partitioning on the most filtered column","Partitioning plus <code>ZORDER BY</code> on both columns","<code>ZORDER BY</code> after every load"],"ok":[0],"w":["Keys can change without rewriting existing data, clustering is incremental, and skew is handled better than with partitions.","Changing the partition column means recreating the table, and skew creates very uneven partitions.","A legacy combination: changing columns means reorganising everything, and Z-order is not incremental.","Each key change reorders the whole table, which is expensive at 1 TB."],"e":"Changing access patterns plus skew: liquid clustering (or <code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code>)."},{"id":"sp-04","sec":["P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>What happens to rows inserted into a liquid-clustered table after the last <code>OPTIMIZE</code>?</p>","o":["Small writes stay unclustered until the next <code>OPTIMIZE</code>, run manually or by predictive optimization","They are clustered immediately during the <code>INSERT</code>","They are rejected if they do not follow the clustering keys","They go to a staging area until a maintenance window"],"ok":[0],"w":["Clustering is applied incrementally by <code>OPTIMIZE</code>, not during each small write.","Ordinary small writes do not reorganise data.","Clustering is a physical layout, not an integrity constraint.","There is no staging area; rows are written to the table."],"e":"Liquid clustering is incremental: run <code>OPTIMIZE</code> often, or let predictive optimization do it."},{"id":"sp-08","sec":["P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A service corrects individual account balances with <code>UPDATE</code> several times a day, and each correction rewrites whole Parquet files. Which feature reduces this rewriting?</p>","o":["Deletion vectors","Change Data Feed","Partitioning by <code>account_id</code>","Optimized writes"],"ok":[0],"w":["Changed rows are marked in a small side file, so the Parquet file is not rewritten immediately.","Records changes for downstream consumers; it does not change how <code>UPDATE</code> executes.","High cardinality creates tiny files, and updates still rewrite files inside the partition.","Improves the size of written files, but the files hit by the update are still rewritten."],"e":"Frequent row-level updates or deletes: deletion vectors."},{"id":"new-layout-01","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A data engineer wants Databricks to choose and adapt the clustering keys of a table automatically, based on how it is queried. What is required?</p>","o":["An external table with a <code>LOCATION</code> clause","A Unity Catalog managed table with predictive optimization enabled, created or altered with <code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code>","A table partitioned by date with <code>ZORDER BY AUTO</code>","A scheduled job that runs <code>ANALYZE TABLE</code> every hour"],"ok":[1],"w":["Automatic clustering is not available for external tables.","Key selection is done by predictive optimization from the query history.","There is no <code>ZORDER BY AUTO</code>, and liquid clustering cannot be combined with partitioning.","<code>ANALYZE</code> collects optimizer statistics; it does not choose clustering keys."],"e":"<code>CLUSTER BY AUTO</code> = managed table + predictive optimization."},{"id":"new-layout-03","sec":["P5","P9"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A large table is partitioned by <code>ingest_date</code> and Z-ordered by <code>store_id</code>. The team wants to move it to liquid clustering on <code>(store_id, ingest_date)</code>. What do they need to do?</p>","o":["<code>ALTER TABLE t CLUSTER BY (store_id, ingest_date)</code> on the existing table","Recreate the table with <code>CLUSTER BY (store_id, ingest_date)</code>, for example with <code>CREATE OR REPLACE TABLE ... CLUSTER BY (...) AS SELECT ...</code>","Run <code>OPTIMIZE t ZORDER BY (store_id, ingest_date)</code>","Enable predictive optimization; it converts the table automatically"],"ok":[1],"w":["Liquid clustering cannot be enabled on a partitioned table in place.","A partitioned table has to be rewritten without partitioning to use liquid clustering.","That keeps the old approach.","Predictive optimization maintains tables; it does not change their layout definition."],"e":"Unpartitioned tables can switch to liquid clustering with <code>ALTER TABLE</code>; partitioned ones must be recreated."},{"id":"new-layout-04","sec":["P9","P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A team designs a 200 TB event table loaded every hour. Queries filter on <code>event_date</code> and often on <code>customer_id</code>. Which design follows good layout principles?</p>","o":["Partition by <code>customer_id</code> and by hour, so every query reads one folder","Cluster on the filter columns (for example <code>event_date, customer_id</code>), avoid high-cardinality partitions, and keep file sizes healthy with regular <code>OPTIMIZE</code> or predictive optimization","No clustering, but run <code>VACUUM</code> every hour","Write one file per load and never compact, to keep writes fast"],"ok":[1],"w":["Very high cardinality and small hourly slices produce millions of tiny files.","Clustering follows the access pattern and compaction keeps files well sized.","<code>VACUUM</code> does not organise data or compact files.","Hourly small files degrade reads over time."],"e":"Partition only on low-cardinality columns that match the data grain; cluster on filter columns; compact regularly."},{"id":"sa-45","sec":["A6","P5","P9"],"topic":"layout","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A transactions table is partitioned by <code>transaction_date</code>, which suits period reports. But the most frequent queries filter on <code>customer_id</code>, which has millions of distinct values, and scan entire partitions. What is the best approach?</p>","o":["Recreate the table with liquid clustering on <code>(customer_id, transaction_date)</code> instead of partitioning","Repartition the table by <code>customer_id</code>","Enable the disk cache and keep everything as it is","Remove the partitioning and replace it with nothing"],"ok":[0],"w":["Liquid clustering handles high-cardinality keys and still serves date filters; it cannot be combined with partitioning, so the table is rewritten once.","Millions of tiny partitions and files.","Speeds up repeated reads but leaves each customer's rows spread over many files.","Loses date pruning without helping customer filters."],"e":"High-cardinality filters go to liquid clustering, never to partitioning; clustering replaces partitioning."},{"id":"new-layout-05","sec":["P9","P5"],"topic":"layout","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>An Iceberg table receives about 50 GB per day, is loaded once a day, and almost every query filters on the event day. Which layout follows the design principles for partitioning and file sizes?</p>","o":["Partition by the full event timestamp","Partition by <code>customer_id</code>","Partition by day (a day transform of the event timestamp), which matches both the load and the query grain, and compact small files regularly","No partitioning and no compaction"],"ok":[2],"w":["Millions of partitions with tiny files.","High cardinality and unrelated to the main filter.","Each partition is large and maps to how data arrives and is read.","Every query scans everything and files stay small."],"e":"Partition only on low-cardinality values that match the data grain; keep files large with compaction."},{"id":"site-004","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Millions of JSON files land continuously in cloud storage, and new fields are added over time. Which ingestion method fits best?</p>","o":["<code>COPY INTO</code> scheduled every minute","Auto Loader (<code>cloudFiles</code>) with schema evolution","<code>CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</code> with <code>read_files</code>","Lakehouse Federation"],"ok":[1],"w":["Good for thousands of files per run, not millions arriving continuously, and its schema evolution is limited to <code>mergeSchema</code>.","It scales to millions of files, processes only new ones through its checkpoint, and evolves the schema.","A one-time batch load that rereads everything each time.","Queries external databases in place; it does not ingest files."],"e":"Millions of files, continuous arrival, changing schema: Auto Loader."},{"id":"site-055","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An analyst wants a SQL-only, idempotent, scheduled load of a few thousand CSV files into an existing Delta table. Which option is most appropriate?</p>","o":["<code>COPY INTO</code>","Auto Loader with file notification","A Python <code>foreach</code> loop over the files","Lakeflow Connect"],"ok":[0],"w":["SQL, incremental and idempotent: already loaded files are skipped.","Built for millions of files and written in Python (or as a pipeline); more than this case needs.","Hand-written, not idempotent, hard to maintain.","Ingests from applications and databases, not from a folder of files."],"e":"Thousands of files, SQL, idempotent: <code>COPY INTO</code>."},{"id":"site-005","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A data engineer runs the same <code>COPY INTO</code> command twice with no new files arriving in between. What happens on the second run?</p>","o":["All files are loaded again, creating duplicates","The command fails because the files were already loaded","No new rows are loaded, because already loaded files are skipped","The target table is overwritten"],"ok":[2],"w":["<code>COPY INTO</code> remembers loaded files.","It succeeds and loads nothing.","<code>COPY INTO</code> is idempotent.","<code>COPY INTO</code> only appends."],"e":"<code>COPY INTO</code> is idempotent, so reruns and job retries are safe."},{"id":"site-094","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A supplier uploads a corrected file with the same name as a file that <code>COPY INTO</code> already loaded. What is the correct way to fix the table?</p>","o":["Run <code>COPY INTO</code> again; it detects that the file changed","Delete the rows that came from the original file, then run <code>COPY INTO</code> with <code>FILES = ('file.csv')</code> and <code>COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')</code>","Run <code>COPY INTO</code> with <code>'force' = 'true'</code> on the whole folder","Run <code>VACUUM</code>, then <code>COPY INTO</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Loaded files are skipped even when their content changed.","<code>force</code> reloads the file but does not replace the old rows, and <code>FILES</code> limits the reload to that one file.","Reloads every file and duplicates all the data.","<code>VACUUM</code> does not reset the list of loaded files."],"e":"<code>force</code> = load again, not replace. Remove the old rows first and target the single file."},{"id":"site-095","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>In <code>COPY INTO</code>, which option lets the TARGET table's schema evolve when the source files gain new columns?</p>","o":["<code>FORMAT_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')</code>","<code>COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema' = 'true')</code>","<code>FORMAT_OPTIONS ('inferSchema' = 'true')</code>","<code>COPY_OPTIONS ('force' = 'true')</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Merges the schemas of the source files being read; it does not change the target table.","This lets the target table gain the new columns.","Infers column types from the files; nothing to do with the target.","Reloads files that were already loaded."],"e":"<code>FORMAT_OPTIONS</code> shape what is read; <code>COPY_OPTIONS ('mergeSchema')</code> evolves the target."},{"id":"site-049","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>What is <code>cloudFiles.schemaLocation</code> used for in Auto Loader?</p>","o":["Where the output table is stored","Where Auto Loader stores the inferred schema and its changes over time","Where bad records are written","Where Kafka offsets are stored"],"ok":[1],"w":["The target table is set by <code>toTable</code> or the pipeline.","It is required when the schema is inferred.","That is <code>badRecordsPath</code>.","Offsets of any streaming source live in the checkpoint."],"e":"Schema location = schema history. Checkpoint location = progress (which files were processed)."},{"id":"site-008","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Auto Loader runs WITHOUT a provided schema and with default settings. A new column appears in the files. What happens?</p>","o":["The stream fails with an <code>UnknownFieldException</code>; after a restart the new column is included","The column is written to <code>_rescued_data</code> and the stream continues","The stream ignores the column forever","The stream fails and the column is never added"],"ok":[0],"w":["The default mode without a schema is <code>addNewColumns</code>: fail, update the stored schema, continue after restart.","That is the <code>rescue</code> mode.","That is the <code>none</code> mode, the default only when you provide a schema.","That is <code>failOnNewColumns</code>."],"e":"Default without a schema: <code>addNewColumns</code>. Run it in a job with retries so the restart is automatic."},{"id":"site-007","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An Auto Loader stream uses <code>cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode = 'rescue'</code>. A new column appears in the source files. What happens?</p>","o":["The stream fails and adds the column on restart","The new column is added to the table schema automatically","The schema does not change; the new data goes to the <code>_rescued_data</code> column","The new column is silently dropped with no trace"],"ok":[2],"w":["That is <code>addNewColumns</code>.","<code>rescue</code> never changes the schema.","The stream keeps running and nothing is lost.","Data that does not fit is kept in <code>_rescued_data</code>."],"e":"<code>rescue</code>: fixed schema, unexpected data rescued, no failure."},{"id":"sa-17","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A team ingests files from an external supplier who has already changed the layout twice without warning. The new policy: if unexpected columns arrive, ingestion must stop until someone confirms the change is intended. Which Auto Loader schema evolution mode implements this?</p>","o":["<code>failOnNewColumns</code>","<code>addNewColumns</code>","<code>rescue</code>","<code>none</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["The stream fails on purpose when a column outside the known schema appears, forcing a human review.","Adds the column and continues after a restart, without anyone validating it.","Keeps the unexpected data in <code>_rescued_data</code> and keeps running; it never stops.","Ignores new columns and carries on."],"e":"\"Stop and check before continuing\" means <code>failOnNewColumns</code>."},{"id":"sa-18","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>On a payments platform, the ingestion pipeline must never stop, but records with unexpected fields must not be lost either: they will be analysed later. Which configuration fits?</p>","o":["<code>rescue</code> mode, which sends data that does not fit to <code>_rescued_data</code>","<code>failOnNewColumns</code>","<code>none</code>","Turn off schema inference and read everything as strings"],"ok":[0],"w":["The pipeline keeps running and nothing is lost.","Stops the pipeline, which is exactly what is forbidden.","Keeps running but drops what does not fit, losing the records to analyse.","Avoids failures but destroys the typing of every column and pushes the problem downstream."],"e":"<code>rescue</code> and <code>failOnNewColumns</code> serve opposite priorities: keep going without loss, or stop to validate."},{"id":"sa-19","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A landing folder receives CSV reports, JSON manifests and compressed logs. The pipeline must process only the CSV files. Assess this code:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df = (spark.readStream.format(&quot;cloudFiles&quot;)\n        .option(&quot;cloudFiles.format&quot;, &quot;csv&quot;)\n        .option(&quot;pathGlobFilter&quot;, &quot;*.csv&quot;)\n        .load(landing_dir))\n</code></pre>","o":["The code is correct","The filter should be in the path, as <code>load(landing_dir + \"/*.csv\")</code>","The chain must end with <code>.append()</code>","<code>cloudFiles.format</code> is unnecessary when <code>pathGlobFilter</code> is set"],"ok":[0],"w":["<code>pathGlobFilter</code> filters by file name, options come before <code>load</code>, and <code>load</code> receives the base directory.","<code>load</code> expects the base directory Auto Loader watches; name filtering belongs in <code>pathGlobFilter</code>.","There is no <code>append</code> on a reader; output modes belong to writers.","One says how to parse content, the other which files to consider."],"e":"Syntax questions: does the method exist, are options before <code>load</code>, does <code>load</code> get a directory?"},{"id":"sa-20","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Before building the real ingestion, an engineer only wants to load the files already in a folder into a DataFrame and check that the content and types are as expected. What is the right approach for this step?</p>","o":["A simple batch read with <code>spark.read</code>","Auto Loader with <code>readStream</code> and <code>cloudFiles</code>","A declarative pipeline with expectations","Create an external table and query it with SQL"],"ok":[0],"w":["To inspect content and schema once, a batch read is direct and sufficient.","The production answer, not the exploration one; it adds checkpoints and state for no reason here.","Sets up orchestration and continuous validation for a one-time inspection.","Possible, but adds a catalog object for a check a DataFrame does in seconds."],"e":"Loading once to check is exploration; ingesting as data arrives is production."},{"id":"sa-21","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>In an architecture review, someone claims that Auto Loader is only for continuous streaming and does not handle schema changes. Which description is correct?</p>","o":["It ingests new files incrementally, works for scheduled batch-style runs and for continuous streaming, and supports schema evolution natively","Streaming only, but with schema evolution","Batch and streaming, but no schema evolution","Batch only, and the schema must always be declared"],"ok":[0],"w":["With <code>availableNow</code> it runs as a scheduled incremental job; it has four schema evolution modes.","The evolution part is right; the streaming-only part is not.","Schema evolution is one of its main features.","Wrong on both counts."],"e":"Incremental, batch-style or continuous, with schema inference and evolution."},{"id":"sa-22","sec":["A2","P1"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>An Auto Loader pipeline was pointed at a new folder, but it keeps reprocessing files that were ingested in a previous run. What is the most likely cause?</p>","o":["The checkpoint location was reused or deleted in a way that does not match the new source","The schema evolution mode is <code>addNewColumns</code>","<code>OPTIMIZE</code> was not run on the target","<code>pathGlobFilter</code> is too permissive"],"ok":[0],"w":["The checkpoint records which files were processed; if it does not match the source, files are read again.","That mode deals with new columns, not with which files were processed.","Layout of the target has nothing to do with tracking source files.","That would let in other file types, not reprocess the same files."],"e":"Exactly-once in Auto Loader rests on the checkpoint: one per stream, never shared or casually deleted."},{"id":"site-098","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An Auto Loader stream in directory listing mode reads a path that now holds millions of files, and every run gets slower. What is the recommended change?</p>","o":["Switch to <code>COPY INTO</code>","Enable file events on the external location and use file notification mode (<code>cloudFiles.useManagedFileEvents</code>), keeping the same checkpoint","Delete the checkpoint and restart","Lower <code>maxFilesPerTrigger</code> to 1"],"ok":[1],"w":["It also lists the folder and is meant for smaller volumes.","New files are discovered from events instead of listing, and switching modes keeps exactly-once processing.","Everything would be reprocessed.","Makes runs slower; the listing cost stays."],"e":"Huge directories: file notification (managed file events) instead of directory listing."},{"id":"site-099","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>The same JSON files are loaded once with <code>read_files</code> and once with Auto Loader, both with default options. What difference do you expect in the column types?</p>","o":["None; both infer types","<code>read_files</code> infers types; Auto Loader infers JSON columns as STRING unless <code>cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes</code> is true","Auto Loader infers types; <code>read_files</code> returns strings","Both return a single VARIANT column"],"ok":[1],"w":["Their defaults differ.","Auto Loader keeps JSON, CSV and XML columns as strings to avoid type conflicts between files.","The other way round.","Only with <code>singleVariantColumn</code> or an explicit VARIANT schema."],"e":"Auto Loader: strings by default for text formats (use <code>inferColumnTypes</code> or <code>schemaHints</code>). <code>read_files</code>: typed."},{"id":"site-053","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>You must record the source file path of every ingested row. Which column can you select during ingestion?</p>","o":["<code>input_file_name()</code> only","<code>_metadata.file_path</code>","<code>_rescued_data</code>","<code>__START_AT</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["The older function, not supported on all Unity Catalog compute.","The hidden <code>_metadata</code> column offers <code>file_path</code>, <code>file_name</code>, <code>file_size</code> and <code>file_modification_time</code>; select it explicitly.","Holds data that did not fit the schema.","Not a file metadata column."],"e":"Lineage to the source file: <code>_metadata.file_path</code>."},{"id":"site-101","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An analyst wants a table from a single 3 GB CSV file on their laptop. The <em>Create or modify table</em> upload page rejects it. What works?</p>","o":["Zip the file and upload it again","Upload the file to a Unity Catalog volume and create the table from there (<code>read_files</code>, <code>COPY INTO</code> or Catalog Explorer)","Split it into 20 files and use the upload page","Use Lakeflow Connect"],"ok":[1],"w":["The upload page does not accept compressed archives.","Volumes accept larger files and keep the file for reloads.","The page takes at most 10 files and under 2 GB in total.","It ingests from applications and databases, not from a laptop."],"e":"Upload page: small, one-off. Bigger files: volume first, then load."},{"id":"site-102","sec":["A2","P4"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A security team ingests delivered audit log files with Auto Loader (default options) and finds events in <code>system.access.audit</code> that never reach their bronze table. Why, and what fixes it?</p>","o":["Audit logs are Parquet; set <code>cloudFiles.format</code> to <code>parquet</code>","Delivered files can be rewritten with extra lines, and Auto Loader processes each path only once; set <code>cloudFiles.allowOverwrites = true</code> and deduplicate","Switch to file notification mode","Start a new checkpoint every day"],"ok":[1],"w":["The format is not the problem.","With overwrites allowed the file is read again, so duplicates must be removed (for example with an insert-only <code>MERGE</code>).","Discovery mode does not change the once-per-path rule.","Reprocesses everything and duplicates data."],"e":"Files rewritten in place are not read again by default. The simplest alternative is to query <code>system.access.audit</code> directly."},{"id":"sp-13","sec":["P1","P2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A folder receives JSON files of very different sizes. How do you limit each micro-batch to about 2 GB, whatever the number of files?</p>","o":["<code>cloudFiles.maxBytesPerTrigger = 2g</code>","<code>cloudFiles.maxFilesPerTrigger = 100</code>","<code>cloudFiles.maxSizePerTrigger</code>","<code>spark.sql.files.maxPartitionBytes</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Limits each micro-batch by volume (a soft limit).","Limits by count; 100 files can be any size.","Not an Auto Loader option.","Controls read partition size in batch, not micro-batch size."],"e":"Rate limits: <code>maxFilesPerTrigger</code> (count) and <code>maxBytesPerTrigger</code> (bytes)."},{"id":"sp-16","sec":["P2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>An engineer declared <code>.schema(\"id INT, amount DOUBLE\")</code> on a <code>cloudFiles</code> JSON stream with a bad records path, and finds syntactically valid JSON records in that path. What is the most likely explanation?</p>","o":["The records are valid JSON but do not fit the declared schema, for example a value of an incompatible type","The bad records path only receives empty files","Auto Loader moves files larger than 128 MB there","The checkpoint is corrupted"],"ok":[0],"w":["An explicit schema is checked record by record; well-formed JSON can still break the type contract.","It receives records that fail parsing or schema conversion.","File size is not a criterion.","A corrupted checkpoint causes a run failure, not bad records."],"e":"Malformed versus mismatched: valid JSON can still be a bad record when it does not fit the schema."},{"id":"sp-27","sec":["A2","P1"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>How do you declare, in pipeline SQL, a table that ingests files from a volume incrementally?</p>","o":["<code>CREATE OR REFRESH STREAMING TABLE bronze AS SELECT * FROM STREAM read_files('/Volumes/...')</code>","<code>CREATE TABLE bronze AS SELECT * FROM read_files('/Volumes/...')</code>","<code>CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW bronze AS SELECT * FROM read_files(...)</code>","<code>INSERT OVERWRITE bronze SELECT * FROM read_files(...)</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["A streaming table processes only new files on each update.","A one-time static load.","A materialized view represents a query result, not append-only file ingestion.","Rewrites the whole table every run."],"e":"Incremental file ingestion in SQL: streaming table over <code>STREAM read_files</code>."},{"id":"new-ingest-files-02","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Auto Loader infers every column of a JSON feed as STRING. You want <code>amount</code> as <code>DECIMAL(10,2)</code> and <code>event_ts</code> as <code>TIMESTAMP</code> without writing the full schema. Which option?</p>","o":["<code>cloudFiles.schemaHints = \"amount DECIMAL(10,2), event_ts TIMESTAMP\"</code>","<code>cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode = \"addNewColumns\"</code>","<code>cloudFiles.allowOverwrites = true</code>","<code>mergeSchema = true</code> on the write"],"ok":[0],"w":["Hints override the inferred type of the named columns only.","Controls what happens with new columns, not types.","Controls reprocessing of rewritten files.","Adds columns to the target; it does not type the source."],"e":"<code>schemaHints</code> for a few columns; <code>inferColumnTypes</code> to infer all types."},{"id":"site-052","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-files","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>What is Auto Loader's default mode for discovering new files?</p>","o":["File notification","Directory listing","Kafka offsets","Table update events"],"ok":[1],"w":["Must be enabled explicitly (recommended with managed file events for large volumes).","Auto Loader lists the input directory on each run unless notification mode is configured.","Kafka is a different source.","A job trigger, not an Auto Loader mode."],"e":"Default: directory listing. At scale: file notification with managed file events."},{"id":"site-009","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A company needs to ingest data from Salesforce into Delta tables without writing or maintaining API code. What should they use?</p>","o":["Auto Loader","A notebook with the <code>requests</code> library","A Lakeflow Connect managed connector","<code>COPY INTO</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Ingests files from cloud storage, not data from a SaaS API.","That is exactly the API code they do not want to maintain.","Managed connectors ingest from SaaS applications and databases incrementally, with no code.","Loads files from storage."],"e":"SaaS or a supported database, no code to maintain: Lakeflow Connect managed connector."},{"id":"site-054","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Events arrive in an Apache Kafka topic and must be ingested with low latency. Which approach?</p>","o":["<code>COPY INTO</code>","Structured Streaming (or a streaming table) reading from Kafka","Lakehouse Federation","Manual upload"],"ok":[1],"w":["Loads files, not messages from a broker.","Kafka is a streaming source for Structured Streaming and for <code>read_kafka</code> in pipelines.","Federates databases, not message brokers.","Not continuous and not low latency."],"e":"Message buses (Kafka, Kinesis, Event Hubs): Structured Streaming or streaming tables."},{"id":"site-096","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An engineer reads a table over JDBC with <code>partitionColumn = 'order_id'</code>, <code>lowerBound = 1</code>, <code>upperBound = 1000</code> and <code>numPartitions = 8</code>, expecting only the first 1,000 orders, but gets millions of rows. Why?</p>","o":["<code>numPartitions</code> is too high","<code>lowerBound</code> and <code>upperBound</code> only define the partition stride; they do not filter rows","JDBC ignores <code>partitionColumn</code> on serverless","The driver needs <code>fetchsize</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["It changes parallelism, not which rows are read.","Rows outside the bounds go to the first and last partitions. Filter with a <code>WHERE</code> in a <code>dbtable</code> subquery.","Not the reason, and partitioned JDBC reads work on serverless.","<code>fetchsize</code> tunes round trips, not filtering."],"e":"JDBC bounds split the work; they do not filter."},{"id":"new-ingest-sources-02","sec":["P2","A2"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A Lakeflow Connect ingestion from SQL Server has two components: an ingestion gateway and an ingestion pipeline. Which statement is correct?</p>","o":["The gateway runs continuously on classic compute, reads snapshots and change data from SQL Server and stages them in a volume; the serverless pipeline applies the staged changes to streaming tables","The gateway runs only during the scheduled pipeline update, to save cost","The pipeline reads SQL Server directly; the gateway only stores credentials","Both run on the analysts' SQL warehouse"],"ok":[0],"w":["The gateway must keep running so no change log entries are missed.","Stopping it risks losing change log entries the database purges.","Credentials live in the connection; the gateway does the extraction.","Neither runs on a SQL warehouse."],"e":"Database connectors: always-on gateway (extract and stage) plus scheduled serverless pipeline (apply)."},{"id":"new-ingest-sources-03","sec":["P2","P1"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Before turning on a Lakeflow Connect ingestion from MySQL, what must be true of the source database?</p>","o":["It must be exported to CSV every night","Change capture must be enabled (the binary log for MySQL) so the connector can read changes incrementally","It must be migrated to Delta Lake first","It must be reachable through a public IP without authentication"],"ok":[1],"w":["Managed connectors read the database directly.","SQL Server needs change tracking or CDC, PostgreSQL logical replication, MySQL the binlog.","The point of the connector is to ingest from the database as it is.","Connections always authenticate, and private networking is supported."],"e":"Incremental database ingestion depends on the database's change log being enabled."},{"id":"new-ingest-sources-04","sec":["A2"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A notebook ingests a paginated REST API every hour. Which design is most robust?</p>","o":["Store the API token in the notebook and write responses to <code>/tmp</code> on the driver, then load them","Read the token from a secret scope, follow the pages, retry with backoff on HTTP 429, write each raw response as a file to a volume, and load the folder with Auto Loader; schedule it in a Lakeflow job","Call the API once per row with a Python UDF on a large cluster","Use Lakehouse Federation on the API URL"],"ok":[1],"w":["The token is exposed and <code>/tmp</code> disappears with the compute.","Secrets stay secret, rate limits are respected, and raw files give replay and lineage.","Floods the API, ignores rate limits and is hard to retry.","Federation connects to databases, not REST APIs."],"e":"REST ingestion: secrets, pagination, backoff, land raw files in a volume, then load incrementally."},{"id":"new-ingest-sources-05","sec":["A2","A1"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A nightly notebook must read an old database through an ODBC driver that is installed with an init script. Which compute can run it?</p>","o":["Serverless jobs","A serverless SQL warehouse","Classic job compute with the init script","Any compute, because ODBC is built in"],"ok":[2],"w":["Serverless does not run init scripts, so the driver cannot be installed.","Runs SQL only.","Classic compute runs init scripts that install drivers.","ODBC drivers for most databases are not preinstalled."],"e":"Anything that needs init scripts or custom drivers runs on classic compute."},{"id":"new-ingest-sources-06","sec":["A2","A7"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A team connected Fivetran through Partner Connect. Fivetran created tables in a schema, but analysts get permission errors when they query them. Why?</p>","o":["Partner Connect tables can only be read through Fivetran","The tables are owned by the partner's service principal, and nobody granted the analysts access","The analysts need <code>MODIFY</code> to read","Partner tables are external tables, which analysts cannot query"],"ok":[1],"w":["They are ordinary Unity Catalog tables.","Grant <code>USE CATALOG</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code> and <code>SELECT</code> (on the schema or tables) to the analysts' group.","Reading needs <code>SELECT</code>, not <code>MODIFY</code>.","Table type has nothing to do with the error."],"e":"The identity that creates a table owns it; others need grants."},{"id":"new-ingest-sources-07","sec":["P1","P2"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A bronze streaming table reads a Kafka topic that keeps messages for 7 days. Someone runs a full refresh of the pipeline. What is the risk and how do you prevent it?</p>","o":["No risk: a full refresh reads Kafka from the first message ever produced","The table is truncated and rebuilt from the last 7 days only, so older data is lost; set <code>pipelines.reset.allowed = false</code> on the bronze table","Duplicates appear because Kafka offsets are reused","The refresh fails because Kafka does not support restarts"],"ok":[1],"w":["Kafka only holds what is still inside its retention period.","That table property protects it from full refreshes.","A full refresh resets the checkpoint and truncates the table, so the issue is loss, not duplication.","It succeeds, which is the problem."],"e":"Sources with limited retention: protect bronze streaming tables from full refresh."},{"id":"site-097","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"ingest-sources","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An admin wants analysts to build ServiceNow ingestion pipelines without ever seeing the ServiceNow credentials. What should the admin do?</p>","o":["Share the password in a secret scope the analysts can read","Create a Unity Catalog connection and grant <code>USE CONNECTION</code> (plus <code>USE CATALOG</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code> and <code>CREATE TABLE</code> on the target)","Grant <code>CREATE CONNECTION</code> on the metastore to the analysts","Make the analysts workspace admins"],"ok":[1],"w":["They could read the secret.","The connection stores the credentials; <code>USE CONNECTION</code> lets analysts build pipelines with it.","Lets them create new connections; not needed to use an existing one.","Far too much access."],"e":"Credentials live in connections; <code>USE CONNECTION</code> uses them without revealing them."},{"id":"site-012","sec":["A2","A3","P3"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A STRING column <code>raw</code> contains JSON such as <code>{\"customer\":{\"address\":{\"city\":\"Lisbon\"}}}</code>. Which SQL expression extracts the city?</p>","o":["<code>raw.customer.address.city</code>","<code>raw:customer.address.city</code>","<code>raw-&gt;customer-&gt;address-&gt;city</code>","<code>get_json(raw, 'city')</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Dot notation is for STRUCT columns; on a string it fails.","The colon path syntax works on JSON strings (and on VARIANT).","Not Databricks SQL syntax.","There is no such function, and it would ignore the nesting."],"e":"JSON string or VARIANT: <code>col:path</code>. STRUCT: <code>col.field</code>."},{"id":"site-057","sec":["A2","P3"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Which approach is recommended to store semi-structured JSON whose structure changes often?</p>","o":["A STRING column","A VARIANT column (<code>parse_json</code>)","One table per field","A BINARY column"],"ok":[1],"w":["Flexible, but parsed again on every query.","VARIANT keeps the flexibility of a string with much better query performance.","Unmanageable and slow to query.","Opaque to SQL."],"e":"Changing JSON structures: VARIANT."},{"id":"site-100","sec":["P3","A2"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"P","src":"site","q":"<p>A table moves a JSON column from STRING to VARIANT, and the expression <code>raw:items[*].sku</code> stops working. What should be used on the VARIANT column?</p>","o":["<code>raw.items.sku</code>","<code>LATERAL variant_explode(raw:items)</code> and then <code>value:sku</code>","<code>explode(raw:items[*])</code>","<code>from_json(raw:items)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Dot notation is for structs.","The <code>[*]</code> wildcard only works on JSON strings; on VARIANT, explode the array.","The wildcard is still not supported on VARIANT.","<code>from_json</code> parses strings and needs a schema."],"e":"On VARIANT: <code>variant_explode</code> instead of <code>[*]</code>, and remember that paths are case sensitive."},{"id":"site-018","sec":["A3"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A column <code>tags</code> is an array; some rows have NULL or empty arrays. Those rows must stay in the output with a NULL tag. Which function?</p>","o":["<code>explode</code>","<code>explode_outer</code>","<code>flatten</code>","<code>posexplode</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Drops rows whose array is NULL or empty.","It keeps those rows and returns NULL for the element.","Turns an array of arrays into one array; it does not create rows.","Adds the position but drops NULL or empty arrays like <code>explode</code>."],"e":"<code>explode</code> loses rows with NULL or empty arrays; <code>explode_outer</code> keeps them."},{"id":"new-semi-01","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Scanned contracts (PDF) and photos (JPEG) arrive in a volume. A pipeline must register every new file with its path, size and raw bytes for later processing. Which reader fits?</p>","o":["<code>format(\"json\")</code> with <code>multiLine</code>","Auto Loader with <code>cloudFiles.format = \"binaryFile\"</code>","<code>format(\"text\")</code>","<code>COPY INTO</code> with <code>FILEFORMAT = CSV</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Parses JSON documents, not binary files.","Each file becomes a row with <code>path</code>, <code>modificationTime</code>, <code>length</code> and <code>content</code>, ingested incrementally.","Splits files into lines of text, which corrupts binary content.","Treats the bytes as delimited text."],"e":"Unstructured files: <code>binaryFile</code>, landed in a volume, then processed (for example with <code>ai_parse_document</code>)."},{"id":"new-semi-02","sec":["P3","P2"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A JSON feed changes shape every few weeks. The team wants bronze ingestion that never fails on new fields and keeps every record whole, extracting fields later in silver. Which Auto Loader setting fits?</p>","o":["<code>cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode = \"failOnNewColumns\"</code>","<code>singleVariantColumn</code> so each record is stored in one VARIANT column","<code>cloudFiles.inferColumnTypes = true</code>","Read the files as text and store each line as a STRING"],"ok":[1],"w":["Stops on every change.","There is no schema to evolve; silver extracts fields with paths such as <code>v:customer.id</code>.","Infers types but still evolves a column schema.","Works, but every later query must parse the string again; VARIANT is faster."],"e":"Whole-record VARIANT ingestion trades schema management for flexible, fast path queries."},{"id":"new-semi-03","sec":["P3"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A query on a VARIANT column fails: <code>SELECT v:country, count(*) FROM events GROUP BY v:country</code>. What fixes it?</p>","o":["Use <code>v.country</code> instead of <code>v:country</code>","Cast the value before grouping, for example <code>GROUP BY v:country::STRING</code>","Add <code>DISTINCT</code>","Convert the table to JSON strings"],"ok":[1],"w":["Dot notation is for structs.","VARIANT values cannot be grouped, sorted or compared directly.","<code>DISTINCT</code> on VARIANT has the same restriction.","Unnecessary; a cast solves it."],"e":"Cast VARIANT paths before <code>GROUP BY</code>, <code>ORDER BY</code>, <code>DISTINCT</code> or comparisons."},{"id":"new-semi-04","sec":["P3"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>The JSON documents use the key <code>\"Customer\"</code>. On the STRING column, <code>raw:customer.id</code> works. After converting to VARIANT, <code>v:customer.id</code> returns NULL. Why?</p>","o":["VARIANT does not support nested objects","Paths on VARIANT are case sensitive, so <code>v:Customer.id</code> is required","<code>parse_json</code> lowercases every key","The value must be extracted with <code>from_json</code> first"],"ok":[1],"w":["It does.","JSON-string paths ignore case; VARIANT paths do not.","It keeps keys as they are.","<code>from_json</code> parses strings into structs; it is not needed on VARIANT."],"e":"VARIANT field names are case sensitive."},{"id":"new-semi-05","sec":["A2","A3"],"topic":"semi-structured","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A silver job must turn a JSON string column into typed columns. Which call parses it into a STRUCT?</p>","o":["<code>from_json(raw)</code>","<code>from_json(raw, \"id INT, customer STRUCT&lt;name: STRING, city: STRING&gt;\")</code>","<code>to_json(raw)</code>","<code>parse_json(raw).*</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>from_json</code> requires a schema.","The schema defines the struct; <code>schema_of_json</code> can derive one from a sample.","Does the opposite: serialises a struct to a string.","<code>parse_json</code> returns a VARIANT, and <code>.*</code> expands structs, not VARIANT."],"e":"String to STRUCT: <code>from_json</code> with a schema. String to VARIANT: <code>parse_json</code>."},{"id":"sa-09","sec":["A2","P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A bank must join lakehouse tables with data in an internal PostgreSQL database. Auditors require the numbers to always reflect the current state of the source and forbid keeping a second copy of the data. Which approach meets both requirements?</p>","o":["Lakehouse Federation, with a connection and a foreign catalog in Unity Catalog","An incremental ingestion pipeline that replicates PostgreSQL every hour","Export the tables to CSV and load them daily","Delta Sharing from PostgreSQL"],"ok":[0],"w":["Queries run against the source when they are executed: no copy, no lag.","Creates the forbidden copy and up to an hour of lag.","Manual, copied and a day late.","Sharing publishes Databricks tables to recipients; it does not query an external database."],"e":"No copy + always current + external database = Lakehouse Federation."},{"id":"sp-36","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>What is the difference between a foreign catalog and an external location?</p>","o":["A foreign catalog exposes an external database for federated queries; an external location grants access to a cloud storage path","They are synonyms","A foreign catalog copies the data into the metastore","An external location is only for streaming"],"ok":[0],"w":["One is for databases, the other for files in object storage.","They serve different purposes.","The data stays in the source.","It governs any access to a cloud path."],"e":"Foreign catalog = database (federation). External location = storage path (files, external tables, volumes)."},{"id":"sa-13","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A hotel group wants to share occupancy tables with a consultancy that also uses Databricks with Unity Catalog, but in another account and another cloud. Which approach applies and what must the consultancy provide?</p>","o":["Databricks-to-Databricks sharing, using the sharing identifier of the consultancy's Unity Catalog metastore","Open sharing with a credential file","Direct access to the storage container that holds the tables","Export to Parquet and transfer by SFTP every week"],"ok":[0],"w":["With Unity Catalog on both sides, sharing goes metastore to metastore; the identifier looks like <code>cloud:region:metastore-id</code>.","It would work, but it is meant for recipients outside Databricks; D2D avoids managing tokens.","Exposes raw files and bypasses Unity Catalog governance and audit.","Creates copies and lag, without object-level access control."],"e":"The recipient's platform decides the mode: Databricks with Unity Catalog on both sides means D2D and a metastore sharing identifier."},{"id":"sa-14","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>The same company now needs to share a subset of those tables with a partner that uses another data platform and its own BI tools, without Databricks. How do they share securely?</p>","o":["OpenSharing with the open protocol (open sharing)","Databricks-to-Databricks sharing","Mount the storage container in the partner's environment","Build a custom API to serve the tables"],"ok":[0],"w":["Any compatible client can read through the REST-based protocol, with a credential, while access stays governed on the provider side.","Requires a recipient with a Unity Catalog workspace, which the partner does not have.","Raw file access, no per-table control, no audit on the provider side.","Reinvents what sharing already does and adds code to maintain."],"e":"Recipient on Databricks with Unity Catalog: D2D. Any other platform: open sharing."},{"id":"sa-15","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Data is shared from an AWS account to a partner on Azure, and the monthly egress bill has grown a lot. The sharing works and will continue. Which strategy specifically reduces the egress cost?</p>","o":["Host the shared dataset in object storage that does not charge egress fees (or replicate it to the partner's region)","Set up a VPN between the two clouds","Switch from D2D to open sharing","Compress the tables with a more aggressive codec"],"ok":[0],"w":["The provider pays for bytes leaving its cloud; where the data lives is what removes the charge.","Improves security, but egress is still billed.","The mode does not change the bytes crossing the cloud boundary.","A marginal gain with extra CPU cost; the fee remains."],"e":"Egress cost is about where the data is stored, not how it is shared."},{"id":"new-sharing-01","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A retailer and a publisher, both on Databricks, want to measure how many customers they have in common. Neither company may see the other's customer records. What should they use?</p>","o":["Each shares its customer table with the other through D2D sharing","A clean room where each adds its table and both approve a notebook that outputs only the overlap counts","Lakehouse Federation between the two workspaces","Export hashed customer lists to CSV and email them"],"ok":[1],"w":["Both would see the other's raw records.","Collaborators run approved code on combined data without direct access to each other's tables.","Federation would give direct query access to the other side's data.","Insecure and ungoverned, and the lists themselves are shared."],"e":"\"Neither side may see the other's data\" = clean room."},{"id":"new-sharing-03","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A provider shares a table with a Databricks recipient through D2D sharing and wants the recipient to be able to read it as a stream and use time travel. How should the table be added to the share?</p>","o":["As a view over the table","<code>ALTER SHARE s ADD TABLE t WITH HISTORY</code>","With <code>VACUUM</code> disabled on the recipient side","As a deep clone in the recipient's metastore"],"ok":[1],"w":["A view does not carry the table's history.","Sharing the table with history lets recipients query previous versions and read it incrementally as a stream.","The recipient cannot change the provider's table maintenance.","That is a copy, which sharing is meant to avoid."],"e":"<code>WITH HISTORY</code> enables time travel and streaming reads for recipients."},{"id":"new-sharing-04","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>After a D2D share is granted to their metastore, how do users at the recipient company start querying the shared tables?</p>","o":["They must copy the tables into their own catalog first","A recipient admin creates a catalog from the share (<code>CREATE CATALOG ... USING SHARE provider.share</code>) and grants their users access to it","The provider adds each recipient user to the share","They download a credential file and use pandas"],"ok":[1],"w":["Shared data is read in place.","The shared catalog then behaves like a read-only catalog in their metastore.","The provider grants the share to the recipient (metastore); the recipient manages its own users.","That is the open sharing flow, not D2D."],"e":"D2D: provider grants to the metastore; recipient mounts the share as a catalog and grants internally."},{"id":"sp-35","sec":["P2"],"topic":"sharing","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which steps let you query an external PostgreSQL database directly from Databricks, without copying the data (Lakehouse Federation)?</p>","o":["Create a <code>CONNECTION</code>, then a <code>FOREIGN CATALOG</code> on it, then grant privileges in Unity Catalog","Create an external location and a storage credential","Mirror everything with Auto Loader","Partner Connect with ACLs in PostgreSQL"],"ok":[0],"w":["Queries are federated; the data stays in the source.","Those govern cloud storage paths, not databases.","Copies data, and Auto Loader ingests files.","Keeps governance outside Unity Catalog."],"e":"Federation = connection + foreign catalog + grants."},{"id":"sp-33","sec":["P2","P6"],"topic":"sharing","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Who can create OpenSharing shares without being a metastore admin?</p>","o":["Users granted the <code>CREATE SHARE</code> privilege on the metastore","Any user with <code>USE CATALOG</code>","Any user with permission on a cluster","Only workspace admins"],"ok":[0],"w":["Share creation can be delegated precisely.","Only lets them reference the catalog.","Compute permissions do not allow sharing data.","Sharing is governed in the metastore, not by the workspace admin role."],"e":"Shares and recipients are metastore-level securables: <code>CREATE SHARE</code>, <code>CREATE RECIPIENT</code>."},{"id":"site-061","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>How do you get the FIRST element of the array produced by this expression?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">split(col(&quot;full_name&quot;), &quot; &quot;)\n</code></pre>","o":["<code>getItem(1)</code>","<code>getItem(0)</code> or <code>element_at(..., 1)</code>","<code>element_at(..., 0)</code>","<code>first()</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>getItem</code> and <code>[i]</code> are 0-based, so index 1 is the second element.","Bracket and <code>getItem</code> indexing start at 0; <code>element_at</code> starts at 1.","<code>element_at</code> is 1-based; index 0 raises an error.","<code>first</code> is an aggregate over rows, not an array accessor."],"e":"<code>[i]</code> and <code>getItem</code> start at 0. <code>element_at</code> starts at 1 and <code>try_element_at</code> returns NULL out of range."},{"id":"site-104","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p><code>order_date</code> strings look like <code>07/03/2026</code>, meaning 7 March. Which expression parses them correctly?</p>","o":["<code>to_date(order_date)</code>","<code>to_date(order_date, 'dd/mm/yyyy')</code>","<code>to_date(order_date, 'dd/MM/yyyy')</code>","<code>CAST(order_date AS DATE)</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Without a format, <code>to_date</code> behaves like a cast and expects <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>.","Lower-case <code>mm</code> means minutes, not months.","<code>dd</code> is the day, <code>MM</code> the month, <code>yyyy</code> the year.","A cast expects the ISO format and fails (or returns NULL) on <code>07/03/2026</code>."],"e":"In datetime patterns, <code>M</code> is month and <code>m</code> is minute. Use <code>try_to_date</code> when bad strings must become NULL instead of failing."},{"id":"sa-50","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>While reviewing SQL scripts, you need the statement that defines the structure of a new table. Which is a DDL statement that creates a table?</p>","o":["<code>CREATE TABLE sensors (id INT, location STRING);</code>","<code>DROP TABLE sensors;</code>","<code>INSERT INTO sensors VALUES (1, 'Porto');</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE sensors ADD COLUMN altitude INT;</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["<code>CREATE TABLE</code> defines a new table's name, columns and types.","Also DDL, but it removes a table instead of creating one.","DML: it changes data inside an existing table.","DDL, but it changes an existing table instead of creating one."],"e":"DDL defines structure (CREATE, ALTER, DROP, TRUNCATE); DML changes data (INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE)."},{"id":"site-013","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>What is the problem with this code?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df1 = spark.createDataFrame([(1, &quot;Ana&quot;)], [&quot;id&quot;, &quot;name&quot;])\ndf2 = spark.createDataFrame([(&quot;Rui&quot;, 2)], [&quot;name&quot;, &quot;id&quot;])\nresult = df1.union(df2)\n</code></pre>","o":["<code>union</code> removes duplicates","<code>union</code> matches columns by position, so names and ids get mixed up; use <code>unionByName</code>","<code>union</code> fails because the column order differs","There is no problem"],"ok":[1],"w":["PySpark <code>union</code> keeps duplicates.","Columns are paired by position, not by name.","It fails only if types cannot be reconciled; otherwise it silently mixes the columns.","The result has a name in the <code>id</code> column."],"e":"Different column order: <code>unionByName</code>."},{"id":"site-014","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Table A has 3 rows and table B has the same 3 rows. How many rows does <code>SELECT * FROM A UNION SELECT * FROM B</code> return?</p>","o":["0","3","6","An error"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>UNION</code> combines rows; it does not subtract them.","SQL <code>UNION</code> removes duplicates (<code>UNION DISTINCT</code>).","That is <code>UNION ALL</code>.","Same schemas, so the query runs."],"e":"SQL <code>UNION</code> deduplicates; <code>UNION ALL</code> and PySpark <code>union</code> do not."},{"id":"new-transform-01","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Two DataFrames hold the same kind of records, but the second one has an extra column <code>channel</code> and the columns are in a different order. You must stack them, filling <code>channel</code> with NULL for the first. Which call?</p>","o":["<code>df1.union(df2)</code>","<code>df1.unionByName(df2, allowMissingColumns=True)</code>","<code>df1.join(df2, \"id\", \"full\")</code>","<code>df1.crossJoin(df2)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Matches by position and fails because the column counts differ.","Columns are matched by name and the missing one is filled with NULL.","Combines columns side by side, not rows.","Every combination of rows."],"e":"Stack rows by name, tolerating missing columns: <code>unionByName(allowMissingColumns=True)</code>."},{"id":"site-015","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A query uses <code>WHERE discount = NULL</code> and returns no rows, although some rows have NULL in <code>discount</code>. What is the fix?</p>","o":["<code>WHERE discount == NULL</code>","<code>WHERE discount IS NULL</code>","<code>WHERE discount = 'NULL'</code>","<code>WHERE isnull = discount</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Any comparison with NULL returns NULL.","Use <code>IS NULL</code> / <code>isNull()</code>, or the null-safe <code>&lt;=&gt;</code>.","Compares with the text \"NULL\".","Not valid."],"e":"Comparisons with NULL are never true; test NULL with <code>IS NULL</code>."},{"id":"new-transform-02","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Two tables must be joined on <code>(customer_id, region)</code>, and <code>region</code> is NULL for some customers in both tables. Those rows must match each other. Which condition?</p>","o":["<code>a.region = b.region</code>","<code>a.region &lt;=&gt; b.region</code> (<code>eqNullSafe</code> in PySpark)","<code>coalesce(a.region) = coalesce(b.region)</code>","A cross join filtered afterwards"],"ok":[1],"w":["NULL never equals NULL, so those rows are lost.","The null-safe operator treats two NULLs as equal.","<code>coalesce</code> with one argument changes nothing.","Expensive and still needs a null-safe comparison."],"e":"Normal joins never match NULL keys; <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> does."},{"id":"site-058","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A column <code>bonus</code> holds the values 100, NULL and 200. What does <code>SELECT count(bonus), count(*)</code> return?</p>","o":["3, 3","2, 3","2, 2","3, 2"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>count(bonus)</code> skips the NULL.","<code>count(col)</code> ignores NULLs; <code>count(*)</code> counts every row.","<code>count(*)</code> counts all rows.","Reversed."],"e":"<code>count(col)</code>, <code>sum</code> and <code>avg</code> ignore NULLs."},{"id":"site-112","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>The average order value looks too high. Some orders have a NULL amount, and the business rule says a missing amount means 0. What should you compute?</p>","o":["<code>avg(amount)</code>","<code>avg(coalesce(amount, 0))</code>","<code>sum(amount) / count(amount)</code>","<code>count(*)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Ignores NULL rows, so the average covers only orders with a value.","NULLs become 0 and count in the average.","Same as <code>avg(amount)</code>.","A count, not an average."],"e":"When NULL means zero, replace it before aggregating."},{"id":"site-059","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p><code>df</code> has the columns <code>qty</code> (int) and <code>region</code> (string). What does <code>df.fillna(0)</code> do?</p>","o":["Fills NULLs in all columns with 0 or \"0\"","Fills NULLs only in numeric columns such as <code>qty</code>","Fails because <code>region</code> is a string","Drops rows with NULL"],"ok":[1],"w":["The value's type decides which columns are filled.","For several types use a dictionary: <code>fillna({\"qty\": 0, \"region\": \"n/a\"})</code>.","It simply skips the string column.","That is <code>dropna</code>."],"e":"<code>fillna</code> fills only columns whose type matches the value."},{"id":"site-103","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>On a recent runtime with ANSI mode enabled, <code>CAST(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2))</code> fails because some bronze rows contain <code>'N/A'</code>. What is the recommended fix?</p>","o":["Set <code>spark.sql.ansi.enabled = false</code> for the session","Use <code>try_cast(amount AS DECIMAL(10,2))</code> and handle the resulting NULLs","<code>fillna(\"0\")</code> before the cast","<code>CAST(... AS STRING)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Changes behaviour for every query in the session (for example, silent overflows).","Invalid values become NULL, which you can count or quarantine.","<code>'N/A'</code> is not NULL, so nothing changes.","The column stays text."],"e":"ANSI mode: use <code>try_cast</code>, <code>try_to_date</code>, <code>try_element_at</code>, <code>try_divide</code>."},{"id":"site-108","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>With ANSI mode on, <code>split(order_ref, '-')[2]</code> fails with <code>INVALID_ARRAY_INDEX</code> for short values such as <code>'GB-LON'</code>. Which expression returns NULL instead?</p>","o":["<code>split(order_ref, '-')[3]</code>","<code>element_at(split(order_ref, '-'), 2)</code>","<code>try_element_at(split(order_ref, '-'), 3)</code>","<code>getItem(2)</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Still out of range, and still fails.","Returns the second element, not the third, and also fails when out of range.","It is 1-based and returns NULL when the index is out of range.","Same as <code>[2]</code>."],"e":"<code>[i]</code> is 0-based and fails out of range under ANSI; <code>try_element_at</code> is 1-based and returns NULL."},{"id":"site-016","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>You need to keep only the most recent record per <code>customer_id</code>, based on <code>updated_at</code>. Which approach guarantees this?</p>","o":["<code>df.dropDuplicates([\"customer_id\"])</code>","<code>df.distinct()</code>","<code>row_number()</code> over a window partitioned by <code>customer_id</code> ordered by <code>updated_at</code> descending, then keep row number 1","<code>df.groupBy(\"customer_id\").count()</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Keeps an arbitrary row per key.","Removes only rows identical in every column.","The order decides which row survives (in SQL, <code>QUALIFY</code>).","Counts rows; the records are lost."],"e":"Latest row per key: <code>row_number()</code> with an ordered window, plus a tiebreaker for equal timestamps."},{"id":"site-017","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Which join returns the customers that have NO matching rows in the orders table, with only the customer columns?</p>","o":["<code>left_outer</code>","<code>left_semi</code>","<code>left_anti</code>","<code>cross</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Returns all customers with order columns (NULL when no match).","Returns customers that DO have orders.","Left rows without a match, left columns only.","Every combination of customers and orders."],"e":"Semi = exists; anti = not exists. Both return only left columns."},{"id":"sa-52","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A sales fact table must be joined with a product dimension, keeping every sale even when the product is missing from the dimension. Which join?</p>","o":["A left join, with sales on the left","An inner join","A cross join","A right join, with sales on the left"],"ok":[0],"w":["All left rows are kept, with NULLs where the dimension has no match.","Drops sales whose product is missing.","Produces every combination of sales and products.","Keeps every product and drops unmatched sales: the opposite."],"e":"Fact on the left with a left join when no fact row may be lost."},{"id":"site-106","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>This <code>select</code> fails with <code>AMBIGUOUS_REFERENCE</code>. What is the simplest fix?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">(orders.join(prices,\n     (orders.product_id == prices.product_id) &amp; (orders.region == prices.region))\n   .select(&quot;product_id&quot;, &quot;unit_price&quot;))\n</code></pre>","o":["Drop <code>\"product_id\"</code> after the join","Join on a list of column names: <code>orders.join(prices, [\"product_id\", \"region\"])</code>","Use a cross join","Use <code>unionByName</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Dropping by name removes both copies.","A join on names keeps one copy of each key.","Changes the result completely.","Stacks rows; it is not a join."],"e":"Expression joins keep both key columns; name-list joins (<code>USING</code>) keep one."},{"id":"new-transform-04","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A report needs one row for every combination of the 365 days of 2026 and the 40 stores, even for days with no sales. Which join builds that grid?</p>","o":["<code>inner</code> join of days and stores on the date","A cross join of the days table and the stores table","<code>left_semi</code> join","<code>unionByName</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Stores do not have a date column to join on.","Every day is combined with every store (365 × 40 rows); sales can then be left-joined onto the grid.","Returns only the left columns of matching rows.","Stacks rows instead of combining them."],"e":"Cross join = every combination; use it deliberately for grids and calendars."},{"id":"sa-48","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>You must compute, per weather station, the maximum temperature, the average temperature and the number of distinct sensors that reported. Assess this PySpark code:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from pyspark.sql import functions as F\n\ndaily = (readings.groupBy(&quot;station_id&quot;)\n    .agg(F.max(&quot;temp_c&quot;).alias(&quot;temp_max&quot;),\n         F.avg(&quot;temp_c&quot;).alias(&quot;temp_avg&quot;),\n         F.countDistinct(&quot;sensor_id&quot;).alias(&quot;n_sensors&quot;)))\n</code></pre>","o":["The code is correct","It should use <code>readings.groupby(...)</code> in lower case","It should pass the aggregations as strings such as <code>\"max(temp_c)\"</code>","It should use a dictionary <code>{\"temp_c\": \"max\", \"temp_c\": \"avg\"}</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["<code>groupBy</code> in camelCase, <code>agg</code> with column expressions, each with an alias; this is the form that allows several aggregations of the same column.","Lower case is the pandas convention; the PySpark method is <code>groupBy</code> (<code>groupby</code> is only an alias).","<code>agg</code> takes column expressions here.","Duplicate keys collapse into the last one, so one aggregation would be lost."],"e":"Several aggregations of the same column need expressions with aliases."},{"id":"sa-49","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A team wants total sales per product category, but the result has a single row summing everything. What is missing?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">total = sales.agg(F.sum(&quot;amount&quot;).alias(&quot;total_sales&quot;))\n</code></pre>","o":["A <code>groupBy</code> on the category column before <code>agg</code>","An alias on the sum","Converting to pandas before aggregating","Sorting with <code>orderBy</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Without <code>groupBy</code>, <code>agg</code> aggregates the whole DataFrame into one row.","The alias is there and only names the result.","Pulls all data to the driver for nothing.","Sorting changes presentation, not the aggregation level."],"e":"<code>agg</code> without <code>groupBy</code> = one row for the whole DataFrame."},{"id":"new-transform-05","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A dashboard needs the number of distinct visitors per day on a 50-billion-row clickstream table. Small inaccuracy is acceptable; cost and speed matter most. Which function?</p>","o":["<code>count(DISTINCT visitor_id)</code>","<code>approx_count_distinct(visitor_id)</code>","<code>count(visitor_id)</code>","<code>collect_set(visitor_id)</code> then <code>size</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Exact, but needs far more memory and shuffle at this scale.","HyperLogLog gives a close estimate (about 5% relative error by default) at a fraction of the cost.","Counts all non-NULL rows, not distinct visitors.","Builds huge arrays in memory."],"e":"Large distinct counts where an estimate is fine: <code>approx_count_distinct</code>."},{"id":"new-transform-06","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>What is the difference between <code>df.describe()</code> and <code>df.summary()</code>?</p>","o":["They are identical","<code>describe()</code> returns count, mean, stddev, min and max; <code>summary()</code> also returns the 25%, 50% and 75% percentiles","<code>describe()</code> shows the schema; <code>summary()</code> shows statistics","<code>summary()</code> only works on numeric columns"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>summary</code> returns more statistics.","","The schema comes from <code>printSchema()</code> or <code>DESCRIBE</code>.","Both handle numeric and string columns (string statistics are limited)."],"e":"Quick profiling: <code>describe()</code> basic statistics, <code>summary()</code> adds quartiles."},{"id":"sp-59","sec":["P3","A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which window specification computes each customer's cumulative spend, ordered by <code>ts</code>, from the first row up to the current row?</p>","o":["<code>Window.partitionBy(\"customer\").orderBy(\"ts\").rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)</code>","<code>Window.orderBy(\"ts\").rowsBetween(Window.unboundedPreceding, Window.currentRow)</code>","<code>Window.partitionBy(\"customer\")</code> without <code>orderBy</code>","<code>Window.partitionBy(\"customer\").orderBy(\"ts\")</code> with no explicit frame"],"ok":[0],"w":["Partitioned per customer, ordered, with an explicit row frame.","Without <code>partitionBy</code>, all customers are mixed.","Without order, the sum is the partition total.","The default frame is RANGE, which adds rows with the same <code>ts</code> together and can differ from a row-by-row running total."],"e":"Running totals: partition, order, and an explicit <code>rowsBetween</code> frame."},{"id":"sp-60","sec":["P3","A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Salespeople must be ranked by total sales within each region. Ties share a position and the numbering must have no gaps. Which function?</p>","o":["<code>dense_rank()</code> over <code>Window.partitionBy(\"region\").orderBy(F.desc(\"total\"))</code>","<code>rank()</code> over the same window","<code>row_number()</code> over the same window","<code>dense_rank()</code> without <code>partitionBy</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Ties get the same rank and the next rank follows without a gap (1, 1, 2).","Leaves gaps after ties (1, 1, 3).","Gives different numbers even to ties.","Ranks all regions together."],"e":"<code>row_number</code> unique, <code>rank</code> with gaps, <code>dense_rank</code> without gaps."},{"id":"sp-65","sec":["P3"],"topic":"transform","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which expression computes, for each sensor, the difference between the current reading and the previous one?</p>","o":["<code>value - F.lag(\"value\").over(Window.partitionBy(\"sensor\").orderBy(\"ts\"))</code>","<code>value - F.lead(\"value\").over(...)</code>","<code>value - F.first(\"value\")</code> without a window","<code>value - F.avg(\"value\").over(Window.partitionBy(\"sensor\"))</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["<code>lag</code> reads the previous row in the ordered partition.","<code>lead</code> reads the next row.","An aggregate without a window has no notion of \"previous\".","Compares with the average."],"e":"<code>lag</code> looks back, <code>lead</code> looks forward."},{"id":"new-transform-07","sec":["P3"],"topic":"transform","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>You need the top 3 products by revenue in each category, including all products tied at third place. Which approach is correct?</p>","o":["<code>orderBy(F.desc(\"revenue\")).limit(3)</code>","<code>row_number()</code> over a window per category ordered by revenue, keep <code>&lt;= 3</code>","<code>dense_rank()</code> (or <code>rank()</code>) over a window partitioned by category ordered by revenue descending, keep rank <code>&lt;= 3</code>","<code>groupBy(\"category\").agg(F.max(\"revenue\"))</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Returns 3 rows overall, not per category.","Cuts ties at third place arbitrarily.","Ties share a rank, so all products tied at third place are kept.","Returns only the top value per category."],"e":"Top-N per group: a ranking window. Choose <code>row_number</code> to cut ties, <code>rank</code>/<code>dense_rank</code> to keep them."},{"id":"new-transform-08","sec":["P3"],"topic":"transform","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A monthly report needs one row per store and one column per month with total sales. Which PySpark call produces it efficiently?</p>","o":["<code>groupBy(\"store\", \"month\").sum(\"sales\")</code>","<code>groupBy(\"store\").pivot(\"month\", [\"Jan\", \"Feb\", \"Mar\"]).sum(\"sales\")</code>","<code>crossJoin</code> of stores and months","A window function partitioned by month"],"ok":[1],"w":["Gives one row per store and month, not one column per month.","Listing the pivot values avoids an extra pass to discover them.","Builds a grid without aggregating.","Keeps one row per input row."],"e":"Rows to columns: <code>pivot</code> (give the values when you know them)."},{"id":"new-transform-09","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A notebook runs <code>df.withColumnRenamed(\"cust_nm\", \"customer_name\")</code>, but the column is actually called <code>cust_name</code>. What happens?</p>","o":["The command fails with a column-not-found error","Nothing changes, silently; the DataFrame keeps <code>cust_name</code>","A new empty column <code>customer_name</code> is added","All columns are renamed"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>withColumnRenamed</code> does not check.","<code>withColumnRenamed</code> and <code>drop</code> ignore columns that do not exist.","No column is added.","Only an existing matching column would be renamed."],"e":"Silent no-ops: <code>withColumnRenamed</code> and <code>drop</code> with a wrong name. Check the schema after renaming."},{"id":"new-transform-10","sec":["A3"],"topic":"transform","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Which PySpark expression splits a comma-separated <code>tags</code> string such as <code>\"red,blue,green\"</code> into one row per tag?</p>","o":["<code>F.split(\"tags\", \",\")</code>","<code>F.explode(F.split(\"tags\", \",\"))</code>","<code>F.flatten(\"tags\")</code>","<code>F.concat_ws(\",\", \"tags\")</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Returns an array in one row.","<code>split</code> builds the array and <code>explode</code> turns each element into a row.","Works on arrays of arrays, not strings.","Joins strings together; the opposite."],"e":"String to rows: <code>split</code> then <code>explode</code>."},{"id":"sp-62","sec":["P1"],"topic":"udf-ai","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Why are pandas UDFs usually faster than plain Python UDFs?</p>","o":["They use Apache Arrow and process vectorised batches, reducing the cost of moving data between the JVM and Python","They remove serialisation entirely","They run inside the JVM, without a Python process","They process one row at a time to save memory"],"ok":[0],"w":["Instead of one call per row, they work on pandas Series.","Serialisation still happens, but in efficient batches.","They still run in Python workers.","That is the model of plain Python UDFs."],"e":"pandas UDF speed comes from Arrow batches and vectorised code."},{"id":"new-udf-01","sec":["P1","A3"],"topic":"udf-ai","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A phone-number normalisation rule written in SQL must be reused by analysts on SQL warehouses and by engineers in pipelines across three workspaces, with access controlled centrally. What should be created?</p>","o":["A Python UDF registered with <code>spark.udf.register</code> in a notebook","A Unity Catalog SQL function (<code>CREATE FUNCTION main.util.normalize_phone(...) RETURN ...</code>) with <code>EXECUTE</code> granted to the groups","A temporary function created at the start of each job","A view that applies the rule to one table"],"ok":[1],"w":["Lives only in that session.","Stored once, governed, usable from SQL and Python everywhere the metastore is attached.","Must be redefined everywhere; no central governance.","Works for one table only."],"e":"Shared, governed logic: Unity Catalog functions; SQL UDFs are also the fastest kind."},{"id":"new-udf-02","sec":["P1"],"topic":"udf-ai","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A pandas UDF scores rows with a model that takes 20 seconds to load. Loading it for every batch makes the job slow. Which pandas UDF form fixes this?</p>","o":["Series to scalar","Iterator of Series to Iterator of Series, loading the model once before looping over the batches","A plain Python UDF","<code>applyInPandas</code> grouped by a constant"],"ok":[1],"w":["An aggregation form.","Expensive setup happens once per task instead of once per batch.","Even slower: one call per row.","Forces all data into one group."],"e":"Expensive initialisation in a UDF: iterator pandas UDF."},{"id":"new-udf-03","sec":["A3"],"topic":"udf-ai","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A notebook uses a Python UDF that returns <code>name.strip().upper()</code>. Which change gives the biggest performance gain?</p>","o":["Convert it to a pandas UDF","Replace it with the built-in functions <code>upper(trim(name))</code>","Cache the DataFrame first","Add more executors"],"ok":[1],"w":["Better than a plain UDF, but still slower than built-ins.","Built-ins run in the engine (and Photon), with no Python serialisation.","The UDF cost remains.","Spends more for the same inefficiency."],"e":"If a built-in function exists, a UDF is the wrong answer."},{"id":"new-udf-04","sec":["P3"],"topic":"udf-ai","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Support tickets must be tagged with one of <code>billing</code>, <code>delivery</code>, <code>product</code> or <code>other</code>, in a SQL pipeline, without training a model. Which function fits?</p>","o":["<code>ai_analyze_sentiment(body)</code>","<code>ai_classify(body, ARRAY('billing', 'delivery', 'product', 'other'))</code>","<code>ai_summarize(body)</code>","<code>regexp_extract(body, 'billing|delivery')</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Returns sentiment, not a category.","It picks one of the given labels for each row.","Produces a summary.","Brittle keyword matching, not classification."],"e":"Fixed label set: <code>ai_classify</code>. Free-form or custom model: <code>ai_query</code>."},{"id":"new-udf-05","sec":["P3"],"topic":"udf-ai","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A batch query calls <code>ai_query</code> on a million rows; a handful of malformed inputs make the whole query fail after an hour. What should you change?</p>","o":["Retry the query until it passes","Call <code>ai_query(..., failOnError =&gt; false)</code> so each row returns a result and an error message, then handle the failed rows","Replace <code>ai_query</code> with a Python UDF calling the endpoint","Run it on a bigger SQL warehouse"],"ok":[1],"w":["The same rows fail again.","One bad row no longer fails the batch.","More code and no better error handling.","Size does not fix malformed input."],"e":"Batch inference: <code>failOnError =&gt; false</code>, then inspect the errors."},{"id":"new-udf-06","sec":["P3"],"topic":"udf-ai","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Product reviews are enriched with <code>ai_analyze_sentiment</code> in a materialized view, and costs grow every day because existing reviews are scored again. Which design avoids paying for old rows again?</p>","o":["Score reviews in a streaming table that processes each new review once","Refresh the materialized view more often","Replace the function with <code>ai_query</code>","Use a regular view"],"ok":[0],"w":["Incremental processing calls the model only for new rows.","Increases the cost.","Same cost profile if old rows are recomputed.","Calls the model on every query."],"e":"Run AI functions incrementally; never re-score unchanged rows."},{"id":"sa-26","sec":["A3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A team is considering moving hand-written Spark pipelines to Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines and wants to know what they gain. Which description matches what the product offers?</p>","o":["Declarative data quality checks, schema evolution support, declarative development and one model for batch and streaming","Manual schema enforcement, high operational overhead and limited scalability","Custom code for every quality check and no streaming support","Batch only, no versioning and fixed infrastructure costs"],"ok":[0],"w":["Expectations for quality, schema handling, dependencies and orchestration managed for you, and the same definitions for batch and streaming.","This describes the hand-written situation the product removes.","Expectations are declarative and streaming is built in.","Pipelines stream, write versioned Delta tables and use autoscaling or serverless compute."],"e":"If a description of declarative pipelines sounds like heavy operational work, it describes the world before them."},{"id":"site-066","sec":["A3","P1"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A source is append-only and every row must be processed exactly once, incrementally. Which dataset type fits best?</p>","o":["Materialized view","Streaming table","View","External table"],"ok":[1],"w":["Keeps a query result current; it may recompute instead of processing each row once.","Each input row is processed once, incrementally.","Not stored; recomputed when used.","A storage choice, not an incremental processing model."],"e":"Append-only and incremental: streaming table."},{"id":"site-065","sec":["A3","P1"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A gold aggregate is expensive to compute, must be refreshed when its sources change, and is queried constantly by BI. Which object?</p>","o":["View","Materialized view","Temporary view","Streaming table"],"ok":[1],"w":["Recomputes the expensive query on every read.","The result is stored and refreshed (on a schedule, on source updates, or incrementally).","Lives only in a session or pipeline.","For append-only incremental processing, not a recomputed aggregate."],"e":"Precomputed, refreshed results for BI: materialized view."},{"id":"sp-20","sec":["P1","A3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A pipeline receives delivery tracking events. It must store the raw events incrementally and expose the latest status of each delivery, recomputed automatically. Which combination?</p>","o":["A streaming table for the raw events and a materialized view for the latest status per delivery","A materialized view for the raw events and a streaming table for the latest status","Two streaming tables","Two materialized views"],"ok":[0],"w":["Streaming tables suit append-only ingestion; the materialized view recomputes the current state per key.","Roles reversed: ingestion would be recomputed, and \"latest status\" needs replacing values.","Streaming tables append; keeping only the latest value per key needs something else.","Recomputing raw ingestion at every refresh is unnecessary and expensive."],"e":"Events: streaming table. Current state per key: materialized view (or <code>AUTO CDC</code>)."},{"id":"sa-25","sec":["A3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>In a code review, an intermediate cleaning step between bronze and silver, used only inside the pipeline, is declared with <code>@dlt.table</code>. It takes storage for nothing. What is the right fix?</p>","o":["Declare it as a view (<code>@dlt.view</code>, or <code>@dp.temporary_view</code>), since it does not need to be persisted","Keep <code>@dlt.table</code> and run <code>VACUUM</code> often","Keep <code>@dlt.table</code> and add a comment saying it is temporary","Replace it with a CTE in the final query"],"ok":[0],"w":["A view chains transformations inside the pipeline without materialising data.","The table is still materialised; this treats the symptom.","A comment changes nothing.","Can work in simple cases, but loses reuse and visibility of the step in the pipeline graph."],"e":"Persist only what someone reads outside the pipeline."},{"id":"sa-27","sec":["A3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>This is the first step of a pipeline that reads JSON order files and must persist the result as a Delta table. Which two problems does the code have?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import dlt\n\n@dlt.view\ndef bronze_orders():\n    return spark.read.parquet(&quot;abfss://landing@account.dfs.core.windows.net/orders/&quot;)\n</code></pre>","o":["It uses <code>@dlt.view</code> instead of <code>@dlt.table</code>, and reads Parquet although the source is JSON","It does not import <code>pyspark.sql.functions</code> or define the schema explicitly","The path should be relative and the function should take a parameter","A mandatory expectation is missing"],"ok":[0],"w":["Persisting needs a table, and the reader must match the source format.","Neither is required here.","Absolute storage paths are normal, and dataset functions take no parameters.","Expectations are recommended, not mandatory."],"e":"In pipeline code check two things: does the decorator persist what must be persisted, and does the reader match the source?"},{"id":"site-064","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>What does an expectation do by default when it has NO <code>ON VIOLATION</code> clause?</p>","o":["Drops the row","Fails the update","Keeps the row and records the violation (warn)","Quarantines the row"],"ok":[2],"w":["That needs <code>ON VIOLATION DROP ROW</code>.","That needs <code>ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE</code>.","The metric is recorded and the row is written.","There is no automatic quarantine."],"e":"Default expectation = warn: keep the row, count the violation."},{"id":"site-020","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>What happens to a row with <code>amount = -5</code>?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-sql\">CONSTRAINT valid_amount EXPECT (amount &gt; 0) ON VIOLATION DROP ROW\n</code></pre>","o":["The pipeline update fails","The row is written and the violation is only logged","The row is dropped and the violation is recorded in the metrics","The row is sent to a quarantine table automatically"],"ok":[2],"w":["That is <code>FAIL UPDATE</code>.","That is the default without <code>ON VIOLATION</code>.","","Quarantine tables must be built yourself."],"e":"<code>DROP ROW</code>: invalid rows are removed and counted; the pipeline continues."},{"id":"sa-23","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Farm sensors send soil humidity readings. Values outside the physically possible range must be discarded, but the flow must not fail because of them. Which mechanism implements this rule?</p>","o":["An expectation with <code>expect_or_drop</code>","An expectation with <code>expect_or_fail</code>","An expectation with <code>expect</code> only","Change data capture"],"ok":[0],"w":["Violating rows are removed and the pipeline keeps running.","Stops the pipeline at the first violation, which is forbidden here.","Records the violation and keeps the row.","Propagates inserts, updates and deletes; it does not validate data."],"e":"Three expectation actions, three decisions: monitor (<code>expect</code>), discard (<code>expect_or_drop</code>), stop (<code>expect_or_fail</code>)."},{"id":"sa-24","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>In another pipeline, records without a parcel identifier mean a serious upstream error. The team wants the run to stop immediately in that case so nobody consumes corrupted data. Which decorator?</p>","o":["<code>expect_or_fail</code>","<code>expect_or_drop</code>","<code>expect</code>","A <code>WHERE</code> filter in the source query"],"ok":[0],"w":["The update fails when the condition is violated, and its writes are rolled back.","Drops the records and continues, hiding the upstream problem.","Only records the violation.","Removes rows with no trace in the quality metrics and no alert."],"e":"<code>expect_or_fail</code> when bad data is a symptom of a broken upstream, not expected noise."},{"id":"site-111","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Auditors need every rejected record and the reason it was rejected. Which approach fits?</p>","o":["<code>ON VIOLATION DROP ROW</code>","Quarantine: write valid rows to silver and invalid rows, with a reason, to a quarantine table","<code>ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE</code>","A warn expectation"],"ok":[1],"w":["Keeps only a count, not the rows.","Make sure each row (including rows with NULLs) lands in exactly one of the two tables.","Stops the pipeline and keeps nothing.","Keeps bad rows in the main table."],"e":"Rejected rows that must be kept: a quarantine table."},{"id":"new-pipelines-01","sec":["P3","A3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A pipeline splits bronze rows with <code>WHERE amount &gt; 0</code> into silver and <code>WHERE NOT (amount &gt; 0)</code> into a quarantine table. Auditors find that rows with a NULL amount appear in neither table. What fixes it?</p>","o":["Add <code>ON VIOLATION DROP ROW</code> to the silver table","Use a NULL-safe condition for the quarantine, such as <code>WHERE NOT coalesce(amount &gt; 0, false)</code> (or compute an <code>is_valid</code> flag once and split on it)","Use <code>UNION</code> instead of two tables","Run <code>OPTIMIZE</code> on both tables"],"ok":[1],"w":["Still loses the rows.","<code>NOT (NULL &gt; 0)</code> is NULL, which a <code>WHERE</code> treats as false.","Mixes valid and invalid rows.","Layout has nothing to do with it."],"e":"NULL makes a condition and its negation both not true. Quarantine logic must handle NULLs explicitly."},{"id":"sp-19","sec":["P1"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A small team needs incremental ingestion, declared quality rules, lineage and low operational effort. Which approach fits best?</p>","o":["Lakeflow Spark Declarative Pipelines with streaming tables, materialized views and expectations","Hand-written Structured Streaming with custom checkpoints and merges","A scheduled notebook with validation UDFs","Shell scripts calling <code>spark-submit</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["The platform manages checkpoints, orchestration, incremental processing and quality metrics.","Works, but needs far more code and maintenance.","No native incremental processing or built-in quality metrics.","Little governance and no pipeline abstraction."],"e":"Standard ETL with low operational effort: declarative pipelines."},{"id":"new-pipelines-03","sec":["P1"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Which requirement is the strongest reason to write Structured Streaming code in a job instead of a declarative pipeline?</p>","o":["The data must be ingested incrementally from cloud storage","Rows that fail validation must be dropped and counted","Each micro-batch must call custom logic that writes to two external systems and a Delta table, using <code>foreachBatch</code>","Lineage must appear in Unity Catalog"],"ok":[2],"w":["Pipelines do this with streaming tables.","Expectations do this.","Arbitrary per-batch side effects are where hand-written streaming gives control that declarative datasets do not.","Pipelines record lineage automatically."],"e":"Declarative pipelines for standard ETL; Structured Streaming when you need custom per-batch or stateful logic."},{"id":"new-pipelines-04","sec":["P1","A3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A streaming table enriches each order with the customer's segment through a join with <code>dim_customers</code>. Marketing re-segments customers, and old orders still show the old segment. Why, and what would show the current segment?</p>","o":["The pipeline is broken; restart it","A streaming table processes each row once, using the dimension as it was at that moment; a materialized view joining orders and customers would reflect current segments","Enable Change Data Feed on the streaming table","Use <code>ON VIOLATION DROP ROW</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["It works as designed.","Stream-static joins do not revisit old rows.","CDF exposes changes; it does not recompute old rows.","Unrelated to joins."],"e":"Streaming tables never revisit processed rows; recomputation is the job of materialized views."},{"id":"new-pipelines-05","sec":["P1"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Engineers changed the <code>SELECT</code> logic of a streaming table to fix a currency conversion. New rows are correct, but rows processed before the change still use the old logic. What reprocesses everything with the new logic?</p>","o":["Nothing; streaming tables recompute old rows on the next update","A full refresh of that streaming table, provided the source still holds all the data","<code>OPTIMIZE</code> on the streaming table","Changing the pipeline to continuous mode"],"ok":[1],"w":["They do not.","It truncates the table and reprocesses the source from the start.","Compacts files only.","Changes latency, not past rows."],"e":"Query changes on streaming tables apply to new rows only; a full refresh rebuilds, if the source can be replayed."},{"id":"new-pipelines-07","sec":["A3","A4"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A team deletes an old declarative pipeline they no longer edit, expecting its tables to remain for reporting. What happens?</p>","o":["The tables remain and become regular managed tables","The streaming tables and materialized views defined by the pipeline are deleted with it","The tables remain but are read-only","Only the event log is deleted"],"ok":[1],"w":["Deleting the pipeline removes the datasets it defines.","Back them up or move the logic before deleting.","They are removed.","The datasets go too."],"e":"Deleting a pipeline deletes its tables. Same for Lakeflow Connect ingestion pipelines."},{"id":"new-pipelines-08","sec":["P1","P2"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A streaming table <code>all_orders</code> is fed by a web orders stream. A new source (mobile orders, another Kafka topic) must be added without restarting the existing flow or doing a full refresh. What do you use?</p>","o":["Rewrite the query as a <code>UNION</code> of both streams","An additional append flow targeting the same streaming table","A materialized view over both topics","A second pipeline that writes to the same table"],"ok":[1],"w":["Changing the query changes the flow and its checkpoint; it may require a full refresh.","Each source has its own flow and checkpoint into one target.","Recomputes instead of appending incrementally.","A table is managed by one pipeline."],"e":"Several sources into one streaming table: append flows."},{"id":"new-pipelines-09","sec":["A3","A4"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Data for a pipeline arrives once a night, and the results are needed at 7:00. The pipeline should not run all day. How should it run?</p>","o":["Continuous mode","Triggered mode, started by a scheduled job (or a file arrival trigger) after the data lands","Development mode","A materialized view with no refresh"],"ok":[1],"w":["Keeps compute running all day for nothing.","A triggered update processes what is available, then stops.","Meant for iteration; it does not retry failures.","Would never pick up new data."],"e":"Triggered pipelines for periodic data; continuous mode only when low latency justifies always-on compute."},{"id":"site-109","sec":["A3","P1"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A materialized view's scheduled refreshes start failing after its owner moved teams and lost access to the source schema. Analysts can still query it. What is the fix?</p>","o":["Grant the analysts <code>SELECT</code> on the source tables","Make an identity that keeps <code>SELECT</code> on the sources, such as a service principal, the owner of the materialized view","Convert the materialized view to a view","Run <code>REFRESH</code> as an analyst"],"ok":[1],"w":["Refreshes do not run as the analysts.","Refreshes run with the owner's privileges.","Loses the precomputation dashboards rely on.","The refresh still runs as the owner."],"e":"Refreshes run as the owner; old results stay readable but go stale."},{"id":"site-110","sec":["A3","P3"],"topic":"pipelines","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A gold table is written by a nightly job, a backfill notebook and manual SQL fixes. It must never contain a negative amount. What enforces this for every writer?</p>","o":["An expectation with <code>ON VIOLATION FAIL UPDATE</code>","A Delta <code>CHECK</code> constraint on the table","A <code>WHERE</code> filter in the nightly job","A row filter"],"ok":[1],"w":["Applies only inside the pipeline that defines it.","Any transaction that violates it fails, whoever writes.","The other writers ignore it.","Controls what users see, not what can be written."],"e":"Table-level rules for all writers: Delta constraints. Pipeline-level rules: expectations."},{"id":"site-011","sec":["A2","P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Which trigger makes a Structured Streaming or Auto Loader query process all data currently available and then stop?</p>","o":["<code>trigger(processingTime=\"0 seconds\")</code>","<code>trigger(continuous=\"1 second\")</code>","<code>trigger(availableNow=True)</code>","No trigger (the default)"],"ok":[2],"w":["Runs micro-batches continuously, forever.","Experimental continuous processing that never stops.","It processes everything available, in rate-limited micro-batches, then stops.","Same as <code>processingTime</code> 0: runs until stopped."],"e":"Scheduled incremental processing: <code>availableNow</code>. <code>once</code> is deprecated."},{"id":"site-092","sec":["A1","P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A Structured Streaming notebook that worked on classic compute fails on serverless with <code>INFINITE_STREAMING_TRIGGER_NOT_SUPPORTED</code>. The code sets no trigger. What is the fix?</p>","o":["<code>trigger(processingTime=\"1 minute\")</code>","<code>trigger(availableNow=True)</code>","Add more workers","Enable Photon"],"ok":[1],"w":["Still an endless stream, which serverless rejects.","Serverless runs streams that stop: <code>availableNow</code> (or the deprecated <code>once</code>).","Serverless has no workers to configure, and the error is about the trigger.","Photon is always on in serverless and unrelated."],"e":"Endless triggers need classic compute, a continuous job, or a continuous pipeline; serverless jobs run <code>availableNow</code> streams."},{"id":"sp-28","sec":["P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>In hand-written Structured Streaming, valid records need low latency and invalid ones are analysed rarely, at minimum cost. Which design fits?</p>","o":["Two independent streams on the same bronze table (one keeps <code>value &gt;= 0</code>, the other <code>value &lt; 0</code>), running on the same compute","One <code>foreachBatch</code> writing both outputs with try/except","One table with a boolean <code>quarantine</code> column","A dedicated cluster plus a daily job for the invalid records"],"ok":[0],"w":["The latency of valid records is isolated from the quarantine path, and the compute is shared.","Couples the two paths: a delay in the quarantine write delays the valid records.","Every consumer must filter, and the physical separation is lost.","Adds cost for no reason."],"e":"Separate streams have separate checkpoints and separate latency."},{"id":"sp-10","sec":["P1","P2"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Messages from a broker can be duplicated with up to 10 days between copies, and each <code>message_id</code> must exist only once in the Delta table. Which approach fits best?</p>","o":["<code>MERGE INTO</code> against the whole target on <code>message_id</code> with only <code>WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT</code>, inside <code>foreachBatch</code>","<code>dropDuplicates</code> with a 1-hour watermark","Enable Change Data Feed","Use time travel to remove duplicates"],"ok":[0],"w":["It checks the entire table, so the time between duplicates does not matter.","State is dropped after the watermark; duplicates 10 days apart get through.","Records changes; it does not remove duplicates.","Reads old versions; it does not deduplicate ingestion."],"e":"Long gaps between duplicates: insert-only <code>MERGE</code>. Short gaps: watermark-based deduplication."},{"id":"sp-25","sec":["P1","A2"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>What does the option <code>skipChangeCommits</code> do when reading a Delta table as a stream?</p>","o":["It ignores commits that update or delete existing rows (<code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, <code>MERGE</code>) and processes only appends","It propagates updates as new records","It enables Change Data Feed","It reprocesses the whole table on each micro-batch"],"ok":[0],"w":["Useful when you only want inserted data and accept losing those changes.","That resembles the legacy <code>ignoreChanges</code>.","CDF is a table property enabled separately.","The stream stays incremental."],"e":"A Delta stream source expects appends. <code>skipChangeCommits</code> ignores changes; CDF processes them."},{"id":"new-streaming-01","sec":["P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A streaming aggregation keeps a running total per customer and must upsert the changed totals into a Delta table every micro-batch. The query fails when written with <code>outputMode(\"update\")</code> to the Delta sink. What should you do?</p>","o":["Switch to <code>append</code> mode without a watermark","Use <code>foreachBatch</code> and <code>MERGE</code> each batch's changed rows into the target","Use <code>complete</code> mode to a memory sink","Disable the checkpoint"],"ok":[1],"w":["Aggregations without a watermark cannot use append mode.","The Delta sink supports append and complete; upserts go through <code>foreachBatch</code>.","Not durable, and memory sinks are for testing.","Removes exactly-once guarantees and solves nothing."],"e":"Streaming upserts into Delta: <code>foreachBatch</code> + <code>MERGE</code>."},{"id":"new-streaming-02","sec":["P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A windowed count uses <code>withWatermark(\"event_ts\", \"15 minutes\")</code>. What does the watermark achieve?</p>","o":["It delays every event by 15 minutes before processing","It tells Spark how late events may arrive, so state for older windows can be finalised and dropped; events later than that are ignored","It makes the stream run every 15 minutes","It deletes table data older than 15 minutes"],"ok":[1],"w":["It does not delay processing.","It bounds state size and allows append mode for aggregations.","That is a trigger.","Watermarks affect state, not table data."],"e":"Watermark = lateness threshold → bounded state, finalised windows."},{"id":"new-streaming-03","sec":["P1","P8"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A <code>foreachBatch</code> function appends each micro-batch to two Delta tables. After a failure and restart, one table contains duplicates of a batch. How do you make the appends safe to retry?</p>","o":["Add <code>.option(\"mergeSchema\", \"true\")</code> to both writes","Write with the options <code>txnAppId</code> (a stable ID for the query) and <code>txnVersion</code> (the <code>batch_id</code>), so Delta skips a batch that was already written","Delete the checkpoint after each batch","Use <code>trigger(once=True)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Controls schema evolution, not duplicates.","These options make each table write idempotent per batch.","Causes full reprocessing.","Deprecated, and it does not change retry behaviour."],"e":"<code>foreachBatch</code> is at-least-once; make its writes idempotent (<code>MERGE</code>, or <code>txnAppId</code> + <code>txnVersion</code>)."},{"id":"new-streaming-04","sec":["P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A streaming job writing to a Delta table crashes in the middle of a micro-batch and is restarted with the same code and checkpoint. What happens?</p>","o":["It starts again from the earliest offset and duplicates data","It replays the unfinished micro-batch from the offsets in the checkpoint; the Delta sink commits it once, so there is no loss and no duplication","It skips the failed batch to avoid duplicates","It cannot restart; a new checkpoint is required"],"ok":[1],"w":["The checkpoint records committed progress.","Replayable source + checkpoint + idempotent sink = exactly once.","That would lose data.","Restarting with the same checkpoint is the normal recovery path."],"e":"Exactly-once recovery rests on the checkpoint."},{"id":"new-streaming-05","sec":["P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Click events are sometimes sent twice within a few minutes. The stream must drop duplicates by <code>event_id</code> while keeping state small. Which code?</p>","o":["<code>.dropDuplicates([\"event_id\"])</code> without a watermark","<code>.withWatermark(\"event_ts\", \"30 minutes\").dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark([\"event_id\"])</code>","<code>.distinct()</code>","A <code>MERGE</code> against the target in <code>foreachBatch</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Keeps every <code>event_id</code> in state forever.","State is kept only for the watermark period.","Compares whole rows and keeps unbounded state.","Works, but scans the target every batch; it is the tool for duplicates days apart."],"e":"Duplicates close in time: <code>dropDuplicatesWithinWatermark</code>."},{"id":"new-streaming-06","sec":["P1"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Ad impressions and clicks are two streams. A click counts only if it happens within one hour after its impression. What does the stream-stream join need so that state does not grow forever?</p>","o":["Watermarks on both streams and a time condition such as <code>click_ts BETWEEN imp_ts AND imp_ts + INTERVAL 1 HOUR</code>","A broadcast hint on the smaller stream","<code>complete</code> output mode","A larger cluster"],"ok":[0],"w":["Together they tell Spark when buffered rows can no longer match and may be dropped.","Streams cannot be broadcast like static tables.","Not supported for this kind of join and does not bound state.","State still grows without limit."],"e":"Stream-stream joins: watermarks on both sides plus a time-range condition."},{"id":"new-streaming-07","sec":["P4","P8"],"topic":"streaming","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p><code>query.lastProgress</code> for a continuous stream shows <code>inputRowsPerSecond</code> around 50,000 and <code>processedRowsPerSecond</code> around 20,000, and batch durations keep growing. What does it mean?</p>","o":["The stream cannot keep up: a backlog is building, so you must add capacity or make each batch cheaper","The stream is healthy; processed rows are always lower","The source is losing data","The checkpoint is corrupted"],"ok":[0],"w":["Processing slower than the input rate means growing latency.","A persistent gap means the backlog grows.","Input rows are still being read; they are simply waiting.","That would fail the query."],"e":"Watch processed versus input rate: the gap is your backlog."},{"id":"sa-28","sec":["A3","P2"],"topic":"cdc","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A customer table in a transactional system receives inserts, updates and deletes. The team wants a lakehouse table kept in sync with that state. Which capability addresses this?</p>","o":["Change data capture, applying the changes to the target table","Data quality expectations on the target table","Rewrite the target table completely every night","Pipeline monitoring with alerts"],"ok":[0],"w":["CDC propagates inserts, updates and deletes without reprocessing everything.","Validate rows; do not synchronise state.","Works for small tables but is slow, expensive and loses change history.","Observes health; changes no data."],"e":"CDC synchronises, expectations validate, monitoring observes."},{"id":"site-019","sec":["A3","P1"],"topic":"cdc","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A customer dimension must keep the full history of address changes with validity periods. Which approach fits in a declarative pipeline?</p>","o":["<code>AUTO CDC ... STORED AS SCD TYPE 1</code>","<code>AUTO CDC ... STORED AS SCD TYPE 2</code>","A materialized view with <code>INSERT OVERWRITE</code>","A view"],"ok":[1],"w":["Overwrites the old address.","Each version gets <code>__START_AT</code> and <code>__END_AT</code> columns.","Not valid for a materialized view, and keeps no history.","Stores nothing."],"e":"History with validity periods = SCD type 2. <code>AUTO CDC</code> defaults to type 1."},{"id":"site-062","sec":["A3","P1"],"topic":"cdc","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A pipeline whose streaming table is built with <code>AUTO CDC ... STORED AS SCD TYPE 2</code> is fully refreshed. What happens to the history?</p>","o":["It is preserved","It is lost; the table is rebuilt from the source data still available","It is moved to an archive table","Only the last version is lost"],"ok":[1],"w":["A full refresh truncates the table.","History built from events the source no longer holds cannot be recreated.","Nothing is archived automatically.","The whole table is rebuilt."],"e":"Protect SCD type 2 targets from full refreshes, or keep the full change history in bronze."},{"id":"sp-23","sec":["P1","P2"],"topic":"cdc","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>CDC events arrive out of order: a late DELETE can arrive after a newer UPDATE of the same key. How do the AUTO CDC APIs guarantee the correct result?</p>","o":["<code>SEQUENCE BY</code> defines the logical order, and delete tombstones prevent late events from resurrecting deleted rows","They apply events in arrival order","They run <code>VACUUM</code> between events","They discard every DELETE that arrives after an UPDATE"],"ok":[0],"w":["Events are applied in logical order, not arrival order.","That would give wrong results with late events.","File maintenance has nothing to do with ordering.","Could lose legitimate deletes; the sequence value decides."],"e":"Out-of-order CDC: <code>SEQUENCE BY</code> on the event's logical time."},{"id":"sp-12","sec":["P5","P1"],"topic":"cdc","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>An SCD type 1 table receives constant updates and deletes. A downstream streaming job must react only to updates and deletes. How should it read the table?</p>","o":["<code>readStream</code> with <code>readChangeFeed = true</code>, filtering <code>_change_type</code> on <code>update_postimage</code> and <code>delete</code>","<code>readStream</code> with <code>ignoreChanges</code>","A batch <code>spark.read</code> every minute","<code>VERSION AS OF</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["The Change Data Feed labels every row change with its type.","Re-emits rewritten files as new data without saying what changed.","Rereads the whole table and is not incremental.","Pins one version; it does not follow changes."],"e":"React to updates and deletes: read the Change Data Feed."},{"id":"sp-24","sec":["P1","P2"],"topic":"cdc","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A second pipeline must incrementally process a streaming table that receives updates (SCD type 1). How should it read the source?</p>","o":["Read its Change Data Feed as a stream and apply the changes with <code>AUTO CDC</code>","<code>readStream</code> with <code>skipChangeCommits</code>","A static <code>spark.read</code>","<code>readStream</code> with no options"],"ok":[0],"w":["CDF provides inserts, updates and deletes in a form <code>AUTO CDC</code> can apply.","Ignores update and delete commits, losing exactly the changes needed.","Not incremental.","Fails when it meets commits that change existing rows."],"e":"A table with updates is not an append-only source: use its change feed."},{"id":"new-cdc-01","sec":["P1"],"topic":"cdc","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A customer dimension uses <code>AUTO CDC ... STORED AS SCD TYPE 2</code>. Changes to <code>last_login_ts</code> happen many times a day and should not create new versions, while address changes must. What do you add?</p>","o":["<code>APPLY AS TRUNCATE WHEN</code>","<code>TRACK HISTORY ON * EXCEPT (last_login_ts)</code>","<code>SEQUENCE BY last_login_ts</code>","<code>COLUMNS * EXCEPT (last_login_ts)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Empties the table; only for SCD type 1.","Only the tracked columns create new versions; other changes update the current row.","Changes the ordering of events, which must stay the change timestamp.","Removes the column from the target entirely."],"e":"SCD type 2 in <code>AUTO CDC</code>: <code>TRACK HISTORY ON</code> picks which columns create versions."},{"id":"new-cdc-02","sec":["P1","P2"],"topic":"cdc","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A legacy system cannot provide a change feed; it only drops a complete extract of the <code>products</code> table every night. You need SCD type 2 history in a declarative pipeline. What should you use?</p>","o":["<code>AUTO CDC</code> from the raw extracts","<code>AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT</code> (Python <code>create_auto_cdc_from_snapshot_flow</code>) with SCD type 2","A materialized view over the latest extract","<code>INSERT OVERWRITE</code> of the extract into a Delta table"],"ok":[1],"w":["Expects change events, not full snapshots.","It compares successive snapshots and derives inserts, updates and deletes.","Shows only the current snapshot, no history.","Replaces the data and keeps no history."],"e":"Full snapshots instead of a feed: <code>AUTO CDC FROM SNAPSHOT</code>."},{"id":"new-cdc-03","sec":["P5"],"topic":"cdc","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Change Data Feed was enabled on <code>silver.orders</code> today at version 120. A team asks for the row-level changes made last week (versions 80 to 110). What can you give them?</p>","o":["<code>table_changes('silver.orders', 80, 110)</code>","Not those changes through CDF; it is not retroactive. Old snapshots are available through time travel while their files are retained","Enable CDF again with <code>startingVersion = 80</code>","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> gives the changed rows"],"ok":[1],"w":["CDF records changes only from the version where it was enabled.","","There is no backfill of change data.","It gives operations and metrics, not rows."],"e":"CDF starts when you enable it. Turn it on early for tables others will consume incrementally."},{"id":"new-cdc-04","sec":["P2","A3"],"topic":"cdc","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A daily <code>MERGE</code> applies a CDC batch to <code>silver.customers</code>. Occasionally an old event, replayed by the source, overwrites a newer value. Which change prevents this?</p>","o":["<code>WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET *</code> without conditions","Keep the latest event per key in the batch, and update only when <code>s.change_ts &gt; t.change_ts</code>","Run <code>OPTIMIZE</code> before the merge","Use <code>INSERT OVERWRITE</code> instead"],"ok":[1],"w":["That is the current behaviour.","Comparing sequence values stops stale events from overwriting newer rows.","Layout does not change merge logic.","Loses all rows not in the batch."],"e":"Hand-written CDC must deduplicate per key and compare sequence values."},{"id":"sa-04","sec":["A3","A2"],"topic":"modeling","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Smart electricity meters publish readings to Event Hubs, which keeps data for seven days. The regulator requires five years of the events exactly as emitted, and the storage budget is tight. In which medallion layer should these events land?</p>","o":["Bronze","Silver","Gold","Increase Event Hubs retention to five years"],"ok":[0],"w":["Bronze keeps raw data as received; Delta on object storage is cheap and durable, and keeps history available for replay and audit.","Silver holds cleaned and typed data derived from bronze; keeping only that loses the original the regulator requires.","Business aggregates, the furthest from the raw events.","The most expensive option per GB, and it keeps history in a messaging system not built for archives or replay."],"e":"Short source retention plus long history requirement: land raw events in bronze."},{"id":"sp-78","sec":["A3"],"topic":"modeling","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>In the medallion architecture, which layer holds clean, deduplicated and conformed data, before business aggregations?</p>","o":["Silver","Bronze","Gold","Landing"],"ok":[0],"w":["Validated, conformed, entity-level data.","Raw data as received.","Aggregates and models ready for consumption.","The file drop zone before bronze."],"e":"Bronze raw, silver clean, gold business-ready."},{"id":"site-022","sec":["A3","P9"],"topic":"modeling","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>In a star schema, which table typically contains quantities and amounts plus foreign keys?</p>","o":["Dimension table","Fact table","Bridge view","Bronze table"],"ok":[1],"w":["Holds descriptive attributes.","Events and measures, with foreign keys to the dimensions.","Resolves many-to-many relationships; not where measures live.","A medallion layer, not a dimensional role."],"e":"Facts = measures + foreign keys; dimensions = who, what, where, when."},{"id":"sp-75","sec":["P9"],"topic":"modeling","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>How should a date dimension be designed for analysis by fiscal quarter, weekday and public holidays?</p>","o":["Precompute and store those attributes as columns of the dimension","Store only the date and compute everything in each query","Store the date as an ISO string","Create one table per calendar"],"ok":[0],"w":["Queries become simple joins and groupings, and business rules are computed once.","Repeats complex logic and cost.","Loses native date functions and comparisons.","Fragments the model; one dimension can carry columns for each calendar."],"e":"Dimensions carry precomputed descriptive attributes."},{"id":"new-modeling-01","sec":["P9"],"topic":"modeling","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Finance, sales and Genie users each compute \"revenue per order\" differently. The company wants one governed definition that is correct whether results are grouped by day, region or product. What should be built?</p>","o":["A materialized view with revenue per order pre-aggregated by month","A Unity Catalog metric view defining <code>revenue</code> and <code>revenue_per_order</code> as measures, queried with <code>MEASURE()</code>","A shared notebook with the SQL formula","A column comment explaining the formula"],"ok":[1],"w":["Fixed grain; a monthly ratio cannot be re-aggregated correctly by day or product.","Measures are computed at query time at any grain, from one governed definition.","Not governed, and every tool would copy it.","Documentation does not enforce anything."],"e":"Governed metric definitions at any grain: metric views. Precomputed speed at a fixed grain: materialized views."},{"id":"new-modeling-02","sec":["P9","A3"],"topic":"modeling","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A BI dashboard queries a large join plus aggregation every few seconds, always at the same grain (day and store), and the data changes hourly. Which object gives the fastest dashboard at reasonable cost?</p>","o":["A standard view","A materialized view at day and store grain, refreshed when the sources change","A metric view with no materialisation","A temporary view in each dashboard query"],"ok":[1],"w":["Recomputes the expensive query on every request.","Results are precomputed and reused until the next refresh.","Consistent definitions, but each query still computes from the base tables.","Same cost as a view."],"e":"Fixed grain and frequent reads: materialized view (a metric view can sit on top of it)."},{"id":"new-modeling-03","sec":["A3","A7"],"topic":"modeling","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Analysts must read <code>gold.sales_summary</code>, a view over <code>silver.orders</code> and <code>silver.customers</code>. They must not query the silver tables directly. Which grants are enough?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT</code> on the view and on both silver tables","<code>USE CATALOG</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code> on <code>gold</code>, and <code>SELECT</code> on the view; the view's owner has <code>SELECT</code> on the silver tables","<code>MODIFY</code> on the view","<code>BROWSE</code> on the silver schema"],"ok":[1],"w":["Lets them query silver directly, which is not allowed.","Unity Catalog resolves the view's sources with the owner's privileges.","Write privilege; reading needs <code>SELECT</code>.","Metadata only, and unrelated to querying the view."],"e":"Views are a security layer: users need <code>SELECT</code> on the view, not on the tables behind it."},{"id":"new-modeling-04","sec":["P9"],"topic":"modeling","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A customer dimension is stored as SCD type 2, so <code>customer_id</code> appears once per version. How should the fact table reference the customer so each sale points to the version valid at the time of the sale?</p>","o":["By <code>customer_id</code>, joining on the current version","By a surrogate key of the dimension version (for example an identity column), assigned when the fact row is loaded","By the customer's name","By storing all customer attributes in the fact"],"ok":[1],"w":["Every old sale would show today's attributes.","Each version has its own key, so the fact keeps the version valid at that time.","Not unique and changes over time.","Duplicates data and breaks the dimensional model."],"e":"SCD type 2 dimensions need surrogate keys; facts store the key of the version that was current."},{"id":"new-modeling-05","sec":["P9"],"topic":"modeling","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A team starts designing a sales fact table for a new gold layer. What should they decide first?</p>","o":["The clustering keys","The grain: what one row represents (for example, one order line)","The dashboard colours","The number of shuffle partitions"],"ok":[1],"w":["Layout follows from the model and the queries.","Measures, keys and aggregations all depend on it.","Presentation, not modelling.","An execution setting."],"e":"Dimensional design starts with the grain of each fact table."},{"id":"new-modeling-06","sec":["P9"],"topic":"modeling","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>BI queries on a gold model join the fact table to <code>dim_product</code>, which is split into <code>product</code>, <code>subcategory</code> and <code>category</code> tables. Queries are slow and hard to write. Which modelling change helps most?</p>","o":["Denormalise the product hierarchy into a single <code>dim_product</code> (star schema)","Normalise the dimension further","Move all dimension attributes into the fact table","Replace the dimensions with views over bronze"],"ok":[0],"w":["One join per dimension is simpler and faster for BI.","More joins, slower queries.","Duplicates attributes on every row and breaks SCD handling.","Pushes cleaning work into every query."],"e":"Star schemas (denormalised dimensions) suit BI; snowflake schemas trade query simplicity for normalisation."},{"id":"new-modeling-07","sec":["P9","A3"],"topic":"modeling","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A dimension table needs a surrogate key that Delta generates automatically and uniquely for every new row. How is it declared?</p>","o":["<code>customer_sk BIGINT DEFAULT rand()</code>","<code>customer_sk BIGINT GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY</code>","<code>customer_sk STRING PRIMARY KEY</code>","<code>monotonically_increasing_id()</code> in every load"],"ok":[1],"w":["Random values are not guaranteed unique.","Identity columns produce unique values on insert.","Primary keys are informational in Unity Catalog; they do not generate values.","Values are unique per run but restart and are not stable across loads."],"e":"Surrogate keys: identity columns."},{"id":"site-023","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job has a cleanup task that must run after the other tasks, whether they succeed or fail. Which Run if condition?</p>","o":["<code>ALL_SUCCESS</code>","<code>ALL_DONE</code>","<code>AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED</code>","<code>NONE_FAILED</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["The default; cleanup would be skipped after a failure.","The task runs once all dependencies finish, whatever their result.","Runs only when something failed.","Skipped when a dependency failed."],"e":"Cleanup that must always run: <code>ALL_DONE</code>."},{"id":"site-068","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A task should send an alert only if any upstream task failed. Which Run if condition?</p>","o":["<code>ALL_SUCCESS</code>","<code>ALL_DONE</code>","<code>AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED</code>","<code>NONE_FAILED</code>"],"ok":[2],"w":["Runs only when everything succeeded.","Runs whatever happened, so it would alert on success too.","Made for alerting and compensation steps.","The opposite."],"e":"Alerting branch: <code>AT_LEAST_ONE_FAILED</code>. A task whose condition is not met ends as Excluded."},{"id":"site-028","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A task occasionally fails because of a transient network error. What addresses this with the least effort?</p>","o":["Increase the cluster size","Set a retry policy on the task (maximum retries and interval)","Change the trigger to Continuous","Add a second identical task"],"ok":[1],"w":["Size does not fix network blips.","Retries are configured per task; by default triggered jobs do not retry.","Changes how runs start, not how a task recovers.","Duplicates work and still fails the run."],"e":"Transient errors: task retries. Make the task idempotent so a retry is safe."},{"id":"site-072","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A task must be stopped if it runs longer than one hour. Which setting?</p>","o":["A duration warning threshold","<code>timeout_seconds = 3600</code>","<code>max_retries = 1</code>","<code>max_concurrent_runs = 1</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Only sends a notification.","The attempt is killed and marked Timed Out.","Controls retries after failure.","Controls parallel runs of the job."],"e":"Timeout kills (per attempt); a duration warning only notifies."},{"id":"new-jobs-01","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A task sometimes hangs on an external call. It should be stopped after 30 minutes and tried again automatically. What do you configure?</p>","o":["A duration warning of 30 minutes and two retries","A 30-minute timeout, retries, and <em>retry on timeout</em> enabled","Two retries only","A continuous trigger"],"ok":[1],"w":["The warning does not stop the task.","By default a timed-out attempt is not retried.","A hung task never fails, so retries never start.","Restarts the whole job, not the task, and continuous jobs do not use retry policies."],"e":"Hung tasks: timeout plus <code>retry_on_timeout</code>."},{"id":"site-025","sec":["A4","P1"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Task A computes a row count that Task B, in the same job run, needs. What is the recommended way to pass the value?</p>","o":["Write it to a global temporary view","<code>dbutils.jobs.taskValues.set()</code> in A and <code>dbutils.jobs.taskValues.get()</code> in B","A widget with the same name","Environment variables on the cluster"],"ok":[1],"w":["Tasks may run on different compute; temporary views do not cross tasks.","Task values are the built-in way to pass small values between tasks.","Widgets receive parameters; they do not carry results between tasks.","Static configuration, not run results."],"e":"Small values between tasks: task values (or the reference <code>{{tasks.A.values.key}}</code>)."},{"id":"site-073","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>How can task B use, in its parameters, the value that task <code>ingest</code> set with key <code>row_count</code>?</p>","o":["<code>{{tasks.ingest.values.row_count}}</code>","<code>${ingest.row_count}</code>","<code>{{job.ingest.row_count}}</code>","<code>$row_count</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["A dynamic value reference to a task value.","Bundle-style substitution, not a job reference.","<code>job.</code> references are for job-level context.","Not a reference syntax."],"e":"<code>{{tasks.&lt;task&gt;.values.&lt;key&gt;}}</code>, <code>{{job.parameters.&lt;name&gt;}}</code>, <code>{{job.run_id}}</code>."},{"id":"new-jobs-02","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A developer runs a notebook interactively that contains <code>dbutils.jobs.taskValues.get(taskKey=\"ingest\", key=\"rows\")</code>, and the call fails. In the job it works. What makes the notebook runnable interactively?</p>","o":["Running it on a SQL warehouse","Passing <code>debugValue=...</code> to <code>get</code>, which is returned when the notebook runs outside a job","Setting a widget called <code>rows</code>","Using <code>default=0</code> only"],"ok":[1],"w":["Task values have nothing to do with the compute type.","","<code>get</code> does not read widgets.","<code>default</code> covers a missing key in a job run; interactive runs need <code>debugValue</code>."],"e":"<code>taskValues.get(taskKey, key, default, debugValue)</code>."},{"id":"site-027","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job parameter and a task parameter have the same key <code>env</code>. Which value does the task receive?</p>","o":["The task parameter","The job parameter","Neither; the job fails","A random one"],"ok":[1],"w":["Job parameters take precedence.","Job parameters are pushed to every task and override task parameters with the same key.","There is no conflict error.","Precedence is defined."],"e":"Job parameters win over task parameters."},{"id":"sp-55","sec":["P1","A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which command and read call store and retrieve a secret correctly?</p>","o":["<code>databricks secrets put-secret SCOPE KEY</code> and <code>dbutils.secrets.get(\"SCOPE\", \"KEY\")</code>","<code>databricks tokens create</code> and <code>dbutils.tokens.get()</code>","<code>databricks secrets put-secret KEY SCOPE</code> and <code>dbutils.secrets.get(\"KEY\", \"SCOPE\")</code>","<code>dbutils.widgets.get(\"KEY\")</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Scope first, then key, on both sides.","Tokens authenticate to Databricks; <code>dbutils.tokens</code> does not exist.","Arguments reversed.","Widgets are notebook parameters, not a secret store."],"e":"Secrets: scopes and keys, read with <code>dbutils.secrets.get(scope, key)</code>; values are redacted in output."},{"id":"site-074","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A branch of a job must run only if the job parameter <code>full_load</code> equals <code>true</code>. Which task type implements this?</p>","o":["For each","If/else condition","Run Job","A SQL alert"],"ok":[1],"w":["Loops over a list.","It evaluates a comparison and runs the true or false branch.","Starts another job.","Notifies on query results; it is not job branching."],"e":"Branching on values: If/else condition task."},{"id":"site-113","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An ingest task sometimes finds no new files, and the transform should not run in that case. What is the best design?</p>","o":["Make ingest fail when there are no files","Set a task value with the row count and add an If/else condition (<code>{{tasks.ingest.values.rows}} &gt; 0</code>) before the transform","Run if <code>ALL_DONE</code> on the transform","A continuous trigger"],"ok":[1],"w":["Marks a healthy run as failed and triggers false alerts.","The decision is about data, so it needs a condition on a value.","Run if reacts to success or failure, not to data.","Changes how runs start, not what runs."],"e":"Run if = outcomes. If/else = values."},{"id":"site-114","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An If/else condition <code>{{tasks.count.values.n}} == 0</code> never matches, although the count is zero. The value arrives as <code>0.0</code>. Why?</p>","o":["Task values cannot be numbers","<code>==</code> and <code>!=</code> compare values as strings; use a numeric operator such as <code>&lt;= 0</code>","If/else only works with job parameters","The condition needs Run if <code>ALL_DONE</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["They can.","<code>\"0.0\"</code> is not equal to <code>\"0\"</code> as text.","It works with any reference.","Unrelated."],"e":"In If/else, <code>==</code>/<code>!=</code> are string comparisons; <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;=</code>, <code>&gt;=</code> are numeric."},{"id":"site-070","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job must run the same notebook once per country code, for a list of 50 codes. Which task type?</p>","o":["If/else condition","For each","Run Job","Pipeline"],"ok":[1],"w":["Chooses between two branches.","It runs a nested task once per element, passing the element as <code>{{input}}</code>.","Starts one other job.","Runs a declarative pipeline."],"e":"Same task over a list: For each."},{"id":"sp-54","sec":["P1","A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A job must download N report types from an unstable API. The list changes over time, each item must be tracked individually, and only failed items should be retried. Which design?</p>","o":["A For each task over the list","A loop inside one notebook","N fixed tasks written by hand","A pandas UDF making the calls"],"ok":[0],"w":["Each iteration is a tracked run with its own retries, and concurrency is configurable.","Failures are hidden inside one task, and a retry reruns everything.","The job must be edited whenever the list changes.","A UDF is not an orchestration or retry mechanism."],"e":"For each gives per-item status, retries and parallelism."},{"id":"new-jobs-03","sec":["P1"],"topic":"jobs","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A For each task processes 300 tenant IDs; each nested run calls a rate-limited API. Runs fail with HTTP 429 when too many iterations run at once. What should you change first?</p>","o":["Replace For each with a Python loop","Lower the For each concurrency and give the nested task a retry policy","Increase the cluster size","Use a continuous trigger"],"ok":[1],"w":["Loses per-item tracking and retries.","Concurrency limits parallel calls; retries absorb the remaining transient errors.","More capacity means even more parallel calls.","Irrelevant to the rate limit."],"e":"For each concurrency (up to 100) is a throttle as well as an accelerator."},{"id":"sp-58","sec":["P1","A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A validation notebook runs in a job and must make the job fail when a check does not pass. How?</p>","o":["Raise an exception when the check fails","Print an error message","Call <code>dbutils.notebook.exit(\"error\")</code>","Ignore it and check manually later"],"ok":[0],"w":["An unhandled exception marks the task as failed.","The task still succeeds.","Ends the notebook successfully, returning the text.","Defeats the automation."],"e":"Fail a task with an exception; <code>dbutils.notebook.exit</code> means success."},{"id":"site-026","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Files from a partner arrive at unpredictable times in a Unity Catalog volume. The job should start shortly after each arrival. Which trigger?</p>","o":["Scheduled","Continuous","File arrival","Manual"],"ok":[2],"w":["Would run at fixed times, too early or too late.","Keeps a run active all the time, even with no files.","It watches a volume or external location and starts the job when new files appear.","Needs a person."],"e":"Unpredictable file drops: file arrival trigger."},{"id":"site-118","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A source system overwrites the same file name in a volume every day, and a file arrival trigger on that path never fires. Why?</p>","o":["File arrival triggers only watch external locations","File arrival triggers fire only for new files, not for overwritten ones","The trigger needs <em>wait after last change</em> set to 0","The volume must be managed"],"ok":[1],"w":["Volumes are supported.","Ask for unique file names, or use another trigger.","That setting delays the trigger; it does not detect overwrites.","Managed and external volumes both work."],"e":"Only new files trigger a run."},{"id":"site-071","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job should run only when the source table <code>silver.orders</code> is updated. Which trigger?</p>","o":["Scheduled","File arrival","Table update","Continuous"],"ok":[2],"w":["Runs whether or not the table changed.","Watches storage paths, not tables.","It starts the job when one or all of the monitored tables are updated.","Keeps a run active permanently."],"e":"Downstream of a table another process refreshes: table update trigger."},{"id":"site-116","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job uses a table update trigger on <code>silver.orders</code>, but it never starts, even though the table is updated. What is the most likely cause?</p>","o":["Table update triggers only work on views","The job's <em>run as</em> identity lacks <code>SELECT</code> on the monitored table","The table is managed","The trigger needs a file arrival path"],"ok":[1],"w":["They work on tables.","The trigger is evaluated with that identity; without access it cannot see updates.","Managed tables are supported.","Different trigger type."],"e":"Triggers evaluate as the job's run-as principal; give it access to what it watches."},{"id":"new-jobs-04","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Upstream data lands reliably by 02:00, and the finance report must be ready by 06:00 every day. Which trigger is the simplest correct choice?</p>","o":["A scheduled trigger at, say, 02:30 in the business time zone","A continuous trigger","A file arrival trigger on a table","Manual runs by the finance team"],"ok":[0],"w":["Predictable arrival plus a fixed deadline suits a time-based trigger.","Keeps compute running all day for one daily load.","File arrival watches storage paths.","Not automated."],"e":"Time-based triggers for predictable data and fixed deadlines; data-driven triggers when arrival is unpredictable."},{"id":"site-115","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Which Quartz cron expression schedules a job every day at 06:30?</p>","o":["<code>30 6 * * *</code>","<code>0 30 6 * * ?</code>","<code>6 30 * * * ?</code>","<code>0 6 30 * * ?</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Unix cron, without the seconds field.","Quartz fields: seconds, minutes, hours, day of month, month, day of week.","Means second 6, minute 30 of every hour.","Hour 30 does not exist."],"e":"Quartz starts with seconds. Also set the time zone of the schedule."},{"id":"site-117","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Which statement about continuous jobs is correct?</p>","o":["They support task dependencies and per-task retry policies","They keep one run active, cannot use task dependencies or retry policies, and restart with exponential backoff after failures","They run on a Quartz schedule every minute","They require a file arrival location"],"ok":[1],"w":["They do not.","","No schedule; a new run starts when the previous one ends.","That is another trigger."],"e":"Continuous = always one active run, restarted with backoff."},{"id":"site-119","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job contains a pipeline task and has <code>max_concurrent_runs = 5</code>. How many runs of this job can be active at the same time?</p>","o":["5","1","Unlimited","0"],"ok":[1],"w":["The pipeline task limits it.","A pipeline runs one update at a time, so a job with a pipeline task allows one active run.","Concurrency is always bounded.","The job still runs."],"e":"Pipeline task in a job = one active run."},{"id":"new-jobs-05","sec":["A4","A6"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A job has <code>max_concurrent_runs = 1</code> and queueing disabled. A run is still active when the next scheduled run is due. What happens to the new run?</p>","o":["It cancels the active run","It is skipped","It runs in parallel anyway","The job is paused"],"ok":[1],"w":["Active runs are not cancelled by new ones.","With queueing on, it would wait (up to 48 hours) instead.","The limit prevents that.","Nothing is paused."],"e":"Concurrency limit reached: queued if queueing is on, otherwise Skipped."},{"id":"site-093","sec":["A4","A1"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job task runs a notebook containing Python cells, and the task is configured to use a SQL warehouse. What happens?</p>","o":["The Python cells are skipped","The task fails, because SQL warehouses run only SQL","The warehouse converts Python to SQL","It runs, but without Photon"],"ok":[1],"w":["Cells are not silently skipped.","Only an all-SQL notebook can run on a warehouse.","There is no such conversion.","It does not run at all."],"e":"Python needs serverless or cluster compute; warehouses are SQL only."},{"id":"new-jobs-06","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A job must run a SQL file against a SQL warehouse after the ETL tasks. Which warehouse types can a SQL task use?</p>","o":["Any warehouse, including classic","Serverless or pro SQL warehouses","Only an all-purpose cluster","Only the job's shared job cluster"],"ok":[1],"w":["Classic warehouses are not supported for SQL tasks.","","SQL tasks run on SQL warehouses.","Job clusters run notebooks and code, not SQL tasks."],"e":"SQL and dashboard tasks: serverless or pro warehouse."},{"id":"new-jobs-07","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A dashboard must show fresh numbers as soon as the nightly pipeline has updated the gold tables, and subscribers should get the refreshed version. How should this be built?</p>","o":["Schedule the dashboard to refresh at a fixed time and hope the pipeline has finished","Add a dashboard task to the job, depending on the pipeline task","Use a continuous trigger on the dashboard","Run the pipeline from the dashboard"],"ok":[1],"w":["Breaks when the pipeline runs late.","It refreshes the dashboard and its subscriptions right after the data is ready.","Dashboards are not jobs with triggers.","Dashboards do not orchestrate pipelines."],"e":"Orchestrate consumers after producers: pipeline task, then dashboard task."},{"id":"new-jobs-08","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Five different jobs must all start the same \"load reference data\" workflow first, and the team wants to maintain that workflow in one place. Which task type helps?</p>","o":["Copy the reference tasks into all five jobs","A Run Job task that calls the reference data job","A For each task","A file arrival trigger on each job"],"ok":[1],"w":["Five copies to maintain.","The shared workflow is defined once and reused.","Loops over a list; it does not reuse another job.","Unrelated to reuse."],"e":"Reuse a whole workflow: Run Job task."},{"id":"sa-35","sec":["A4","P8"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A six-task workflow takes four hours. It failed at the fifth task because of a code error, now fixed. The first four tasks succeeded and are the slowest. What should you do?</p>","o":["Repair run","Run the whole workflow again","Restart the cluster and wait","Create a new workflow with only the last two tasks"],"ok":[0],"w":["Only the failed task and its dependents run again; the successful tasks are reused.","Repeats four hours of finished work.","The cause was code, already fixed.","Duplicates definitions that someone must maintain."],"e":"Repair run reuses successful work. Watch out for appending tasks, which rerun from the start."},{"id":"sa-36","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Another workflow of the same team has a single task, which failed because of a code error that has now been fixed. What should you do?</p>","o":["Run the job again","Repair run, to save time","Move the job to another cluster","Turn the workflow into a multi-task job before rerunning"],"ok":[0],"w":["With one task there is nothing successful to preserve; repairing and rerunning give the same result.","Nothing to save: the only task is the one that failed.","The error was in the code.","Restructuring brings nothing now."],"e":"Repair versus rerun depends on the job's structure, not on the severity of the error."},{"id":"sp-53","sec":["P8","A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A job has four parallel tasks. One failed because a new cluster policy banned its instance type. How do you fix it without redoing the others?</p>","o":["Change the failed task's compute configuration and run a repair run of that task","Rerun the whole job","Disable the cluster policy","Create a cluster by hand and run everything again"],"ok":[0],"w":["A repair uses the current task settings and reuses the successful tasks.","Wastes the successful work.","Removes cost governance for everyone.","Leaves the declarative configuration and reprocesses everything."],"e":"Repairs run with the current settings, so you can fix configuration before repairing."},{"id":"new-jobs-09","sec":["P8"],"topic":"jobs","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A run of a parameterised job failed because it was started with the wrong <code>processing_date</code>. Three of five tasks succeeded but used values that do not depend on the date. How do you correct the run without starting from scratch?</p>","o":["Edit the job definition, change the default date, and run it again","Repair the run with a parameter override for <code>processing_date</code>","Clone the job with a new default","Cancel and delete the run"],"ok":[1],"w":["Changes the job for every future run and redoes all tasks.","A repair can override job parameters and reruns only the tasks that need it.","Creates a second job to maintain.","Loses the run history and fixes nothing."],"e":"Repair runs accept parameter overrides."},{"id":"sa-37","sec":["A4","A6"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A notebook job failed in production with an error about the size of its output, although the transformation logic is correct and the data was written. What is the most likely cause?</p>","o":["The notebook's output exceeded the limit for notebook job runs (20 MB in total, 8 MB per cell)","The target table reached a maximum number of files","The cluster ran out of memory during the write","The driver lost its connection to storage"],"ok":[0],"w":["Displaying large DataFrames or verbose printing makes the run fail even when the writes succeeded.","No such limit shows up this way.","That would raise an out-of-memory error, and the data would not have been written.","That gives an I/O or authentication error, and the write would not have completed."],"e":"Do not <code>display</code> big DataFrames in production notebooks; log counts instead."},{"id":"new-jobs-10","sec":["P1"],"topic":"jobs","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A platform team must create 150 similar ingestion jobs (one per source system) and keep them in sync with a configuration file. Which approach is most maintainable?</p>","o":["Create them by hand in the UI","Generate them from the configuration with a bundle (or the Jobs API / Python SDK in a CI pipeline)","Clone one job 150 times and edit each","Put all 150 sources in one notebook loop"],"ok":[1],"w":["Slow, error-prone, and drifts from the configuration file.","Jobs defined as code are created, updated and reviewed consistently.","Still manual and hard to keep in sync.","Loses per-source tracking, retries and scheduling."],"e":"Many jobs: define them as code (bundles, API, SDK)."},{"id":"site-069","sec":["A4"],"topic":"jobs","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Three tasks need the same classic cluster configuration and should share one cluster within a job run. What do you configure?</p>","o":["An all-purpose cluster","A shared job cluster referenced by <code>job_cluster_key</code>","Three separate job clusters","A SQL warehouse"],"ok":[1],"w":["Higher DBU rate and it is not tied to the job run.","Tasks that reference the same <code>job_cluster_key</code> run on the same job cluster, which starts once per run.","Three start-ups and three times the overhead.","A SQL warehouse only runs SQL tasks."],"e":"<code>job_cluster_key</code> lets several tasks in one run share a job cluster. Libraries are then declared on each task, not on the shared cluster."},{"id":"site-034","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A developer finished work in a Databricks Git folder and wants it reviewed and merged into <code>main</code>. Where is the pull request created?</p>","o":["In the Git folder dialog in Databricks","In the Git provider (for example GitHub or Azure DevOps)","With <code>databricks bundle deploy</code>","In Catalog Explorer"],"ok":[1],"w":["Git folders branch, commit, push, pull, merge and rebase, but do not open pull requests.","Review and merge happen in the provider.","Deploys resources; it has nothing to do with code review.","Catalog Explorer manages data objects."],"e":"Pull requests live in the Git provider."},{"id":"site-124","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A user has CAN EDIT on a colleague's Git folder. Editing works, but switching branches, pull and commit and push fail. Why?</p>","o":["The Git credential expired","Those operations require CAN MANAGE on the Git folder","Git folders do not support branches","The repository must be public"],"ok":[1],"w":["Editing would still work, and the error would mention authentication.","Better still, each person works in their own Git folder.","They do.","Private repositories work with linked credentials."],"e":"Git operations need CAN MANAGE; a Git folder's branch is shared by everyone who uses that folder."},{"id":"sp-48","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>How should a team organise its work with Databricks Git folders so that nobody overwrites anyone else?</p>","o":["Each person clones the repository into their own Git folder and works on their own branch","Everyone uses one shared Git folder","Edit notebooks in a shared folder and copy them to Git later","Run Git from a cluster's terminal"],"ok":[0],"w":["Work is isolated and integrated through pull requests.","Switching branches changes the files for everyone, and changes get lost.","Manual, with no version control while developing.","Possible, but loses the native integration with notebooks and jobs."],"e":"One Git folder per person; one feature branch per change."},{"id":"new-cicd-01","sec":["P8","A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Production jobs currently run notebooks from a Git folder that engineers sometimes edit directly in the workspace. How should production code be kept consistent with the reviewed <code>main</code> branch?</p>","o":["Ask engineers to be careful when editing the production folder","Never edit production by hand: deploy with a bundle from CI, or have tasks run from the remote Git reference, or let CI update the production Git folder as a service principal","Give everyone CAN MANAGE on the production folder","Copy notebooks to <code>/Shared</code> after each merge by hand"],"ok":[1],"w":["Relies on discipline, with no audit trail.","Production then always matches a reviewed commit.","Makes manual edits easier, which is the problem.","Manual and error-prone."],"e":"Production code comes from Git through automation, never from manual edits."},{"id":"sa-41","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A team keeps its pipeline code in a Git repository connected to Databricks and wants to promote it to production as a job, reproducibly and with version control. What is the recommended approach?</p>","o":["Declarative Automation Bundles with Git integration","Create and manage the job manually in the UI","A JSON configuration file applied by a custom script","Export the notebook and import it into the production workspace after each change"],"ok":[0],"w":["YAML next to the code declares resources and per-environment targets, and plugs into CI/CD.","Not versioned, not reproducible across environments.","Rebuilds by hand what bundles already do.","Manual and untraceable."],"e":"Code in Git + promotion to production = bundles."},{"id":"sa-42","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>In a discussion about bundles, there is doubt about what defines the project. What is a bundle made of?</p>","o":["YAML configuration (<code>databricks.yml</code> plus included files) that declares the resources, artifacts and per-environment settings, next to the code","A Docker image with the runtime and the source code","A compressed file of artifacts without metadata","A text file listing the artifacts to migrate"],"ok":[0],"w":["It declares what to deploy (jobs, pipelines, dashboards) and how each environment differs.","Bundles are not containers.","Could not define resources or environments.","A bundle defines deployment behaviour, not just a list."],"e":"A bundle is declarative YAML plus code: infrastructure as code for Databricks."},{"id":"site-075","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Where must the main bundle configuration file be?</p>","o":["One <code>databricks.yml</code> at the root of the bundle project","One <code>databricks.yml</code> in every folder","Inside <code>resources/</code> only","Anywhere, as long as it is called <code>bundle.json</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Other YAML files can be included from it (for example <code>resources/*.yml</code>).","Only one root file.","Resources can live there, but the root file is required.","Wrong name and format."],"e":"One root <code>databricks.yml</code>; resource files are included from it."},{"id":"site-030","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Which command checks a bundle's configuration for errors and shows the resolved configuration, without deploying anything?</p>","o":["<code>databricks bundle init</code>","<code>databricks bundle validate</code>","<code>databricks bundle run</code>","<code>databricks bundle destroy</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Creates a new project from a template.","Nothing changes in the workspace; <code>--output json</code> prints the resolved configuration.","Runs a deployed resource.","Deletes deployed resources."],"e":"Lifecycle: init → validate → (plan) → deploy → run."},{"id":"sp-44","sec":["P8","A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>What is the typical sequence of CI commands run on every pull request with bundles?</p>","o":["<code>bundle validate</code>, <code>bundle deploy</code>, <code>bundle run</code> (to a test target)","<code>bundle deploy</code>, <code>bundle run</code>, <code>bundle validate</code>","<code>bundle init</code>, <code>bundle deploy</code>, <code>bundle run</code>","<code>bundle destroy</code>, <code>bundle deploy</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Validate the configuration, deploy to the test environment, run the test job.","Validating last lets invalid configuration get deployed.","<code>init</code> creates a new project; it is not part of the recurring flow.","Destroying first deletes the environment without validating anything."],"e":"CI on pull requests: validate, unit tests, deploy to test, run integration tests."},{"id":"site-031","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>How do you deploy a bundle to the <code>prod</code> target?</p>","o":["<code>databricks bundle deploy --env prod</code>","<code>databricks bundle deploy -t prod</code>","<code>databricks deploy prod</code>","<code>databricks bundle run prod</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["There is no <code>--env</code> flag.","<code>-t</code> (or <code>--target</code>) chooses a target from <code>databricks.yml</code>.","Not a CLI command.","Runs a resource; it does not deploy."],"e":"Always pass <code>-t</code>; without it the default target (usually dev) is used."},{"id":"site-032","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>The same job must write to <code>dev_catalog</code> in dev and <code>prod_catalog</code> in prod. What is the recommended bundle approach?</p>","o":["Two separate bundles","A custom variable with a default, overridden in <code>targets.&lt;name&gt;.variables</code>, referenced as <code>${var.catalog}</code>","Hard-code the catalog and edit it before each deploy","A widget in the notebook"],"ok":[1],"w":["Duplicates the code and configuration.","","Manual and error-prone.","Per-run input, not environment configuration."],"e":"Environment differences live in target overrides of variables."},{"id":"new-cicd-02","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A bundle's <code>prod</code> target repeats the whole job definition from the top level just to change the cluster size. A reviewer says this is unnecessary. Why?</p>","o":["Targets cannot change clusters","Target settings are merged with the top-level definition, so the target only needs the values that differ (for example the job cluster's worker count)","Each target must be in a separate bundle","Variables cannot be used in targets"],"ok":[1],"w":["They can.","Less duplication, fewer drift bugs.","One bundle has many targets.","They can, and are often the cleaner way."],"e":"Override only what changes per target."},{"id":"site-076","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A variable is set in <code>targets.prod.variables</code>, in the environment variable <code>BUNDLE_VAR_catalog</code>, and with <code>--var</code> on the CLI. Which value wins?</p>","o":["The target value","The <code>BUNDLE_VAR_</code> environment variable","The <code>--var</code> CLI flag","The default"],"ok":[2],"w":["Lower precedence than environment variables and the CLI.","Lower than <code>--var</code>.","Order: <code>--var</code> &gt; <code>BUNDLE_VAR_x</code> &gt; overrides file &gt; target &gt; default.","The lowest precedence."],"e":"Variable precedence: CLI flag, environment, overrides file, target, default."},{"id":"site-120","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An engineer runs <code>databricks bundle run orders_job -t test --var=\"catalog=other\"</code>, but the job still writes to <code>test_catalog</code>. Why, and what should be done?</p>","o":["<code>--var</code> is only valid on <code>validate</code>","Bundle variables are resolved at deploy time; expose the value as a job parameter and pass it with <code>--params</code>, or redeploy","The variable needs a default","The test target is in production mode"],"ok":[1],"w":["It is accepted by several commands, but variables are fixed at deploy.","","The issue is when it is resolved.","Unrelated."],"e":"Deploy-time variables versus run-time job parameters."},{"id":"new-cicd-03","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A bundle needs a database password for one task. Where should the value come from?</p>","o":["The variable's <code>default</code> in <code>databricks.yml</code>","The <code>prod</code> target's <code>variables</code> block","At runtime from a secret scope (<code>dbutils.secrets.get</code>), or, if it must be a variable, from <code>BUNDLE_VAR_&lt;name&gt;</code> set from the CI system's secret store","A comment in the YAML with instructions"],"ok":[2],"w":["Committed to Git for everyone to read.","Also committed to Git.","Secrets never live in YAML.","Not a mechanism."],"e":"Secrets: secret scopes at runtime; CI secret store at deploy time; never YAML."},{"id":"site-033","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>How should a CI/CD pipeline (for example GitHub Actions) authenticate to deploy bundles to production?</p>","o":["With the lead engineer's personal access token","As a service principal using OAuth (machine-to-machine, or OIDC workload identity federation)","With a username and password in the YAML","Anonymously"],"ok":[1],"w":["Ties production to a person and a long-lived secret.","","Exposes credentials in Git.","Not possible."],"e":"CI/CD = service principal; prefer federation so no secret is stored."},{"id":"new-cicd-04","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A security team wants the GitHub Actions deployment to Databricks to work without any long-lived Databricks secret stored in GitHub. What should be configured?</p>","o":["A personal access token stored as a GitHub secret","OAuth M2M with the client secret stored in GitHub","Workload identity federation: a federation policy for the service principal that trusts GitHub's OIDC tokens (for example <code>DATABRICKS_AUTH_TYPE=github-oidc</code>)","Disable authentication for the CI runner's IP"],"ok":[2],"w":["Long-lived and personal.","A service principal, but still a stored secret.","GitHub issues a short-lived token per run; nothing is stored.","Not possible and unsafe."],"e":"Best CI authentication: service principal with OIDC federation."},{"id":"site-121","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A developer runs <code>databricks bundle deploy -t prod</code> from a feature branch. The <code>prod</code> target has <code>mode: production</code> and <code>git.branch: main</code>, and validation fails. What is the right response?</p>","o":["Add <code>--force</code>","Merge the change to <code>main</code> and let CI deploy from <code>main</code> as the production service principal","Switch the target to development mode","Delete <code>git.branch</code> from the target"],"ok":[1],"w":["Bypasses exactly the protection that prevented a bad deploy.","","Turns production into a personal deployment.","Removes the safety check."],"e":"Production mode checks the branch; the fix is the process, not <code>--force</code>."},{"id":"site-122","sec":["A5","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A team generates YAML for a job created in the UI and deploys it without binding. What happens?</p>","o":["The existing job is updated","A second, duplicate job is created","The deploy fails","The UI job is deleted"],"ok":[1],"w":["Only a bound job is updated.","To adopt the existing job, bind it (<code>bundle deployment bind</code>, or <code>generate ... --bind</code>).","It succeeds, which is the problem.","Deploy does not touch unbound jobs."],"e":"Generate + bind adopts; generate alone duplicates."},{"id":"sp-45","sec":["P8","A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>To bring an existing production job under a bundle without creating a duplicate, which steps do you follow?</p>","o":["<code>bundle generate job --existing-job-id &lt;id&gt;</code>, then <code>bundle deployment bind &lt;key&gt; &lt;id&gt;</code> (or use <code>--bind</code> on generate)","Only <code>bundle deploy</code> after generating","Export the job's JSON and run <code>bundle deploy</code>","<code>bundle init</code> with the job's name"],"ok":[0],"w":["Generate writes the YAML and downloads the files; bind links the resource key to the existing job.","Without binding, deploy creates a new job.","Still no link to the existing job.","Creates a new project; adopts nothing."],"e":"Adopt existing resources with bind."},{"id":"new-cicd-06","sec":["P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>An engineer deletes an old job's YAML file from a bundle and deploys to prod. What happens to the job in the workspace, and how can critical resources be protected from this?</p>","o":["Nothing; deleted YAML is ignored","The job is deleted on that deploy; set <code>lifecycle.prevent_destroy: true</code> on resources that must never be destroyed by a deploy","The job is paused","The deploy fails because the job still exists"],"ok":[1],"w":["Bundles reconcile the workspace with the configuration.","","It is removed.","It succeeds and deletes it."],"e":"Bundles own their resources: removing them from YAML removes them from the workspace."},{"id":"sp-46","sec":["P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>In <code>databricks.yml</code>, an app is declared as <code>resources.apps.sales_app</code> with <code>name: 'app-sales'</code>. How do you reference its ID in another resource?</p>","o":["<code>${resources.apps.sales_app.id}</code>","<code>${resources.apps.app-sales.id}</code>","<code>${apps.sales_app}</code>","<code>${bundle.apps.sales_app.id}</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["References use the resource key in YAML.","<code>name</code> is the display name, not the key.","Missing the <code>resources</code> prefix and the attribute.","No such path."],"e":"<code>${resources.&lt;type&gt;.&lt;key&gt;.&lt;field&gt;}</code> uses the YAML key."},{"id":"new-cicd-07","sec":["P8","A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A job in a bundle must use the SQL warehouse called \"Reporting\" in every workspace, but its ID differs per workspace. How do you avoid hard-coding IDs?</p>","o":["Put each workspace's ID in a comment","A <code>lookup</code> variable that resolves the warehouse ID from its name at deploy time","A job parameter the operator types on each run","One bundle per workspace"],"ok":[1],"w":["Comments do nothing.","<code>variables: warehouse_id: lookup: warehouse: \"Reporting\"</code>.","Manual and error-prone.","Duplicates everything."],"e":"<code>lookup</code> variables resolve IDs of warehouses, clusters, service principals and more by name."},{"id":"site-123","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A CI step runs <code>databricks bundle destroy -t test</code> and never finishes. What is missing?</p>","o":["<code>--force-lock</code>","<code>--auto-approve</code>","<code>-o json</code>","<code>--select</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Only for a lock left by an interrupted deploy.","<code>destroy</code> asks for confirmation; non-interactive runs must approve it explicitly.","Changes the output format only.","Not used for this."],"e":"Destructive commands in CI need <code>--auto-approve</code>."},{"id":"new-cicd-08","sec":["P1","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A team's transformation logic lives in notebooks chained with <code>%run</code>, and nobody can unit-test it. Which restructuring best supports modular development and CI/CD?</p>","o":["Merge all notebooks into one large notebook","Move the logic into a Python package (functions that take and return DataFrames) built as a wheel, with pytest tests; keep notebooks as thin entry points","Keep <code>%run</code> but add more comments","Rewrite everything in SQL files"],"ok":[1],"w":["Even harder to test and review.","Packages can be imported, tested in CI and deployed with bundles.","Comments do not make code testable.","Changes the language, not the structure."],"e":"Testable projects: package + wheel + tests; notebooks orchestrate."},{"id":"sp-49","sec":["P1","P8"],"topic":"cicd","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A cluster without internet access must install internal Python libraries for automated jobs. What should you do?</p>","o":["Package them as a wheel, publish it to a volume, and declare it as a library of the job or bundle","<code>%pip install</code> from PyPI in each notebook","Install them by hand on the driver of an interactive cluster","An init script reading from the DBFS root"],"ok":[0],"w":["Reproducible, versioned and independent of internet access.","Fails without internet and does not standardise jobs.","Manual and not reproducible.","An obsolete practice, restricted on shared compute."],"e":"Private code: wheels in volumes (or a private index), declared as job or environment dependencies."},{"id":"sa-38","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>An engineer defines a variable in Python and, in the same cell, tries to use it directly inside a <code>SELECT</code> statement. The cell fails. Why?</p>","o":["Each notebook cell runs in one language","A notebook supports only one language","Interoperability only exists between Scala and SQL","A special interpolation character is missing"],"ok":[0],"w":["The cell's language is the notebook default or the magic command at its top; mixing Python and SQL in one cell does not work.","Notebooks support several languages, one per cell.","No such rule.","There is none that makes two languages share a cell."],"e":"One language per cell. Pass Python values to SQL with <code>spark.sql(...)</code> parameters, temporary views or widgets."},{"id":"sa-39","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A transformation function returns odd results, and one argument may arrive with the wrong type. The engineer wants to confirm it quickly without changing the code. Which tool?</p>","o":["The notebook debugger with the Variable Explorer","Add <code>print()</code> calls around the code","The cluster logs","The SQL tab of the Spark UI"],"ok":[0],"w":["Breakpoints and the explorer show values and types in memory without editing code.","Works, but changes the code and must be cleaned up.","Show driver and executor logs, not notebook variables.","Shows query plans and metrics, not Python variables."],"e":"Values and types at runtime: debugger and Variable Explorer."},{"id":"sa-40","sec":["A5","P1"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A developer wants to write and debug pipeline code in her usual IDE, with its extensions and Git integration, while execution happens on Databricks compute next to the data. What enables this?</p>","o":["Databricks Connect","Databricks Connect, but only with VS Code","Export notebooks and work offline","Nothing; development must happen in the web UI"],"ok":[0],"w":["Code is written and debugged locally; Spark execution happens on the remote cluster or serverless compute.","It works with VS Code, PyCharm, IntelliJ, Jupyter and others.","Execution is remote, so a connection is required.","The opposite of what Databricks Connect is for."],"e":"Databricks Connect separates where you write code from where it runs."},{"id":"site-079","sec":["A5"],"topic":"cicd","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A bundle target uses <code>mode: development</code>. Which behaviour applies?</p>","o":["Resources are prefixed with <code>[dev &lt;user&gt;]</code>, and schedules and triggers are paused","Resources are deployed as the service principal","Deployment requires approval","All variables are ignored"],"ok":[0],"w":["Personal deployments do not collide and do not run on their own.","That is a production setting (<code>run_as</code>).","No approval step is involved.","Variables work in every mode."],"e":"Development mode: prefixed names, paused triggers, concurrent runs allowed, pipelines in development mode."},{"id":"sp-61","sec":["P1"],"topic":"testing","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which approach makes PySpark transformations modular and easy to test?</p>","o":["Pure functions that take and return a DataFrame, chained with <code>df.transform(f1).transform(f2)</code>","One large function that does everything, called from the notebook","Global variables shared between cells","Copy and paste the code into each pipeline"],"ok":[0],"w":["Each step can be tested on its own.","Hard to test in parts.","Couples the steps and hides dependencies.","Drift and duplicated maintenance."],"e":"Small pure functions + <code>DataFrame.transform</code> = composable and testable."},{"id":"new-testing-01","sec":["P1"],"topic":"testing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A unit test must check that <code>clean_orders(raw)</code> returns exactly the expected rows and schema, and print a readable difference when it does not. Which assertion fits?</p>","o":["<code>assert clean_orders(raw).count() == expected.count()</code>","<code>pyspark.testing.assertDataFrameEqual(clean_orders(raw), expected)</code>","<code>assert clean_orders(raw).collect() == expected.collect()</code>","<code>clean_orders(raw).show()</code> and inspect the output"],"ok":[1],"w":["Same counts can hide wrong values.","It compares schema and rows and shows a diff on failure.","Fails on row order and gives no useful diff.","Not an automated test."],"e":"<code>assertDataFrameEqual</code> for data, <code>assertSchemaEqual</code> for schemas."},{"id":"new-testing-02","sec":["P1"],"topic":"testing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A test compares the output of a transformation with an expected DataFrame using <code>assertDataFrameEqual</code>. The rows are the same but come back in a different order. What happens?</p>","o":["The test passes, because row order is ignored by default (<code>checkRowOrder=False</code>)","The test fails unless both DataFrames are sorted first","The test fails because Spark row order is random","The assertion only compares schemas"],"ok":[0],"w":["Set <code>checkRowOrder=True</code> only when order is part of the requirement.","Sorting is not needed by default.","The assertion is designed for that.","That is <code>assertSchemaEqual</code>."],"e":"Spark does not guarantee order; the testing helpers ignore it unless asked."},{"id":"new-testing-03","sec":["P1"],"topic":"testing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A function adds a computed column. A test must fail if the column's type or nullability changes, regardless of the data. Which check?</p>","o":["<code>assertSchemaEqual(actual.schema, expected_schema)</code>","<code>assertDataFrameEqual</code> on an empty DataFrame only","<code>actual.printSchema()</code>","<code>len(actual.columns) == 5</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["It compares names, types and nullability of the columns.","Compares data; a schema check states the intent directly.","Prints, does not assert.","Misses type and nullability changes."],"e":"Schema contracts: <code>assertSchemaEqual</code>."},{"id":"new-testing-04","sec":["P1"],"topic":"testing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A transformation notebook reads <code>main.bronze.orders</code>, cleans it and writes <code>main.silver.orders</code>, all in one function. The team cannot write unit tests for it. What should they change?</p>","o":["Add expectations to the silver table","Move reading and writing out of the function, so it takes a DataFrame and returns a DataFrame, then test it with small in-memory inputs","Run the notebook against production and compare row counts","Wrap the function in <code>try</code>/<code>except</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Validates data at runtime; does not test the logic.","I/O at the edges, logic in pure functions.","Slow, risky and not a unit test.","Hides errors."],"e":"Testable code keeps I/O out of the transformation."},{"id":"new-testing-05","sec":["P1","P8"],"topic":"testing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Unit tests pass, but the team wants to verify before each release that the deployed job works end to end with real services. Which practice fits?</p>","o":["More unit tests with mocked Spark","An integration test: deploy the bundle to a <code>test</code> target, run the job on representative test data, and fail the run with an exception when output checks fail","Run the job in production and roll back if it fails","Rely on expectations in production"],"ok":[1],"w":["Still does not exercise the deployed job.","CI runs it after <code>bundle deploy -t test</code>.","Tests in production with real consequences.","Catches bad data, not broken deployments."],"e":"Unit tests for functions; integration tests for deployed jobs in a test environment."},{"id":"new-testing-06","sec":["P1"],"topic":"testing","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Tests for a package run locally with a plain <code>SparkSession</code>, but one function reads Unity Catalog tables and uses Databricks-specific features. How can that test run from the developer's IDE?</p>","o":["It cannot; such code can only be tested by hand in notebooks","Use Databricks Connect (<code>DatabricksSession</code>) in the test fixture so the Spark code runs on Databricks compute","Install Unity Catalog locally","Replace the function with a SQL query"],"ok":[1],"w":["Databricks Connect exists for this.","The test runs from the IDE or CI while executing remotely.","Not how Unity Catalog is used.","Changes the code instead of testing it."],"e":"Local Spark for pure logic; Databricks Connect when the code needs Databricks features."},{"id":"site-087","sec":["A7","P4","P6"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Where can you query who accessed which tables in Unity Catalog?</p>","o":["<code>system.access.audit</code>","<code>system.billing.usage</code>","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code>","<code>information_schema.tables</code> only"],"ok":[0],"w":["Audit events record who did what, when and from where.","Usage and cost, not access.","Operations on one Delta table, not reads by users.","Lists tables, not access events."],"e":"Who did what: <code>system.access.audit</code>."},{"id":"sa-10","sec":["A2","P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>To meet compliance requirements, a security team wants to ingest the workspace's activity logs into a SIEM tool. In which format are Databricks audit logs delivered?</p>","o":["JSON","CSV","XML","Unstructured plain text"],"ok":[0],"w":["Each event is a JSON object with time, identity, action, affected resource and request origin.","A flat format does not fit the nested, variable fields of events.","Structured, but not the format used.","Would not allow consistent processing."],"e":"Audit logs: JSON files, or the <code>system.access.audit</code> table."},{"id":"sp-42","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which query returns daily DBU usage per SKU?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT date_trunc('day', usage_start_time) AS d, sku_name, SUM(usage_quantity) FROM system.billing.usage GROUP BY d, sku_name</code>","The same query with <code>COUNT(usage_quantity)</code>","The same query with <code>SUM(dbus)</code>","The same query with <code>GROUP BY d</code> only"],"ok":[0],"w":["Sums consumption by both dimensions.","Counts usage records, not consumption.","There is no <code>dbus</code> column; it is <code>usage_quantity</code>.","<code>sku_name</code> is neither grouped nor aggregated."],"e":"Usage = <code>SUM(usage_quantity)</code> from <code>system.billing.usage</code>; cost = join with <code>list_prices</code>."},{"id":"new-mon-01","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Finance wants the estimated list cost in dollars per job for last month. Which approach is correct?</p>","o":["<code>SUM(usage_quantity)</code> from <code>system.billing.usage</code> grouped by job","Join <code>system.billing.usage</code> with <code>system.billing.list_prices</code> on SKU and the price validity period, multiply <code>usage_quantity</code> by the price, and group by <code>usage_metadata.job_id</code>","Count runs per job in <code>system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline</code>","Read the cloud provider bill only"],"ok":[1],"w":["DBUs, not dollars.","","Runs are not cost.","Misses the DBU cost split by job."],"e":"Cost = usage × list price, attributed through <code>usage_metadata</code> and tags."},{"id":"sp-43","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Executives need a daily report of consumption per team that updates itself. What should you build?</p>","o":["A dashboard on system tables with a scheduled refresh and a subscription","Share the query so each executive runs it","Ask each team to report its own usage","Revoke access to SQL warehouses"],"ok":[0],"w":["Generated and delivered with no manual work.","Needs manual runs every time.","Incomplete, late and not auditable.","Produces no report."],"e":"Recurring reporting: dashboard + schedule + subscription."},{"id":"sp-64","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A SQL alert must fire when more than 15% of customers have no email AND more than 10% have an invalid phone number. Which query pattern works for the alert?</p>","o":["Compute both percentages in a subquery and return <code>CASE WHEN p_email &gt; 15 AND p_phone &gt; 10 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END</code>","Return both percentages in separate columns","<code>SELECT COUNT(*)</code> with <code>OR</code> between the conditions","List the customers matching both conditions"],"ok":[0],"w":["The alert evaluates one value, so the combined logic must live in it.","The alert condition checks one column.","Uses OR instead of AND and counts rows instead of percentages.","A list of rows has no single value to compare."],"e":"Alerts compare one value; build composite conditions in the query."},{"id":"new-mon-02","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>The platform team wants to be notified when any SQL warehouse has been running for more than 2 hours without executing queries. What is the simplest approach?</p>","o":["Ask users to stop warehouses when they finish","A SQL alert on a query over the compute system tables (warehouse events and query history) that returns the number of idle warehouses, triggering when it is above 0","A Spark UI listener","Disable auto stop"],"ok":[1],"w":["Manual and unreliable.","Alerts on system tables cover warehouse health and cost.","Warehouses do not expose the Spark UI.","Makes idle time worse."],"e":"System tables + SQL alerts = operational monitoring without extra tools."},{"id":"sp-22","sec":["P4","P3"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Where can you programmatically extract the number of records that passed and failed each expectation of a declarative pipeline?</p>","o":["In the event log, in <code>flow_progress</code> events, under <code>details.flow_progress.data_quality.expectations</code>","In events of type <code>expectation_result</code>","In <code>system.billing.usage</code>","In <code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> of the target table"],"ok":[0],"w":["The pipeline records quality metrics per expectation there.","That event type does not exist.","Usage and cost only.","Lists Delta operations, not expectation results."],"e":"Pipeline health and quality: the event log."},{"id":"new-mon-03","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A materialized view in a pipeline suddenly takes much longer to refresh. How can you find out whether it was refreshed incrementally or recomputed in full, and why?</p>","o":["<code>DESCRIBE DETAIL</code> on the materialized view","Query the pipeline event log for <code>planning_information</code> events","Check <code>system.billing.usage</code>","Look at the Spark UI Storage tab"],"ok":[1],"w":["Shows files and properties, not refresh decisions.","They record the refresh technique chosen and the reason.","Shows cost, not the refresh method.","Shows cached data."],"e":"<code>planning_information</code> explains incremental versus full refreshes."},{"id":"site-082","sec":["A6"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job run shows task D as <em>Upstream failed</em>. How should you troubleshoot?</p>","o":["Fix task D's code","Go up the task graph to the first failed task and inspect it","Increase D's retries","Change D's compute"],"ok":[1],"w":["D never ran.","","D did not fail; it was not started.","Irrelevant to D."],"e":"<em>Upstream failed</em> points upstream: find the first failure."},{"id":"new-mon-04","sec":["A6"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A job ends with the status <em>Succeeded with failures</em>. What does this tell you?</p>","o":["At least one non-leaf task failed, but the leaf tasks succeeded (for example because a downstream task runs with <code>ALL_DONE</code>)","Every task succeeded, with warnings","A leaf task failed","The job timed out"],"ok":[0],"w":["The final state is derived from the leaf tasks.","A task did fail.","The run would be Failed.","The status would be Timed out."],"e":"Final status comes from leaf tasks; check failed non-leaf tasks even when the run \"succeeded\"."},{"id":"site-125","sec":["A6"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job got 10 minutes slower with no code or data change. The run's duration breakdown shows the extra time in <em>Waiting for resources</em>. Which change helps?</p>","o":["Tune <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code>","Reduce compute acquisition time: instance pools, serverless (performance optimized), or grouping jobs into one multi-task job","Add a broadcast hint","Run <code>OPTIMIZE</code> on the tables"],"ok":[1],"w":["The running time did not change.","The problem is getting compute, not the code.","Affects query execution, not start-up.","Affects reads, not waiting for compute."],"e":"Read the duration breakdown before tuning anything."},{"id":"new-mon-05","sec":["A6","P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A manager asks whether a daily job has been getting slower over the last six months. The job's Runs tab only shows about two months. Where do you get the data?</p>","o":["Export screenshots of the matrix view every week","<code>system.lakeflow.job_run_timeline</code>, aggregating run durations by <code>run_id</code> and date","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> of the job's output table","The cluster event log"],"ok":[1],"w":["Manual and too late for past runs.","System tables keep about a year of run history.","Shows table versions, not job durations.","Covers cluster events, not job run history."],"e":"Beyond the UI's window: <code>system.lakeflow</code> tables."},{"id":"new-mon-06","sec":["A6"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>In the matrix view of a job's run history, the last five bars are clearly taller than the previous twenty, and all task cells are green. What does this indicate and what is a good next step?</p>","o":["Nothing; the bars show data volume, not time","Runs are succeeding but taking longer; compare the slow runs' task durations and duration breakdown with earlier runs to find which task and which phase grew","The job is failing silently","The job's schedule changed"],"ok":[1],"w":["Bar height is run duration.","","Green cells mean the tasks succeeded.","The view shows durations, not schedules."],"e":"Matrix view: height = duration, cells = task status. Compare with the baseline, then drill down."},{"id":"sp-52","sec":["P4","P1"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>An external orchestrator must know whether job X has already finished today. Which <code>databricks jobs list-runs</code> options should it use?</p>","o":["<code>--start-time-from</code> (today's midnight in epoch milliseconds) and <code>--completed-only</code>","<code>--start-time-to</code> and <code>--active-only</code>","<code>--start-time-to</code> and <code>--completed-only</code>","<code>--start-time-from</code> and <code>--active-only</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Runs started today that have finished.","Bounds the end of the window and returns only running runs.","The time filter points the wrong way.","Returns only runs still in progress."],"e":"CLI, API and SDK expose run state for external monitoring."},{"id":"new-mon-07","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A Python script outside Databricks must check the result state of the last 20 completed runs of job 123. Which code is idiomatic?</p>","o":["Scrape the Jobs UI HTML","<code>WorkspaceClient().jobs.list_runs(job_id=123, completed_only=True, limit=20)</code> from the Databricks SDK, reading <code>run.state.result_state</code>","<code>spark.sql(\"SHOW JOB RUNS 123\")</code>","<code>dbutils.jobs.taskValues.get(...)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Fragile and unsupported.","","Not a SQL command.","Reads task values inside a run."],"e":"SDK, CLI and REST API offer the same run information."},{"id":"new-mon-08","sec":["P4"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A team wants to be warned automatically when any table in its <code>sales</code> schema stops receiving fresh data or receives far fewer rows than usual, without writing a check for every table. What should they enable?</p>","o":["A Delta <code>CHECK</code> constraint on every table","Anomaly detection (data quality monitoring) on the schema, which checks freshness and completeness of its tables automatically","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> reviews every morning","Predictive optimization"],"ok":[1],"w":["Validates rows, not freshness or volume.","","Manual.","Maintains tables; does not watch data arrival."],"e":"Freshness and completeness across many tables: anomaly detection."},{"id":"new-mon-09","sec":["P4","P6"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>The security team must be notified within an hour whenever someone changes permissions on objects of the <code>finance</code> catalog. What is a simple, governed way to do this?</p>","o":["Ask catalog owners to report their changes","A scheduled SQL alert on a query over <code>system.access.audit</code> that counts permission-change events on <code>finance</code> objects in the last hour, triggering when the count is above 0","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> on every table","A row filter on the finance tables"],"ok":[1],"w":["Manual and unreliable.","Audit system tables plus alerts cover security events.","Shows data operations, not grants.","Restricts rows; it does not detect changes."],"e":"Security monitoring: alerts on <code>system.access.audit</code>."},{"id":"new-mon-10","sec":["P4","A6"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A job usually takes 40 minutes; the business needs to know when it takes longer than 90 minutes, but the run must not be stopped. What should you configure?</p>","o":["A timeout of 90 minutes","A duration warning (health rule on run duration) of 90 minutes with a notification","Two retries","A continuous trigger"],"ok":[1],"w":["Kills the run.","It notifies and lets the run continue.","Unrelated to duration.","Changes how runs start."],"e":"Warn with health rules; stop with timeouts."},{"id":"new-mon-11","sec":["P4","P1"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A continuous streaming job must alert the on-call engineer when it falls behind its source by more than 10 minutes. What should you configure on the job?</p>","o":["A duration warning of 10 minutes","A streaming backlog health rule (for example backlog in seconds above 600) with a notification","A timeout of 10 minutes","<code>max_concurrent_runs = 1</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["A continuous run always lasts longer than that.","Jobs can watch streaming backlog metrics.","Would stop the stream repeatedly.","Already the case for continuous jobs; unrelated to lag."],"e":"Streams: alert on backlog, not on duration."},{"id":"new-mon-12","sec":["P4","P3"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A declarative pipeline update failed overnight. Which query finds the error messages quickly?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT timestamp, message FROM event_log(TABLE(main.sales.silver_orders)) WHERE level = 'ERROR' ORDER BY timestamp DESC</code>","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY main.sales.silver_orders</code>","<code>SELECT * FROM system.billing.usage WHERE ...</code>","<code>SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE main.sales.silver_orders</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["The event log records errors with their messages and details.","Shows committed operations, not failed updates.","Usage, not errors.","Privileges."],"e":"Pipeline troubleshooting starts in the event log."},{"id":"new-mon-13","sec":["P4","P5"],"topic":"monitoring","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Users complain that a SQL warehouse became slow this week. Which system table helps find the slowest and most frequent queries, with who ran them?</p>","o":["<code>system.billing.list_prices</code>","<code>system.query.history</code>","<code>system.access.table_lineage</code>","<code>system.storage.predictive_optimization_operations_history</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Prices per SKU.","It records each query's duration, user, warehouse and bytes read.","Lineage between objects.","Table maintenance."],"e":"Query-level monitoring: <code>system.query.history</code>; then open the slowest queries' profiles."},{"id":"site-035","sec":["A6","P8"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>In the Spark UI, one task of a stage takes 20 minutes while the median task takes 30 seconds. What is the most likely cause?</p>","o":["Too many workers","Data skew","Photon is disabled","The table has too few versions"],"ok":[1],"w":["Would not make one task slow.","One partition holds far more data than the others.","Would slow all tasks evenly.","Irrelevant."],"e":"Max far above the median = skew. More workers do not help one huge task."},{"id":"site-126","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An aggregation by <code>device_id</code> is dominated by one test device. AQE is on, but the stage is still skewed. Why, and what helps?</p>","o":["AQE splits skewed partitions only in joins, not in aggregations; salt the key and aggregate in two stages","AQE needs more workers","Lower <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code>","Broadcast the table"],"ok":[0],"w":["","Workers do not change how AQE plans.","Makes every partition, including the skewed one, bigger.","Broadcasting applies to joins."],"e":"Aggregation skew: salting (or filtering out a known junk key)."},{"id":"sp-71","sec":["P5","A6"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A join between two large tables is slow because one key holds most of the records. What helps?</p>","o":["Keep AQE enabled with skew join handling, or salt the hot key","Increase driver memory","Turn the join into a cross join","Disable the shuffle"],"ok":[0],"w":["AQE splits skewed partitions into smaller tasks.","The driver does not execute the join.","Explodes the data volume.","Distributed joins need a shuffle unless one side is broadcast."],"e":"Join skew: AQE skew join, or salting."},{"id":"site-037","sec":["A6","A3","P5"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A join between a 2 TB fact table and a 5 MB lookup table shows a huge shuffle. What is the best optimisation?</p>","o":["Broadcast the small table","Repartition the large table into 10,000 partitions","Use a cross join","Cache the large table"],"ok":[0],"w":["Each executor receives the small table, so the large one is not shuffled.","Adds another shuffle.","Multiplies rows.","Avoids rereads, not the join shuffle."],"e":"Large-small joins: broadcast the small side (<code>F.broadcast</code> or a <code>BROADCAST</code> hint)."},{"id":"site-080","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>The Spark UI shows a large amount of spill to disk in a stage. What is the likely cause?</p>","o":["Partitions too large for the executors' memory","Too few Delta versions","The table is managed","Photon is enabled"],"ok":[0],"w":["Use more (smaller) partitions, memory-optimised nodes, or fewer columns.","Unrelated.","Unrelated.","Photon does not cause spill."],"e":"Spill = partitions that do not fit in memory."},{"id":"sp-70","sec":["P5","P8"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>The query profile shows a sort operator with a long time, a high memory peak and frequent spill to disk. Which action helps?</p>","o":["Increase the number of shuffle partitions (or use <code>auto</code>) so each partition is smaller","Repartition everything into a single partition","Replace the sort with a filter","Use a broadcast join"],"ok":[0],"w":["Smaller partitions fit in memory and spill less.","Concentrates the sort in one task and worsens the spill.","Changes the query result.","Optimises joins, not sorts."],"e":"Spill fixes: smaller partitions or more memory."},{"id":"site-036","sec":["A6","P8"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A notebook calls <code>df.collect()</code> on a 500 GB DataFrame and fails with an out-of-memory error on the driver. What fixes it?</p>","o":["Add more workers","Do not bring the data to the driver: write it to a table, aggregate it, or use <code>limit</code>","Increase <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code>","Enable Photon"],"ok":[1],"w":["Workers do not add driver memory.","","Affects executors, not the driver's collect.","Does not change what is collected."],"e":"Driver OOM comes from <code>collect()</code>, <code>toPandas()</code> and large broadcasts."},{"id":"sa-44","sec":["A6","A3"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A Spark SQL ETL fails with <code>java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space</code> in the executors. The logic is correct and all the data must be processed. Which TWO actions address the cause?</p>","o":["Use larger worker nodes and enable auto-optimized shuffle","Set a larger number of shuffle partitions explicitly","Cache the dataset to speed up the query","Use a larger driver node and disable auto-optimized shuffle","Filter out data so less is processed"],"ok":[0,1],"w":["More memory per executor, and partitions sized to the real data volume.","Less data per partition means less memory per task.","Caching uses more memory.","The error is in executors, and disabling auto shuffle removes a helpful mechanism.","Changes the business result."],"e":"Executor OOM: more memory per executor or less data per partition. Driver size and caching are distractors."},{"id":"sa-43","sec":["A6","P8"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A pipeline has consistent bottlenecks. In the Spark UI, task CPU time is high relative to total task time. How do you interpret this and what do you do?</p>","o":["The work is CPU-bound: tune executors and cores, or resize the cluster","The cluster is underused: repartition to spread the work","It is a memory problem: increase executor memory","Everything is efficient; change nothing"],"ok":[0],"w":["Executors spent their time computing, not waiting.","That is the reading for low CPU time relative to task time.","Memory pressure shows as spill, GC time or OOM.","Contradicts the bottleneck in the scenario."],"e":"High CPU time = computing; low CPU time = waiting."},{"id":"site-081","sec":["A6","P8"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A query runs on serverless compute and you need to investigate skew. Where do you look?</p>","o":["The Spark UI","The query profile","Ganglia","Only the driver logs"],"ok":[1],"w":["Not available for serverless.","It shows per-operator metrics and flags skew and spill.","A legacy metrics UI for classic clusters.","No per-operator metrics there."],"e":"Serverless and SQL warehouses: query profile instead of Spark UI."},{"id":"sp-68","sec":["P5","P8"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>In Databricks SQL, a slow query shows excessive shuffle. What is the most direct way to find the responsible operator?</p>","o":["Open the query profile and review the top operators","Run <code>EXPLAIN</code> and estimate sizes by hand","Compare the query with and without <code>LIMIT</code>","Look only at overall warehouse utilisation"],"ok":[0],"w":["It shows real time and metrics per operator, such as costly shuffles and joins.","Shows the plan, not actual runtime metrics.","Changes the plan and does not isolate the cause.","Does not show which operator is the problem."],"e":"Query profile = actual metrics per operator."},{"id":"new-perf-01","sec":["P5"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>The query profile of a query filtering <code>orders</code> by <code>customer_id</code> shows that 9,800 of the table's 10,000 files were read. What does this indicate and what is the fix?</p>","o":["The warehouse is too small; scale it up","Poor data skipping: values of <code>customer_id</code> are spread across files; cluster the table on <code>customer_id</code>","The disk cache is disabled","The join strategy is wrong"],"ok":[1],"w":["More compute reads the same files faster but does not read fewer.","Files read versus pruned is the data skipping signal.","Caching helps repeated reads, not file pruning.","The question is about a filter on one table."],"e":"Files read ≈ total files: fix the layout."},{"id":"sp-69","sec":["P8","A6"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Where in the Spark UI do you see the execution plan of a Spark SQL query or DataFrame operation?</p>","o":["The SQL / DataFrame tab","The Jobs tab","The Storage tab","The Environment tab"],"ok":[0],"w":["It shows the physical plan with metrics per operator.","A high-level view of jobs, without the query plan.","Cached data.","Settings and properties."],"e":"Spark UI tabs: Jobs, Stages, SQL/DataFrame (plans), Storage, Environment, Executors."},{"id":"sp-74","sec":["P5","A3"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>How do you reduce the number of output files of a DataFrame without a full shuffle?</p>","o":["<code>coalesce(n)</code>","<code>repartition(n)</code>","<code>cache()</code>","<code>persist(DISK_ONLY)</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["It merges existing partitions without a full shuffle.","Performs a full shuffle.","Does not change partitions.","Only changes where data is stored."],"e":"<code>coalesce</code> reduces partitions cheaply; <code>repartition</code> shuffles."},{"id":"sa-47","sec":["A6","P5"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A dashboard queries the same dataset many times a day with the same filters, and every execution reads everything again from cloud storage. What helps in this specific case?</p>","o":["Caching (the disk cache on the compute, and the warehouse's query result cache), because the pattern is repeated reads of the same data","Liquid clustering on any high-cardinality column","More shuffle partitions","Convert the table to plain Parquet"],"ok":[0],"w":["Data is served from local SSDs or cached results instead of cloud storage.","Helps varied filters skip files; the issue here is rereading.","Affects shuffles, not rereads.","Loses transactions and skipping with no gain."],"e":"Repeated reads of the same data: caching. It does not fix layout or memory problems."},{"id":"new-perf-02","sec":["P5"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Why does the disk cache (Delta cache) speed up repeated queries on the same Delta table?</p>","o":["It keeps the results of previous queries in the driver","It stores copies of the remote data files on the workers' local SSDs in an efficient format, so later reads avoid cloud storage","It compacts small files","It keeps DataFrames in executor memory"],"ok":[1],"w":["That would be a result cache, and not in the driver.","It also detects changed files automatically.","That is <code>OPTIMIZE</code>.","That is the Spark cache (<code>df.cache()</code>)."],"e":"Disk cache = local SSD copies of remote files; Spark cache = memory, manual."},{"id":"new-perf-03","sec":["A3","A6"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A notebook sets <code>spark.default.parallelism = 1000</code> to speed up a DataFrame <code>groupBy</code>, but the number of tasks after the shuffle does not change. Why?</p>","o":["The setting needs a cluster restart","<code>spark.default.parallelism</code> only affects RDD operations; DataFrame shuffles use <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code> (or auto-optimized shuffle)","AQE ignores every setting","Photon disables parallelism"],"ok":[1],"w":["The setting is not the one that controls this.","","AQE coalesces partitions but respects the shuffle settings.","Unrelated."],"e":"DataFrames: <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions</code>. RDDs: <code>spark.default.parallelism</code>."},{"id":"new-perf-04","sec":["A3","A6"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A cluster's driver runs out of memory when collecting results. An engineer runs <code>spark.conf.set(\"spark.driver.memory\", \"64g\")</code> in a notebook, but nothing changes. Why, and what works?</p>","o":["The value must be in megabytes","Driver memory is fixed when the driver starts; choose a larger driver node type (or avoid collecting the data)","<code>spark.driver.memory</code> only applies to serverless","It needs <code>spark.executor.memory</code> too"],"ok":[1],"w":["Units are not the problem.","Adding workers does not help either.","Serverless does not let you set it at all.","Executors are not the problem."],"e":"Driver memory = driver node size, chosen at start."},{"id":"new-perf-05","sec":["A3","A6"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>An old notebook sets <code>spark.sql.shuffle.partitions = 64</code> for a job that has since grown tenfold, and stages now spill heavily. What is the recommended change on Databricks?</p>","o":["Set it to 1 to reduce overhead","Remove the legacy fixed value (or set it to <code>auto</code>) so auto-optimized shuffle sizes partitions to the data, then measure again","Turn off AQE","Cache the input"],"ok":[1],"w":["One huge partition makes spill far worse.","A fixed value disables the automatic tuning.","Removes runtime optimisations.","Uses more memory."],"e":"Prefer auto-optimized shuffle; legacy fixed values silently stop it."},{"id":"new-perf-06","sec":["A3","A6"],"topic":"performance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>An engineer changes three Spark settings at once and the job gets faster on a day with less input data. What is wrong with this tuning approach?</p>","o":["Nothing; faster is faster","Change one setting at a time and compare runs on the same input; otherwise you cannot tell which change helped, or whether any did","Settings must be changed in alphabetical order","Tuning is only allowed on serverless"],"ok":[1],"w":["The cause of the improvement is unknown.","","Not a real rule.","Serverless allows few settings."],"e":"Re-measure after each single change, on comparable input."},{"id":"new-perf-07","sec":["P8","A6"],"topic":"performance","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A nightly job on classic compute fails intermittently with \"executor lost\" errors, and the cluster uses spot workers. Where do you confirm whether spot instances were reclaimed?</p>","o":["The query profile","The cluster event log (and the Spark UI Executors tab) for node loss and spot termination events","<code>system.billing.list_prices</code>","The Delta table history"],"ok":[1],"w":["Not available for classic job clusters' Spark jobs, and it shows operators, not nodes.","","Prices, not node events.","Table operations, not compute events."],"e":"Node-level problems: cluster event log; task-level problems: Spark UI and driver logs."},{"id":"site-129","sec":["A7","P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A schema owner grants <code>SELECT</code> on <code>finance.reporting.invoices</code> to the auditors, but they still get a permission error. What is missing?</p>","o":["<code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> on the table","<code>USE CATALOG</code> on <code>finance</code> and <code>USE SCHEMA</code> on <code>finance.reporting</code>","<code>MODIFY</code> on the table","<code>BROWSE</code> on the metastore"],"ok":[1],"w":["Far more than needed, and still not enough without the parents.","Every access needs the usage privileges on the parents; only their owners (or <code>MANAGE</code> holders) can grant them.","Write access is unrelated to reading.","Metadata visibility, not data access."],"e":"Reading = <code>USE CATALOG</code> + <code>USE SCHEMA</code> + <code>SELECT</code>."},{"id":"sa-12","sec":["A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>An analyst can see the catalog and the schema in Catalog Explorer, but every <code>SELECT</code> on its tables returns a permission error. What is the most likely explanation?</p>","o":["The analyst has <code>USE CATALOG</code> and <code>USE SCHEMA</code> but lacks <code>SELECT</code> on the tables","The metastore is not attached to the workspace","The tables are corrupted","<code>REFRESH</code> must be run on the catalog"],"ok":[0],"w":["Usage privileges let you navigate; reading is a separate privilege.","Then the catalog would not be visible at all.","Corruption gives read errors, not permission errors.","The issue is authorisation, not a metadata cache."],"e":"Seeing that something exists and reading it are different privileges."},{"id":"sa-06","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Pharmaceutical analysts must query the tables of the clinical trials catalog but must not change any data. Which privilege on the tables gives exactly that?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT</code>","<code>MODIFY</code>","<code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code>","<code>USE SCHEMA</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Read access and nothing more: least privilege.","Allows inserts, updates and deletes, exactly what is forbidden.","Includes every write and create privilege.","Only lets you traverse the schema; on its own it reads nothing."],"e":"Traverse with <code>USE</code>, read with <code>SELECT</code>, write with <code>MODIFY</code>."},{"id":"sa-07","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A logistics team must create tables inside the schema <code>operations.routes</code>. It must not create new schemas or catalogs. What is the minimum set of grants?</p>","o":["<code>USE CATALOG</code> on <code>operations</code>, <code>USE SCHEMA</code> and <code>CREATE TABLE</code> on <code>operations.routes</code>","<code>CREATE CATALOG</code> and <code>CREATE TABLE</code> on <code>operations.routes</code>","<code>USE CATALOG</code> on <code>operations</code> and <code>CREATE SCHEMA</code> on <code>operations.routes</code>","<code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> on the catalog"],"ok":[0],"w":["Traverse the catalog, traverse the schema, create tables there. Nothing more.","Creating catalogs goes far beyond the request, and <code>USE SCHEMA</code> is missing.","Allows new schemas, which is excluded, and does not allow creating tables.","Grants everything, including dropping other teams' objects."],"e":"\"Least privilege\" answers never include <code>CREATE SCHEMA</code> or <code>CREATE CATALOG</code> for a create-table task."},{"id":"site-088","sec":["A7","P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A privilege is granted on a schema. Which objects does it apply to?</p>","o":["Only tables that existed at grant time","All current and future objects in the schema","Only views","Only the schema itself"],"ok":[1],"w":["Future objects are included.","Privileges are inherited downward.","All object types in the schema.","Inheritance applies to its children."],"e":"Grants on catalogs and schemas flow down to current and future objects."},{"id":"site-041","sec":["A7","P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A user has <code>SELECT</code> through a grant on the schema. An admin runs <code>REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE t FROM user</code>. What is the result?</p>","o":["The user loses access to <code>t</code>","The command fails","The user still has access to <code>t</code> through the schema-level grant","The user loses access to the whole schema"],"ok":[2],"w":["The schema-level grant still applies.","It succeeds and changes nothing.","Revoke at the level where the privilege was granted.","A table-level revoke cannot affect the schema."],"e":"<code>REVOKE</code> only removes grants at the level where you run it."},{"id":"site-042","sec":["A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An admin wants to block one user explicitly from a table with <code>DENY</code> in Unity Catalog. What should they know?</p>","o":["<code>DENY</code> overrides any <code>GRANT</code>","<code>DENY</code> does not exist in Unity Catalog; anything not granted is denied","<code>DENY</code> only works on views","<code>DENY</code> must be combined with <code>REVOKE</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["There is no <code>DENY</code> in Unity Catalog.","Restructure grants (for example a smaller group) to exclude someone.","It does not exist.","It does not exist."],"e":"<code>DENY</code> belonged to legacy <code>hive_metastore</code> table ACLs. Unity Catalog is grant-only."},{"id":"new-gov-01","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>The group <code>sales</code> has <code>SELECT</code> on schema <code>main.crm</code>. One contractor in that group must not read <code>main.crm.contracts</code>, while everyone else keeps access. What works in Unity Catalog?</p>","o":["<code>DENY SELECT ON TABLE main.crm.contracts TO contractor</code>","<code>REVOKE SELECT ON TABLE main.crm.contracts FROM contractor</code>","Restructure the grants: grant on the schema to a group without the contractor (or grant table by table), so the contractor is never granted that table","Add a row filter that hides all rows from the contractor"],"ok":[2],"w":["<code>DENY</code> does not exist.","The contractor still inherits <code>SELECT</code> from the schema grant through the group.","","Possible as a workaround, but grants are the right tool for access to a table."],"e":"Exclusions are designed with group structure, not with deny rules."},{"id":"site-089","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>To which principals should privileges be granted in Unity Catalog?</p>","o":["Workspace-local groups","Account-level groups and service principals","Only individual users","Only the metastore admin"],"ok":[1],"w":["Not usable for Unity Catalog grants across workspaces.","Service principals are referenced by their application ID.","Hard to maintain as people join and leave.","Others need access too."],"e":"Grant to account groups and service principals."},{"id":"site-084","sec":["A7","P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A group should be able to see table names and metadata in Catalog Explorer, but NOT read data. Which privilege?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT</code>","<code>BROWSE</code>","<code>MODIFY</code>","<code>USE CONNECTION</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Reads data.","Shows objects and metadata without data access.","Writes data.","Uses connections for federation and connectors."],"e":"Discovery without data: <code>BROWSE</code>."},{"id":"new-gov-02","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A security officer must manage grants on the <code>finance</code> catalog (grant, revoke, transfer ownership) but must not be able to read the data. Which privilege fits?</p>","o":["<code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code>","<code>MANAGE</code>","Ownership of the catalog","<code>BROWSE</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Includes <code>SELECT</code> and <code>MODIFY</code>, and still not <code>MANAGE</code>.","It allows managing privileges, transferring ownership, renaming and dropping, without data access.","Owners have all privileges, including reading.","Metadata only; cannot grant."],"e":"<code>MANAGE</code> = administration without data access."},{"id":"sp-31","sec":["P6","A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>A project group must create and manage schemas, tables and volumes in a catalog and give colleagues access to what it creates, but must not rename, drop or transfer ownership of the catalog. What should be granted?</p>","o":["<code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> on the catalog","Make the group the owner of the catalog","<code>USE CATALOG</code> and <code>USE SCHEMA</code>","<code>SELECT</code> on the catalog"],"ok":[0],"w":["It implies every catalog-level privilege (create schemas, tables, volumes, read, write) but not <code>MANAGE</code>; the group owns what it creates and can grant on it, while renaming or dropping the catalog needs ownership or <code>MANAGE</code>.","The owner can rename and drop it, which is to be avoided.","Only navigation.","Read only."],"e":"<code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> does not include <code>MANAGE</code> or ownership."},{"id":"site-090","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A group must use a masking function defined in Unity Catalog. Which privilege on the function?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT</code>","<code>EXECUTE</code>","<code>MODIFY</code>","<code>USE SCHEMA</code> only"],"ok":[1],"w":["Applies to tables and views.","Functions and models are used with <code>EXECUTE</code>.","Applies to table data.","Needed, but not sufficient."],"e":"Functions: <code>EXECUTE</code>."},{"id":"site-045","sec":["A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A group needs to read files stored in a Unity Catalog volume. Which privilege is required, besides <code>USE CATALOG</code> and <code>USE SCHEMA</code>?</p>","o":["<code>SELECT</code>","<code>READ VOLUME</code>","<code>READ FILES</code>","<code>BROWSE</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["For tables and views.","Volumes use <code>READ VOLUME</code> and <code>WRITE VOLUME</code>.","For paths in external locations.","Metadata only."],"e":"Volumes: <code>READ VOLUME</code> / <code>WRITE VOLUME</code>. External locations: <code>READ FILES</code> / <code>WRITE FILES</code>."},{"id":"new-gov-03","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A service principal runs a pipeline that reads <code>main.bronze</code> tables and writes new tables in <code>main.silver</code>. Which grants follow least privilege?</p>","o":["<code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> on the <code>main</code> catalog","<code>USE CATALOG</code> on <code>main</code>; <code>USE SCHEMA</code> and <code>SELECT</code> on <code>main.bronze</code>; <code>USE SCHEMA</code>, <code>SELECT</code>, <code>MODIFY</code> and <code>CREATE TABLE</code> on <code>main.silver</code>","<code>SELECT</code> on both schemas only","Ownership of both schemas"],"ok":[1],"w":["Far more than needed.","","Cannot traverse the catalog or write silver.","Includes dropping them."],"e":"Least privilege per layer: read where you read, write where you write."},{"id":"site-040","sec":["A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>What happens when you run <code>DROP TABLE</code> on an EXTERNAL table in Unity Catalog?</p>","o":["Metadata and data files are deleted","Only the metadata is removed; the data files remain in the external location","Nothing; external tables cannot be dropped","The table becomes managed"],"ok":[1],"w":["That is the managed table behaviour.","","They can.","Dropping never converts a table."],"e":"External: drop metadata only. Managed: drop data too (recoverable with <code>UNDROP</code> for 7 days)."},{"id":"site-086","sec":["A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A MANAGED table was dropped by mistake two days ago. What can recover it?</p>","o":["<code>RESTORE TABLE</code>","<code>UNDROP TABLE</code>","<code>VACUUM</code>","It cannot be recovered"],"ok":[1],"w":["Restores a version of an existing table, not a dropped one.","Dropped tables can be recovered for 7 days, with their grants and properties.","Deletes files; the opposite.","It can, within 7 days."],"e":"<code>UNDROP</code> within 7 days; <code>SHOW TABLES DROPPED</code> lists candidates."},{"id":"site-134","sec":["A7","P5"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>You need to move an external Delta table to managed storage while keeping its name, permissions and history. Which command?</p>","o":["<code>CREATE TABLE new AS SELECT * FROM old</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE t SET MANAGED</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE t SET EXTERNAL</code>","<code>CONVERT TO DELTA</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["A new table without history or grants.","Converts in place and keeps name, grants and history.","There is no managed-to-external conversion for Unity Catalog tables.","Turns Parquet into Delta; does not change managed or external."],"e":"<code>SET MANAGED</code> brings external tables into managed storage, unlocking predictive optimization."},{"id":"new-gov-04","sec":["A7","P5"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A team creates new Delta tables that only Databricks reads and writes. It wants the least maintenance and the best performance over time. Which table type should it use?</p>","o":["Unity Catalog managed tables","External tables in a team-owned path","Parquet tables without Delta","Tables in <code>hive_metastore</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Predictive optimization, automatic clustering and file sizing, metadata caching and <code>UNDROP</code> come with them.","Need their own maintenance jobs and get no predictive optimization.","Lose transactions and history.","Legacy, outside Unity Catalog governance."],"e":"Default to managed tables; use external tables when other systems must own the files."},{"id":"new-gov-05","sec":["A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>Which command shows whether <code>main.sales.orders</code> is a managed or an external table?</p>","o":["<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY main.sales.orders</code>","<code>DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED main.sales.orders</code> (the <em>Type</em> row), or <code>information_schema.tables</code>","<code>SHOW GRANTS ON TABLE main.sales.orders</code>","<code>OPTIMIZE main.sales.orders</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Shows versions and operations.","","Shows privileges.","Compacts files."],"e":"Table type: <code>DESCRIBE TABLE EXTENDED</code>, Catalog Explorer or <code>information_schema.tables</code>."},{"id":"new-gov-06","sec":["A7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>To create an external table on <code>abfss://raw@acct.dfs.core.windows.net/events/</code>, what must exist in Unity Catalog first?</p>","o":["A volume on that path","A storage credential for the cloud identity and an external location covering that path, plus <code>CREATE EXTERNAL TABLE</code> on the location","A connection of type <code>abfss</code>","Nothing; any path can be used directly"],"ok":[1],"w":["Volumes hold files; a table needs an external location.","","Connections are for databases and services.","Unity Catalog governs access to storage paths."],"e":"Storage credential (identity) + external location (path) = governed access to cloud storage."},{"id":"site-091","sec":["A1","P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A development workspace and an analytics workspace in the same region both need the governed <code>sales</code> catalog with the same permissions. What is the recommended approach?</p>","o":["Deep clone the tables into each workspace","Attach both workspaces to the region's single Unity Catalog metastore","Create a second metastore for the analytics workspace","Share the catalog with OpenSharing between the two workspaces"],"ok":[1],"w":["Two copies of the truth.","The catalog appears in both, and grants apply in both.","One metastore per region is the model.","Sharing is for other metastores or organisations."],"e":"Same region, same metastore, same data and grants."},{"id":"new-gov-07","sec":["P6","P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>The <code>prod</code> catalog must only be accessible from the production workspace, even though development workspaces are attached to the same metastore. What do you configure?</p>","o":["Separate metastores per workspace","Bind the <code>prod</code> catalog to the production workspace (workspace-catalog binding)","Revoke all grants in development","A row filter on every table"],"ok":[1],"w":["One metastore per region; not the tool for this.","Other workspaces cannot access it, whatever the user's grants.","Users with grants could still reach it from any attached workspace.","Filters rows by user, not by workspace."],"e":"Workspace bindings limit which workspaces can reach a catalog."},{"id":"sp-56","sec":["P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>When configuring job permissions under least privilege, what should you do?</p>","o":["Give each user only the level their role needs, such as Can View or Can Manage Run","Give everyone Can Manage to avoid support tickets","Give everyone Can Manage Run and one group Can Manage","Use only folder permissions, never per-job permissions"],"ok":[0],"w":["","Lets anyone change or delete jobs.","Running every job without need is still too much.","Loses per-job control."],"e":"Workspace objects have their own ACLs; apply least privilege there too."},{"id":"new-gov-08","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A table created by an engineer who is leaving the company feeds production. What should be done with it?</p>","o":["Nothing; tables do not have owners","Transfer ownership to a group or service principal (<code>ALTER TABLE ... SET OWNER TO ...</code>)","Recreate it with <code>CREATE TABLE AS SELECT</code>","Grant <code>ALL PRIVILEGES</code> to the replacement engineer"],"ok":[1],"w":["Every securable has an owner.","Production objects should not depend on one person's account.","Loses history and grants.","Ownership stays with the departing person."],"e":"Production ownership belongs to groups or service principals."},{"id":"sp-32","sec":["P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>In a workspace where Unity Catalog was enabled automatically, new users can create tables in the <code>default</code> schema of the workspace catalog but cannot read its other schemas. Why?</p>","o":["They received <code>USE CATALOG</code> plus privileges on the <code>default</code> schema only","Unity Catalog has no privilege inheritance","They lack <code>BROWSE</code>","Schemas created by other users are always private"],"ok":[0],"w":["Other schemas need explicit grants.","It does: grants on catalogs and schemas are inherited.","<code>BROWSE</code> shows metadata; it does not explain data access.","No such automatic rule."],"e":"The workspace catalog gives every user <code>USE CATALOG</code>; everything else must be granted."},{"id":"sp-37","sec":["P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which command adds a tag to a table in Unity Catalog?</p>","o":["<code>ALTER TABLE t SET TAGS ('domain' = 'finance')</code>","<code>COMMENT ON TABLE t TAGS ('domain' = 'finance')</code>","<code>APPLY TAGS ON t VALUES (...)</code>","<code>SET TAGS FOR t AS (...)</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["","<code>COMMENT</code> sets the description.","Not a command.","Not a command."],"e":"<code>SET TAGS</code> / <code>UNSET TAGS</code> on catalogs, schemas, tables, volumes and columns."},{"id":"new-gov-09","sec":["P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A data steward wants a list of every column tagged <code>pii</code> across the <code>crm</code> catalog. What is the quickest way?</p>","o":["Open each table in Catalog Explorer","Query <code>crm.information_schema.column_tags WHERE tag_name = 'pii'</code>","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code> on every table","<code>SHOW GRANTS ON CATALOG crm</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Slow for hundreds of tables.","Tags are queryable metadata.","History does not list tags.","Lists privileges, not tags."],"e":"<code>information_schema</code> makes tags and comments queryable."},{"id":"sp-38","sec":["P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>There are hundreds of legacy tables without column descriptions. How can comments be produced at scale with AI?</p>","o":["In Catalog Explorer, use AI-generated comments and review them before saving","<code>DESCRIBE HISTORY</code>","<code>df.describe()</code>","Write every comment by hand with <code>COMMENT ON COLUMN</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["Suggestions are based on names, types and sample patterns.","Shows operations, not meaning.","Numeric statistics, not descriptions.","Works, but takes months for thousands of columns."],"e":"Comments and tags make data discoverable; AI suggestions speed up documentation."},{"id":"sa-08","sec":["A7","P7"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>Before changing the type of a column in a heavily used table, an engineer wants to know which notebooks, jobs and dashboards depend on it. Which Unity Catalog feature gives this view?</p>","o":["Data lineage, which shows upstream and downstream dependencies","The audit logs filtered by table name","The Spark UI of the cluster that read the table","The Delta table history"],"ok":[0],"w":["Lineage is captured automatically for tables, columns, notebooks, jobs, dashboards and queries.","Record accesses over time, not a dependency graph.","Shows execution, not dependencies.","Shows operations on the table, not who consumes it."],"e":"Impact analysis = lineage. History = what happened to the table. Audit = who accessed it."},{"id":"sa-11","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"governance","level":"A","src":"sa","q":"<p>A data engineer manages several external tables whose files sit in different storage containers. Each team must access only the tables of its own domain. How should access be managed?</p>","o":["Grant privileges per object in Unity Catalog, table by table or schema by schema","Set permissions on the storage containers in the cloud provider","Create one role with full access and give it to every team","Grant permissions at workspace level so they apply to every table"],"ok":[0],"w":["Unity Catalog governs external tables just like managed ones, with auditable SQL grants at any level.","Coarse (all or nothing per container) and outside Unity Catalog governance and audit.","Removes the separation between domains that the requirement asks for.","Far too broad; it cannot separate teams."],"e":"An external table is still a Unity Catalog object. Managing access in the storage layer is the classic anti-pattern."},{"id":"site-130","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Sales staff are in account groups <code>sales_UK</code>, <code>sales_FR</code> and so on. Each team should see only its country's rows. Which row filter function body fits?</p>","o":["<code>RETURN is_member('sales_' || country)</code>","<code>RETURN is_account_group_member('sales_' || country)</code>","<code>RETURN current_user() = country</code>","<code>RETURN country IN (SELECT * FROM groups)</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["<code>is_member</code> checks workspace-local groups.","One function on one table serves every team.","Compares a user name with a country.","Not tied to the current user."],"e":"Filters and masks check account groups with <code>is_account_group_member</code>."},{"id":"site-044","sec":["A7"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Regional managers should only see rows whose <code>region</code> matches their own. What should be used?</p>","o":["A column mask","A row filter","A dynamic view with <code>SELECT *</code>","<code>OPTIMIZE ... ZORDER BY region</code>"],"ok":[1],"w":["Changes values in a column, not which rows appear.","<code>ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER f ON (region)</code>.","<code>SELECT *</code> alone filters nothing.","A layout command, not security."],"e":"Hide rows: row filter. Hide values: column mask."},{"id":"site-043","sec":["A7"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Only members of <code>hr_admins</code> should see the real salary column; everyone else should see NULL. What is the best solution?</p>","o":["A copy of the table without the column","A column mask function using <code>is_account_group_member('hr_admins')</code>","A row filter","Revoke <code>SELECT</code> from everyone"],"ok":[1],"w":["Duplicates data and must be kept in sync.","<code>ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN salary SET MASK f</code>.","Hides rows, not values.","Nobody could use the table."],"e":"Per-group visibility of a column: column mask."},{"id":"sp-29","sec":["P6","A7"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>In the <code>customers</code> table, the sales team may only see rows of its own region, and users outside <code>admins</code> must see emails masked, without duplicating data. Which solution?</p>","o":["A row filter and a column mask, defined as SQL functions and applied to the table","One view per region","One copy of the table per region","Filtering in the BI tool"],"ok":[0],"w":["Applied once to the table, enforced for every tool.","More objects to maintain, and the base table stays unprotected.","Duplicates data, which is forbidden.","Security outside the data platform can be bypassed."],"e":"Fine-grained access belongs on the table, not in copies or tools."},{"id":"sp-30","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which command applies a masking function to a column of a Unity Catalog table?</p>","o":["<code>ALTER TABLE t ALTER COLUMN email SET MASK mask_email</code>","<code>ALTER TABLE t SET ROW FILTER mask_email ON (email)</code>","<code>GRANT MASK ON email TO analysts</code>","<code>CREATE MASKING POLICY ...</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["","Filters rows instead of masking values.","Not a command.","Syntax from other platforms."],"e":"<code>SET MASK</code> on a column; <code>SET ROW FILTER</code> on a table."},{"id":"new-privacy-01","sec":["A7"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A column mask hides the real <code>salary</code> from analysts. An analyst runs <code>SELECT * FROM employees WHERE salary &gt; 100000</code>. What happens?</p>","o":["The filter uses the real salary, so the analyst can infer hidden values","The filter is evaluated on the masked values, so the real salaries cannot be inferred this way","The query fails because masked columns cannot be filtered","The mask is suspended for <code>WHERE</code> clauses"],"ok":[1],"w":["Masks are applied before the user's filters.","","It runs on masked values.","It is not."],"e":"Masks apply before <code>WHERE</code> and joins."},{"id":"site-135","sec":["A7"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>An admin drops a SQL UDF that a table still uses as a column mask. What happens?</p>","o":["The mask is removed automatically","The table becomes inaccessible until the mask is dropped (<code>ALTER COLUMN ... DROP MASK</code>)","The column shows NULL to everyone","Nothing"],"ok":[1],"w":["The table still references it.","","Queries fail instead.","The table breaks."],"e":"Drop the mask or row filter first, then the function."},{"id":"new-privacy-02","sec":["A7"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"new","q":"<p>A report must combine <code>orders</code> and <code>customers</code>, showing customer emails only to the support group and hiding orders from other regions, for a BI team that should never query the base tables. Which approach fits best?</p>","o":["A column mask on the report","A dynamic view joining both tables, using <code>is_account_group_member</code> in <code>CASE</code> expressions and in the <code>WHERE</code>, with <code>SELECT</code> granted on the view only","Copy both tables into a reporting schema without emails","Give the BI team <code>SELECT</code> on both tables and trust the BI tool"],"ok":[1],"w":["Masks cannot be set on views.","Dynamic views suit protected results built from several tables.","Duplicates data.","Exposes the base tables."],"e":"Single table: filters and masks. Combined or reshaped result: dynamic view."},{"id":"site-132","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>Every email column in the <code>crm</code> catalog (300 tables, growing weekly) must be masked, and table owners must not be able to remove the masking. What is the best approach?</p>","o":["A column mask on each table","Tag the email columns and create one ABAC column mask policy on catalog <code>crm</code> with <code>MATCH COLUMNS has_tag_value('pii', 'email')</code>","A dynamic view per table","Revoke <code>SELECT</code> from everyone"],"ok":[1],"w":["300 manual steps, owners can remove them, and new tables are missed.","It covers current and future tagged columns, and table owners cannot override it.","300 views, and the base tables stay open.","Nobody could use the data."],"e":"Central rules at scale: ABAC policies on governed tags."},{"id":"site-133","sec":["A7","P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A table is tagged <code>pii</code>, but its columns are not. An ABAC column mask policy uses <code>MATCH COLUMNS has_tag_value('pii', ...)</code>. Are the columns masked?</p>","o":["Yes, columns inherit table tags","No, <code>MATCH COLUMNS</code> only checks tags set directly on columns","Yes, but only for new rows","Only on SQL warehouses"],"ok":[1],"w":["Column conditions only see tags on the columns.","Tag the columns.","Tags do not work per row.","Compute type is not the issue."],"e":"Column tags must be set on the columns themselves."},{"id":"new-privacy-03","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>After an ABAC row filter policy was created on a schema for the group <code>analysts</code>, a manager asks whether it gives analysts access to the schema's tables. What is the answer?</p>","o":["Yes, policies grant <code>SELECT</code> on matching tables","No; the policy only changes what analysts see in tables they can already read; access still comes from <code>GRANT</code>","Yes, but only for tables created after the policy","Only if the tables are managed"],"ok":[1],"w":["Policies do not grant privileges.","","Policies never grant.","Unrelated."],"e":"ABAC policies restrict; grants allow."},{"id":"new-privacy-04","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>An ABAC mask policy hides <code>ssn</code> columns from <code>account users</code>. A nightly pipeline, running as a service principal, must compute a hash of the real SSN. The pipeline now hashes the masked value. What should be changed?</p>","o":["Disable the policy during the nightly run","Add the pipeline's service principal to the policy's <code>EXCEPT</code> list","Run the pipeline as a regular user","Copy the table without the policy"],"ok":[1],"w":["Leaves data exposed and is not auditable.","Pipelines evaluate policies as their run-as identity.","Users are also in <code>account users</code>, and production should not run as a person.","Creates an unprotected copy."],"e":"Policies apply to pipelines too; exempt the service principal explicitly when it needs real values."},{"id":"sp-41","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>Which practice best guarantees consistent PII masking across dozens of batch and streaming pipelines?</p>","o":["Central masking functions in Unity Catalog, applied to the sensitive columns","Each pipeline implements its own masking function","Mask only direct identifiers","Trust external systems to inherit the mask"],"ok":[0],"w":["One definition applies to every query and pipeline.","Different logic and gaps in coverage.","Combined quasi-identifiers can still re-identify people.","Outside Unity Catalog the policy does not apply."],"e":"One governed function, applied everywhere."},{"id":"new-privacy-05","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Customer support notes are free text and sometimes contain names, phone numbers and emails. Analysts need the notes, without the personal data. What can a pipeline apply?</p>","o":["A column mask that returns NULL for the whole notes column","<code>ai_mask(notes, ARRAY('person', 'phone', 'email'))</code> when building the silver table","<code>sha2(notes, 256)</code>","A row filter"],"ok":[1],"w":["Hides everything, including useful content.","Entities of the listed types are replaced inside the text.","Destroys the text entirely.","Removes whole rows."],"e":"PII inside free text: entity masking (<code>ai_mask</code>), applied in the pipeline."},{"id":"sp-40","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"sp","q":"<p>When copying a production sample to a sandbox, users' emails must not appear. Which code fits?</p>","o":["<code>df.withColumn(\"email\", sha2(col(\"email\"), 256))</code>","<code>df.withColumn(\"email_hash\", sha2(col(\"email\"), 256))</code>","<code>regexp_replace</code> changing only the domain","<code>df.withColumn(\"email\", expr(\"uuid()\"))</code>"],"ok":[0],"w":["The original column is overwritten with a deterministic hash, so joins by user still work.","The original email stays in the DataFrame.","The part before <code>@</code> still identifies the person.","Removes the PII but prevents any consistent per-user analysis."],"e":"Pseudonymise in place; add a secret salt so the hash cannot be reversed with a list of known emails."},{"id":"new-privacy-06","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>Payment card numbers must be replaced in analytics tables, but the fraud team must occasionally recover the real number for a specific case. Which technique fits?</p>","o":["Hashing with SHA-256","Tokenization: replace the number with a random token and keep the token-to-value mapping in a separate, tightly restricted table","Suppression of the column","Generalization to the card's first four digits"],"ok":[1],"w":["One-way: the fraud team could not recover the value.","Reversible only for authorised users.","Nothing could be recovered.","Loses the value permanently."],"e":"Reversible for a few, opaque for everyone else: tokenization."},{"id":"new-privacy-07","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A research dataset must not allow re-identification. It contains exact birth dates, full postcodes and gender. Which technique reduces the risk from these quasi-identifiers while keeping the data useful?</p>","o":["Hash each column","Generalization: birth date to year or age band, postcode to district","Tokenization of each column","Leave them; they are not direct identifiers"],"ok":[1],"w":["Hashes of low-cardinality values are easy to reverse and keep the same linkability.","Less precise values match many people.","Keeps exact linkability through the tokens.","Combined, they often identify a person."],"e":"Quasi-identifiers: generalize (and suppress rare combinations)."},{"id":"new-privacy-08","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A customer exercises the GDPR right to erasure. Their rows were deleted with <code>DELETE</code> from every table yesterday. Which statement is true?</p>","o":["The data is gone for good; nothing else is needed","The rows can still be read through time travel (and the change data feed) until old files are removed: run <code>REORG TABLE ... APPLY (PURGE)</code> where deletion vectors are used, then <code>VACUUM</code> after the retention period","<code>OPTIMIZE</code> alone removes the old files","<code>RESTORE</code> permanently deletes history"],"ok":[1],"w":["Old files still hold it.","","It rewrites files but old ones stay until vacuumed.","It does the opposite."],"e":"Erasure on Delta = <code>DELETE</code> + purge + <code>VACUUM</code>, within the legal deadline."},{"id":"new-privacy-09","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A table has Change Data Feed enabled for downstream pipelines. After a GDPR delete, the privacy officer asks whether the deleted values still exist anywhere in the table's storage. What is the answer?</p>","o":["No; CDF never stores deleted values","Yes; the change files hold the deleted row until <code>VACUUM</code> removes them after the retention period","Only if the table is external","Only if deletion vectors are disabled"],"ok":[1],"w":["It records deletes with the deleted row's values.","Set retention so files disappear within the deadline.","Same for managed tables.","CDF files exist either way."],"e":"Change feeds and time travel are copies of history; erasure must account for them."},{"id":"new-privacy-10","sec":["P6"],"topic":"privacy","level":"P","src":"new","q":"<p>A policy requires clickstream events older than two years to be deleted permanently, every day. Which implementation fits?</p>","o":["Set <code>delta.logRetentionDuration</code> to two years","A scheduled job that runs <code>DELETE FROM clicks WHERE event_date &lt; current_date() - INTERVAL 730 DAYS</code>, with <code>VACUUM</code> (or predictive optimization) removing the old files after the file retention period","<code>VACUUM clicks RETAIN 17520 HOURS</code>","A row filter hiding old rows"],"ok":[1],"w":["Controls history entries, not data rows.","","Removes old file versions, not current rows older than two years.","Hides them but keeps the data."],"e":"Retention = scheduled delete + vacuum. Hiding is not deleting."},{"id":"site-131","sec":["A7"],"topic":"privacy","level":"A","src":"site","q":"<p>A job on dedicated access mode with Databricks Runtime 15.3 cannot read a table that has a column mask. What fixes it?</p>","o":["Remove the mask","Use Databricks Runtime 15.4 LTS or later (with serverless compute enabled in the workspace), or standard access mode, or a SQL warehouse","Grant <code>MODIFY</code> on the table","Add the user to the mask's EXCEPT list"],"ok":[1],"w":["That defeats the security requirement.","On dedicated compute, filtering for masked or filtered tables runs on serverless and needs 15.4 LTS or later.","Write privileges do not change how masks are evaluated.","Masks are SQL functions; there is no EXCEPT list on a mask, and exempting users is not the problem here."],"e":"Reading tables with row filters or column masks: SQL warehouses, standard access mode, or dedicated access mode on 15.4 LTS or later with serverless enabled."}]};
